    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/Vet-Med-Student-Laboratory-Assistant_JR0000742-1": {
      "title": "Vet Med Student Laboratory Assistant",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-17\nPosted: Posted 19 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nDepartment: Carlson College of Veterinary Medicine - Vet Diagnostic Lab Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents: ● A Resume ● A Cover Letter For additional information please contact: Donna Mulrooney at Donna.mulrooney@oregonstate.edu We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status. This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a Criminal History Check and be determined to be position qualified as per OSU Standard 576-055-0000 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in Youth Programs may have additional Criminal History Checks every 2 years. OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire. Note: All job offers are contingent upon Human Resources final approval Job Description: This recruitment will be used to fill one part-time (a maximum of 24 hours per week) Student Laboratory Assistant and Support Services position for the Carlson College of Veterinary Medicine-Vet Diagnostic Lab at Oregon State University (OSU). This position will provide daily support services for laboratories throughout the Oregon Veterinary Diagnostic Laboratory. This includes light janitorial tasks, including empty garbage, recycling, and general cleaning. Additionally, assistance is needed daily to unpack specimens shipped to the laboratory for testing. Strict protocols must be followed to ensure specimen integrity, chain of custody and biosafety. Must be available to work during the hours that packages are received (10 am - 2 pm). If funding permits, employment may be extended through the upcoming school year. Position Duties Open packages containing specimens sent to the lab for testing. May involve use of a biosafety cabinet if packaging is compromised Triage, sort and stabilize specimens, record information for chain of custody Deliver specimens to laboratories within VRL and Magruder Hall Empty regular trash and recycling from multiple rooms throughout the laboratory General cleaning; may include sweeping, mopping, dusting, etc. Other support tasks as assigned Transferable Skill Development OSU is committed to ensuring students are prepared for success after graduation through intentional skill development as student employees. The competencies students develop while working at OSU are defined by the National Association of Colleges and Employers (NACE) as the essential skills employers seek in their hires. In this position, students learn and build vital career-readiness skills in the primary areas below:   Communication, Teamwork, Technology Professionalism Qualifications: Full Employment Eligibility Requirements can be found here:   https://hr.oregonstate.edu/student-employment-manual/500-employment-eligibility-requirements Must be academically enrolled in a high school, community college, or university and pursuing a program or course of study Must meet Academic Standing Requirements; students on academic suspension are not eligible for employment Must meet the applicable minimal enrollment standard High School student: Regularly enrolled in a high school or participating in a home-schooling program Undergraduate and post-baccalaureate student: 6 credit hours per term Undergraduate international student: 12 credit hours per term* Graduate student officially admitted to Graduate School: 5 credit hours per term Graduate international student officially admitted to Graduate School: 9 credit hours per term* *International students may be allowed to carry fewer hours than specified above and still be considered “full-time” by the United States Citizenship and Immigration Services ( USCIS ). A reduced course load is approved by the   Office of International Services   ( OIS ), and must be provided to the Student Employment Center. Required Ability to follow verbal and written instructions without deviation Experience following biological laboratory safety procedures Ability to use both hands to open boxes and lift up to 25 pounds Must be able to document required information for each shipment with legible handwriting Completed courses in biology, zoology, chemistry, medical terminology or related coursework This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a Criminal History Check and be determined to be position qualified as per OSU Standard 576-055-0000 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in Youth Programs may have additional Criminal History Checks every 2 years. Preferred Previous work experience in a laboratory A working knowledge of basic medical terminology Working Conditions/Schedule: Work schedule is 10:00 AM – 2:00 PM, Monday – Friday, except university holidays when we are closed. Possibility of additional hours if warranted • Timeliness and attendance during your assigned shift is expected. Unexcused or repeat absences will result in termination. • May be exposed to specimens that have strong odors, degrading tissue, bodily fluids, blood, fecal material and may contain human pathogens. Salary Range: Anticipated Start Date: 2026-08-19 Anticipated End Date:"
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Bend/Student-Dining-Services-Assistant_JR0000156": {
      "title": "Student Dining Services Assistant",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-07-28\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nPart time\n\nDepartment: OSU Cascades Housing and Dining Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents: A Resume For additional information please contact: Kirsten Side at Kirsten.Side@osucascades.edu Offers of employment are contingent upon meeting all minimum qualifications including the Criminal History Check requirement. We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status. This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a Criminal History Check and be determined to be position qualified as per OSU Standard 576-055-0000 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in Youth Programs may have additional Criminal History Checks every 2 years. OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire. Note: All job offers are contingent upon Human Resources final approval. Job Description: Join the Dining Services team at OSU-Cascades in Bend, Oregon! We operate The Beaver Dam, our campus dining hall serving students, faculty, staff, and the community; Study Grounds, our campus coffee shop; and EDGE Catering, supporting events across campus. Most student employees begin in The Beaver Dam, where you'll learn the fundamentals of food service while building skills in customer service, teamwork, food preparation, and working in a fast-paced environment. As you gain experience, you may have opportunities to cross-train in Study Grounds or assist with EDGE Catering, and Study Grounds baristas are selected from our Beaver Dam crew. We operate as one team, and wherever you're scheduled, you'll support each other across all three areas. We know you're a student first. You'll work 10–24 hours per week on a schedule that's as consistent as we can make it, always built around your class schedule and medical or religious obligations, with our best effort to work around other commitments. Students aren't required to work during university break periods. Dining Services covers the cost of your Food Handler Card upon hire, provides a work shirt and hat, and offers food and beverage perks for employees. Hired in fall? You're on the team for the full academic year (or the remainder of the year if hired later) and returning students don't need to reapply after summer break. Whether you're serving dinner in the Beaver Dam, crafting coffee drinks in Study Grounds, or helping cater a campus event, you'll play an important role in creating a positive experience for the OSU-Cascades community while gaining practical skills you can take into any future career. About Us We operate The Beaver Dam, our campus dining hall serving students, faculty, staff, and the community; Study Grounds, our campus coffee shop; and EDGE Catering, supporting events across campus. We operate as one team, and wherever you're scheduled, you'll support each other across all three areas. Position Duties: Prepare, portion, serve and replenish food on the service line in The Beaver Dam dining hall while following food safety and sanitation standards Provide friendly, efficient customer service for students, faculty, staff, campus and community guests ​Operate point-of-sale (POS) systems and accurately process cash and credit transactions Maintain food and safety sanitation standard, including organizing, well-stocked dining, dishwashing and cleaning of service and kitchen/service areas Assist with opening, closing, and routine cleaning tasks to support daily operations​ Work collaboratively with teammates to provide a positive dining experience in a fast-paced environment Support EDGE Catering events, including setup, service, and breakdown ​ Support daily dining operations by adapting to changing priorities and assisting in a variety of service areas Prepare espresso drinks and coffee shop items at Study Grounds (assignment selected from Beaver Dam team members) Stock, rotate, and restock food, beverages, and supplies across service areas Work flexibly across all Dining Services locations as one team, supporting wherever needed Schedule and Terms: Schedules are as consistent as we can make them, always built around your class schedule and medical or religious obligations, with our best effort to work around other commitments. Students aren't required to work during university break periods. Hired in fall? You're on the team for the full academic year (or the remainder of the year if hired later) and returning students don't need to reapply after summer break. Perks: Work shirt and hat provided, food and beverage perks for employees, and practical customer service and food industry skills you can take into any future career. Transferable Skill Development OSU is committed to ensuring students are prepared for success after graduation through intentional skill development as student employees. The competencies students develop while working at OSU are defined by the National Association of Colleges and Employers (NACE) as the essential skills employers seek in their hires. In this position, students learn and build vital career-readiness skills in the primary areas below:   Communication, Critical Thinking, Teamwork, Qualifications: Full Employment Eligibility Requirements can be found here:   https://hr.oregonstate.edu/student-employment-manual/500-employment-eligibility-requirements Must be academically enrolled in a high school, community college, or university and pursuing a program or course of study Must meet Academic Standing Requirements; students on academic suspension are not eligible for employment Must meet the applicable minimal enrollment standard High School student: Regularly enrolled in a high school or participating in a home-schooling program Undergraduate and post-baccalaureate student: 6 credit hours per term Undergraduate international student: 12 credit hours per term* Graduate student officially admitted to Graduate School: 5 credit hours per term Graduate international student officially admitted to Graduate School: 9 credit hours per term* *International students may be allowed to carry fewer hours than specified above and still be considered “full-time” by the United States Citizenship and Immigration Services ( USCIS ). A reduced course load is approved by the   Office of International Services   ( OIS ), and must be provided to the Student Employment Center. Working Conditions/Schedule: • Student Dining Services Assistants support daily operations in the Beaver Dam, with opportunities to cross-train in Study Grounds and assist with EDGE Catering as operational needs allow. Student employees work 10–24 hours per week and gain hands-on experience in food preparation, customer service, food safety, and teamwork while helping create a welcoming dining experience for the OSU-Cascades community. • This is an entry-level position, and no prior food service experience is required. On-the-job training is provided, and applicants with previous food service, hospitality, or customer service experience are encouraged to apply. This position's pay range is $16.80 - $18.50 per hour. • Location: This is an in-person student employment position located at the OSU-Cascades campus in Bend, Oregon. It is not located on the Corvallis campus. https://osucascades.edu/dining • Interviews will generally be conducted in person during fall, winter, and spring terms. During summer or other periods when students may not be on campus, interviews may be conducted remotely. Salary Range: $16.30 - $18.50 Anticipated Start Date: 2026-09-01 Anticipated End Date: 2027-06-30"
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/Lead-Teacher-Pool---2027_JR0001025": {
      "title": "Lead Teacher Pool - 2027",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-04\nPosted: Posted Yesterday\nFull time\n\nDepartment: Family Resource Center Special Instructions to Applicants: Applications will be reviewed on an on-going basis as vacancies arise. To ensure full consideration, applications must be received by September 18, 2026. Applications will continue to be accepted after the full consideration date, until a sufficient applicant pool has been achieved or the position is filled. The closing date is subject to change without notice to applicants. When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents:   1) A resume   2) A cover letter indicating how your qualifications and experience have prepared you for this position.    Starting salary will be commensurate with skills, education, and experience.   For additional information please contact:  Tania Henry tania.henry@oregonstate.edu 541-737-4906 Job Summary: The Family Resource Center’s Corvallis Early Care and Education Centers are seeking multiple Lead Teachers. These are a full-time (1.00   FTE ), 12-month, professional faculty positions. The Lead Teacher is the lead teacher of a single classroom serving children in one of the Family Resource Center’s Corvallis Early Care and Education (ECE) centers. Campus early care and education centers are committed to continually discovering, creating, and sustaining a teaching and learning environment that supports inquiry, diversity and equity.  This position requires knowledge of child development, excellent communication skills and tremendous patience to provide a safe, emotionally secure and nurturing environment for the healthy development of young children.  The Lead Teacher has responsibility for all classroom program operations including the engagement and support of children and families, coordination and supervision of staff, center procedures, curriculum planning and implementation, developmental assessments, medication management as needed and record keeping. Guidelines used for operation will follow Department of Early Learning and Care’s Child Care regulations, state health regulations, and program rules and philosophy. This position supervises staff and provides, as needed, appropriate role modeling and close supervision and mentoring for students enrolled in the Human Development and Family Sciences (HDFS) program. The Lead teacher will build and maintain relationships with the parent(s) of each child and ensure clear communication on each child’s progress and care plan. The Lead Teacher will work collaboratively with other program staff to provide a high quality early care and education program of direct service to young children and their families. This position reports to the Site Director of Azalea ECE Center. This position is informed by the fields of Early Childhood Education, Child Development and Human Development and maintains a working knowledge and compliance with the Oregon DELC Child Care licensing standards and is knowledgeable about the trends and best practices in the field of early care and education. Key Responsibilities: Develop and implement a developmentally appropriate, play-based, culturally responsive, and equity-oriented educational program with an emergent curriculum for an assigned program and age range of children. Model exemplary and high quality practice and professionalism for students who are engaged in early childhood professional preparation and other related fields. Family Engagement: ensure consistent and clear communication with parents, maintaining an atmosphere of warmth and openness. Discuss information about their child and program information; provide parenting skills and child development theory educational opportunities for parents of children enrolled in program; write parent updates and reports; participate in \"open house\" for parents; share individualized care plans with parents. Prepare and maintain a safe, attractive, neat, and functional environment, both indoors and outdoors, and utilize the environment as a teaching tool. Repeatedly lift, reach (overhead, at and below shoulder level), squat, crouch, climb, sit, bend, stretch, twist, grasp/move small objects, push and pull, and engage in all other physical movements needed to fully participate in supervision and curriculum and care delivery activities, which take place while standing, sitting (including on floor or low chairs), and walking. Form a positive, supportive team atmosphere with other program teachers and staff members and contribute to the Center’s professional community through attendance and participation at staff meetings, classroom team meetings, and other Center staff development opportunities. Train and supervise assistant teachers, student aides, practicum students, and/or volunteers. Qualifications: Required Qualifications: BS or BA in Human Development, Child Development, Early Childhood Education, or related field OR Step 9 in ORO. 3+ year of experience in an ECE program. Proven experience applying ECE theory in daily activities, and ability to adapt to the individual needs of children. Experience mentoring and supervising classroom assistants/support staff Demonstrable contributions to promoting and enhancing inclusive excellence. Effective verbal and written communication. Must be enrolled in the Oregon’s Office of Child Care Central Background Registry, hold a current Oregon Approved Food Handlers card, and a current First Aid/CPR card by time of appointment. This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a criminal history check and be determined to be position qualified as per University Standard: 05-010 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in youth programs may have additional criminal history checks every   24 months .   Preferred Qualifications: 5+ years of experience in birth to three ECE program Experience using child development assessment Experience with ECE quality assurance and program accreditation. Experience teaching or working with children who have special developmental needs. Working Conditions/Schedule: Salary Range: $25.96 - $29.32 Anticipated Start Date: 2026-09-14 Anticipated End Date: We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status. OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire. OSU is a fair chance employer committed to inclusive hiring. We encourage applications from candidates who bring a wide range of lived experience including involvement with the justice system. If this position has “critical or security-sensitive” responsibilities and/or driving duties and you are selected as a finalist, your initial job offer will be contingent upon the results of a job-related pre-employment check (such as a background check, motor vehicle history check, sexual misconduct reference check, etc.). Background check results do not automatically disqualify a candidate. Take a look at our Background Checks website including the for candidates section for more details. If you have questions or concerns about the pre-employment check, please contact OSU’s Employee and Labor Relations team at employee.relations@oregonstate.edu ."
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/Early-Childhood-Associate-Teacher-Pool---2027_JR0001041": {
      "title": "Early Childhood Associate Teacher Pool - 2027",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-04\nPosted: Posted Yesterday\nFull time\n\nDepartment: Family Resource Center Special Instructions to Applicants: Applications will be reviewed on an on-going basis as vacancies arise. To ensure full consideration, applications must be received by September 12, 2026. Applications will continue to be accepted after the full consideration date, until a sufficient applicant pool has been achieved or the position is filled. The closing date is subject to change without notice to applicants. When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents:  1) A resume 2) A cover letter indicating how your qualifications and experience have prepared you for this position. 3) Copy of a current Oregon Approved Food Handlers card. Starting salary will be commensurate with skills, education, and experience.  For additional information please contact:  Tania Henry tania.henry@oregonstate.edu 541-737-4906 Job Summary: This recruitment will be used to fill future full-time Early Childhood Associate Teacher positions for the Family Resource Center’s Corvallis Early Care and Education Centers at Oregon State University ( OSU ). The Associate Teacher supports the lead teachers in one of the Family Resource Center’s Corvallis Early Care and Education (ECE) centers. Campus ECE centers are committed to continually discovering, creating, and sustaining a teaching and learning environment that supports inquiry, diversity and equity. Associate Teachers are responsible (as outlined below) for the areas to which they are assigned and the young children in their care. Associate Teachers support the lead teacher(s), the Center Director, help with other Center wide duties, and work with parents, as assigned. Associate Teachers report to the lead teachers of their respective classrooms. This position requires knowledge of child development, excellent communication skills and tremendous patience to provide a safe, emotionally secure, and nurturing environment for the healthy development of young children.  The Associate Teacher will assist all classroom program operations including the engagement and support of children and families, center procedures, curriculum planning and implementation, and developmental assessments. Guidelines used for operation will follow Early Learning Division (ELD) Office of Child Care regulations, state health regulations, and program rules and philosophy. The Associate Teacher will build and maintain relationships with the parent(s) of each child and ensure clear communication on each child’s progress and care plan. This position will assist the Lead Teacher in the development and mentoring of Early Childhood Assistant Teachers and student Interns in the classroom. The Associate Teacher will work collaboratively with other program staff to provide a high-quality early care and education program of direct service to young children and their families. This position is informed by the fields of Early Childhood Education, Child Development and Human Development and maintains a working knowledge and compliance with the Oregon ELD Office of Child Care licensing standards and is knowledgeable about the trends and best practices in the field of early care and education. Key Responsibilities: Assist the Lead Teacher in the implementation of a developmentally appropriate, play-based, culturally responsive, and equity-oriented educational program with an emergent curriculum for an assigned program and age range of children from a variety of socio-economic and cultural backgrounds. Prepare and maintain a safe, attractive, neat, and functional environment, both indoors and outdoors, and utilize the environment as a teaching tool. Assist program staff with lunch and break time schedules. Assume lead teacher duties when needed. Family Engagement: ensure consistent and clear communication with parents, maintaining an atmosphere of warmth and openness. Assist the lead teacher child assessments and communication with the child’s parents; participate in \"open house\" for parents. Repeatedly lift, reach (overhead, at and below shoulder level), squat, crouch, climb, sit, bend, stretch, twist, grasp/move small objects, push and pull, and engage in all other physical movements needed to fully participate in supervision and curriculum and care delivery activities, which take place while standing, sitting (including on floor or low chairs), and walking. Form a positive, supportive team atmosphere with other program teachers and staff members and contribute to the Center’s professional community through attendance and participation at staff meetings, classroom team meetings, and other Center staff development opportunities. Model exemplary and high-quality practice and professionalism for students who are engaged in early childhood professional preparation. Qualifications:   Required Qualifications: An Associate's degree in Early Childhood Education or Child Development from a recognized program; OR a Child Development Associate (CDA) credential; OR A minimum of twenty semester hours (thirty quarter hours) of college courses in Early Childhood Education or Child Development; AND one year of experience in a certified day care center or comparable group care program; OR Two years of experience, including at least one year as a teacher, in a certified day care center or comparable group child care program. Experience in Early Care and Education Demonstrable contributions to promoting and enhancing inclusive excellence. Effective verbal and written communication. Must be enrolled in the Oregon’s Office of Child Care Central Background Registry, hold a current Oregon Approved Food Handlers card, and a current First Aid/CPR card by time of appointment. T his position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a criminal history check and be determined to be position qualified as per University Standard: 05-010 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in youth programs may have additional criminal history checks every   24 months.  Preferred Qualifications: BS or BA in Human Development, Child Development, Early Childhood Education, or related field and meets   NAEYC   accreditation requirements. 3+ years of experience in birth to three   ECE   program Experience using child development assessment Experience teaching or working with children who have special developmental needs.   Working Conditions/Schedule: Salary Range: $19.36 - $28.75 Anticipated Start Date: 2026-09-14 Anticipated End Date: We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status. OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire. OSU is a fair chance employer committed to inclusive hiring. We encourage applications from candidates who bring a wide range of lived experience including involvement with the justice system. If this position has “critical or security-sensitive” responsibilities and/or driving duties and you are selected as a finalist, your initial job offer will be contingent upon the results of a job-related pre-employment check (such as a background check, motor vehicle history check, sexual misconduct reference check, etc.). Background check results do not automatically disqualify a candidate. Take a look at our Background Checks website including the for candidates section for more details. If you have questions or concerns about the pre-employment check, please contact OSU’s Employee and Labor Relations team at employee.relations@oregonstate.edu ."
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/Information-Technology-Consultant-3--Video-Producer_JR0000982": {
      "title": "Information Technology Consultant 3: Video Producer",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-04\nPosted: Posted Yesterday\nFull time\n\nDepartment: Division of Ecampus Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents:   1) A resume/CV; and   2) A cover letter indicating how your qualifications and experience have prepared you for this position. Starting salary will be commensurate with skills, education, and experience. For additional information please contact: Gina Morford at Gina.Morford@oregonstate.edu   Job Summary: This recruitment will be used to fill one full-time Information Technology Consultant 3: Video Producer position for the Division of Ecampus at Oregon State University (OSU). The Division of Ecampus is a growing, innovative, and collaborative organization with a commitment to providing high-quality online education. The division extends Oregon State University’s exceptional learning experiences including microcredentials, certificates, and degrees available to students throughout Oregon and around the world. The division includes Ecampus, offering over 130 online and hybrid degree programs; the Ecampus Research Unit (ECRU), conducting and supporting original research in online teaching and learning; the Corporate and Workforce Education Unit (CWE), creating pathways for learners employed by collaborators in business and government; the Open Educational Resources Unit (OER), aiding faculty in adopting open course materials; and the Alternative Credential Unit (ACU), developing short-form educational opportunities. The Video Producer will be part of a creative, collaborative team shaping the future of online education. Our Multimedia team partners with faculty and subject matter experts to transform ideas into engaging, high-quality learning experiences through video, audio, animation, and other emerging multimedia technologies. This is an opportunity to combine your technical expertise and creative vision with a meaningful mission: helping create accessible, innovative learning experiences for students around the world. At the Division of  Ecampus, you’ll work on diverse projects ranging from studio productions and field-based storytelling. You’ll collaborate across disciplines, explore emerging technologies and production practices, and contribute to a team that values innovation, knowledge sharing, and continuous improvement. Oregon State University and the Division of Ecampus maintain and enhance a collaborative and inclusive community that is dedicated to equity and equal opportunity. All employees of this division are responsible for ensuring that these commitments are achieved. This position supports these goals directly by recruiting and supporting qualified employees who demonstrate this commitment and by contributing to a positive office culture.   Ecampus currently operates on a hybrid schedule. Key Responsibilities: Produce Video Content – Plan, film, record, and edit video/ audio content for online courses both in studio and on location. Manage Video Production Projects – Plan and coordinate multiple concurrent video projects, managing schedules, resources, equipment, stakeholders, and competing deadlines from pre-production through final delivery. Collaborate with Faculty and Subject Matter Experts – Consult with faculty, subject matter experts, and project collaborators to develop effective video and multimedia solutions that support online learning and storytelling. Ensure Quality and Accessibility – Apply university branding and accessibility standards to video productions, including coordinating transcription and captioning workflows to meet WCAG AA requirements. Provide Technical and Creative Expertise – Operate and troubleshoot video production equipment and software, develop creative approaches to production challenges, and contribute to decisions regarding video equipment and software. Qualifications: Required Qualifications: This classification requires a basic foundation of knowledge and skills of technology and information systems generally obtained through an AA degree in computer science, information systems, educational technology, communications, or related fields, or similar certified course work in applicable fields of study and at least one year of related work experience. Demonstrated work experience and skills in video editing and motion graphics. Experience with Adobe Premiere Pro, After Effects, and Media Encoder or similar software. Experience and skills in video filming and production equipment.  Proven project management experience and ability to work on multiple projects with competing deadlines.  Ability to work collaboratively on a team and across multiple teams/units.  Excellent written and oral communication skills; demonstrated work experience with process documentation, training or technical writing. Demonstrable contribution to promoting and enhancing inclusive excellence. Ability to lift up to 50 lbs. This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a criminal history check and be determined to be position qualified as per University Standard: 05-010 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in youth programs may have additional criminal history checks every 24 months. This position requires driving a university vehicle or a personal vehicle on behalf of the university; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a motor vehicle history check, possess and maintain a current, valid driver's license in their state of residence, be determined to be position qualified and self-report convictions as per University Policy 05-030. Preferred Qualifications: Bachelor’s degree in film, marketing, broadcast journalism or related field, or an equivalent amount of training and experience to obtain the foundation of knowledge and skills in media production. Skilled and/or trained in multimedia design and/or other animation tools.  Experience with enterprise level video storage and networking systems. Demonstrates experience using Adobe Creative Cloud software. Experience with color correction. Experience with audio recording, dialogue and music editing, and post-audio workflows. Experience with video production systems, including switching and recording via Blackmagic systems or similar hardware and software. Experience producing content for online learning. Experience working in higher education. Experience with customer service or client relations. Working Conditions/Schedule: Ecampus currently operates on a hybrid schedule, Tuesdays and Thursdays are currently designated in-office days as well as additional days as needed. The hybrid schedule can be changed at any time based on organizational needs. This position may require travel. This position may require working events after hours or on the weekends. This position may occasionally be required to assist other video producers at locations off campus. During these times the position may be required to pack, transport, unload and set up 20-50lbs. of video production equipment in a variety of working environments. Salary Range: $63,624 - $103,128 Anticipated Start Date: 2026-10-12 Anticipated End Date: We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status. OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire. OSU is a fair chance employer committed to inclusive hiring. We encourage applications from candidates who bring a wide range of lived experience including involvement with the justice system. If this position has “critical or security-sensitive” responsibilities and/or driving duties and you are selected as a finalist, your initial job offer will be contingent upon the results of a job-related pre-employment check (such as a background check, motor vehicle history check, sexual misconduct reference check, etc.). Background check results do not automatically disqualify a candidate. Take a look at our Background Checks website including the for candidates section for more details. If you have questions or concerns about the pre-employment check, please contact OSU’s Employee and Labor Relations team at employee.relations@oregonstate.edu ."
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/Veterinary-Technician-2--Large-Animal_JR0000763": {
      "title": "Veterinary Technician 2: Large Animal",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-04\nPosted: Posted Yesterday\nFull time\n\nDepartment: Veterinary Teaching Hospital Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents:   1) A resume/CV; and   2) A cover letter indicating how your qualifications and experience have prepared you for this position. 3) Licenses or Certifications: please attach as a separate document 4) Supplemental Questions: please attach as a separate document *Please describe your large animal experience specifically any veterinary experience. For additional information please contact: Garland Burdock burdockg@oregonstate.edu 541-737-6910 Job Summary: This recruitment will be used to fill one full-time Large Animal Veterinary Technician 2 position for the Lois Bates Acheson Veterinary Teaching Hospital in the Carlson College of Veterinary Medicine at Oregon State University (OSU). Oregon State University’s Veterinary Teaching Hospital is adding talented certified veterinary technicians to the Large Animal Surgery Department. We are looking for self-motivated people with a passion for teaching. It is a fast paced, team-oriented hospital that focuses on an optimal level of patient care while providing supportive instruction to our students, and excellent customer service to our clients. Key Responsibilities: Assist in Surgery Inpatient Care Student Instruction Qualifications: REQUIRED QUALIFICATIONS: Must have current certification as a Veterinary Technician and ability to obtain and maintain Oregon certification within six months of hire date AND, demonstrated experience in animal handling and restraint in a clinical setting, as well as demonstrated experience and knowledge of: Drugs and medications typically used in veterinary care Aseptic techniques, patient preparation and medical procedure assistance Medical terminology Operating room procedures Anesthesia equipment and procedures Record keeping procedures for patient charts Animal anatomy and physiology Lifesaving techniques for animals Veterinary care procedures Basic mathematics (including drug dosage and fluid rate calculation) General computer skills Finalists for this position will be required to complete a criminal background history and motor vehicle background criteria form. Offers of employment are contingent upon a satisfactory background check. This position may require a valid driver license. Demonstrated high level of attention to detail. Excellent verbal and written communication and interpersonal skills; must have the ability to communicate professionally with a variety of individuals. Demonstrated ability to work in a team environment and collaborate effectively with students, stall, faculty, and clients. This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a criminal history check and be determined to be position qualified as per University Standard: 05-010 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in youth programs may have additional criminal history checks every 24 months. P REFERRED QUALIFICATIONS: 2 or more years of experience providing medical care for large animals in a veterinary teaching hospital or veterinary clinic. Extensive knowledge and experience working with large animals in all aspects of veterinary nursing. Demonstrated ability to effectively instruct others in veterinary techniques. A demonstrable commitment to promoting and enhancing inclusive excellence. Working Conditions/Schedule: o The VTH is a seven day per week, 24 hour per day hospital; therefore, weekend and holiday work may be required. Shift assignment may change based on operational needs and/or to maintain appropriate staffing levels. o This position is located in an extremely busy Veterinary Teaching Hospital and works primarily with large animals. o This position may be exposed to noxious odors, infectious diseases, chemicals, gas anesthetics, radiation, loud noises, wet and slippery floors, and machinery. o This position has access to controlled substances and is responsible for administering controlled drugs and maintaining controlled drug logs. o This position will often be required to move, which may include lifting and/or carrying, up to 50 pounds, as well as pushing or pulling objects weighing up to 200 pounds. o This position requires walking, standing, running, stooping, bending, squatting, kneeling, and crawling on hard or uneven surfaces as necessary to provide care to animals. o Working with and around small and large animals has certain inherent risks. Animal handling and restraint can lead to injury. To minimize risk to the employee in this positions, Oregon State University offers a rabies vaccination at the employer’s expense (acceptance/declination at the discretion of the appointee). o Overtime, evening, on-call, and/or weekend hours required. o This position is deemed essential. The employee in this position may be expected to report to work during inclement weather, emergency, and other University work curtailments or closures. Salary Range: Min $24.99 - Max $38.15 Anticipated Start Date: 2026-09-28 Anticipated End Date: We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status. OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire. OSU is a fair chance employer committed to inclusive hiring. We encourage applications from candidates who bring a wide range of lived experience including involvement with the justice system. If this position has “critical or security-sensitive” responsibilities and/or driving duties and you are selected as a finalist, your initial job offer will be contingent upon the results of a job-related pre-employment check (such as a background check, motor vehicle history check, sexual misconduct reference check, etc.). Background check results do not automatically disqualify a candidate. Take a look at our Background Checks website including the for candidates section for more details. If you have questions or concerns about the pre-employment check, please contact OSU’s Employee and Labor Relations team at employee.relations@oregonstate.edu ."
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Bend/ACTG-317-and-BA-211-Teaching-Assistant_JR0000831": {
      "title": "ACTG 317 and BA 211 Teaching Assistant",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-04\nPosted: Posted Yesterday\nPart time\n\nDepartment: College of Business (BUS) – OSU-Cascades Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents: A Resume A Cover Letter describing how your coursework and experience have prepared you for this position Unofficial OSU transcript (used to verify completion of ACTG 317 with a grade of B- or better) Supplemental Questions (Optional): Which courses in the OSU accounting curriculum have you completed, and what grade did you earn in ACTG 317? Describe a financial accounting topic that students commonly find difficult. How would you help a student work through it without simply giving them the answer? Grading requires applying the same rubric consistently across many submissions and returning work on deadline. Describe your experience with detail-oriented work under a deadline. Describe your anticipated fall term class schedule and the blocks of time you would be available for grading and for weekly help hours. For additional information please contact: Logan Steele at logan.steele@oregonstate.edu We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status.   OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire.   Note: All job offers are contingent upon Human Resources final approval Job Description: This recruitment will be used to fill one part-time (a maximum of 10 hours per week) Teaching Assistant position supporting ACTG 317 (External Reporting I) and BA 211 (Financial Accounting) for the College of Business at Oregon State University (OSU), based at the OSU-Cascades campus in Bend. Accounting at OSU-Cascades is a small program where students know their instructors and each other by name. In this position you will work directly with the course instructor and with students at two different points in the accounting curriculum: the introductory financial accounting course that nearly every business major takes, and the first course in the upper-division External Reporting sequence that accounting majors build their degree on. Explaining this material to other students is one of the most effective ways to deepen your own command of it, and the experience is strong preparation for graduate study, the CPA exam, and recruiting conversations with public accounting firms. Learn more: College of Business: https://business.oregonstate.edu Accounting at OSU-Cascades: https://osucascades.edu/accountancy-course-listing Position Duties: Grade homework, quizzes, cases, and exams for ACTG 317 and BA 211 using instructor-provided rubrics and solution keys, returning student work within agreed turnaround times. Enter and verify grades in Canvas and flag scoring discrepancies or gradebook errors to the instructor. Hold scheduled weekly help hours for students in both courses, working through practice problems and clarifying concepts rather than supplying answers. Proctor exams and quizzes and assist with in-class logistics, including distributing and collecting materials. Prepare and check practice problem sets, solution keys, and review materials at the instructor's direction. Maintain course pages, announcements, and posted materials in Canvas. Handle student grades and course records confidentially at all times, consistent with FERPA. Transferable Skill Development OSU is committed to ensuring students are prepared for success after graduation through intentional skill development as student employees. The competencies students develop while working at OSU are defined by the National Association of Colleges and Employers (NACE) as the essential skills employers seek in their hires. In this position, students learn and build vital career-readiness skills in the primary areas below:   Communication, Critical Thinking, Technology, Professionalism Qualifications: Full Employment Eligibility Requirements can be found here:   https://hr.oregonstate.edu/student-employment-manual/500-employment-eligibility-requirements Must be academically enrolled in a high school, community college, or university and pursuing a program or course of study Must meet Academic Standing Requirements; students on academic suspension are not eligible for employment Must meet the applicable minimal enrollment standard High School student: Regularly enrolled in a high school or participating in a home-schooling program Undergraduate and post-baccalaureate student: 6 credit hours per term Undergraduate international student: 12 credit hours per term* Graduate student officially admitted to Graduate School: 5 credit hours per term Graduate international student officially admitted to Graduate School: 9 credit hours per term* *International students may be allowed to carry fewer hours than specified above and still be considered “full-time” by the United States Citizenship and Immigration Services ( USCIS ). A reduced course load is approved by the   Office of International Services   ( OIS ), and must be provided to the Student Employment Center. Required Qualifications: Successful completion of ACTG 317 (External Reporting I) at Oregon State University with a grade of B- or better. Enrolled at Oregon State University during fall term 2026 and meeting the enrollment and academic standing requirements above. Ability to explain financial accounting concepts clearly, both in writing and in person, to students encountering them for the first time. Demonstrated accuracy and attention to detail, including the ability to apply a grading rubric consistently across many submissions. Ability to meet grading deadlines and keep a predictable weekly schedule throughout the term. Ability to handle student grades and course records confidentially. Preferred Qualifications: Accounting major or minor. Completion of ACTG 318 and/or ACTG 319. Prior experience tutoring, teaching, or peer mentoring, in any subject. Familiarity with Canvas and with Microsoft Excel. Cumulative GPA of 3.0 or higher. Working Conditions/Schedule: • This position works in a standard office and classroom environment at the OSU-Cascades campus in Bend, Oregon. The appointment runs for fall term 2026, from September 23, 2026 through December 15, 2026. • The successful candidate is expected to work up to 10 hours per week. Weekly hours vary with the course calendar and are heaviest during exam and case-grading weeks. Weekly help hours are scheduled in consultation with the instructor and can be arranged around the student's own class schedule, but the successful candidate should be available to proctor exams during scheduled class meeting times and during finals week (December 7-11, 2026). • Grading and Canvas work may be completed remotely. Help hours and exam proctoring are in person on the Bend campus. Salary Range: Anticipated Start Date: 2026-09-23 Anticipated End Date:"
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/CFSL-Office-Assistant_JR0001028-1": {
      "title": "CFSL Office Assistant",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-04\nPosted: Posted Yesterday\nPart time\n\nDepartment: Center for Fraternity and Sorority Life Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents: A Resume A Cover Letter For additional information please contact: Jason Markette at markettj@oregonstate.edu We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status.     OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire.   Note: All job offers are contingent upon Human Resources final approval Job Description: This recruitment will be used to fill three part-time (a maximum of 24 hours per week) CFSL Office Student Assistant for the Center for Fraternity and Sorority Life at Oregon State University (OSU). The Center for Fraternity & Sorority Life is seeking creative and engaging students who are highly motivated, enthusiastic, team-oriented, and gifted at visual storytelling. This position will work to perform tasks such as staffing the front desk, greeting visitors to the office, answering phone calls and emails, distributing mail, keeping the office tidy, and other office administrative duties. Office Student Assistants will also assist in office attendance and participation tracking of CFSL events. Contribute to small and large scale events planning for the CFSL community. Students in this position will be collaborating with pro-staff on projects when needed. https://gogreek.oregonstate.edu/ Position Duties: This position will work to perform tasks such as staffing the front desk, answering phone calls, welcoming visitors, and answering emails. Assist in office attendance and participation tracking of CFSL events. Help with tabling for new student programs like Orange and Black days and Beaver Open House. Contribute to small- and large scale events planning for the CFSL community. Support marketing efforts for the CFSL and the community. Transferable Skill Development OSU is committed to ensuring students are prepared for success after graduation through intentional skill development as student employees. The competencies students develop while working at OSU are defined by the National Association of Colleges and Employers (NACE) as the essential skills employers seek in their hires. In this position, students learn and build vital career-readiness skills in the primary areas below:   Communication, Teamwork, Professionalism, Career & Self Development Qualifications: Full Employment Eligibility Requirements can be found here:   https://hr.oregonstate.edu/student-employment-manual/500-employment-eligibility-requirements Must be academically enrolled in a high school, community college, or university and pursuing a program or course of study Must meet Academic Standing Requirements; students on academic suspension are not eligible for employment Must meet the applicable minimal enrollment standard High School student: Regularly enrolled in a high school or participating in a home-schooling program Undergraduate and post-baccalaureate student: 6 credit hours per term Undergraduate international student: 12 credit hours per term* Graduate student officially admitted to Graduate School: 5 credit hours per term Graduate international student officially admitted to Graduate School: 9 credit hours per term* *International students may be allowed to carry fewer hours than specified above and still be considered “full-time” by the United States Citizenship and Immigration Services ( USCIS ). A reduced course load is approved by the   Office of International Services   ( OIS ), and must be provided to the Student Employment Center. Required Qualifications: Membership in a social fraternity or sorority Excellent written and verbal communication skills Ability to take direction and complete assignments on deadline Efficacy in Microsoft Office platforms and other forms of communication technology. Demonstrated effective customer service Skills Organizational skills and ability to pay great attention to detail Working Conditions/Schedule: Salary Range: Anticipated Start Date: 2026-09-14 Anticipated End Date:"
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/Office-Specialist-2_JR0000998": {
      "title": "Office Specialist 2",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-04\nPosted: Posted Yesterday\nFull time\n\nDepartment: Office of Admissions Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents:   1) A resume/CV; and   2) A cover letter indicating how your qualifications and experience have prepared you for this position. Starting salary will be commensurate with skills, education, and experience. For additional information please contact: Cheyenne Pulsipher at Cheyenne.pulsipher@oregonstate.edu or 541-737-5686.  Job Summary: This recruitment will be used to fill one full-time Office Specialist 2 position for the Office of Admissions at Oregon State University (OSU). The Office of Admissions includes the recruitment of prospective students, responding to requests for admission, general information and processing applications from all persons seeking entry to Oregon State University. The purpose of this position is computer loading & maintaining document files for material received in the admission office pertaining to prospective students. Process, evaluate credentials, & maintain files of applicants for admission arriving to admit or deny status, applying state system, university & office regulations & requirements. Information is used for advising by campus faculty, high school, college counselors & students both prospective & current. These tasks are carried out within designated time frames. Occasional contact with frustrated or agitated people. Very high volume of computer data entry and desk work. All work is carried out under pressure of deadlines, fluctuating workload and changes in regulations, policies and procedures.  Key Responsibilities: Determine which documents are necessary for applicant files to be completed for evaluation. Evaluate credentials for admission eligibility for all undergraduate domestic students. Determine if student level is correct and change in CRM and Banner SIS when appropriate. Process suspended or errored WEB applications in Banner SIS. Make appropriate admit decisions based on requirements, and in cases where qualifications are marginal, refer file to secondary review process (Holistic Review). Process all campus, term and major changes for prospective students in CRM. Create new applications in CRM for campus and term changes. Compare credentials of applicants against stated requirements for eligibility. Fill out an informational reader form for each applicant to document their review process and eligibility for admission. Qualifications: Required Qualifications: Two years of general clerical experience. One year of which included typing, word processing, or other experience generating documents; OR An Associate’s degree in Office Occupations or Office Technology; OR Graduation from a private school of business with a Certificate in Office Occupations or Office Technology and one year of general clerical experience. College courses in Office Occupations or Office Technology will substitute for the required experience on a year-for-year basis. Must be competent in Microsoft Windows, Email, Word Processing, and spreadsheets. Demonstrated professional communication skills and ability to communicate with diverse audiences. Experience with using computers and computer software to ensure accurate and efficient data entry. Independently research, assess, and retrieve information from the Internet. Provide and receive information in person, by phone, or electronically. Explain information regarding specific programs or services. Provide in depth technical information regarding policies, procedures, and programs. Sit at a keyboard/computer/workstation for the majority of a workday. Be proficient in typing using standard qwerty keyboard. This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a criminal history check and be determined to be position qualified as per University Standard: 05-010 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in youth programs may have additional criminal history checks every 24 months.  Preferred Qualifications: Competency in using Slate CRM, Banner SIS, credit articulation software and document management systems (i.e. On Base). Experience in higher education. Working Conditions/Schedule: Typical Office Hours M – F, 8am – 5pm. Remote or hybrid work is possible. Salary Range: $19.3-$28.75 Anticipated Start Date: 2026-10-01 Anticipated End Date: We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status. OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire. OSU is a fair chance employer committed to inclusive hiring. We encourage applications from candidates who bring a wide range of lived experience including involvement with the justice system. If this position has “critical or security-sensitive” responsibilities and/or driving duties and you are selected as a finalist, your initial job offer will be contingent upon the results of a job-related pre-employment check (such as a background check, motor vehicle history check, sexual misconduct reference check, etc.). Background check results do not automatically disqualify a candidate. Take a look at our Background Checks website including the for candidates section for more details. If you have questions or concerns about the pre-employment check, please contact OSU’s Employee and Labor Relations team at employee.relations@oregonstate.edu ."
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/Student-Accounts-Assistant_JR0000888": {
      "title": "Student Accounts Assistant",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-04\nPosted: Posted Yesterday\nPart time\n\nDepartment: Student Account Services Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents: A Resume A Cover Letter A supplemental document answering the following questions: Please describe your experience working toward a project deadline while dealing with frequent interruptions. What Microsoft applications have you used, your experience with data entry and how do you maintain accuracy?  For additional information please contact: Gregory Balck at Greg.balck@oregonstate.edu We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status. This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a Criminal History Check and be determined to be position qualified as per OSU Standard 576-055-0000 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in Youth Programs may have additional Criminal History Checks every 2 years. OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire.   Note: All job offers are contingent upon Human Resources final approval Job Description: This recruitment will be used to fill one part-time (a maximum of 24 hours per week) Student Account Services position for the Division of Finance and Admin at Oregon State University (OSU). Student will provide great customer service to students and non students in person at the window and on the phone. Student employees assist in drafting email responses, uploading documents into OnBase, processing payments and assisting the Student Account Services staff. Typically up to 20 hours per week, M-F, between the hours of 830am – 430pm. Extended periods sitting/standing at a workstation in Student Accounts, which is a limited access area in the Business Affairs department. Position Duties In person Customer service to both students and non-students at the student account window’ Drafting Email responses to student account services email inquiries Inbound Phone call customer service for both students and non-students Uploading documents into OnBase Returned mail processing External Scholarship processing Taking credit card payments over the phone for non-registered students and non-students Transferable Skill Development OSU is committed to ensuring students are prepared for success after graduation through intentional skill development as student employees. The competencies students develop while working at OSU are defined by the National Association of Colleges and Employers (NACE) as the essential skills employers seek in their hires. In this position, students learn and build vital career-readiness skills in the primary areas below:   Communication, Teamwork, Professionalism Qualifications: Full Employment Eligibility Requirements can be found here:   https://hr.oregonstate.edu/student-employment-manual/500-employment-eligibility-requirements Must be academically enrolled in a high school, community college, or university and pursuing a program or course of study Must meet Academic Standing Requirements; students on academic suspension are not eligible for employment Must meet the applicable minimal enrollment standard High School student: Regularly enrolled in a high school or participating in a home-schooling program Undergraduate and post-baccalaureate student: 6 credit hours per term Undergraduate international student: 12 credit hours per term* Graduate student officially admitted to Graduate School: 5 credit hours per term Graduate international student officially admitted to Graduate School: 9 credit hours per term* *International students may be allowed to carry fewer hours than specified above and still be considered “full-time” by the United States Citizenship and Immigration Services ( USCIS ). A reduced course load is approved by the   Office of International Services   ( OIS ), and must be provided to the Student Employment Center. Required Six months experience in an office setting or customer service role Able to answer a multi-line phone, and direct incoming calls to appropriate department This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a Criminal History Check and be determined to be position qualified as per OSU Standard 576-055-0000 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in Youth Programs may have additional Criminal History Checks every 2 years. Preferred Proficient with Microsoft Office applications.  Ability to complete accurate data entry.  Ability to multi-task and also concentrate on detailed projects with multiple interruptions.  Ability to deliver quality customer service. Working Conditions/Schedule: - Typically up to 24 hours per week, M-F, between the hours of 830am – 430pm. - Extended periods sitting/standing at a workstation in Student Accounts, which is a limited access area in the Business Affairs department. Salary Range: 15.75/hour Anticipated Start Date: 2026-09-10 Anticipated End Date:"
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/ASOSU-Student-Advisory-Board-Member-At-Large_JR0000923": {
      "title": "ASOSU Student Advisory Board Member At-Large",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-04\nPosted: Posted Yesterday\nPart time\n\nDepartment: Student Affairs - ASOSU Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents: ●        A Resume ●        A Cover Letter For additional information please contact: Keri Simonet at simonetk@oregonstate.edu We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status.   OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire.   Note: All job offers are contingent upon Human Resources final approval Job Description: This recruitment will be used to fill one part-time (a maximum of 24 hours per week) ASOSU Student Advisory Board Member At-Large for the ASOSU at Oregon State University (OSU). The ASOSU STUDENT GOVERNMENT Student Advisory Board is responsible for supporting the ASOSU Student Government through the incidental fee process. This unit is a student-centered department at OSU that fosters student engagement through employment, programming, legislation, and through shared dialogue and advisory recommendations with the Student Advisory Board. The ASOSU STUDENT GOVERNMENT Student Advisory Board shall serve the following purposes for the benefit of students of Oregon State University: Review and make recommendations regarding the proposed annual budget of the ASOSU STUDENT GOVERNMENT unit to the Budget Manager. Recommend the student fee amount required to support the ASOSU STUDENT GOVERNMENT annual budget request to ASOSU through the incidental fee process. Review unit programs and services to make advisory recommendations to staff to support and enhance the success of OSU students. Serve as a liaison between the ASOSU STUDENT GOVERNMENT units and ASOSU through the Student Fee Committee. The Student Advisory Board is made up of representatives from each of those units as well as At-Large positions. These positions are tasked with representing the broader student body’s interest when reviewing and evaluating the ASOSU STUDENT GOVERNMENT programs and services and making the fee recommendation. Position Duties Attend all SAB meetings Fall meetings typically occur weekly for approximately one hour. Winter meetings typically occur bi-weekly for approximately one hour. Spring meetings typically occur monthly for approximately one hour. Be proactive about communicating your availability; find a suitable proxy when you are not available to attend meetings for a prolonged period of time. Contribute meaningfully to discussions, with a focus on contributing the perspective of the student body at large, beyond personal insights and experiences. Vote on discussion items and resolutions, taking into account the perspectives discussed in SAB meetings as well as the interests of the student body at large. Review documents and provide feedback for the good of the advisory board. Regularly check and respond to email, Teams messages, and other electronic communication. Serve as an Officer of the Student Advisory Board when applicable (e.g. Chair, Vice-Chair, Student Fee Committee Liaison). Transferable Skill Development OSU is committed to ensuring students are prepared for success after graduation through intentional skill development as student employees. The competencies students develop while working at OSU are defined by the National Association of Colleges and Employers (NACE) as the essential skills employers seek in their hires. In this position, students learn and build vital career-readiness skills in the primary areas below:   Critical Thinking, Teamwork, Professionalism Qualifications: Full Employment Eligibility Requirements can be found here:   https://hr.oregonstate.edu/student-employment-manual/500-employment-eligibility-requirements Must be academically enrolled in a high school, community college, or university and pursuing a program or course of study Must meet Academic Standing Requirements; students on academic suspension are not eligible for employment Must meet the applicable minimal enrollment standard High School student: Regularly enrolled in a high school or participating in a home-schooling program Undergraduate and post-baccalaureate student: 6 credit hours per term Undergraduate international student: 12 credit hours per term* Graduate student officially admitted to Graduate School: 5 credit hours per term Graduate international student officially admitted to Graduate School: 9 credit hours per term* *International students may be allowed to carry fewer hours than specified above and still be considered “full-time” by the United States Citizenship and Immigration Services ( USCIS ). A reduced course load is approved by the   Office of International Services   ( OIS ), and must be provided to the Student Employment Center. Required Demonstrate ability to successfully manage time and competing priorities. Excellent written and verbal communication skills. Demonstrate ability to work collaboratively with a diverse team. Not otherwise employed by SafeRide, Office of Advocacy, or Student Legal Services. Preferred Knowledge or use of services provided by ASOSU Student Government. Demonstrated experience with budgets, fee-setting, or decision making for an advisory board, committee, or task force. Demonstrated commitment to improving experiences of others. Working Conditions/Schedule: This is an hourly position. At-Large members will receive compensation of $17.60/hour for all hours worked. There may be as many as 6 hours per month in Fall and early winter and as little as 2 hours per month in Summer and Spring terms. Salary Range: Anticipated Start Date: 2026-09-14 Anticipated End Date:"
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Newport-Campus/Administrative-Program-Assistant--Hatfield-Marine-Science-Center-Housing-Coordinator_JR0000792": {
      "title": "Administrative Program Assistant: Hatfield Marine Science Center Housing Coordinator",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-03\nPosted: Posted 2 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nDepartment: Hatfield Marine Science Center Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents:   1) A resume/CV; and   2) A cover letter indicating how your qualifications and experience have prepared you for this position. Starting salary will be commensurate with skills, education, and experience. For additional information please contact: Sidney Noble at Sidney.Noble@oregonstate.edu or 541-876-0377.   Job Summary: This recruitment will be used to fill one part-time (approximately 20-hours per week) Administrative Program Assistant: Hatfield Marine Science Center Housing Coordinator position for the Hatfield Marine Science Center at Oregon State University (OSU). This position is located in Newport, Oregon. Position Summary: The HMSC Housing Coordinator supports HMSC Housing logistics and daily operations under the direction of the HMSC Resident Director, including processing incoming applications, housing assignments & reservations, and participating in on-call duties. In addition, the HMSC Housing Coordinator will coordinate activities/events that promote healthy behaviors, foster community, and support an inclusive living environment.      The position is an on-call, live-in professional. As an essential member of the HMSC Student and Campus Services Team, the HMSC Housing Coordinator will join a collaborative team that ensures the unique programmatic needs of HMSC are met.     About OSU Hatfield:    HMSC is OSU’s 49-acre coastal research campus that has operated in Newport for 62 years. The   greater Hatfield enterprise employs over 410 professionals, and hosts over 460 students. Hatfield’s annual economic impact to the coast is $52 million, $25 million representing ‘core’ operations funding, and $27 million in competitive grants, and, through this research enterprise, it provides undergraduate and graduate students with hands-on research opportunities and experiential instruction. HMSC also supports K-12 STEM education programs (~35K students per year) and public outreach through programming in our Visitor Center (~ 150,000 visitors per year). In addition to Oregon State University personnel, HMSC is an oceanographic research base for 5 state and federal agencies working in the marine sector: NOAA, EPA, USF&W, USDA, and ODFW). With its world-class facilities, research activities, academic programming, and innovative environment, HMSC has wide-reaching relationships with industry, community government, policymakers, formal and informal education networks, Non-Governmental Organizations, and partner universities     About Hatfield Housing:    HMSC offers unique housing accommodations for upwards of 150 college students, instructors, researchers, and agency partners. HMSC recently expanded housing operations with the addition of a newly built residence hall, including 77 apartment fully furnished apartment style units. The HMSC Housing Coordinator will play a vital role in the logistics, and implementation of residential services and student support systems.   Key Responsibilities: Process housing applications and maintain records. Assist with bookings and assignments, and all associated administrative tasks. Correspond with prospective and incoming residents. Serve in on-call capacity for lock-outs and emergency response. Assist with residential events and resource development. Qualifications: Required Qualifications: Three years of office experience which included two years at full performance level and experience generating documents; and Lead work responsibility or coordination of office procedures. Excellent customer service orientation, ability to communicate clearly and effectively both verbally and in written form. Proficiency in Microsoft Office Suite (with emphasis on Excel, Word, Outlook, Teams), databases, social media and hybrid meeting platforms such as Zoom. Demonstrated ability to maintain a flexible, team-oriented, professional, and cooperative attitude. Must have the ability to work irregular hours including night and weekend work. Must have a valid driver’s license and good driving record. This position requires driving a university vehicle or a personal vehicle on behalf of the university; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a motor vehicle history check, possess, and maintain a current, valid driver’s license in their state of residence, be determined to be position qualified and self-report convictions as per University Policy 05-030. This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a criminal history check and be determined to be position qualified as per University Standard: 05-010 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in youth programs may have additional criminal history checks every 24 months. Preferred Qualifications: Bachelor's degree 2 years of experience in scheduling, housing, lodging accommodations or residence life.   Experience working with and supporting diverse populations in university housing, higher education, or K-12 education.  Experience working with individuals and young adults in high stress situations. Ability to manage emergency and crisis response.  Experience planning events, developing resources, and coordinating support services.   Working Conditions/Schedule: Working Conditions/Work Schedule: This is a live-in position. The HMSC Housing Coordinator performs essential functions and may be required to report to work during emergencies, university closures and holidays. This is a half-time position that requires some weekend, evening, and early morning time commitments, on-call responsibilities, as well as supervisory work. The HMSC Housing Coordinator will receive a one-bedroom apartment, utilities included, at the convenience of the employer. Necessary ability to lift up to 25 lbs. Necessary ability to traverse 1 mile over uneven, coastal terrain. This position is required to drive university owned vehicles. The position is based at the OSU Hatfield Marine Science Center in Newport, Oregon, which is in a tsunami hazard zone. HMSC’s evacuation plan and other tsunami safety-related information may be reviewed at hmsc.oregonstate.edu/tsunami-preparedness and oregontsunami.org. Salary Range: $20.98 - $31.63 Anticipated Start Date: 2026-09-21 Anticipated End Date: We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status. OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire. OSU is a fair chance employer committed to inclusive hiring. We encourage applications from candidates who bring a wide range of lived experience including involvement with the justice system. If this position has “critical or security-sensitive” responsibilities and/or driving duties and you are selected as a finalist, your initial job offer will be contingent upon the results of a job-related pre-employment check (such as a background check, motor vehicle history check, sexual misconduct reference check, etc.). Background check results do not automatically disqualify a candidate. Take a look at our Background Checks website including the for candidates section for more details. If you have questions or concerns about the pre-employment check, please contact OSU’s Employee and Labor Relations team at employee.relations@oregonstate.edu ."
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/Instructor-Senior-Instructor-1--Healthcare-Administration_JR0000205": {
      "title": "Instructor/Senior Instructor 1: Healthcare Administration",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-03\nPosted: Posted 2 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nDepartment: School of Nutrition and Public Health Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents:   1) A resume/CV; and   2) A cover letter indicating how your qualifications and experience have prepared you for this position. 3) Statement of Teaching Starting salary will be commensurate with skills, education, and experience. For additional information please contact: Veronica Irvin Veronica.Irvin@oregonstate.edu 541-737-8784 Job Summary: The School of Nutrition and Public Health invites applications for a full-time (1.00 FTE), 12-month, fixed-term, Instructor/Senior Instructor I position. Reappointment is at the discretion of the School Head. Appointment at the Instructor rank is anticipated; however, appointment at a promoted rank may be considered depending upon the qualifications of the successful candidate. We passionately believe that health is for all people. Our world-leading faculty and committed students work to prevent disease and injury, identify factors that affect health and well-being, inform policy, and shape environments that improve lives and help everyone, from children to seniors, thrive. Through nationally recognized online degree programs, certificates, and microcredentials offered via OSU Ecampus, we expand access to high-quality education and serve diverse learners wherever they live and work. The School of Nutrition and Public Health at Oregon State University advances knowledge and prepares future professionals through excellence in teaching, research, and community engagement. We foster a collaborative and innovative environment where faculty and students work together to address complex health issues, promote equity, and develop solutions that make a meaningful impact in Oregon, across the nation, and around the world. Key Responsibilities: Teaching and other assigned duties Service Qualifications: Required Qualifications for rank of Instructor and Senior Instructor I: Master’s degree in Healthcare Administration or related field Experience teaching online courses and supervising applied projects A demonstrable commitment to inclusive excellence and ability to work collaboratively across disciplines. Ability to work on campus as this position cannot be performed remotely. Additional Qualifications for the Rank of Senior Instructor I: Sustained record of exceptional teaching, advising and service, at the college or university level, as evidenced by student evaluations of teaching. Experience in course development. Preferred Qualifications: Doctoral degree in Healthcare Administration or related field Experience with accreditation or assessment activities Experience with CAHME and NCHL competencies Working Conditions/Schedule: Work is done in an office environment. Typical schedule is 9-5pm. Salary Range: Salary commensurate with skills, education and experience. Anticipated Start Date: 2027-07-01 Anticipated End Date: 2028-06-30 We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status. OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire. OSU is a fair chance employer committed to inclusive hiring. We encourage applications from candidates who bring a wide range of lived experience including involvement with the justice system. If this position has “critical or security-sensitive” responsibilities and/or driving duties and you are selected as a finalist, your initial job offer will be contingent upon the results of a job-related pre-employment check (such as a background check, motor vehicle history check, sexual misconduct reference check, etc.). Background check results do not automatically disqualify a candidate. Take a look at our Background Checks website including the for candidates section for more details. If you have questions or concerns about the pre-employment check, please contact OSU’s Employee and Labor Relations team at employee.relations@oregonstate.edu ."
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/Faculty-Research-Assistant--Research-Coordinator_JR0000719": {
      "title": "Faculty Research Assistant: Research Coordinator",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-03\nPosted: Posted 2 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nDepartment: Fisheries, Wildlife, & Conservation Sciences Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents: 1) A resume/CV; and 2) A cover letter indicating how your qualifications and experience have prepared you for this position. Starting salary will be commensurate with skills, education, and experience. For additional information please contact: Kelly Biedenweg kelly.biedenweg@oregonstate.edu 206-883-5612 Job Summary: The Department of Fisheries & Wildlife & Conservation Sciences invites applications for a full-time (1.00 FTE), 12-month, fixed-term Faculty Research Assistant position. Reappointment is at the discretion of the Department Head. The Conservation and Human Wellbeing Lab in the Fisheries, Wildlife and Conservation Sciences Department seeks an interdisciplinary social science Faculty Research Assistant to coordinate a large project looking at Socio-Cultural impacts of Harmful Algal Blooms in the Puget Sound, WA and Southern California. This is a 3-year project funded by NOAA. Applicants must reside in Corvallis, OR or the Puget Sound. Harmful algal blooms can affect coastal communities through disruptions to fisheries, aquaculture, recreation, tourism, cultural practices, public health, and access to coastal resources. This multi- institutional project will examine how these effects are experienced and distributed across communities in Puget Sound, Washington, and the Santa Barbara Channel, California. The project combines surveys, interviews, focus groups, geospatial analysis, and analysis of digital and social-media data and will produce both peer-reviewed scholarship and practical, user-oriented resources for coastal communities and decision-makers. Bring your research and project management skills in environmental social science. You will: Work collaboratively with three PIs based at different universities v to identify and implement steps for meeting project goals Develop research protocols, collect data and lead data analysis in   the Puget Sound, Washington, including Hood Canal, Bellingham   Bay, and Quartermaster Harbor. Methods will include geospatial   analysis of existing datasets, public-beach encounter surveys, shoreline homeowner surveys and interviews, focus groups, and social media large data analyses. Lead outputs of project components, including at least one peer- reviewed manuscript as well as more creative, user-focused   products Mentor one MS student to complete independent research   projects as part of overall project Facilitate and support an advisory group to provide feedback on   project implementation and outputs Act as project manager, organizing research team, providing work   reports and managing project timelines and deliverables. Perform other related duties consistent with the position   classification, project objectives, and evolving research needs as   identified by the PIs in consultation with the project team. ​ The Human Wellbeing and Conservation Lab ( https://kellybiedenweg.weebly.com ) is within the Department of Fisheries, Wildlife, and Conservation Sciences ( https://fwcs.oregonstate.edu) . The Human Wellbeing and Conservation Lab is part of the Human Dimensions lab led by three faculty with about 19 lab members including an Instructor, Research Coordinators, and graduate students. We are a highly collaborative group that values inclusivity, curiosity, and mutual support in our learning spaces. Jointly, we also run the People and Nature seminar series and student workshops. https://Peopleandnatureosu.weebly.com FWCS is an academic unit that reports to the College of Agricultural Sciences. The Department is composed of approximately 300 staff, including academic faculty, research assistants, classified staff, graduate students, and temporary staff. Over $8 million of externally funded grants and contracts are received each year in support of science focused on basic ecological research, conservation, and sustainable use of fish and wildlife and their habitats. The Department is the largest in the College of Agricultural Sciences, with over 40 professors and 1200 students in 8 degree and certificate programs, including one Bachelor of Science degree, five graduate degrees, and two graduate certificates. We are a vibrant department that promotes a supportive atmosphere for all students and employees. Key Responsibilities: Act as project manager, organizing research team, providing work   reports and managing project timelines and deliverables. Develop research protocols, collect data and lead social-scientific   data analysis . Facilitate and support an advisory group to provide feedback on   project implementation and outputs. Lead outputs of project components, including at least one peer- reviewed manuscript as well as more creative, user-focused products. Mentor one MS student to complete independent research   projects as part of overall project. Qualifications: Required Qualifications: Graduate degree in environmental social sciences (geography, planning, psychology, anthropology, environmental studies, etc.) or related fields that included social scientific research methods, analyses, and products Proficiency with geospatial statistical analysis Proficiency in or capacity to quickly learn quantitative and qualitative analyses with some support (regressions, topic modeling, hierarchical clustering) Knowledge of best practices in social scientific research Outstanding organizational and project management skills Outstanding communication skills based in curiosity, self-awareness, and collaboration Computing skills, including statistical software & cloud-based file sharing    Professional competence in coordinating and facilitating stakeholders and interdisciplinary science teams in a respectful and efficient manner Demonstrated proficiency in professional writing  A demonstrable commitment to inclusive excellence in environmental management This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a criminal history check and be determined to be position qualified as per University Standard: 05-010 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in youth programs may have additional criminal history checks every 24 months. Preferred Qualifications: Experience managing a large research program with multiple researchers and sub-projects Experience mentoring student researchers Demonstrated proficiency in writing peer-reviewed manuscripts and/or accessible white papers, reports, and other forms of visualization to communicate the role of social science in conservation/restoration to diverse audiences Experience with encounter surveys, focus groups, scientific interviewing, geographically-weighted regressions, and/or the use of AI for large data analyses (e.g., topic modeling)  Working Conditions/Schedule: Full time Potential for hybrid Must reside in Corvallis, OR or the Puget Sound, WA Infrequent overnight travel for meetings, and occasional lengthier trips (up to 1 or 2 weeks) to oversee field work. Salary Range: Salary is commensurate with skills, education and experience. Anticipated Start Date: 2026-09-07 Anticipated End Date: 2027-06-30 We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status. OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire. OSU is a fair chance employer committed to inclusive hiring. We encourage applications from candidates who bring a wide range of lived experience including involvement with the justice system. If this position has “critical or security-sensitive” responsibilities and/or driving duties and you are selected as a finalist, your initial job offer will be contingent upon the results of a job-related pre-employment check (such as a background check, motor vehicle history check, sexual misconduct reference check, etc.). Background check results do not automatically disqualify a candidate. Take a look at our Background Checks website including the for candidates section for more details. If you have questions or concerns about the pre-employment check, please contact OSU’s Employee and Labor Relations team at employee.relations@oregonstate.edu ."
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Astoria/Office-Specialist-1_JR0000993-1": {
      "title": "Office Specialist 1",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-03\nPosted: Posted 2 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nDepartment: Division of Extension and Engagement Special Instructions to Applicants: To ensure full consideration, applications must be received by September 11, 2026. Applications will continue to be accepted after the full consideration date, until a sufficient applicant pool has been achieved or the position is filled. The closing date is subject to change without notice to applicants.     When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents:     1) A resume/CV; and      2) A cover letter indicating how your qualifications and experience have prepared you for this position.     Starting salary will be commensurate with skills, education, and experience.     For additional information please contact:  Geoff Puryear, Search Chair geoff.puryear@oregonstate.edu   Job Summary: This recruitment will be used to fill one part-time (approximately 35 hours per week) Office Specialist 1 position for the Oregon State University Division of Extension and Engagement’s Extension Clatsop County Office in Astoria, Oregon. The mission of the OSU Division of Extension and Engagement (division) is to work with communities throughout Oregon to support lifelong learning and co-create innovative solutions to address local challenges.  Together, we envision a future where Oregon’s people, communities and ecosystems prosper. As a team member, this OS1 provides general office support and program support for Extension Clatsop County office. This OS1 provides a wide range of office support including serving as the office’s reception, providing educational resources, performing record and database management, editing/updating/maintaining web/social media content and digital marketing, and providing general assistance to the public or referring individuals to the appropriate OSU Extension employee. The OS1 support includes providing excellent and empathetic customer service while engaging in culturally appropriate and inclusive communication. This individual serves broad and diverse populations and provides services that are accessible and inclusive. Key Responsibilities Serve as the front desk reception, which includes answering multiple phone lines, screen and direct incoming calls; responding to emails and in-person requests; and returning voicemails promptly. Provide excellent and empathetic customer service while engaging in culturally appropriate and inclusive communication. Provide general office support, which includes preparing mailings; making copies; processing incoming mail; reserving meeting room; and ordering supplies. Provide general program support, which includes assisting the public and program participants with enrollment, resources, and ongoing relevant information; and creating original, public-facing documents with accurate program and office information. Edit, update, and maintain web/social media content and digital marketing materials. Assist with 4-H enrollment and fair management activities, which include attending the fair to help with fair event activities; and data entry (e.g., fair participant and events records) into the applicable system. Collect merchandise payments and registration fees (cash and check) for programs, issue receipts, and prepare for bank depositing. Qualifications: Required Qualifications: Completion of courses or training in office technology; OR One year of general clerical experience which included typing, word processing, or other generation of documents; OR An equivalent combination of training and experience. Additional Required Qualifications: Excellent customer service skills. A commitment to collaborative and respectful learning and working environments that are safe, inclusive, and welcoming.   General knowledge and ability to use computers, including proficiency with information technology, professional office software and collaborative work platforms. Ability to engage and communicate successfully and inclusively, both in writing and verbally, with broad and diverse audiences in a professional and culturally responsible manner. Ability to work independently under general supervision and use judgment in determining work methods and priorities. This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a criminal history check and be determined to be position qualified as per University Standard: 05-010 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in youth programs may have additional criminal history checks every 24 months. This position requires driving a university vehicle or a personal vehicle on behalf of the university; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a motor vehicle history check, possess and maintain a current, valid driver’s license in their state of residence, be determined to be position qualified and self-report convictions as per University Policy 05-030. Preferred Qualifications: Ability to work as a team to provide quality programs and outstanding customer service. Ability to manage multiple projects while completing assignments on time. Ability to learn and adapt to using multiple forms of digital communication systems, including websites, email newsletters, social media, and other tools in a changing technological environment.   Working Conditions/Schedule: This position is responsible for primary office coverage. Ability to lift, carry, push and pull educational materials and/or equipment up to 30 pounds. Access to a vehicle is necessary as duties will require driving/traveling to perform office support duties. Business mileage will be reimbursed based on OSU policies. Salary Range: $18.06 - $24.99 Anticipated Start Date: 2026-11-16 Anticipated End Date: We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status. OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire. OSU is a fair chance employer committed to inclusive hiring. We encourage applications from candidates who bring a wide range of lived experience including involvement with the justice system. If this position has “critical or security-sensitive” responsibilities and/or driving duties and you are selected as a finalist, your initial job offer will be contingent upon the results of a job-related pre-employment check (such as a background check, motor vehicle history check, sexual misconduct reference check, etc.). Background check results do not automatically disqualify a candidate. Take a look at our Background Checks website including the for candidates section for more details. If you have questions or concerns about the pre-employment check, please contact OSU’s Employee and Labor Relations team at employee.relations@oregonstate.edu ."
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/UHDS-Fac-Maintenance-Student_JR0001039": {
      "title": "UHDS Fac Maintenance Student",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-03\nPosted: Posted 2 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nDepartment: University Housing and Dining Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents: A Resume A Cover Letter For additional information please contact: Mauricio De Leon at mauricio.deleon@oregonstate.edu We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status.   This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a Criminal History Check and be determined to be position qualified as per OSU Standard 576-055-0000 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in Youth Programs may have additional Criminal History Checks every 2 years. This position requires driving a university vehicle or a personal vehicle on behalf of the University; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a Motor Vehicle History Check, possess and maintain a current, valid driver's license in their state of residence, be determined to be position qualified and self-report convictions (as per Voluntary and Compulsory Driver Standards OSU Standard 125-155-0200) as per OSU Standard 576-056-0000 et seq.   OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire.   Note: All job offers are contingent upon Human Resources final approval Job Description: This recruitment will be used to fill eight Building Services Assistant part-time (a maximum of 24 hours per week) for the University Housing & Dining Services (UHDS) at Oregon State University (OSU). Join the UHDS Building Services team and gain hands-on experience while helping maintain and support OSU’s residence halls and dining facilities. Student employees work alongside experienced maintenance staff and assist with a variety of projects, including general maintenance, furniture assembly, furniture movement, loft installations, room preparation, repairs, preventative maintenance, and other building-related work. This position is a good opportunity for students interested in developing practical skills, learning how building maintenance operations work, and gaining experience using tools and equipment in a team environment. No extensive maintenance experience is required; we are looking for dependable students who are willing to learn, work safely, and contribute to the team. Position Duties: Assist with lofting and adjusting beds and other residence hall furniture. Move, assemble, disassemble, and stage furniture, equipment, and materials throughout UHDS facilities. Assist with the installation and replacement of dispensers, fixtures, and other building accessories. Perform basic maintenance and minor repair tasks under the direction of maintenance staff. Assist with residence hall room preparation, including furniture setup and other move-in and move-out related work. Transport tools, supplies, furniture, and materials between work areas and buildings. Support Building Maintenance staff with preventative maintenance, special projects, and other assigned facilities-related tasks. Transferable Skill Development OSU is committed to ensuring students are prepared for success after graduation through intentional skill development as student employees. The competencies students develop while working at OSU are defined by the National Association of Colleges and Employers (NACE) as the essential skills employers seek in their hires. In this position, students learn and build vital career-readiness skills in the primary areas below:   Communication, Teamwork, Professionalism, Equity & Inclusion Qualifications: Full Employment Eligibility Requirements can be found here:   https://hr.oregonstate.edu/student-employment-manual/500-employment-eligibility-requirements Must be academically enrolled in a high school, community college, or university and pursuing a program or course of study Must meet Academic Standing Requirements; students on academic suspension are not eligible for employment Must meet the applicable minimal enrollment standard High School student: Regularly enrolled in a high school or participating in a home-schooling program Undergraduate and post-baccalaureate student: 6 credit hours per term Undergraduate international student: 12 credit hours per term* Graduate student officially admitted to Graduate School: 5 credit hours per term Graduate international student officially admitted to Graduate School: 9 credit hours per term* *International students may be allowed to carry fewer hours than specified above and still be considered “full-time” by the United States Citizenship and Immigration Services ( USCIS ). A reduced course load is approved by the   Office of International Services   ( OIS ), and must be provided to the Student Employment Center. Additional Required Qualifications: This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a Criminal History Check and be determined to be position qualified as per OSU Standard 576-055-0000 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in Youth Programs may have additional Criminal History Checks every 2 years. This position requires driving a University vehicle or a personal vehicle on behalf of the University; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a Motor Vehicle History Check, possess and maintain a current, valid driver's license in their state of residence, be determined to be position qualified and self-report convictions (as per Voluntary and Compulsory Driver Standards OSU Standard 125-155-0200) as per OSU Standard 576-056-0000 et seq. Working Conditions/Schedule: Salary Range: Anticipated Start Date: 2026-09-07 Anticipated End Date:"
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/Student-Crew-Member---Soap-Creek-Beef-Ranch_JR0000851": {
      "title": "Student Crew Member - Soap Creek Beef Ranch",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-03\nPosted: Posted 2 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nDepartment: Animal and Rangeland Sciences Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents: 1. A Resume/Vita 2. A cover letter indicating how your qualifications and experience have prepared you for this position   For additional information please contact: Michael Hammerich at michael.hammerich@oregonstate.edu   We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status.   This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a criminal history check and be determined to be position qualified as per OSU Standard 576-055-0000 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in youth programs may have additional criminal history checks every 24 months. This position requires driving a University vehicle or a personal vehicle on behalf of the University; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a Motor Vehicle History Check, possess and maintain a current, valid driver's license in their state of residence, be determined to be position qualified and self-report convictions (as per Voluntary and Compulsory Driver Standards OSU Standard 125-155-0200) as per OSU Standard 576-056-0000 et seq.   Note: All job offers are contingent upon Human Resources final approval.   OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire. Job Description: This recruitment will be used to create a pool of part-time (a maximum of 24hours per week) Student Technical Assistant positions for the Department of Animal and Rangeland Sciences at Oregon State University (OSU). The positions are based out of the off-campus Soap Creek Beef Ranch. Qualified applicants will be contacted as positions become available. The positions include animal care and maintenance of the facility and pastures, and may include research and data collection. The role of this position is to assist the Beef Ranch manager in daily operations of the Soap and Berry Creek Beef Ranches. Duties include: Feeding, watering, and caring for the animals and ensuring proper health is a top priority. Collaboration with the farm manager and fellow student employees, as well as some individual work. Keep a safe, clean, and organized environment for people and livestock. Facility upkeep for the main beef ranches as well as other campus locations. Build and repair fence. Assist with cattle management including calving, vaccinating, and doctoring, as needed. Assist with setting up for labs and research studies, as needed. Drive ATVs and farm equipment, as needed Qualifications: Full Employment Eligibility Requirements can be found here:   https://hr.oregonstate.edu/student-employment-manual/500-employment-eligibility-requirements   Must be academically enrolled in a high school, community college, or university and pursuing a program or course of study Must meet Academic Standing Requirements; students on academic suspension are not eligible for employment Must meet the applicable minimal enrollment standard High School student: Regularly enrolled in a high school or participating in a home-schooling program Undergraduate and post-baccalaureate student: 6 credit hours per term Undergraduate international student: 12 credit hours per term* Graduate student officially admitted to Graduate School: 5 credit hours per term Graduate international student officially admitted to Graduate School: 9 credit hours per term* *International students may be allowed to carry fewer hours than specified above and still be considered “full-time” by the United States Citizenship and Immigration Services ( USCIS ). A reduced course load is approved by the   Office of International Services   ( OIS ), and must be provided to the Student Employment Center. Required Qualifications: This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a criminal history check and be determined to be position qualified as per OSU Standard 576-055-0000 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in youth programs may have additional criminal history checks every 24 months. This position requires driving a University vehicle or a personal vehicle on behalf of the University; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a Motor Vehicle History Check, possess and maintain a current, valid driver's license in their state of residence, be determined to be position qualified and self-report convictions (as per Voluntary and Compulsory Driver Standards OSU Standard 125-155-0200) as per OSU Standard 576-056-0000 et seq. Preferred Qualifications: Past experience that demonstrates the ability to work as a team Experience working around or with cattle Working Conditions/Schedule: Working Conditions / Work Schedule This is an off campus facility, employee must have a reliable form of transportation. Employee may have exposure to noxious odors, infectious diseases, chemicals, loud noises, slippery floors, and machinery. Employee will be exposed to inclement weather conditions. Employee will need to maneuver items that weigh up to 50 pounds Employee may be required to stoop, bend, and kneel to provide care of the animals. Overnight shifts on rare occasion Employees are expected to work at least one weekend per month. Employees work schedule will be set according to class schedule. Salary Range: 15.55 Anticipated Start Date: 2026-08-28 Anticipated End Date:"
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/BA-347-Student-Teaching-Assistant---International-Business_JR0000990": {
      "title": "BA 347 Student Teaching Assistant – International Business",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-03\nPosted: Posted 2 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nDepartment: College of Business – School of Management, Entrepreneurship, and Supply Chain Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents: ●        A Resume ●        A Cover Letter For additional information please contact: Elizabeth French at elizabeth.french@oregonstate.edu This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a Criminal History Check and be determined to be position qualified as per OSU Standard 576-055-0000 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in Youth Programs may have additional Criminal History Checks every 2 years. OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire.   Note: All job offers are contingent upon Human Resources final approval Job Description: This recruitment will be used to fill 1 part-time (a maximum of 24 hours per week) student job for the College of Business at Oregon State University (OSU). The person in this position will assist Elizabeth French with various duties in support of BA 347 International Business. Assisting in tracking student attendance, grading discussion and in-class assignments will be the main duties. Occasional attendance for the class may be required. Pay rate for this position is $16.55 per hour. Position Duties Grade and record attendance from in-class worksheets. Printing and copying as assigned. Assist students with various requests. Transferable Skill Development OSU is committed to ensuring students are prepared for success after graduation through intentional skill development as student employees. The competencies students develop while working at OSU are defined by the National Association of Colleges and Employers (NACE) as the essential skills employers seek in their hires. In this position, students learn and build vital career-readiness skills in the primary areas below:   Communication, Critical Thinking, Professionalism Qualifications: Full Employment Eligibility Requirements can be found here:   https://hr.oregonstate.edu/student-employment-manual/500-employment-eligibility-requirements Must be academically enrolled in a high school, community college, or university and pursuing a program or course of study Must meet Academic Standing Requirements; students on academic suspension are not eligible for employment Must meet the applicable minimal enrollment standard High School student: Regularly enrolled in a high school or participating in a home-schooling program Undergraduate and post-baccalaureate student: 6 credit hours per term Undergraduate international student: 12 credit hours per term* Graduate student officially admitted to Graduate School: 5 credit hours per term Graduate international student officially admitted to Graduate School: 9 credit hours per term* *International students may be allowed to carry fewer hours than specified above and still be considered “full-time” by the United States Citizenship and Immigration Services ( USCIS ). A reduced course load is approved by the   Office of International Services   ( OIS ), and must be provided to the Student Employment Center. Required: Must have English fluency – Both written and verbal This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a Criminal History Check and be determined to be position qualified as per OSU Standard 576-055-0000 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in Youth Programs may have additional Criminal History Checks every 2 years. Preferred: International Graduate Student Working Conditions/Schedule: Work schedule varies, 4-6 hours per week. Salary Range: Anticipated Start Date: 2026-09-23 Anticipated End Date:"
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/CoE-Student-Engagement-Coordinator_JR0000996": {
      "title": "CoE Student Engagement Coordinator",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-03\nPosted: Posted 2 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nDepartment: College of Engineering Marketing Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents: ●        A Resume ●        A Cover Letter For additional information please contact: Alaina Hill at Alaina.hill@oregonstate.edu This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a Criminal History Check and be determined to be position qualified as per OSU Standard 576-055-0000 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in Youth Programs may have additional Criminal History Checks every 2 years. This position requires driving a University vehicle or a personal vehicle on behalf of the University; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a Motor Vehicle History Check , possess and maintain a current, valid driver's license in their state of residence, be determined to be position qualified and self-report convictions (as per Voluntary and Compulsory Driver Standards OSU Standard 125-155-0200) as per OSU Standard 576-056-0000 et seq. OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire.   Note: All job offers are contingent upon Human Resources final approval Job Description: This recruitment will be used to fill two part-time (a maximum of 24 hours per week) CoE Student Engagement Coordinator for the College of Engineering at Oregon State University (OSU). The College of Engineering (CoE) External Relations team is seeking an organized, detail-oriented student to join our team part-time (an average of 6-9 hours per week). This position supports both the College of Engineering Events and Donor Engagement programs, contributing to meaningful experiences for alumni, donors, students, faculty, and friends of the college. The Engagement Team Student Assistant will divide their time between event support and donor engagement activities. The position reports directly to the Associate Director of Events, who serves as the supervisor of record, while also providing regular support to the Associate Director of Donor Engagement. The student will work closely with both team members and will receive assignments and project direction based on operational needs. This role requires exceptional attention to detail, strong organizational skills, professionalism, and the ability to manage multiple projects effectively. Position Duties Data entry and maintaining event-related records and documentation Drafting event communications and documents Creating and printing name tags Delivering/picking up event equipment, materials or mail to COE event locations Editing, formatting, and preparing donor engagement materials and reports Printing, assembling, and mailing stewardship and donor communications Drafting stewardship materials such as impact pieces, thank-you notes, and related collateral Assisting with data analysis and reporting projects to improve stewardship efforts Transferable Skill Development OSU is committed to ensuring students are prepared for success after graduation through intentional skill development as student employees. The competencies students develop while working at OSU are defined by the National Association of Colleges and Employers (NACE) as the essential skills employers seek in their hires. In this position, students learn and build vital career-readiness skills in the primary areas below:   Communication, Critical Thinking, Teamwork, Technology Qualifications: Full Employment Eligibility Requirements can be found here:   https://hr.oregonstate.edu/student-employment-manual/500-employment-eligibility-requirements Must be academically enrolled in a high school, community college, or university and pursuing a program or course of study Must meet Academic Standing Requirements; students on academic suspension are not eligible for employment Must meet the applicable minimal enrollment standard High School student: Regularly enrolled in a high school or participating in a home-schooling program Undergraduate and post-baccalaureate student: 6 credit hours per term Undergraduate international student: 12 credit hours per term* Graduate student officially admitted to Graduate School: 5 credit hours per term Graduate international student officially admitted to Graduate School: 9 credit hours per term* *International students may be allowed to carry fewer hours than specified above and still be considered “full-time” by the United States Citizenship and Immigration Services ( USCIS ). A reduced course load is approved by the   Office of International Services   ( OIS ), and must be provided to the Student Employment Center. Required Ability to work an average of 6-9 hours per week during the academic year, with occasional flexibility for special events in the office (Covell Hall) or at event spaces on campus. Availability during Fall, Winter, and Spring terms of the 2026-2027 school year unless otherwise discussed with hiring manager. Optional opportunity to work remotely approx. 3hrs per week in the summer. Ability to communicate in a professional, positive, and friendly manner. Experience in an office and/or event setting. Ability to lift up to 30 lbs. Experience with Microsoft Office Suite and/or Google Workspace. Strong organizational and time management skills. Exceptional attention to detail and accuracy. Ability to manage multiple priorities and projects simultaneously. Demonstrated commitment to promoting and enhancing diversity and inclusion. Able to maintain confidentiality. Ability to represent the College of Engineering with maturity and professionalism. Ability to work independently and collaboratively as part of a team. Responsible, dependable, punctual, and adaptable. This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a Criminal History Check and be determined to be position qualified as per OSU Standard 576-055-0000 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in Youth Programs may have additional Criminal History Checks every 2 years. This position requires driving a University vehicle or a personal vehicle on behalf of the University; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a Motor Vehicle History Check , possess and maintain a current, valid driver's license in their state of residence, be determined to be position qualified and self-report convictions (as per Voluntary and Compulsory Driver Standards OSU Standard 125-155-0200) as per OSU Standard 576-056-0000 et seq. Preferred Currently enrolled at Oregon State University. Experience handling sensitive/confidential information. Experience working in an office, customer service, event, or administrative environment. Working Conditions/Schedule: - Office, storage areas, events, work schedule will vary. - The Engagement Team Student Assistant works collaboratively with both the Events and Donor Engagement teams. - While the Associate Director of Events serves as the supervisor of record, the student will regularly support projects and assignments from both functional areas and is expected to communicate proactively with each supervisor regarding priorities, deadlines, and scheduling. Salary Range: Anticipated Start Date: 2026-09-18 Anticipated End Date:"
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/CoE-Innovation-Labs-Student-Assistant--iLabs-_JR0000999-1": {
      "title": "CoE Innovation Labs Student Assistant (iLabs)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-03\nPosted: Posted 2 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nDepartment: College of Engineering Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents: ●        A Resume ●        A Cover Letter For additional information please contact: Erin McDowell at Erin.mcdowell@oregonstate.edu We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status. This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a Criminal History Check and be determined to be position qualified as per OSU Standard 576-055-0000 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in Youth Programs may have additional Criminal History Checks every 2 years. OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire.   Note: All job offers are contingent upon Human Resources final approval Job Description: This recruitment will be used to fill 4 part-time (a maximum of 24 hours per week) Innovation Labs Students for the College of Engineering at Oregon State University (OSU). Position Duties The CoE Innovation Labs Student Technical Assistant responsibilities include: Operate , calibrate, and maintain machines, equipment, and tools. Consult with full time staff to diagnose existing problems, and assist in the generation of new solutions as appropriate. Under the guidance and advice of full-time staff, develop and apply creative engineering solutions intended to facilitate improved shop operations and maintain a safe, functional working environment. Advise iLabs users, build connections with peers and other user groups, and improve user experience through the application of thoughtful advice. Direct users toward appropriate solution(s) while maintaining a respectful and empowering environment. Support full-time Machinists with research prototyping and production jobs, implementing strategies and techniques developed via trainings and co-work with other staff. Cooperate with full-time staff and other student workers to develop and implement shop layout and organizational solutions. Assist with relocation of large machines, equipment, and materials, both manually and with mechanical assistance. Perform duties and tasks in support of the College of Engineering and its facilities, under guidance from iLabs and/or College of Engineering Facilities staff. Transferable Skill Development OSU is committed to ensuring students are prepared for success after graduation through intentional skill development as student employees. The competencies students develop while working at OSU are defined by the National Association of Colleges and Employers (NACE) as the essential skills employers seek in their hires. In this position, students learn and build vital career-readiness skills in the primary areas below:   Communication, Critical Thinking, Teamwork, Technology, Leadership, Professionalism Qualifications: Full Employment Eligibility Requirements can be found here:   https://hr.oregonstate.edu/student-employment-manual/500-employment-eligibility-requirements Must be academically enrolled in a high school, community college, or university and pursuing a program or course of study Must meet Academic Standing Requirements; students on academic suspension are not eligible for employment Must meet the applicable minimal enrollment standard High School student: Regularly enrolled in a high school or participating in a home-schooling program Undergraduate and post-baccalaureate student: 6 credit hours per term Undergraduate international student: 12 credit hours per term* Graduate student officially admitted to Graduate School: 5 credit hours per term Graduate international student officially admitted to Graduate School: 9 credit hours per term* *International students may be allowed to carry fewer hours than specified above and still be considered “full-time” by the United States Citizenship and Immigration Services ( USCIS ). A reduced course load is approved by the   Office of International Services   ( OIS ), and must be provided to the Student Employment Center. Required Demonstrated ability to safely work in a shop environment or similarly hazardous workspace. Experience or significant interest in manual and computer-driven manufacturing processes. Experience or significant interest in designing products and/or engineering solutions. This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a Criminal History Check and be determined to be position qualified as per OSU Standard 576-055-0000 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in Youth Programs may have additional Criminal History Checks every 2 years. Preferred 1+ year of experience in CNC machining or CNC related coursework and/or work experience. 1+ year of experience in product design using CAD software, and/or experience programming CNC toolpaths using CAM software. 1+ year of experience with lathes, mills, or similar. Experience with woodworking and/or metalworking tools. Working Conditions/Schedule: Work to be performed in Innovation Labs located in Rogers, Merryfield, and Graf Halls. Salary Range: Anticipated Start Date: 2026-09-18 Anticipated End Date:"
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/Annual-Giving---Gift-Planning-Engagement-Student-Assistant_JR0001008": {
      "title": "Annual Giving & Gift Planning Engagement Student Assistant",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-03\nPosted: Posted 2 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nDepartment: OSU Foundation Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents: A Resume A Cover Letter For additional information please contact: Jason Thomas at Jason.thomas@osufoundation.org We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status.   OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire.   Note: All job offers are contingent upon Human Resources final approval Job Description: This recruitment will be used to fill one part-time (a maximum of 24 hours per week) Annual Giving & Gift Planning Engagement Student Assistant for the OSU Foundation at Oregon State University (OSU). The Oregon State University Foundation is seeking a student to join the Annual Giving and Gift Planning teams. Reporting jointly to the Senior Director of Annual Giving Programs and the Assistant Vice President of Gift Planning, this student worker will play a key role in supporting donor engagement, fundraising, stewardship, research, and marketing efforts across both programs. Responsibilities include assisting with donor outreach, stewardship communications, legacy donor engagement, student philanthropy initiatives, prospect research, database management, and campaign support, including Dam Proud Day and OSU Legacy Society activities. This position offers a unique opportunity to gain hands-on experience in nonprofit advancement while developing professional skills in communications, fundraising, donor relations, research, and data analysis. The student worker will contribute meaningfully to the culture of philanthropy at Oregon State University while gaining exposure to both annual and planned giving strategies. Position Duties: Promote philanthropy through student and donor engagement, stewardship calls, surveys, and outreach activities. Support Dam Proud Day, OSU Legacy Society, and other fundraising initiatives through event logistics, communications, and outreach. Thank donors, share impact stories, gather feedback, and assist with legacy donor engagement. Conduct market, donor, and prospect research to support fundraising and engagement strategies. Assist with donor cultivation efforts, including research, outreach preparation, and stewardship activities. Support the creation and editing of marketing, stewardship, and donor communications. Maintain donor records through data entry, contact reports, coding updates, and honor roll maintenance. Shadow advancement professionals and gain exposure to annual giving, gift planning, and donor stewardship. Provide general administrative support for the Annual Giving and Gift Planning teams. Perform other duties and special projects as assigned. Transferable Skill Development OSU is committed to ensuring students are prepared for success after graduation through intentional skill development as student employees. The competencies students develop while working at OSU are defined by the National Association of Colleges and Employers (NACE) as the essential skills employers seek in their hires. In this position, students learn and build vital career-readiness skills in the primary areas below:   Communication, Critical Thinking, Teamwork, Technology, Leadership Qualifications: Full Employment Eligibility Requirements can be found here:   https://hr.oregonstate.edu/student-employment-manual/500-employment-eligibility-requirements Must be academically enrolled in a high school, community college, or university and pursuing a program or course of study Must meet Academic Standing Requirements; students on academic suspension are not eligible for employment Must meet the applicable minimal enrollment standard High School student: Regularly enrolled in a high school or participating in a home-schooling program Undergraduate and post-baccalaureate student: 6 credit hours per term Undergraduate international student: 12 credit hours per term* Graduate student officially admitted to Graduate School: 5 credit hours per term Graduate international student officially admitted to Graduate School: 9 credit hours per term* *International students may be allowed to carry fewer hours than specified above and still be considered “full-time” by the United States Citizenship and Immigration Services ( USCIS ). A reduced course load is approved by the   Office of International Services   ( OIS ), and must be provided to the Student Employment Center. Required Qualifications: Strong computer skills, including proficiency with Microsoft Office (Word, Excel, Teams, PowerPoint) and experience with databases or CRM systems.  Ability to research information through multiple electronic and paper files.  Strong organizational, oral and interpersonal skills and eye for detail.  Excellent interpersonal skills and ability to create strong professional relationships with colleagues at the OSU Foundation, OSU Alumni Association, and OSU. Strategic, hardworking, and able to work with a high degree of flexibility in a highly collaborative, fast-paced, goal-oriented environment. Ability to apply creative and entrepreneurial thinking while using sound judgment. Knowledge of appropriate research techniques and methods. Demonstrated sound judgment and high integrity, especially when handling confidential information.  Customer service orientation and a positive, proactive attitude.  Working Conditions/Schedule: This position will be based in Corvallis, Oregon and will work out of the OSU Foundation Corvallis office. The Foundation offers a program called Core Work Days, a hybrid approach combining remote and in-office work days. Unless traveling for work,  Foundation employees are in office on Tuesday, Wednesday and Thursday, and work remotely, if preferred, on Monday and Friday. This is a part-time position working up to 15-20 hours per week. Salary Range: Anticipated Start Date: 2026-09-21 Anticipated End Date:"
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/Guest-Experience-Associate_JR0000384": {
      "title": "Guest Experience Associate",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-03\nPosted: Posted 2 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nDepartment: Patricia Valian Reser Center for the Creative Arts Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents: A Resume A Cover Letter For additional information please contact: Tanner Buffy at Tanner.Buffy@oregonstate.edu Note for current OSU student employees: If you already have an active Workday profile, you must log in to Workday and apply through your employee account.  Please do not apply through the external career site, as this may prevent your application from being processed correctly. We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status. This position requires driving a university vehicle or a personal vehicle on behalf of the University; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a Motor Vehicle History Check, possess and maintain a current, valid driver's license in their state of residence, be determined to be position qualified and self-report convictions (as per Voluntary and Compulsory Driver Standards OSU Standard 125-155-0200) as per OSU Standard 576-056-0000 et seq.   OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire.   Note: All job offers are contingent upon Human Resources final approval Job Description: This recruitment will be used to fill two part-time (a maximum of 24 hours per week) Guest Experience Associate positions for the Patricia Valian Reser Center for the Creative Arts (PRAx) at Oregon State University (OSU). The Undergraduate Student Outreach Assistant performs a variety of work related to outreach programs, including assistance at camps, youth programs, nutrition and education programs, and physical activity courses. Guest Experience Associates will help with event setup, ticket scanning, ushering, housekeeping, and support the Box Office and Guest Experience Manager and Front of House Coordinator with on and off-campus events and will be assigned where needed on any given day. Guest Experience Associates will be familiar with the current and upcoming events at PRAx, the performing and gallery spaces, policies, and will display a high level of customer service. Guest Experience Associates will be required to learn and follow the safety policies and procedures of PRAx. The position requires a demonstrated high level of discretion and sense of appropriateness, ability to maintain a calm and professional demeanor when working in stressful situations, as well as excellent communication and customer service skills. Guest Experience Associates are expected to use good judgement, to conduct themselves professionally while at work, to follow the guidelines for proper work attire, and to be punctual and reliable. Position Duties: Welcome, greet guests, and scan/verify tickets from event attendees. Politely direct and escort guests to their seats and/or venue entrances. Provide wayfinding of facilities and spaces at PRAx. Assist with crowd control and security. Calmly assists with disputes regarding tickets and seats. Attend staff meetings, pre-event meetings and other trainings as required by management. Visually sweep and tidy public spaces to check for potential safety issues, lost and found items, and cleanliness. Have knowledge and follow the proper procedures for assisting patrons with disabilities. Demonstrate knowledge of all spaces within PRAx and understand the evacuation procedures from all points within PRAx. Be punctual, work successfully, and communicate professionally with guests and staff. Other duties as assigned Transferable Skill Development OSU is committed to ensuring students are prepared for success after graduation through intentional skill development as student employees. The competencies students develop while working at OSU are defined by the National Association of Colleges and Employers (NACE) as the essential skills employers seek in their hires. In this position, students learn and build vital career-readiness skills in the primary areas below:   Communication, Teamwork, Professionalism, Equity & Inclusion Qualifications: Full Employment Eligibility Requirements can be found here:   https://hr.oregonstate.edu/student-employment-manual/500-employment-eligibility-requirements Must be academically enrolled in a high school, community college, or university and pursuing a program or course of study Must meet Academic Standing Requirements; students on academic suspension are not eligible for employment Must meet the applicable minimal enrollment standard High School student: Regularly enrolled in a high school or participating in a home-schooling program Undergraduate and post-baccalaureate student: 6 credit hours per term Undergraduate international student: 12 credit hours per term* Graduate student officially admitted to Graduate School: 5 credit hours per term Graduate international student officially admitted to Graduate School: 9 credit hours per term* *International students may be allowed to carry fewer hours than specified above and still be considered “full-time” by the United States Citizenship and Immigration Services ( USCIS ). A reduced course load is approved by the   Office of International Services   ( OIS ), and must be provided to the Student Employment Center. Required Qualifications: Prior customer service experience. Ability to prove strong attention to detail. Ability to prove punctuality and reliability. Interest in performing arts, live events, music or technical theatre. Willingness to learn new skills and follow directions. Ability to work independently and as part of a team. Ability to communicate effectively across different forms (radio, comms, email, etc) Ability to follow safety guidelines. Comfortable with moderate physical activity, including lifting up to 50 lbs. Additional Required Qualifications: This position requires driving a university vehicle or a personal vehicle on behalf of the University; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a Motor Vehicle History Check, possess and maintain a current, valid driver's license in their state of residence, be determined to be position qualified and self-report convictions (as per Voluntary and Compulsory Driver Standards OSU Standard 125-155-0200) as per OSU Standard 576-056-0000 et seq. Preferred Qualifications: Experience with theatre, concerts, or event production. Previous successful experience working in events, live performance, customer service, or hospitality. CPR certification. Working Conditions/Schedule: Work Conditions/Work Schedule: · Hours are based on the needs of events. Therefore, the schedule is variable and there is not a set number of hours guaranteed each week. · Must be able to work flexible hours, including weekdays, evenings, and weekends, depending on event needs. · Work is performed in theatres, studios, and other performance venues. · May involve standing for long periods, working in dim lighting and/or noisy environments, and working in confined and/or crowded areas. · Possible exposure to weather or outdoor conditions. · Potential driving and possible travel to work at events offsite. Ability to drive is preferred. · Preference given to those who can work 10-24 hours a week. This position will require flexible weekday, evening, and weekend work. Salary Range: 15.55-22.00 Anticipated Start Date: 2026-09-01 Anticipated End Date:"
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/Faculty-Research-Assistant--Upland-Game-Bird-Analyst--Short-Term-_JR0000423": {
      "title": "Faculty Research Assistant: Upland Game Bird Analyst (Short-Term)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-03\nPosted: Posted 2 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nDepartment: Special Instructions to Applicants: The closing date has been extended in order to expand the original applicant pool. When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents:   1) A resume/CV; and   2) A cover letter indicating how your qualifications and experience have prepared you for this position.   For additional information please contact: Jonathan Dinkins jonathan.dinkins@oregonstate.edu Job Summary: The Department of Animal and Rangeland Science is seeking an hourly, short-term Faculty Research Assistant: Upland Game Bird Analyst. This is a part-time (0.25 FTE), 12-month position. This position is expected to last approximately 12-months. The incumbent will compile research data for abundance, occupancy, population dynamics, and habitat use analyses for studies conducted by the Dinkins Lab. Focus will be on peer-reviewed publication writing for game bird studies, including sage-grouse, sooty grouse or similar species. Other research topics will be analysis of hunting systems related to upland game birds. Develop and implement methodology for research and collection of data, maintain accurate records, create and maintain database management systems, run quantitative analyses of data, and interpret results/provide conclusions based on findings. Prepare reports and manuscripts focused on effectiveness of management actions on game birds (sage-grouse, sooty grouse or similar species) for presentation and use in peer-reviewed manuscripts and Extension publications and conferences. KEY RESPONSIBILITIES: Compile research data for abundance, occupancy, population dynamics, and habitat use analyses for studies conducted by the Dinkins Lab. A nalysis of hunting systems related to upland game birds. Develop and implement methodology for research and collection of data, maintain accurate records. C reate and maintain database management systems, run quantitative analyses of data. I nterpret results/provide conclusions based on findings. Prepare reports and manuscripts focused on effectiveness of management actions on game birds (sage-grouse, sooty grouse or similar species) for presentation and use in peer-reviewed manuscripts and Extension publications and conferences. Qualifications: Required Qualifications : Doctoral-level degree Familiarity with upland game birds, particularly sage-grouse and sooty grouse, and their hunting systems necessary. Knowledge of data collection techniques, including using and analyzing data from population surveys, marked birds, and hunting survey data necessary. Knowledge of estimating detection probability in statistical models necessary. Experience working with sagebrush wildlife or similar species necessary. Knowledge in using Program R and ArcGIS. Knowledge of wildlife statistics necessary. Demonstrated collaboration with wildlife management agencies or analogous stakeholder groups. Demonstrated ability to write technical and peer-reviewed research papers necessary. Demonstrated ability to work collaboratively with partner agencies. Ability to foster an inclusive and collaborative research environment. Working Conditions/Schedule: Salary Range: Anticipated Start Date: 2026-08-24 Anticipated End Date: 2027-09-15 We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status. OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire. OSU is a fair chance employer committed to inclusive hiring. We encourage applications from candidates who bring a wide range of lived experience including involvement with the justice system. If this position has “critical or security-sensitive” responsibilities and/or driving duties and you are selected as a finalist, your initial job offer will be contingent upon the results of a job-related pre-employment check (such as a background check, motor vehicle history check, sexual misconduct reference check, etc.). Background check results do not automatically disqualify a candidate. Take a look at our Background Checks website including the for candidates section for more details. If you have questions or concerns about the pre-employment check, please contact OSU’s Employee and Labor Relations team at employee.relations@oregonstate.edu ."
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/Learning-Student-Assistant---Ecampus-ENT-300_JR0001021": {
      "title": "Learning Student Assistant - Ecampus ENT 300",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-03\nPosted: Posted 2 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nDepartment: Horticulture Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents: A Resume List of relevant course-work For additional information please contact: Betsey Miller at Betsey.miller@oregonstate.edu We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status.   OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire.   Note: All job offers are contingent upon Human Resources final approval Job Description: This recruitment will be used to fill four part-time (a maximum of 24 hours per week) Learning Student Assistant – Ecampus ENT 300 positions for the Horticulture Department at Oregon State University (OSU). Position Duties: Grade and provide feedback on weekly student assignments. Transferable Skill Development OSU is committed to ensuring students are prepared for success after graduation through intentional skill development as student employees. The competencies students develop while working at OSU are defined by the National Association of Colleges and Employers (NACE) as the essential skills employers seek in their hires. In this position, students learn and build vital career-readiness skills in the primary areas below:   Communication, Critical Thinking, Teamwork, Professionalism Qualifications: Full Employment Eligibility Requirements can be found here:   https://hr.oregonstate.edu/student-employment-manual/500-employment-eligibility-requirements Must be academically enrolled in a high school, community college, or university and pursuing a program or course of study Must meet Academic Standing Requirements; students on academic suspension are not eligible for employment Must meet the applicable minimal enrollment standard High School student: Regularly enrolled in a high school or participating in a home-schooling program Undergraduate and post-baccalaureate student: 6 credit hours per term Undergraduate international student: 12 credit hours per term* Graduate student officially admitted to Graduate School: 5 credit hours per term Graduate international student officially admitted to Graduate School: 9 credit hours per term* *International students may be allowed to carry fewer hours than specified above and still be considered “full-time” by the United States Citizenship and Immigration Services ( USCIS ). A reduced course load is approved by the   Office of International Services   ( OIS ), and must be provided to the Student Employment Center. Required Qualifications: Must have successfully completed ENT 300 and be majoring in a biological or natural science field. Working Conditions/Schedule: Student will work an average of 8-10 hours per week grading and providing feedback for weekly assignments. Work is entirely online. Salary Range: Anticipated Start Date: 2026-09-16 Anticipated End Date:"
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/Laboratory-Assistant--Short-Term---Internal-Employment-Opportunity-_JR0000957": {
      "title": "Laboratory Assistant (Short-Term)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-02\nPosted: Posted 3 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nDepartment: College of Forestry Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents:   1) A resume/CV; and   2) A cover letter indicating how your qualifications and experience have prepared you for this position. For additional information please contact: Katy Kavanagh katy.kavanagh@oregonstate.edu Job Summary: This recruitment will be used to fill one part-time (approximately 20 hours per week), short-term, Laboratory Assistant position for the College of Forestry at Oregon State University (OSU). This position is expected to last approximately 3-months. The Lab Aide supports various tasks associated with the Cooperative Chemical Analytical Lab (CCAL) which specializes in high quality, trace level analysis of nutrients, ions, and physical properties of lake, stream, precipitation and groundwater research samples. Key Responsibilities: Receive, lift, and process/document samples delivered in heavy (up to 50#) coolers and 14-L carboys. Filter bulk samples. Samples from plastic carboys need to be filtered into heavy glass carboys that require multiple washes between and after use. Setup of sample digests and sample dilutions. Load and unload gas tanks from forklift, move into position and transfer regulators. Stock chemical and waste pours for reagent preparation and disposal. Move, lift, and organize boxes of paper files to loft storage facility. Qualifications: REQUIRED QUALIFICATIONS: Training or experience demonstrating the: knowledge of basic laboratory techniques; knowledge of laboratory terminology, equipment, and materials; knowledge of basic math; ability to follow formulas accurately; ability to handle, package, and store laboratory specimens and materials; ability to operate and maintain standard laboratory equipment; ability to learn routing testing methods, procedures, and techniques; ability to keep accurate records in conformance with laboratory procedures; and i) ability to follow written and verbal instructions. High school courses, in chemistry, biology, mathematics, or any course which included laboratory work, are considered qualifying. This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a criminal history check and be determined to be position qualified as per University Standard: 05-010 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in youth programs may have additional criminal history checks every 24 months. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS: Experience working in a laboratory setting Commitment to working professionally and inclusively with people from all backgrounds On your application, be specific in addressing your training and/or experience to the areas listed in the required qualifications. Working Conditions/Schedule: Work is done in a lab setting and may require long periods of standing and sitting. Regular shift hours and off-hours as approved by the supervisor not exceeding 20 hours per week. Work includes lifting and carrying heavy items such as water samples and supplies. Includes handling hazardous chemicals. Incumbent must know and follow safety and reporting protocols. Salary Range: Min $18.06 - Max $22.84 Anticipated Start Date: 2026-09-14 Anticipated End Date: 2026-12-18 We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status. OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire. OSU is a fair chance employer committed to inclusive hiring. We encourage applications from candidates who bring a wide range of lived experience including involvement with the justice system. If this position has “critical or security-sensitive” responsibilities and/or driving duties and you are selected as a finalist, your initial job offer will be contingent upon the results of a job-related pre-employment check (such as a background check, motor vehicle history check, sexual misconduct reference check, etc.). Background check results do not automatically disqualify a candidate. Take a look at our Background Checks website including the for candidates section for more details. If you have questions or concerns about the pre-employment check, please contact OSU’s Employee and Labor Relations team at employee.relations@oregonstate.edu ."
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Bend/Faculty-Research-Assistant--Methods-to-Optimize-Contextualized-Science-Instruction_JR0000715": {
      "title": "Faculty Research Assistant: Methods to Optimize Contextualized Science Instruction",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-02\nPosted: Posted 3 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nDepartment: OSU Cascades Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents:   1) A resume/CV; and   2) A cover letter indicating how your qualifications and experience have prepared you for this position.   3) A Statement of Research 4) Transcripts   Starting salary will be commensurate with skills, education, and experience. For additional information please contact: Johana Hernandez Johana.Hernandez@osucascades.edu Job Summary: OSU Cascades invites applications for four part-time (0.50 FTE), 12-month, fixed-term Faculty Research Assistant: Methods to Optimize Contextualized Science Instruction (MOCSI) positions. Reappointment is at the discretion of the Department Head. This position is located in Bend, Oregon. The MOSCI Faculty Research Assistant will work on collecting and analyzing qualitative and quantitative data, preparing/presenting publications and conference reports, recruiting and communicating with project participants, collaborating with team members and partners.  Key Responsibilities: Collect and analyze qualitative and quantitative data Prepare and present publications and conference reports Participate in recruiting and communicating with project participants Collaborate with team members and partners on MOSCI research Qualifications: Required Qualifications: Earned research-based masters in a social science field Two years experience collecting, analyzing, and reporting on qualitative and quantitative data Established peer-reviewed publication record Preferred Qualifications: Experience in science education research Earned doctorate degree(s) in a social science field Earned degree in science education  K-12 classroom experience  Working Conditions/Schedule: Work schedule will be variable throughout the project and up to 20 hours per week. Researchers will set a schedule with the PI each month. Some work may be remote.   Salary Range: $24.00-$31.25 Anticipated Start Date: 2026-09-22 Anticipated End Date: 2027-06-30 We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status. OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire. OSU is a fair chance employer committed to inclusive hiring. We encourage applications from candidates who bring a wide range of lived experience including involvement with the justice system. If this position has “critical or security-sensitive” responsibilities and/or driving duties and you are selected as a finalist, your initial job offer will be contingent upon the results of a job-related pre-employment check (such as a background check, motor vehicle history check, sexual misconduct reference check, etc.). Background check results do not automatically disqualify a candidate. Take a look at our Background Checks website including the for candidates section for more details. If you have questions or concerns about the pre-employment check, please contact OSU’s Employee and Labor Relations team at employee.relations@oregonstate.edu ."
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/Assistant-or-Associate-Athletic-Trainer_JR0000995": {
      "title": "Assistant or Associate Athletic Trainer",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-02\nPosted: Posted 3 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nDepartment: Department of Athletics Special Instructions to Applicants: To ensure full consideration, applications must be received by 9/16/2026. Applications will continue to be accepted after the full consideration date, until a sufficient applicant pool has been achieved or the position is filled. The closing date is subject to change without notice to applicants.   When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents:   1) A resume/CV; and   2) A cover letter indicating how your qualifications and experience have prepared you for this position. 3) Please upload responses to the following questions as a separate document. Oregon State University Athletics believes strong teams are built through mutual respect, broad perspectives, and meaningful opportunity. In your experience, how have you contributed to developing a team culture that reflects these values? Share concrete examples of initiatives or practices you have supported. Please explain if you are an IAWP for any high school PSA, two-year junior college PSA, four year college transfer PSA, or any other OSU recruit in the sports of Football or Men’s or Women’s Basketball.  If yes, please explain.  If no, put N/A Please explain if you are an IAWP for any current OSU student-athlete in their first two years of enrollment at OSU in the sports of Football or Men’s or Women’s Basketball.  If yes, please explain.  If no, put N/A. 4) Attach a copy of your valid BOC certification 5) Attach a copy of your valid CPR / AED / First Aid Certification Starting salary will be commensurate with skills, education, and experience. For additional information please contact: Heather Elkinton at heather.elkinton@oregonstate.edu Job Summary: The Department of Athletics is seeking qualified applicants for an Assistant or Associate Athletic Trainer position with the sport assignment of Men’s Basketball. This is a full-time (1.00   FTE ), 12-month, professional faculty position. Oregon State University Athletic Training is seeking qualified applicants for an Assistant/Associate Athletic Trainer position with the sport assignment of Men’s Basketball. The successful candidate will join a team of Certified Athletic Trainers, Athletic Training Fellows and Master of Athletic Training students in providing comprehensive care for the Men’s Basketball team.  This position is highly collaborative in nature, and benefits from the easy access and support of a multidisciplinary health care team. Located in the mid-Willamette Valley, this classic “college town” enjoys easy access to the Oregon Coast, Cascade Mountain range and Oregon wine country.  Oregon State prides itself in a tight-knit community atmosphere where all members of the athletic department work to support one another and achieve goals. Key Responsibilities: Injury/illness prevention, evaluation, treatment, and rehabilitation. Practice/event coverage. Day-to-day communication with coaches, supervising athletic trainers, and other healthcare providers.  Responsible for supervision of entry-level master’s athletic training students, assisting in the daily operations of the Athletic Training facilities and administrative duties as assigned. Qualifications: Required Qualifications: Both Assistant/Associate Athletic Trainer Master’s degree. BOC Certified Athletic Trainer. Minimum of 1 year certified athletic training experience. Oregon State Athletic Trainer license or eligible. Certified Health Care Provider (CPR/AED). The ability to lift/carry/push/pull objects weighing 50 pounds. Demonstrates excellent written & verbal communication skills. Demonstrated ability to communicate in a manner that shows respect and inclusivity for all co-workers and internal customers. This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a criminal history check and be determined to be position qualified as per University Standard: 05-010 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in youth programs may have additional criminal history checks every 24 months. This position requires driving a university vehicle or a personal vehicle on behalf of the university; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a motor vehicle history check, possess and maintain a current, valid driver's license in their state of residence, be determined to be position qualified and self-report convictions as per University Policy 05-030. Additionally, if Associate Athletic Trainer: Minimum of 8 years certified athletic training experience. Preferred Qualifications: Both Assistant and Associate: Ability to work in a fast-paced, stressful environment. Basic understanding of NCAA Division I culture. Experience working in collegiate athletics. NBA or NCAA Division 1 Basketball Athletic Training Experience Additionally, if Associate Athletic Trainer: Demonstrates leadership experience Demonstrates experience managing complex administrative duties Demonstrates experience in a supervisory role Working Conditions/Schedule: Working Conditions/Work Schedule: • Evening and weekend work required. • Exposure to bodily fluids. • Indoor and outdoor working conditions including being exposed to varied climates and weather including heat, cold, rain, snow • Must be able to lift and move up to 50 lbs. • Access to confidential student records. • May have access to persons under 18 years of age. • Access to expensive equipment. • Contingent upon being cleared to drive, may be asked to transport student athletes or others within a university owned vehicle, or assist with team travel Salary Range: Salary is commensurate with skills, education and experience. Anticipated Start Date: 2026-09-21 Anticipated End Date: We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status. OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire. OSU is a fair chance employer committed to inclusive hiring. We encourage applications from candidates who bring a wide range of lived experience including involvement with the justice system. If this position has “critical or security-sensitive” responsibilities and/or driving duties and you are selected as a finalist, your initial job offer will be contingent upon the results of a job-related pre-employment check (such as a background check, motor vehicle history check, sexual misconduct reference check, etc.). Background check results do not automatically disqualify a candidate. Take a look at our Background Checks website including the for candidates section for more details. If you have questions or concerns about the pre-employment check, please contact OSU’s Employee and Labor Relations team at employee.relations@oregonstate.edu ."
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/Cook-1_JR0000679": {
      "title": "Cook 1",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-02\nPosted: Posted 3 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nDepartment: University Housing & Dining Services Special Instructions to Applicants: Special Instructions to the Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents: 1) A resume 2) A cover letter indicating how your qualifications and experience have prepared you for this position. Starting salary will be commensurate with skills, education, and experience. For additional information please contact: David Wilber david.wilber@oregonstate.edu 541-737-1004 Job Summary: This recruitment will be used to fill four full-time Cook 1 positions for University Housing and Dining Services at Oregon State University (OSU).  The purpose of this position is to assist the Cook 2 to ensure that customers of the dining center will get an appealing, nutritious and satisfying meal. This includes maintaining quality of service, preparation of menu items using good cooking methods as well as adhering to safety/sanitation requirements. This position in collaboration with management team is responsible for training and lead work (scheduling, assigning work, and approving work) of student employees. This position is required to support the established professional standards, policies and procedures of the work unit, department, and university. Key Responsibilities:   Meal Preparation Maintain Standards of Safety & Sanitation Customer Service Instruct Student Workers Miscellaneous Qualifications: Required Qualifications: One year of experience as a cook in a commercial food service facility; OR Possession of a Certificate of Completion in culinary arts and/or Foods and Hospitality Service from a certified community college or technical school. This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a criminal history check and be determined to be position qualified as per University Standard: 05-010 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in youth programs may have additional criminal history checks every   24 months .   Preferred Qualifications: Prior experience working in a high volume food service operation. Lead work experience in training, scheduling, and assigni ng work. Cashiering experience, and accurate cash handling skills. Ability to use computer to fill out forms, complete timesheet and check e-mail. Working Conditions/Schedule: Salary Range: Pay grade 13 $18.06-$23.88 Anticipated Start Date: 2026-09-13 Anticipated End Date: We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status. OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire. OSU is a fair chance employer committed to inclusive hiring. We encourage applications from candidates who bring a wide range of lived experience including involvement with the justice system. If this position has “critical or security-sensitive” responsibilities and/or driving duties and you are selected as a finalist, your initial job offer will be contingent upon the results of a job-related pre-employment check (such as a background check, motor vehicle history check, sexual misconduct reference check, etc.). Background check results do not automatically disqualify a candidate. Take a look at our Background Checks website including the for candidates section for more details. If you have questions or concerns about the pre-employment check, please contact OSU’s Employee and Labor Relations team at employee.relations@oregonstate.edu ."
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/KidSpirit-Swim-Instructor-School-Year-2026-2027_JR0000641": {
      "title": "KidSpirit Swim Instructor School Year 2026-2027",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-02\nPosted: Posted 3 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nDepartment: KidSpirit Extension and Engagement Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents: A Resume For additional information please contact: kidspirit@oregonstate.edu In addition to applying to the OSU job website portal please go to KidSpirit website to join our team and apply to KidSpirit. https://kidspirit.oregonstate.edu/employment We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status.   This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a Criminal History Check and be determined to be position qualified as per OSU Standard 576-055-0000 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in Youth Programs may have additional Criminal History Checks every 2 years.   OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire.   Note: All job offers are contingent upon Human Resources final approval Job Description: This recruitment will be used to fill 30 part-time (a maximum of 24 hours per week) Swim Instructor for the KidSpirit Youth Organization at Oregon State University (OSU). The Youth Program Instructor is responsible for delivering high‑quality instruction to youth while fostering a safe, supportive, and growth‑oriented environment. The position requires strong communication, critical thinking, teamwork, and a commitment to ongoing professional development. Responsibilities include lesson planning, safety response, equipment management, program promotion, and effective collaboration with staff, families, and supervisors. WSI certification preferred or ability to participate in WSI training. Swim Lesson Experience / Training Preferred or High Level of Swim Experience Join Our Team | OSU–KidSpirit | Extension and Engagement | Oregon State University Position Duties: Create and maintain an instructional environment that prioritizes skill mastery, emotional safety, and positive youth development. Plan, prepare, and implement age-appropriate lessons and activities that support participant growth and engagement. Use observation, critical thinking, and feedback strategies to evaluate participants and support staff performance. Monitor participant safety always and administer first aid when necessary. Initiate emergency action procedures appropriately and calmly. Attend required orientation sessions, program meetings, and in‑service training. Check out, request, and properly use equipment and supplies for activities. Transferable Skill Development OSU is committed to ensuring students are prepared for success after graduation through intentional skill development as student employees. The competencies students develop while working at OSU are defined by the National Association of Colleges and Employers (NACE) as the essential skills employers seek in their hires. In this position, students learn and build vital career-readiness skills in the primary areas below:   Communication, Critical Thinking, Teamwork, Professionalism Qualifications: Full Employment Eligibility Requirements can be found here:   https://hr.oregonstate.edu/student-employment-manual/500-employment-eligibility-requirements Must be academically enrolled in a high school, community college, or university and pursuing a program or course of study Must meet Academic Standing Requirements; students on academic suspension are not eligible for employment Must meet the applicable minimal enrollment standard High School student: Regularly enrolled in a high school or participating in a home-schooling program Undergraduate and post-baccalaureate student: 6 credit hours per term Undergraduate international student: 12 credit hours per term* Graduate student officially admitted to Graduate School: 5 credit hours per term Graduate international student officially admitted to Graduate School: 9 credit hours per term* *International students may be allowed to carry fewer hours than specified above and still be considered “full-time” by the United States Citizenship and Immigration Services ( USCIS ). A reduced course load is approved by the   Office of International Services   ( OIS ), and must be provided to the Student Employment Center. Required Qualifications: Current, valid First Aid certification by the start of programming. Recognizing and Reporting Child Abuse and Neglect (RRCAN) certification by start of programming. Introduction to Child Care Health & Safety certification by start of programming. Fundamentals of Learning. Additional Required Qualifications: This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a Criminal History Check and be determined to be position qualified as per OSU Standard 576-055-0000 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in Youth Programs may have additional Criminal History Checks every 2 years. Working Conditions/Schedule: • On campus pools, Langton and Women Building. • Afternoons Monday thru Friday, weekdays may vary like Tuesday, Thursday, Friday. Weekends, Saturday and Sunday. • Can possibly accommodate working one day on the weekend, Saturday or Sunday. • KidSpirit swim lessons run both Saturday and Sunday. Salary Range: Anticipated Start Date: 2026-08-31 Anticipated End Date:"
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/Student-Project-Assistant_JR0001004": {
      "title": "Student Project Assistant",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-02\nPosted: Posted 3 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nDepartment: Fisheries, Wildlife, and Conservation Sciences Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents: A 1-page (or less) cover letter and resume that specifically addresses required and desired qualifications listed in this post. For additional information please contact: Lauren Rice at lauren.rice@oregonstate.edu We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status.   OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire.   Note: All job offers are contingent upon Human Resources final approval Job Description: This recruitment will be used to fill two part-time (a maximum of 24 hours per week) Project Assistant positions for the Human Dimensions Lab in the Department of Fisheries, Wildlife, and Conservation Sciences at Oregon State University (OSU). The Human Dimensions Lab at Oregon State University is seeking two research assistants to provide technical and data entry support for a study on human wellbeing in the Puget Sound. The project assistants will input survey data into an Excel database, ensuring accuracy, consistency, and efficiency. They will work closely under the project lead, Lauren Rice, to develop strategies and expectations for their work. Position Duties Completion of human subjects training through the CITI Program Read literature relevant to the project Enter survey data using Microsoft Excel Develop strategies for issues that may arise during data entry Communicate often with project lead, including check-ins and deadlines Transferable Skill Development OSU is committed to ensuring students are prepared for success after graduation through intentional skill development as student employees. The competencies students develop while working at OSU are defined by the National Association of Colleges and Employers (NACE) as the essential skills employers seek in their hires. In this position, students learn and build vital career-readiness skills in the primary areas below:   Communication, Teamwork, Professionalism, Career & Self Development Qualifications: Full Employment Eligibility Requirements can be found here:   https://hr.oregonstate.edu/student-employment-manual/500-employment-eligibility-requirements Must be academically enrolled in a high school, community college, or university and pursuing a program or course of study Must meet Academic Standing Requirements; students on academic suspension are not eligible for employment Must meet the applicable minimal enrollment standard High School student: Regularly enrolled in a high school or participating in a home-schooling program Undergraduate and post-baccalaureate student: 6 credit hours per term Undergraduate international student: 12 credit hours per term* Graduate student officially admitted to Graduate School: 5 credit hours per term Graduate international student officially admitted to Graduate School: 9 credit hours per term* *International students may be allowed to carry fewer hours than specified above and still be considered “full-time” by the United States Citizenship and Immigration Services ( USCIS ). A reduced course load is approved by the   Office of International Services   ( OIS ), and must be provided to the Student Employment Center. Required Able to complete high-quality work doing repetitive tasks Detail oriented and able to work independently but not shy to seek advice Comfortable with Microsoft or similar software products Able to follow confidentiality procedures and be committed to ethical research practices Undergraduate and graduate students welcome to apply Work study and non-work study eligible students welcome to apply On campus and Ecampus students welcome but must work on OSU, Corvallis campus Preferred Some experience with survey data Some experience with Microsoft Excel Some exposure to qualitative research methods Completion of IRB CITI human subjects training Working Conditions/Schedule: All work will be completed on a laptop computer indoors. Laptops and software are provided. Salary Range: 17 Anticipated Start Date: 2026-10-15 Anticipated End Date:"
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/Creative---Design-Services-Student-Team-Member_JR0000984-1": {
      "title": "Creative & Design Services Student Team Member",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-02\nPosted: Posted 3 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nDepartment: Printing & Mailing Services Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents: A Resume A Cover Letter A Porfolio For additional information please contact: Kent St Clair at kent.stclair@oregonstate.edu We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status.   This position requires driving a University vehicle or a personal vehicle on behalf of the University; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a Motor Vehicle History Check , possess and maintain a current, valid driver's license in their state of residence, be determined to be position qualified and self-report convictions (as per Voluntary and Compulsory Driver Standards OSU Standard 125-155-0200) as per OSU Standard 576-056-0000 et seq.   OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire.   Note: All job offers are contingent upon Human Resources final approval Job Description: This recruitment will be used to fill eight part-time (a maximum of 24 hours per week) Creative & Design Services Team Member position for the Printing and Mailing Services at Oregon State University (OSU). Marketing Services and Solutions DAM Creative team is looking for Creatives! (Student graphic designers, illustrators, animators, photographers, and video editors) to join our creative team. This position offers a unique opportunity for students to: Create visually compelling designs for a wide range of marketing materials, including websites, posters, social media graphics, and more. Work closely with team members to bring fresh ideas to life. Use the latest design tools and technologies to create high-quality, impactful visual content. Contribute to a culture of creativity and collaboration, delivering designs that exceed expectations. Why Join Us? Gain real-world experience working with university departments and clients across OSU. Build a strong portfolio with creative projects that stand out in the industry. Work in a fast-paced, collaborative environment where your ideas are valued and your growth is encouraged. Access cutting-edge tools and technologies to sharpen your design skills. Position Duties Graphic Designers: Design and produce marketing materials such as flyers, posters, banners, digital ads, impact reports As a part of the DAM Creative team, you will work collaboratively to ensure that all designs meet the program’s needs and brand standards. Contribute to the visual design aspects of DAM Creative web presence and promotional campaign Experience with Adobe Creative Suite Photographers: Capture high-quality photographs of programs, events, and activities, ensuring coverage of key moments and features. Edit, process, and prepare photos for marketing materials, social media, and web content. Collaborate with graphic designers to ensure that photos are integrated effectively (in a way that enhances the overall design and message) into various design projects. Video Editors: Develop and edit video content highlighting programs, events, and promotional efforts. Assist in the planning, filming, and producing video projects, ensuring content is engaging Work with the team to produce videos that effectively tell the story of DAM Creatives impact and offerings. Transferable Skill Development OSU is committed to ensuring students are prepared for success after graduation through intentional skill development as student employees. The competencies students develop while working at OSU are defined by the National Association of Colleges and Employers (NACE) as the essential skills employers seek in their hires. In this position, students learn and build vital career-readiness skills in the primary areas below:   Communication, Teamwork, Technology Career & Self Development Qualifications: Full Employment Eligibility Requirements can be found here:   https://hr.oregonstate.edu/student-employment-manual/500-employment-eligibility-requirement Must be academically enrolled in a high school, community college, or university and pursuing a program or course of study Must meet Academic Standing Requirements; students on academic suspension are not eligible for employment Must meet the applicable minimal enrollment standard High School student: Regularly enrolled in a high school or participating in a home-schooling program Undergraduate and post-baccalaureate student: 6 credit hours per term Undergraduate international student: 12 credit hours per term* Graduate student officially admitted to Graduate School: 5 credit hours per term Graduate international student officially admitted to Graduate School: 9 credit hours per term* *International students may be allowed to carry fewer hours than specified above and still be considered “full-time” by the United States Citizenship and Immigration Services ( USCIS ). A reduced course load is approved by the   Office of International Services   ( OIS ), and must be provided to the Student Employment Center. Required This position requires driving a University vehicle or a personal vehicle on behalf of the University; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a Motor Vehicle History Check , possess and maintain a current, valid driver's license in their state of residence, be determined to be position qualified and self-report convictions (as per Voluntary and Compulsory Driver Standards OSU Standard 125-155-0200) as per OSU Standard 576-056-0000 et seq. Preferred Working towards a degree in Graphic Design, Photography, Videography, Visual Arts, Media Studies, or a related field Experience in the field are encouraged to apply Demonstrated experience in graphic design, photography, or video editing, with a strong portfolio of previous work Experienced in Adobe Creative Suite (InDesign, Photoshop, Illustrator, Premiere Pro, etc.), and other relevant multimedia design and editing tools (such as Lightroom, Final Cut Pro, or After Effects) Excellent communication and teamwork skills, proactive attitude and a willingness to take on multiple projects Ability to work flexibly Opportunity to work creatively and solve complex problems with visual solutions, A demonstrable commitment to promoting and enhancing diversity Working Conditions/Schedule: Salary Range: Anticipated Start Date: 2026-10-23 Anticipated End Date:"
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/Beaver-Classic-Retail-Manager_JR0000232": {
      "title": "Beaver Classic Retail Manager",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-01\nPosted: Posted 4 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nDepartment: Food Science & Technology Special Instructions to Applicants: The closing date has been extended in order to expand the original applicant pool. To ensure full consideration, applications must be received by August 16, 2026. Applications will continue to be accepted after the full consideration date, until a sufficient applicant pool has been achieved or the position is filled. The closing date is subject to change without notice to applicants. When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents:   1) A resume/CV; and   2) A cover letter indicating how your education, training, and professional experience have prepared you for this position.   Starting salary will be commensurate with skills, education, and experience.   For additional information please contact: Lisbeth Goddik lisbeth.goddik@oregonstate.edu Job Summary: The Department of Food Science & Technology is seeking a Beaver Classic Retail Manager. This is a full-time (1.00 FTE), 12-month, professional faculty position. Join the Beaver Classic™ program within Oregon State University's Department of Food Science and Technology and play a leadership role in a unique, student-centered retail enterprise that connects food production, product innovation, and hands-on learning. The Beaver Classic Retail Manager oversees sales and operations across the Beaver Classic Market, food truck, farmers markets, e-commerce, and community events while working closely with faculty, staff, and production teams to bring high-quality, student-made products to consumers. This position offers the opportunity to shape Beaver Classic retail strategy, expand product offerings, and strengthen community engagement while supporting OSU's mission of teaching, research, and outreach. As part of the Department of Food Science and Technology , this position helps provide meaningful experiential learning opportunities for students while managing a growing and highly visible Beaver Classic retail program. The role combines business operations, marketing, financial management, and customer engagement in a collaborative environment dedicated to innovation, food science education, and excellence in product development and production. Learn more about Beaver Classic and the Department of Food Science and Technology at https://beaverclassic.com/ and https://foodsci.oregonstate.edu/ Key Responsibilities: Management of Beaver Classic Market, Food Truck, Events, and Online Sales Provide leadership and oversight for Beaver Classic retail operations, including the Beaver Classic Market, Food Truck, catering and special events, and online sales. Manage daily operations, sales activities, merchandising, product fulfillment, and customer service to ensure a high-quality customer experience and achievement of revenue goals. Strategic Planning and Partnership Development Develop, implement, and evaluate strategic plans that support Beaver Classic retail growth, profitability, and brand awareness. Administrative Management Evaluate and improve operational procedures, policies, training materials, and business practices to ensure efficient, customer-focused operations. Service Serve on department, college, and university committees and participate in activities that contribute to the success of the Department of Food Science and Technology, the College of Agricultural Sciences, and Oregon State University. Qualifications: Required Qualifications: Associate degree in food service management, nutritional care, food science, business, or related field AND four years of food service supervisory experience; OR a bachelor’s degree with a major course work in restaurant or hotel management, food science, business, or a closely related field and one year of food service supervisory experience. Demonstrated experience in food safety including microbial and allergen controls. Experience managing budgets, calculating Cost of Goods Sold (COGS), and profit and loss accounting. Experience supervising and mentoring entry-level employees and/or students. Ability to work irregular hours including evenings and weekends, including communication outside of working hours. For example, may include Saturday Farmers Market, and College of Agricultural Sciences special events. Ability to lift and carry items weighing up to 50 lbs, and push/pull items weighing 50-100 lbs. Must acquire a Oregon Food Handler’s license within 15 days of hire, and maintain license by renewing every 3 years. Commitment to promoting and enhancing inclusive excellence. This position requires driving a university vehicle or a personal vehicle on behalf of the university; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a motor vehicle history check, possess and maintain a current, valid driver's license in their state of residence, be determined to be position qualified and self-report convictions as per University Policy 05-030. This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a criminal history check and be determined to be position qualified as per University Standard: 05-010 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in youth programs may have additional criminal history checks every 24 months.   Preferred Qualifications: Strong preference for a bachelor’s degree with a major course work in restaurant or hotel management or a closely related field. Demonstrated effective customer relations, interpersonal skills, and communication. Able to prioritize and complete assigned multiple tasks and responsibilities promptly. Hazard Analysis and Critical Control Points (HACCP) training. Experience running a small business. Working Conditions/Schedule: Working Conditions/Work Schedule: Work environment includes working in various environments including office, food truck in outdoor environment for events, retail store, storage and freezer or refrigerated areas, and other environments as needed. Position requires working some irregular hours including evenings and weekends. Position requires travel to event locations with food truck or other vehicle. Event venues include on and off campus college or university events, local farmers markets, festivals/fairs, and others. Position will lift and carry items weighing up to 50 lbs, push/pull items weighing 50-100 lbs, will require climbing stairs and repetitive motions. Salary Range: $55,000 - $67,000 Anticipated Start Date: 2026-11-02 Anticipated End Date: We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status. OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire. OSU is a fair chance employer committed to inclusive hiring. We encourage applications from candidates who bring a wide range of lived experience including involvement with the justice system. If this position has “critical or security-sensitive” responsibilities and/or driving duties and you are selected as a finalist, your initial job offer will be contingent upon the results of a job-related pre-employment check (such as a background check, motor vehicle history check, sexual misconduct reference check, etc.). Background check results do not automatically disqualify a candidate. Take a look at our Background Checks website including the for candidates section for more details. If you have questions or concerns about the pre-employment check, please contact OSU’s Employee and Labor Relations team at employee.relations@oregonstate.edu ."
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/Auto-Detailer-Office-Student-Assistant_JR0000897-1": {
      "title": "Auto Detailer/Office Student Assistant",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-01\nPosted: Posted 4 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nDepartment: University Motor Pool Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents: A Resume A Cover Letter A copy of class schedule in day/time format For additional information please contact: Justin Fleming at Justin.Fleming@oregonstate.edu We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status.   OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire.   Note: All job offers are contingent upon Human Resources final approval Job Description: This recruitment will be used to fill six part-time (a maximum of 24 hours per week) Auto Detailer/ Office Student Assistant positions for the Motor pool at Oregon State University (OSU). Position Duties: Vehicle detailing including vacuuming, shampooing, wiping down surfaces, retrieving lost and found and checking for vehicle damage. Vehicle fueling Parking in designated areas Transferable Skill Development OSU is committed to ensuring students are prepared for success after graduation through intentional skill development as student employees. The competencies students develop while working at OSU are defined by the National Association of Colleges and Employers (NACE) as the essential skills employers seek in their hires. In this position, students learn and build vital career-readiness skills in the primary areas below:   Communication, Teamwork, Professionalism, Career & Self Development Qualifications: Full Employment Eligibility Requirements can be found here:   https://hr.oregonstate.edu/student-employment-manual/500-employment-eligibility-requirements Must be academically enrolled in a high school, community college, or university and pursuing a program or course of study Must meet Academic Standing Requirements; students on academic suspension are not eligible for employment Must meet the applicable minimal enrollment standard High School student: Regularly enrolled in a high school or participating in a home-schooling program Undergraduate and post-baccalaureate student: 6 credit hours per term Undergraduate international student: 12 credit hours per term* Graduate student officially admitted to Graduate School: 5 credit hours per term Graduate international student officially admitted to Graduate School: 9 credit hours per term* *International students may be allowed to carry fewer hours than specified above and still be considered “full-time” by the United States Citizenship and Immigration Services ( USCIS ). A reduced course load is approved by the   Office of International Services   ( OIS) and must be provided to the Student Employment Center. Required Qualifications: 18 years old or older Valid driver license Preferred Qualifications: Ability to work independently as well as part of a team Working Conditions/Schedule: Lifting, working outdoors, extended periods of standing, traversing uneven terrain etc. Some weekend and evening shifts can be expected. Salary Range: N/A Anticipated Start Date: 2026-09-23 Anticipated End Date:"
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Newport-Campus/Custodain_JR0000313": {
      "title": "Custodian",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-01\nPosted: Posted 4 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nDepartment: Hatfield Marine Science Center Special Instructions to Applicants: The closing date has been extended and a full consideration date has been added in order to expand the original applicant pool.   To ensure full consideration, applications must be received by August 6, 2026. Applications will continue to be accepted after the full consideration date, until a sufficient applicant pool has been achieved or the position is filled. The closing date is subject to change without notice to applicants. When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents:   1) A resume/CV; and   2) A cover letter indicating how your qualifications and experience have prepared you for this position. Starting salary will be commensurate with skills, education, and experience.   For additional information please contact: James Bielenberg at James.Bielenberg@oregonstate.edu or 541-867-0149. Job Summary: This recruitment will be used to fill four full-time Custodian positions for the Hatfield Marine Science Center at Oregon State University (OSU). This position is located in Newport, Oregon. The Hatfield Marine Science Center (HMSC) is Oregon State University’s campus for research, education, and outreach in marine and coastal sciences. The Marine Science Center is located 55 miles west of the OSU main campus in Newport, OR. Through its partnerships, HMSC improves scientific understanding of marine systems, coastal processes and resources, and applies this knowledge to social, economic, and environmental issues. The HMSC Custodian position is part of a team working in the evening shift with the primary purpose of ensuring that OSU and specified federal buildings on the HMSC campus are clean, well lit, secure and provide a conducive environment for quality learning and research. This position works closely with the Custodial Services Coordinator and reports to the Custodial Supervisor. This position is a member of the HMSC Custodial Team located in Newport, Oregon and provides custodial support to over 20 facilities on 50 acres of the HMSC campus. This position is eligible for paid health benefits and retirement plans, and paid vacation, sick, holiday and personal time off. It is also eligible for reduced tuition. As a shift worker, you will receive shift differential pay and overtime for work on holidays. Key Responsibilities: Cleaning and Sanitization: Clean restrooms, large classrooms, laboratories, museum space, library, high density public access areas and touchpoints, staff lounge, areas around the buildings, empty trash and recycling, clean walls and other surfaces, cleans windows and mirrors, clean and sanitize toilets, sinks and showers, fill soap and paper dispensers, dust, sweep, mop, strip floors, wax and buff floors, vacuum and shampoo carpets, pick up litter, shovel snow, sweep steps, and wash exterior windows. General Security and Safety: Report problems or concerns to the Custodial Services Coordinator or the Custodial Supervisor. Check to make sure buildings are secured, doors and gates are locked at appropriate times and lights are turned off. Report any suspicious activity. Resupply: Stock shelves and/or cleaning carts with needed supplies, clean the carts and equipment at the end of the shift, test new products and make a list of supplies needed. Keep custodial closets neat and orderly. Event Set up: Assist with set-up and breakdown (tables, chairs, waste receptacles, etc.) during special events. Other: Other duties as assigned. Qualifications: Required Qualifications: At least one year of professional cleaning experience Must be available to work weekends and some holidays. The custodian shift is 40 hours per week. It begins at 5:00 pm and ends at 1:00 am. Must be highly motivated, have experience working in a team environment and have exceptional customer service skills. Ability to lift, carry, push and pull objects weighing up to 50 lbs. In accordance with the Department of Commerce Background Investigation policy, the incumbent must successfully obtain the appropriate security clearance within 90 days of the employment start date. This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a criminal history check and be determined to be position qualified as per University Standard: 05-010 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in youth programs may have additional criminal history checks every 24 months. This position requires driving a university vehicle or a personal vehicle on behalf of the university; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a motor vehicle history check, possess and maintain a current, valid driver's license in their state of residence, be determined to be position qualified and self-report convictions as per University Policy 05-030. Preferred Qualifications: Prior experience cleaning research labs, high density public access areas, classrooms, and/or library space. Demonstrable ability to communicate in a manner that shows respect and inclusivity for all co-workers. Working Conditions/Schedule: Working Conditions/Work Schedule: 8am - 4:30pm, 12:30pm - 9pm, (2) 5pm-1pm shifts. Must be available to work some weekends and holidays. Additional shifts differential is paid for shift and weekend work. Overtime for worked holidays. Salary Range: $18.06 - $22.84 Anticipated Start Date: 2026-08-17 Anticipated End Date: We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status. OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire. OSU is a fair chance employer committed to inclusive hiring. We encourage applications from candidates who bring a wide range of lived experience including involvement with the justice system. If this position has “critical or security-sensitive” responsibilities and/or driving duties and you are selected as a finalist, your initial job offer will be contingent upon the results of a job-related pre-employment check (such as a background check, motor vehicle history check, sexual misconduct reference check, etc.). Background check results do not automatically disqualify a candidate. Take a look at our Background Checks website including the for candidates section for more details. If you have questions or concerns about the pre-employment check, please contact OSU’s Employee and Labor Relations team at employee.relations@oregonstate.edu ."
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/Greenhouse-Student-Crew_JR0000976": {
      "title": "Greenhouse Student Crew",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-01\nPosted: Posted 4 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nDepartment: College of Agricultural Sciences Greenhouse Operations Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents: A Resume A Cover Letter Fall 2026 schedule of availability A supplemental document answering the following question: Do you have weekly availability to work on Mondays and Wednesdays from 8-11am? For additional information please contact: Lydia Graber at Lydia.graber@oregonstate.edu We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status.   OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire.   Note: All job offers are contingent upon Human Resources final approval Job Description: This recruitment will be used to fill two part-time (a maximum of 24 hours per week) Greenhouse Student Crew Positions for the OSU West Greenhouses at Oregon State University (OSU). Position will perform routine to complex technical or specialized tasks in support of greenhouse research. Primary tasks include daily temperature and HVC checks, hand watering, and assisting in greenhouse maintenance tasks. Occasional weekend work required. Selection is dependent on availability and class schedule. Position Duties 100% Duties to be determined by hiring unit. Other duties include: Hand watering Checks of environmental control system General greenhouse clean up and sanitation Occasional greenhouse maintenance and upkeep Transferable Skill Development OSU is committed to ensuring students are prepared for success after graduation through intentional skill development as student employees. The competencies students develop while working at OSU are defined by the National Association of Colleges and Employers (NACE) as the essential skills employers seek in their hires. In this position, students learn and build vital career-readiness skills in the primary areas below:   Communication, Critical Thinking, Teamwork Qualifications: Full Employment Eligibility Requirements can be found here:   https://hr.oregonstate.edu/student-employment-manual/500-employment-eligibility-requirements Must be academically enrolled in a high school, community college, or university and pursuing a program or course of study Must meet Academic Standing Requirements; students on academic suspension are not eligible for employment Must meet the applicable minimal enrollment standard High School student: Regularly enrolled in a high school or participating in a home-schooling program Undergraduate and post-baccalaureate student: 6 credit hours per term Undergraduate international student: 12 credit hours per term* Graduate student officially admitted to Graduate School: 5 credit hours per term Graduate international student officially admitted to Graduate School: 9 credit hours per term* *International students may be allowed to carry fewer hours than specified above and still be considered “full-time” by the United States Citizenship and Immigration Services ( USCIS ). A reduced course load is approved by the   Office of International Services   ( OIS ), and must be provided to the Student Employment Center. Required Full/part-time undergraduate student Ability to work independently with minimum supervision. Ability to communicate verbally and in writing to greenhouse staff and clientele. Ability to lift up to 30 lbs, use ladders, and manipulate watering hoses. Ability to work occasional weekends and holidays. Ability to work in extreme temperatures, lighting, humidity, and dust conditions Preferred A demonstrable commitment to promoting and enhancing diversity. Basic understanding of plant cultural needs such as watering and environment. Working Conditions/Schedule: - Weekly schedule of 6-12 hours plus occasional required shifts on weekends and holidays - Fall 2026 applicants must have weekly availability on Mondays and Wednesdays from 8am-11am to be considered for the position - May have exposure to noxious odors, loud noises, wet and slippery floors, and machinery. - May be exposed to extreme temperatures, lighting, humidity, and dust conditions. - May maneuver up to 30 lbs. and may be required to stoop, bend and kneel on hard floors as necessary, and push or pull carts. Salary Range: Anticipated Start Date: 2026-09-18 Anticipated End Date:"
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/Assistant-Professor--Sociology---Crime--Law--and-Deviance_JR0000609": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor: Sociology - Crime, Law, and Deviance",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-01\nPosted: Posted 4 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nDepartment: College of Liberal Arts Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents:   1) A Curriculum Vitae   2) A Cover letter indicating how your qualifications and experience have prepared you for this position.   3) A Statement of Research 4) A Statement of Teaching (including teaching philosophy) 5) Please provide a Writing Sample Letters of Reference will be requested for those advanced to the reference stage. At that time, you will be asked to provide contact information for your referees who will be sent a secure quicklink that will allow them to upload the requested letters of reference on your behalf. Starting salary will be commensurate with skills, education, and experience. For additional information please contact: Scott Akins Scott.Akins@oregonstate.edu 541-737-2811 Job Summary: The College of Liberal Arts invites applications for a full-time (1.00 FTE), 9-month, tenure-track Assistant Professor position, to begin Fall 2027. Appointment at the Assistant Professor rank is anticipated; however, appointment at a promoted rank may be considered depending upon the qualifications of the successful candidate. Any hiring at the rank of Associate Professor/Professor with indefinite tenure will be subject to a successful out-of-cycle tenure review process: https://academicaffairs.oregonstate.edu/resources-policies-legislation/policies-and-resources/promotion-and-tenure-guidelines/out-cycle . Candidates should have a PhD in Sociology, Criminology, or closely associated field, with a research focus in crime, law, and deviance .  Advanced ABDs will be considered, but completion of PhD is required by time of hire. Candidates should be prepared to teach five courses (in a quarter system, 2/2/1) in Sociology and Criminology, both in-person and online. Experience teaching and developing online classes is preferred. Preferred candidates will also contribute to graduate education, through teaching and advising graduate students in the School of Public Policy. Successful candidates will have an active research agenda and demonstrate potential for eventual promotion and tenure.  Key Responsibilities: Teach 5 courses each academic year Be able to teach both in person and online Be able to develop online courses Contribute to Graduate education Advise graduate students Have active research Qualifications: Required Qualifications: PhD in Sociology, Criminology, or closely associated field, with a research focus in crime, law, and deviance.  Advanced ABDs will be considered, but completion of PhD is required by time of hire. Candidates should be prepared to teach five courses (quarter system 2/2/1) in Sociology and Criminology, both in-person and online. Preferred Qualifications: Preferred candidates will be those able and willing to teach both in-person and online. Candidates will be expected to teach courses in criminology as well as general sociology. Experience teaching and developing online classes is preferred. Preferred candidates will also contribute to graduate education, through teaching and mentoring graduate students in the School of Public Policy. Successful candidates will have an active research agenda and demonstrate potential for eventual promotion and tenure. Working Conditions/Schedule: The faculty member should expect to work primarily on campus and be available for regular on-campus in-person teaching and meetings. This is not a remote position. Salary Range: Salary is commensurate with skills, education, and experience. Anticipated Start Date: 2026-12-11 Anticipated End Date: We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status. OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire. OSU is a fair chance employer committed to inclusive hiring. We encourage applications from candidates who bring a wide range of lived experience including involvement with the justice system. If this position has “critical or security-sensitive” responsibilities and/or driving duties and you are selected as a finalist, your initial job offer will be contingent upon the results of a job-related pre-employment check (such as a background check, motor vehicle history check, sexual misconduct reference check, etc.). Background check results do not automatically disqualify a candidate. Take a look at our Background Checks website including the for candidates section for more details. If you have questions or concerns about the pre-employment check, please contact OSU’s Employee and Labor Relations team at employee.relations@oregonstate.edu ."
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/Assistant-Professor--Sr-Res-_JR0000967": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor (Sr Res)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-01\nPosted: Posted 4 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nDepartment: College of Engineering Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents:   1) A Curriculum Vitae   2) A Cover letter indicating how your qualifications and experience have prepared you for this position. Starting salary will be commensurate with skills, education, and experience.   For additional information please contact: T. Matthew Evans matt.evans@oregonstate.edu Job Summary: The College of Engineering, School of Civil & Construction Engineering, invites applications for a full-time (1.00 FTE), 12-month, fixed-term Assistant Professor (Senior Research) position. Reappointment is at the discretion of the School Head. This position conducts research in granular mechanics and granular-continuum wear mechanisms. Incumbent will have expertise in several of the following research topics: solid-fluid transitions in granular materials; discrete and continuum modeling methods; granular-continuum mechanical interactions; and rock, soil, and debris behavior. Incumbent will secure external funding to directly support this position and other aspects of this research program. Collaborate and engage in interdisciplinary research with faculty in mechanics and materials and across OSU, nationally, and internationally, mentor students, help in the development of models, algorithms, and software for granular materials research. Key Responsibilities: Perform duties of principal investigator (PI) or co-principal investigator (co-PI), or senior personnel including leading or contributing to the development of research proposals, maintaining project budgets, corresponding with program managers, and preparing required project reports. Publish high impact scientific journal articles. Support or co-lead sponsored research within the Geotechnical Engineering group. Support undergraduate and graduate students, and postdoctoral researchers in data analysis, simulation, and writing. Provide training to undergraduate and graduate students and postdoctoral researchers on specific software and simulation methods. Provide mentorship to undergraduate and graduate student researchers, and postdoctoral researchers. Periodically organize and teach seminars and workshops. Qualifications: Required Qualifications: Ph.D. in Civil Engineering or a closely related discipline. Demonstrated ability to acquire research funding, lead research projects, and contribute to the research program in Civil and Construction Engineering. Strong publication record in quality peer-reviewed journals and presentations at conferences. 3 years of demonstrated experience in granular mechanics, or closely related materials area, in industry, academic, and/or postdoctoral research. Experience working effectively as part of a multi-disciplinary team with diverse groups of students, researchers, and staff. This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a criminal history check and be determined to be position qualified as per University Standard: 05-010 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in youth programs may have additional criminal history checks every 24 months. Preferred Qualifications: Ph.D. focus in constitutive of discrete modeling of granular materials. Demonstrated experience working with various discrete element method (DEM) software. Proposal preparation experience. Mentoring of undergraduate, graduate and/or postdoctoral researchers. Working Conditions/Schedule: The incumbent will typically have flexibility in their specific working hours but would be expected to generally be available during normal business hours for meetings and discussions related to projects. They may occasionally be subject to tight deadlines, which may sometimes require extended work hours for limited periods. The incumbent may periodically travel to conferences and scientific meetings. Salary Range: Salary is commensurate with skills, education, and experience. Anticipated Start Date: 2026-09-16 Anticipated End Date: 2027-06-30 We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status. OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire. OSU is a fair chance employer committed to inclusive hiring. We encourage applications from candidates who bring a wide range of lived experience including involvement with the justice system. If this position has “critical or security-sensitive” responsibilities and/or driving duties and you are selected as a finalist, your initial job offer will be contingent upon the results of a job-related pre-employment check (such as a background check, motor vehicle history check, sexual misconduct reference check, etc.). Background check results do not automatically disqualify a candidate. Take a look at our Background Checks website including the for candidates section for more details. If you have questions or concerns about the pre-employment check, please contact OSU’s Employee and Labor Relations team at employee.relations@oregonstate.edu ."
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/Instructor-Pool---2026-2027--Chemistry_JR0000827": {
      "title": "Instructor Pool - 2026/2027: Chemistry",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-01\nPosted: Posted 4 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nDepartment: College of Science Special Instructions to Applicants: Applications will be reviewed and considered as opportunities arise throughout the 2026/2027 academic year. When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents:   1) A resume/CV; and   2) A cover letter indicating how your qualifications and experience have prepared you for this position. Starting salary will be commensurate with skills, education, and experience. For additional information please contact: Paula Christie Paula.christie@oregonstate.edu 541-737-1681 Job Summary: The Department of Chemistry invites applications for one or more fixed-term, non-tenure-track, full/part-time, Instructor positions to teach on a term-by-term basis for the 2026-2027 academic year. Some of these appointments may be reviewed for renewal or transition to an instructional position on an annual basis at the discretion of the Department Chair. Applications submitted to this recruitment pool are reviewed on an as needed basis. Qualified candidates will be contacted when positions become available. Instructors may be needed to teach courses, either on-campus or Ecampus, in the following areas: analytical, biological, bio-organic, general, environmental, materials, nuclear, Organic, and physical chemistry. The OSU  Department of Chemistry plays a central role in the University's educational, research, and outreach missions, delivering nearly 100,000 student credit hours of instruction annually while maintaining a vibrant graduate program with approximately 100 PhD students. The Department conducts cutting-edge research and scholarly activities focused on all areas of chemistry important to biology, materials, and the environment, including analytical, biological, bio-organic, environmental, materials, nuclear, and physical chemistry. This dynamic setting is ideally suited for nurturing and supporting instructional faculty in their efforts to support the Department’s educational mission, which impacts over 10,000 students each year. The College of Science provides a core instructional role at OSU , supporting the ideals of learning, discovery, and engagement that are the foundation of a land-grant university. The College embraces instruction and research in disciplines ranging from the physical, mathematical, and statistical sciences to the life sciences, grounded in unbiased inquiry and a dedication to discovery and innovation. The College of Science is committed to partnering with industry and public agencies to address some of the most compelling challenges of today and tomorrow. Key Responsibilities: Instruction Administer and teach up to 4 on campus or ecampus (training prior to teaching will be provided and is required) undergraduate courses Hold regular Office hours to assist students with coursework and answer questions regarding the curriculum Provide supervision/lead work for up to 5 Graduate or Undergraduate student employees Following college practices on early warnings, student feedbacks, and grading policies Qualifications: Required Qualifications: Master’s degree in Chemistry or a closely related discipline appropriate to the field of teaching/instructional tasks. Teaching experience at the college and/or university level. An evident commitment to educational equity. This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a criminal history check and be determined to be position qualified as per University Standard: 05-010 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in youth programs may have additional criminal history checks every 24 months. Preferred Requirements: Ph.D. in Chemistry or a closely related discipline to the field of teaching/instructional tasks. Working Conditions/Schedule: Salary Range: Salary is commensurate with skills, education, and experience. Anticipated Start Date: 2026-09-16 Anticipated End Date: 2027-06-15 We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status. OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire. OSU is a fair chance employer committed to inclusive hiring. We encourage applications from candidates who bring a wide range of lived experience including involvement with the justice system. If this position has “critical or security-sensitive” responsibilities and/or driving duties and you are selected as a finalist, your initial job offer will be contingent upon the results of a job-related pre-employment check (such as a background check, motor vehicle history check, sexual misconduct reference check, etc.). Background check results do not automatically disqualify a candidate. Take a look at our Background Checks website including the for candidates section for more details. If you have questions or concerns about the pre-employment check, please contact OSU’s Employee and Labor Relations team at employee.relations@oregonstate.edu ."
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Eugene/Open-Campus---Juntos-Coordinator_JR0000943": {
      "title": "Open Campus & Juntos Coordinator",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-01\nPosted: Posted 4 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nDepartment: The Division of Extension and Engagement Special Instructions to Applicants: To ensure full consideration, applications must be received by September 15, 2026. Applications will continue to be accepted after the full consideration date, until a sufficient applicant pool has been achieved or the position is filled. The closing date is subject to change without notice to applicants.  When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents:    1) Curriculum Vitae/Resume: Finalists will be required to give an open presentation, which may include the distribution of their redacted curriculum vitae/resume and a short biography.   2) A cover letter indicating how your qualifications and experience have prepared you for this position.    Due to funding, OSU will not sponsor immigration work authorization for this position (e.g., H-1B, etc.).   Starting salary will be commensurate with skills, education, and experience.    For additional information please contact:   Nayeli Contreras nayeli.contreras@oregonstate.edu Job Summary: The Division of Extension and Engagement is seeking an Open Campus and Juntos Coordinator. This is a full-time (1.00 FTE), 12-month, professional faculty position that serves Lane County , Oregon. This position is located in Eugene, Oregon. The mission of the OSU Division of Extension and Engagement (division) is to work with communities throughout Oregon to support lifelong learning and co-create innovative solutions to address local challenges.  Together, we envision a future where Oregon’s people, communities and ecosystems prosper. This Open Campus & Juntos Coordinator is a member of the OSU Division of Extension and Engagement’s Partnerships Unit . This coordinator provides local leadership as well as coordinating and fostering partnerships needed to advance and sustain Open Campus and Juntos programs and goals. This individual advances partnerships between Oregon State, University, local school districts within Lane County, as well as Lane Community College and community-based organizations. Key Responsibilities Develop and maintain positive relationships with local stakeholders including school districts, community-based organizations, and higher education institutions to increase the programming of Open Campus and Juntos throughout Lane County. Deliver non-credit educational presentations at community events, K-12 schools, and community college partners. Coordinate Open Campus and Juntos program delivery throughout Lane County through building relationships, recruiting into program offerings, marketing programs following OSU guidelines, and performing data collection. Coordinate local college and career opportunities for students interested in attending Oregon State University and other higher education institutions. Provide fiscal management for the Open Campus and Juntos program in Lane County, including budget management, donations, grant management, and local support for programs. Report to the community and other stakeholders on local impacts. Develop and maintain Open Campus and Juntos university and community college partnerships regionally, particularly with Lane Community College. Implement continuous assessment of the community’s educational needs and align identified educational needs with current, changing, and emerging societal trends and resources. Qualifications: Required Qualifications: Bachelor’s degree in a related field. Degree must be completed by the application date. Experience in cross-cultural communication with the ability to engage Spanish speaking audiences through the utilization of bi-literate and bicultural skills. Strong verbal and written communication skills (English and Spanish) including effective use of digital communication tools (traditional and web-based) and printed media with both English and Spanish. Experience building collaborations with external organizations and maintaining professional relationships with external stakeholders. A commitment to collaborative and respectful learning and working environments that are safe, inclusive, and welcoming. Ability to work with audiences from diverse backgrounds and various cultural identities and to build community around culturally relevant programming. General knowledge and ability to use computers, including information technology, professional office software and collaborative work platforms. Ability to learn and adapt to using multiple forms of digital communication systems, including websites, email newsletters, social media, and other tools in a changing technological environment.  Ability to work independently with minimal supervision and manage competing time demands, while also working as a collaborative member of a geographically separated team. Ability to successfully interact with youth 12-19 years of age (7th through 12th grade) as well as adult learners. This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a criminal history check and be determined to be position qualified as per University Standard: 05-010 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in youth programs may have additional criminal history checks every 24 months.  This position requires driving a university vehicle or a personal vehicle on behalf of the university; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a motor vehicle history check, possess and maintain a current, valid driver’s license in their state of residence, be determined to be position qualified and self-report convictions as per University Policy 05-030. Preferred Qualifications: Master’s degree in education, education administration, adult education, Extension education, or related field. Experience within public education, higher education, vocational education or Extension Service education. Experience providing leadership with the Juntos program or other culturally responsive college access programming. Experience coordinating Extension Service programs.  Experience serving Latinx students in Oregon. Experience developing and or delivering curriculum around college access, financial aid, scholarships, or youth development (e.g., College Bound, AVID, ASPIRE). Ability to secure external funding support for educational programs. Ability to use web, social media, or other digital technologies in outreach activities to achieve goals. Working Conditions/Schedule: Working Conditions/Work Schedule: The primary work location for this position is the OSU Extension Lane County Office in Eugene, Oregon. A flexible work schedule may be a future option via OSU’s Flexible Work Arrangement Agreement Form, with pre-determined periodic reviews of on-going work for continuation of the agreement. This position requires flexibility to work in a variety of settings and in the evenings and on weekends. This position will serve Lane County, Oregon, and travel/driving will be required to meet with Partnerships Unit team and partners, attend trainings and/or unit programs, and to share information about the Partnerships Unit at conferences and other outreach opportunities as well as to fulfill the general work requirements of this position. Salary Range: $64,200 - $71,000 Anticipated Start Date: 2026-10-12 Anticipated End Date: We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status. OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire. OSU is a fair chance employer committed to inclusive hiring. We encourage applications from candidates who bring a wide range of lived experience including involvement with the justice system. If this position has “critical or security-sensitive” responsibilities and/or driving duties and you are selected as a finalist, your initial job offer will be contingent upon the results of a job-related pre-employment check (such as a background check, motor vehicle history check, sexual misconduct reference check, etc.). Background check results do not automatically disqualify a candidate. Take a look at our Background Checks website including the for candidates section for more details. If you have questions or concerns about the pre-employment check, please contact OSU’s Employee and Labor Relations team at employee.relations@oregonstate.edu ."
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/P-M-Production_JR0000886": {
      "title": "P&M Production",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-01\nPosted: Posted 4 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nDepartment: Printing & Mailing Services Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents: A Resume A Cover Letter Supplemental Question Supplemental Question: What’s your walk-up song if you were entering the print shop like a baseball player? For additional information please contact: Michelle Dunks at Michelle.dunks@oregonstate.edu We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status.   OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire.   Note: All job offers are contingent upon Human Resources final approval Job Description: This recruitment will be used to fill three (3) part-time (a maximum of 24 hours per week) Printing and Mailing Services Student Production Staff for the Printing & Mailing Services Department at Oregon State University (OSU). This position supports the daily operations of Oregon State University’s Printing and Mailing Services. Student employees will gain hands-on experience in a fast-paced production environment, contributing to the preparation, packaging, and distribution of printed and mailed materials. This role offers opportunities to develop professional competencies and transferable skills applicable across industries. Position Duties: Production Operations Operate mailing, bindery, and dye sublimation equipment Assemble and package printed materials Perform hand-finishing tasks such as envelope stuffing and labeling Facility and Inventory Support Restock paper and supplies Assist with inventory tracking Maintain cleanliness of production floor (e.g., sweeping, trash removal, cardboard breakdown) Other Duties as Assigned Support special projects and cross-functional tasks as needed Transferable Skill Development OSU is committed to ensuring students are prepared for success after graduation through intentional skill development as student employees. The competencies students develop while working at OSU are defined by the National Association of Colleges and Employers (NACE) as the essential skills employers seek in their hires. In this position, students learn and build vital career-readiness skills in the primary areas below:   Communication, Critical Thinking, Teamwork Qualifications: Full Employment Eligibility Requirements can be found here:   https://hr.oregonstate.edu/student-employment-manual/500-employment-eligibility-requirements Must be academically enrolled in a high school, community college, or university and pursuing a program or course of study Must meet Academic Standing Requirements; students on academic suspension are not eligible for employment Must meet the applicable minimal enrollment standard High School student: Regularly enrolled in a high school or participating in a home-schooling program Undergraduate and post-baccalaureate student: 6 credit hours per term Undergraduate international student: 12 credit hours per term* Graduate student officially admitted to Graduate School: 5 credit hours per term Graduate international student officially admitted to Graduate School: 9 credit hours per term* *International students may be allowed to carry fewer hours than specified above and still be considered “full-time” by the United States Citizenship and Immigration Services ( USCIS ). A reduced course load is approved by the   Office of International Services   ( OIS ), and must be provided to the Student Employment Center. Required Qualifications: Ability to travel to off campus production facility on Research Way Preferred Qualifications: Previous experience in printing, mailing, or general production environments Familiarity with warehouse or inventory systems Working Conditions/Schedule: Salary Range: Anticipated Start Date: 2026-09-23 Anticipated End Date:"
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/Analyst-Programmer--Analytics-Engineer--Internal-Employment-Opportunity-_JR0000571": {
      "title": "Analyst Programmer: Analytics Engineer (Internal Employment Opportunity)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-01\nPosted: Posted 4 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nDepartment: University Information Technology Special Instructions to Applicants: This is an Internal Employment Opportunity. When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents:   1) A resume/CV; and   2) A cover letter indicating how your qualifications and experience have prepared you for this position. 3) Please upload responses to the following questions as a separate document. Note: Tabular and/or bullet response format is welcome.   For each item below, briefly describe your Experience summary / Example work Specific tools + level of proficiency Years of use Type of experience (e.g. coursework only, internship, professional job)   Categories: SQL and data transformation. dbt or code-based transformation and modeling frameworks. Python or other scripting languages. Data modeling and analytical data products. Dashboard/report development (e.g. Power BI, Tableau) Cloud analytics platforms and modern data tooling (e.g., Fabric, Snowflake, AWS). GitHub for version control. Building solutions using enterprise level systems and data domains (ERP, Banner, Workday, Student, HR, Finance, Research, etc.). AI - Co-Development : experience and understanding of agentic development focused on data tasks.  Starting salary will be commensurate with skills, education, and experience. For additional information please contact: Chrysanthemum Hayes at chrysanthemum.hayes@oregonstate.edu Job Summary: This is an Internal Employment Opportunity This recruitment will be used to fill one full time (1.00 FTE), Analyst Programmer: Analytics Engineer, competency level 2, position for University Information Technology at Oregon State University (OSU). At Oregon State University (OSU), University Information and Technology (UIT) is at the forefront of the university's technological transformation. UIT is dedicated to empowering the entire university community by providing secure access to the right data, tools, and services needed to navigate and shape their unique paths to success. By protecting OSU’s technological assets and managing resources efficiently, we enable the university to thrive in an ever-evolving landscape.  Led by the Office of the Chief Information Officer (CIO), UIT manages IT operations for OSU’s three campuses, serving students in Oregon’s 36 counties and in more than 100 countries. UIT's leadership philosophy centers around a human-centric and fully architected technology landscape, leveraging enterprise solutions for common good services while empowering individual colleges and business units to innovate and invest in their missions and initiatives.  UIT designs and implements a seamless, interconnected digital ecosystem that aligns to the university’s strategic goals and maximizes impact. The division is comprised of trusted partners and consultants committed to igniting innovation and fostering a culture of continuous improvement by providing expert guidance and innovative solutions that support the university’s teaching, research, outreach and extension mission. Together, we are building a future where technology and education converge to create limitless possibilities.  Key Responsibilities: Plan, Build, and Maintain Enterprise-Facing Data Assets: Design, build, and maintain enterprise-ready analytical models, semantic layers, dashboards, reports, and analytical views that support institutional operations, planning, and decision making.  Sustainable Solution Design: Translate business requirements into well-structured, accurate, and reusable data models and reporting products in collaboration with technical and functional partners across OSU.  Validation, Testing and Documentation : Develop and optimize data transformations, analytical models, and reporting solutions within OSU’s modern data platform, including medallion architecture and enterprise semantic model patterns. Conduct structured testing, peer review, validation, and documentation of data transformations, model logic, business rules, and reporting outputs to support trusted enterprise use. Innovation, Emerging Technologies and Continuous Learning: Support continuous improvement of OSU’s analytical ecosystem by reducing duplication, improving data quality and usability, and advancing sustainable self-service analytics through continuous learning, community enablement, and exploratory work with emerging analytics capabilities including responsible use of AI/ML where appropriate. Data Community, Learning, and Service: Strengthen OSU’s data culture by building collaborative relationships, supporting knowledge sharing and data literacy, engaging in professional development, and contributing to mentoring, service, and community initiatives that enhance team effectiveness and the university’s reporting ecosystem. Qualifications: REQUIRED QUALIFICATIONS: This classification requires a basic foundation of knowledge and skills in systems analysis and related programming support functions generally obtained by a bachelor's degree in computer science, or an equivalent amount of training and applied experience. Demonstrated proficiency using SQL in a professional setting for modeling or reporting work Experience developing, validating, and maintaining data models, transformations, or analytical data products in a production environment Experience developing dashboards and reports in enterprise grade tools (Power BI, Tableau etc). Demonstrated ability to work effectively in a complex, fast‑paced team-based environment with changing priorities. Strong communication skills, with demonstrated ability to convey technical concepts clearly to both technical and non‑technical audiences. Demonstrated ability to work independently and collaboratively as part of a diverse team. Demonstrated commitment to inclusive excellence. This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a criminal history check and be determined to be position qualified as per University Standard: 05-010 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in youth programs may have additional criminal history checks every 24 months. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS: Master’s degree in Computer Science, Data Engineering, Analytics, Information Systems, or a related field. 2+ years experience developing, validating, and maintaining data models, transformations, or analytical data products in a production environment. Experience using at least one additional programming or scripting language (such as Python) to support data preparation, validation, or prototyping. Experience working in a production environment for a large, complex organization. Ability to translate business needs into technical requirements. Professional experience working with modern, cloud‑based platforms. Professional experience developing data transformations using dbt or similar SQL or other programming-based transformation frameworks. Demonstrated applied professional experience performing data modeling and data analytics from an operational system or ERP. Demonstrated experience with advanced statistical and effective storytelling techniques with data. Demonstrated experience independently completing data, analytics, and/or Business Intelligence related projects. Working Conditions/Schedule: Schedule This position will work a typical Monday – Friday schedule and will be based on the Corvallis campus.     This position may at times require work outside of normal business hours and/or weekends.   Modality Hybrid: This position is designated as hybrid, working both on-site and remotely. The exact amount of on-site and remote work and schedule will be discussed with the supervisor. Hybrid work is dependent on business needs. Salary Range: $66,132 - $122,736 Anticipated Start Date: 2026-10-01 Anticipated End Date: We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status. OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire. OSU is a fair chance employer committed to inclusive hiring. We encourage applications from candidates who bring a wide range of lived experience including involvement with the justice system. If this position has “critical or security-sensitive” responsibilities and/or driving duties and you are selected as a finalist, your initial job offer will be contingent upon the results of a job-related pre-employment check (such as a background check, motor vehicle history check, sexual misconduct reference check, etc.). Background check results do not automatically disqualify a candidate. Take a look at our Background Checks website including the for candidates section for more details. If you have questions or concerns about the pre-employment check, please contact OSU’s Employee and Labor Relations team at employee.relations@oregonstate.edu ."
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/Our-Beaver-Nation-Student-Membership-Digital-Growth-Coordinator_JR0000821": {
      "title": "Our Beaver Nation Student Membership Digital Growth Coordinator",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-01\nPosted: Posted 4 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nDepartment: Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents: ●        A Resume ●        A Cover Letter For additional information please contact: Terri Grigsby at Terri.grigsby@osufoundation.org We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status. OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire.   Note: All job offers are contingent upon Human Resources final approval Job Description: This recruitment will be used to fill one part-time (a maximum of 24 hours per week) Our Beaver Nation Membership Digital Growth Coordinator position for the OSU Foundation at Oregon State University (OSU). Our Beaver Nation, the fundraising arm of Oregon State Athletics, brings people together to create meaningful experiences, tell impactful stories, and build community. Through philanthropy, we connect fans, donors, alumni, and friends to the mission of Oregon State Athletics, while sharing the positive impact college athletics has on our student-athletes and the world around us. The Our Beaver Nation Membership Digital Growth Coordinator combines content creation, storytelling, and digital marketing to grow membership and engagement across Beaver Nation. Through game-day coverage, social media strategy, creative content development, and audience analytics, this role helps convert fans into active supporters and advocates of Oregon State Athletics. The position works collaboratively with student-athletes, coaches, and creative teams to deliver authentic content, identify growth opportunities, and build meaningful connections with current and prospective members. Applicants should be self-starters, creative, and have the ability to balance independent and collaborative work. Position Duties Use content, storytelling, and digital engagement strategies to convert fans into active Our Beaver Nation members and supporters. Develop and execute digital engagement strategies that increase awareness, engagement, and membership growth for Our Beaver Nation. Create, capture, edit, and publish compelling content across social media and digital platforms. Collaborate with Athletics staff, student-athletes, coaches, and creative content teams to develop authentic and engaging content. Assist with game-day and event coverage, capturing photos, videos, interviews, and member experiences for future marketing and promotional use. Monitor social media performance, audience behavior, and membership conversion metrics; use data and analytics to recommend and implement improvements. Identify highly engaged supporters and assist in moving audiences into additional communication, membership, and engagement opportunities. Support lead generation efforts by identifying and cultivating prospective members through digital engagement. Recommend strategies to convert social media followers into active members and advocates of Our Beaver Nation. Stay current on emerging social media trends, digital marketing best practices, and content strategies to bring fresh ideas and innovation to the organization. Serve as a positive ambassador for Our Beaver Nation while maintaining professionalism in interactions with student-athletes, fans, donors, and campus partners. Transferable Skill Development OSU is committed to ensuring students are prepared for success after graduation through intentional skill development as student employees. The competencies students develop while working at OSU are defined by the National Association of Colleges and Employers (NACE) as the essential skills employers seek in their hires. In this position, students learn and build vital career-readiness skills in the primary areas below:   Communication, Critical Thinking, Teamwork, Technology Qualifications: Full Employment Eligibility Requirements can be found here:   https://hr.oregonstate.edu/student-employment-manual/500-employment-eligibility-requirements Must be academically enrolled in a high school, community college, or university and pursuing a program or course of study Must meet Academic Standing Requirements; students on academic suspension are not eligible for employment Must meet the applicable minimal enrollment standard High School student: Regularly enrolled in a high school or participating in a home-schooling program Undergraduate and post-baccalaureate student: 6 credit hours per term Undergraduate international student: 12 credit hours per term* Graduate student officially admitted to Graduate School: 5 credit hours per term Graduate international student officially admitted to Graduate School: 9 credit hours per term* *International students may be allowed to carry fewer hours than specified above and still be considered “full-time” by the United States Citizenship and Immigration Services ( USCIS ). A reduced course load is approved by the   Office of International Services   ( OIS ), and must be provided to the Student Employment Center. Required A preference for a student majoring in marketing or sports business. Communication skills in areas including but not limited to interpersonal, public speaking, digital, verbal, and written communication. Handle interpersonal relationships maturely, thoughtfully, and empathetically, including the ability to work with individuals from diverse backgrounds. Organizational skills, including managing time and working accurately with interruptions to meet a deadline Work well under pressure, exercising flexibility, initiative, good judgment, and discretion. Flexibility in work schedule is required, as occasional evening and weekend work is required. Experience with Adobe Suite, photography, and videography for social media purposes. Use of Instagram, Facebook, X, LinkedIn for business purposes. General understanding of office practices.   Working Conditions/Schedule: - This is a part-time position working up to 15-20 hours per week. - The selected candidate will start in August and work through the fall term. - Extension for additional terms will be evaluated based on budget and performance. Salary Range: Anticipated Start Date: 2026-09-01 Anticipated End Date:"
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/Potato-Greenhouse-Student-Assistant_JR0000877-1": {
      "title": "Potato Greenhouse Student Assistant",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-01\nPosted: Posted 4 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nDepartment: Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents: A Resume A Cover Letter A document with an outline of your fall term schedule and availability For additional information please contact: Terry Burr at Terry.burr@oregonstate.edu We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status. This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a Criminal History Check and be determined to be position qualified as per OSU Standard 576-055-0000 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in Youth Programs may have additional Criminal History Checks every 2 years. OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire.   Note: All job offers are contingent upon Human Resources final approval Job Description: This recruitment will be used to fill 9 part-time (a maximum of 24 hours per week) Student Technical Assistant positions for the Crop & Soil Science Extension Department at Oregon State University (OSU). Season project involving preparations and planting seed potato samples in soil-floored greenhouses. Conditions are sometimes dusty and include working with hand tools to cut seed pieces, planting the pieces, and helping to keep detailed records. Position is located at Oregon State University main campus, in Corvallis, and is approximately from October 1, 2026 to February 17, 2027. Position Duties Applicants will need to be detail oriented to keep and maintain careful records. This position will be conducted in a greenhouse and involves the use of hand tools. Duties include, but are not limited to: Preparation of greenhouses for planting Raking and watering Arranging seed potato samples for planting Treating to break dormancy Lifting and stacking 50lb bags of potatoes Cutting seed Planting by hand General clean-up Transferable Skill Development OSU is committed to ensuring students are prepared for success after graduation through intentional skill development as student employees. The competencies students develop while working at OSU are defined by the National Association of Colleges and Employers (NACE) as the essential skills employers seek in their hires. In this position, students learn and build vital career-readiness skills in the primary areas below:   Communication, Critical Thinking, Teamwork, , Leadership, Professionalism Qualifications: Full Employment Eligibility Requirements can be found here:   https://hr.oregonstate.edu/student-employment-manual/500-employment-eligibility-requirements Must be academically enrolled in a high school, community college, or university and pursuing a program or course of study Must meet Academic Standing Requirements; students on academic suspension are not eligible for employment Must meet the applicable minimal enrollment standard High School student: Regularly enrolled in a high school or participating in a home-schooling program Undergraduate and post-baccalaureate student: 6 credit hours per term Undergraduate international student: 12 credit hours per term* Graduate student officially admitted to Graduate School: 5 credit hours per term Graduate international student officially admitted to Graduate School: 9 credit hours per term* *International students may be allowed to carry fewer hours than specified above and still be considered “full-time” by the United States Citizenship and Immigration Services ( USCIS ). A reduced course load is approved by the   Office of International Services   ( OIS ), and must be provided to the Student Employment Center. Required Capable of lifting 50lb bags of potatoes Ability to work in a dusty environment Detail-oriented and able to carefully record and maintain records Must be able to work both independently and as part of a team This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a Criminal History Check and be determined to be position qualified as per OSU Standard 576-055-0000 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in Youth Programs may have additional Criminal History Checks every 2 years. Preferred Able to work in 2+ hour blocks of time Working Conditions/Schedule: - Conditions are sometimes dusty and include working with hand tools - Flexible work schedule: weekdays around classes and between the hours of 8:00am-5:00pm; 10 or more hours per week depending on workload Salary Range: Anticipated Start Date: 2026-09-30 Anticipated End Date:"
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/MB-303-Student-Teaching-Assistant-Fall-2026_JR0000958": {
      "title": "MB 303 Student Teaching Assistant Fall 2026",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-31\nPosted: Posted 5 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nDepartment: Microbiology Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents: A Cover Letter For additional information please contact: Linda Bruslind at Linda.Bruslind@oregonstate.edu We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status.   This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a Criminal History Check and be determined to be position qualified as per OSU Standard 576-055-0000 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in Youth Programs may have additional Criminal History Checks every 2 years.   OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire.   Note: All job offers are contingent upon Human Resources final approval Job Description: This recruitment will be used to fill thirteen (13) part-time (a maximum of 24 hours per week) Teaching Student Assistant positions for the Department of Microbiology at Oregon State University (OSU). This position is for undergraduate teaching assistants for MB 303: General Microbiology Lab. https://microbiology.oregonstate.edu/ Position Duties: TAs will be doing set-up and clean-up of lab, supervising students during lab, checking off in-class tasks, and helping with grading of class assignments, after FERPA training. Transferable Skill Development OSU is committed to ensuring students are prepared for success after graduation through intentional skill development as student employees. The competencies students develop while working at OSU are defined by the National Association of Colleges and Employers (NACE) as the essential skills employers seek in their hires. In this position, students learn and build vital career-readiness skills in the primary areas below:   Communication, Teamwork, Leadership, Professionalism Qualifications: Full Employment Eligibility Requirements can be found here:   https://hr.oregonstate.edu/student-employment-manual/500-employment-eligibility-requirements Must be academically enrolled in a high school, community college, or university and pursuing a program or course of study Must meet Academic Standing Requirements; students on academic suspension are not eligible for employment Must meet the applicable minimal enrollment standard High School student: Regularly enrolled in a high school or participating in a home-schooling program Undergraduate and post-baccalaureate student: 6 credit hours per term Undergraduate international student: 12 credit hours per term* Graduate student officially admitted to Graduate School: 5 credit hours per term Graduate international student officially admitted to Graduate School: 9 credit hours per term* *International students may be allowed to carry fewer hours than specified above and still be considered “full-time” by the United States Citizenship and Immigration Services ( USCIS ). A reduced course load is approved by the   Office of International Services   ( OIS ), and must be provided to the Student Employment Center. Required Must have completed MB 303 with a C- or higher. This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a Criminal History Check and be determined to be position qualified as per OSU Standard 576-055-0000 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in Youth Programs may have additional Criminal History Checks every 2 years. Preferred Prior completion of MB 303 with a B- or higher. MB or BHS major. Current enrollment in MB 406. Working Conditions/Schedule: - Teaching laboratory environment Salary Range: Anticipated Start Date: 2026-09-16 Anticipated End Date:"
