    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Aurora/Vegetable-and-Specialty-Seeds-Crop-Student-Assistant_JR0000951-1": {
      "title": "Vegetable and Specialty Seeds Crop Student Assistant",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-31\nPosted: Posted 5 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nDepartment: North Willamette Research and Extension Center Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents: A Resume/Vita (required) A cover letter indicating how your qualifications and experience have prepared you for this position (optional) For additional information please contact Kristine Buckland at kristine.buckland@oregonstate.edu We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status.   OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire.   Note: All job offers are contingent upon Human Resources final approval   Job Description: This recruitment will be used to fill 1 part-time (a maximum of 24 hours per week) Student Assistant position for the North Willamette Research & Extension Center at Oregon State University (OSU). Typical activities may include collecting data, checking irrigation, monitoring plant health, plant maintenance and harvesting, controlling weeds and pests, data entry, posting current events to websites and social media.  Position Duties Plant Research 75% Data Collection. 20% : Assists with sampling, harvest, and data collection for trials according to study protocols. Miscellaneous. 5% : Keeps records of activities; assists with preparing orders for procurement; assists the program as needed in conducting tours, training, and field days for the public. Transferable Skill Development OSU is committed to ensuring students are prepared for success after graduation through intentional skill development as student employees. The competencies students develop while working at OSU are defined by the National Association of Colleges and Employers (NACE) as the essential skills employers seek in their hires. In this position, students learn and build vital career-readiness skills in the primary areas below:   Communication, Teamwork, Technology, Professionalism Qualifications: Full Employment Eligibility Requirements can be found here:   https://hr.oregonstate.edu/student-employment-manual/500-employment-eligibility-requirements Must be academically enrolled in a high school, community college, or university and pursuing a program or course of study Must meet Academic Standing Requirements; students on academic suspension are not eligible for employment Must meet the applicable minimal enrollment standard High School student: Regularly enrolled in a high school or participating in a home-schooling program Undergraduate and post-baccalaureate student: 6 credit hours per term Undergraduate international student: 12 credit hours per term* Graduate student officially admitted to Graduate School: 5 credit hours per term Graduate international student officially admitted to Graduate School: 9 credit hours per term* *International students may be allowed to carry fewer hours than specified above and still be considered “full-time” by the United States Citizenship and Immigration Services ( USCIS ). A reduced course load is approved by the   Office of International Services   ( OIS ), and must be provided to the Student Employment Center. Required Students who are under 18 years old are required to have passed a tractor safety certification class prior to operating a tractor. Attention to detail Perform assigned tasks without close supervision Capable of working outside and standing/walking for extended periods of time. Ability to lift 25 lbs Preferred Experience with tractors (for students over 18) Working Conditions/Schedule: - Work is mainly performed outdoors at the research farm, in scientific laboratory, research greenhouses, and office settings. - The ability work in and dress appropriately for all weather conditions is required. - Duties may include lifting heavy objects (up to 50 lb); standing, kneeling or crawling for long periods of time; walking over plowed fields or uneven surfaces; and other strenuous activities such as hoeing weeds, shoveling, operating push mowers, and string trimmers. Salary Range: Anticipated Start Date: 2026-10-01 Anticipated End Date:"
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/Facility-Maintenance-Specialist_JR0000974": {
      "title": "Facility Maintenance Specialist",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-31\nPosted: Posted 5 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nDepartment: Recreational Sports Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents: A Resume A Cover Letter For additional information please contact: Joe Dupray at Joe.Dupray@oregonstate.edu We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status.   OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire. Note:  All job offers are contingent upon Human Resources final approval Job Description: This recruitment will be used to fill four part-time (a maximum of 24 hours per week) facility maintenance specialist position for Recreational Sports at Oregon State University (OSU). Our Values in Action As a student employee, you play a vital role in bringing our mission and values to life. We expect every team member to embody the following values in their work: We Care About People: Treat all patrons and colleagues with kindness, courtesy and inclusion. We Are Solution-Oriented: Identify and implement solutions that enhance the member and employee experience. Address conflict in a positive, calm and solution-focused manner. We Are Stewards: Use resources responsibly and ethically while working efficiently. We Are Innovative: Pioneer programs, services and facilities that serve as catalysts for student success. Create space to encourage, share and evolve new ideas. We Are Educators: Foster environments where learning and personal growth thrive for both participants and staff. Grant others the same good intentions you grant yourself. We Extend Respect & Build Trust : Communicate with clarity, transparency and integrity, fostering trust across our community. Every action rooted in our mission and values strengthens our team and makes Recreational Sports a place where everyone belongs, grows and thrives. We create engaging environments for student growth and success. We inspire healthy living by providing quality recreational and educational opportunities for the Oregon State University community. Position Duties Perform preventative maintenance on building facilities and equipment, demonstrating professionalism, reliability, and attention to detail to ensure safe and functional recreational spaces. Assist with diagnosing and repairing building systems and equipment, applying critical thinking and technical problem-solving skills. upport maintenance and repair of mechanical and electrical systems (including pumps, motors, controls, and lighting), utilizing technology and operational knowledge under limited supervision. Complete general facility maintenance tasks, including lighting replacement, to support daily operations and user safety. Execute electrical, plumbing, and carpentry tasks independently or with minimal supervision, reflecting strong professionalism, adaptability, and accountability Transferable Skill Development OSU   is committed to ensuring students are prepared for success after graduation   through intentional skill development as student employees. The competencies students develop while working at   OSU   are defined by the   National Association of Colleges and Employers ( NACE )   as the essential skills employers seek in their hires. In this position, students learn and build vital career-readiness skills in the primary areas below: Critical Thinking, Leadership, Professionalism Qualifications: Full Employment Eligibility Requirements can be found here:   https://hr.oregonstate.edu/student-employment-manual/500-employment-eligibility-requirements   Must be academically enrolled in a high school, community college, or university and pursuing a program or course of study Must meet Academic Standing Requirements; students on academic suspension are not eligible for employment Must meet the applicable minimal enrollment standard High School student: Regularly enrolled in a high school or participating in a home-schooling program Undergraduate and post-baccalaureate student: 6 credit hours per term Undergraduate international student: 12 credit hours per term* Graduate student officially admitted to Graduate School: 5 credit hours per term Graduate international student officially admitted to Graduate School: 9 credit hours per term* *International students may be allowed to carry fewer hours than specified above and still be considered “full-time” by the United States Citizenship and Immigration Services ( USCIS ). A reduced course load is approved by the   Office of International Services   ( OIS ), and must be provided to the Student Employment Center. Required First Aid, CPR/AED for the Healthcare Provider and DRS Bloodborne Pathogens certifications is required before employment begins and will be provided by the department upon hiring Working Conditions/Schedule: Period of Employment: One academic year (continuing employment is contingent upon satisfactory completion of a 30 day probationary period and satisfactory job performance as evaluated by immediate supervisor). 8-24 hours per week. Early morning, late night and weekend hours may be required. Compensation: Student employees are paid an hourly wage consistent with the OSU Student Employment Pay Rate Guidelines. The Department of Recreational Sports identifies pay ranges base upon job duties, level of responsibility and complexity of the work to be performed. Salary Range: Anticipated Start Date: 2026-09-21 Anticipated End Date:"
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/Instructor-Pool---2026-2027--Theatre-Arts_JR0000802": {
      "title": "Instructor Pool - 2026/2027: Theatre Arts",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-31\nPosted: Posted 5 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nDepartment: College of Liberal Arts Special Instructions to Applicants: Applications will be reviewed and considered as opportunities arise throughout the 2026/2027 academic year. When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents:   1) A resume/CV; and   2) A cover letter indicating how your qualifications and experience have prepared you for this position. Starting salary will be commensurate with skills, education, and experience.   For additional information please contact: Coleen Keedah Coleen.keedah@oregonstate.edu 541-737-5003 Job Summary: The School of Visual, Performing and Design Arts, Department of Theatre, invites applications for one or more fixed-term, non-tenure-track full/part-time Instructor positions to teach on a term-by-term basis for the 2026/2027 academic year. Some of these appointments may be reviewed for renewal or transition to an instructional position on an annual basis at the discretion of the Director. Applications submitted to this recruitment pool are reviewed on an as needed basis. Qualified candidates will be contacted when positions become available Instructors may be needed to teach undergraduate courses on campus or through Ecampus in areas of Theatre Arts such as acting, introduction to Theatre Arts, technical theatre, costuming, stage and lighting design, etc. The primary mission of Theatre Arts at OSU is undergraduate education. Students may seek BA and BS degrees in Theatre Arts as well as a Theatre Arts minor. While Theatre Arts, on occasion, participates in the MAIS graduate program, most students in this program are undergraduates. Key Responsibilities: Administer and teach undergraduate courses on campus or through eCampus in areas of Theatre Arts Teach courses such as:  acting, introduction to Theatre Arts, technical theatre, costuming, stage and lighting design, etc. Qualifications: Required Qualifications: Master’s degree in Theatre Arts or closely related field in-hand by start date. Evident commitment to educational equity. Record of and/or demonstrated interest in engaging, educating, and mentoring a diverse group of learners and enhancing inclusive exellence through teaching, outreach, or programming. Ability to work collegially with other faculty, staff, and students. Preferred Qualifications: Teaching experience at the college or university level. Record of artistic and/or scholarly achievement in the field. Terminal degree in Theatre or closely related field. Working Conditions/Schedule: Salary Range: Salary commensurate with skill, education and experience. Anticipated Start Date: 2026-09-16 Anticipated End Date: 2027-06-15 We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status. OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire. OSU is a fair chance employer committed to inclusive hiring. We encourage applications from candidates who bring a wide range of lived experience including involvement with the justice system. If this position has “critical or security-sensitive” responsibilities and/or driving duties and you are selected as a finalist, your initial job offer will be contingent upon the results of a job-related pre-employment check (such as a background check, motor vehicle history check, sexual misconduct reference check, etc.). Background check results do not automatically disqualify a candidate. Take a look at our Background Checks website including the for candidates section for more details. If you have questions or concerns about the pre-employment check, please contact OSU’s Employee and Labor Relations team at employee.relations@oregonstate.edu ."
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/Student-Lib-Cataloging_JR0000844": {
      "title": "Student Lib Cataloging",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-31\nPosted: Posted 5 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nDepartment: Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents: A Resume A Cover Letter Class schedule (current term) A Supplemental document answering the following questions: This position requires a high level of accuracy and attention to detail. Talk about a task you have performed where accuracy was critical. Have you ever found a way to improve a process/project you were working on? How did you communicate that to your supervisor/partner? What was the result? For additional information please contact: Vance Woods at vance.woods@oregonstate.edu We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status. This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a Criminal History Check and be determined to be position qualified as per OSU Standard 576-055-0000 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in Youth Programs may have additional Criminal History Checks every 2 years. OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire.   Note: All job offers are contingent upon Human Resources final approval Job Description: This recruitment will be used to fill one (1) part-time (a maximum of 20 hours per week) Cataloging Assistant position for the Cataloging Unit of the Research & Discovery Division within the Valley Library at Oregon State University (OSU).  Position Duties  In this position you will:   Assist cataloging and other staff to correctly process and distribute print materials to appropriate destinations. (Teamwork; Critical Thinking; Communication)  Partner with cataloging staff to check in and process government documents. (Teamwork; Communication)  Acquire and apply knowledge of Library of Congress classification and library layout to retrieve materials from the shelves. (Critical Thinking; Communication)  As skills develop over time, you will:  Collaborate with cataloging staff to complete assorted inventory projects involving spreadsheets, databases, and physical materials. (Teamwork; Communication; Critical Thinking; Technology)  Acquire and apply knowledge of online library catalogs and databases to locate and record data from bibliographic records in OCLC and OSULP’s catalog. (Technology)  Identify and evaluate online library catalog and database issues in order to repair errors, retrieve data, and improve patron access to library resources (Critical Thinking; Technology)  Support OSULP employees on process improvements or additional projects. (Teamwork; Communication; Critical Thinking)  Transferable Skill Development OSU is committed to ensuring students are prepared for success after graduation through intentional skill development as student employees. The competencies students develop while working at OSU are defined by the National Association of Colleges and Employers (NACE) as the essential skills employers seek in their hires. In this position, students learn and build vital career-readiness skills in the primary areas below:   Communication, Critical Thinking, Teamwork, Technology Qualifications: Full Employment Eligibility Requirements can be found here:   https://hr.oregonstate.edu/student-employment-manual/500-employment-eligibility-requirements Must be academically enrolled in a high school, community college, or university and pursuing a program or course of study Must meet Academic Standing Requirements; students on academic suspension are not eligible for employment Must meet the applicable minimal enrollment standard High School student: Regularly enrolled in a high school or participating in a home-schooling program Undergraduate and post-baccalaureate student: 6 credit hours per term Undergraduate international student: 12 credit hours per term* Graduate student officially admitted to Graduate School: 5 credit hours per term Graduate international student officially admitted to Graduate School: 9 credit hours per term* *International students may be allowed to carry fewer hours than specified above and still be considered “full-time” by the United States Citizenship and Immigration Services ( USCIS ). A reduced course load is approved by the   Office of International Services   ( OIS ), and must be provided to the Student Employment Center. Required Attention to detail.  Ability to follow precise instructions and ask clarifying questions as needed.  Team oriented.  Ability to work independently.  Basic knowledge of Microsoft Office and/or comparable Google tools (Excel/Sheets, Word/Docs).  Ability to stay focused on tasks in an office environment.  This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a Criminal History Check and be determined to be position qualified as per OSU Standard 576-055-0000 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in Youth Programs may have additional Criminal History Checks every 2 years. Preferred A vailability to work the full academic year as well as Summer term.  Working Conditions/Schedule: • Work schedule is during regular business hours (M-F, 8-5). • Must be able to lift and carry objects up to 50 lbs. • Must be able to push a cart weighing up to 100 lbs., stand for up to 3 hours at a time, sit for up to 3 hours at a time, reach overhead and bend down for materials on shelves. Salary Range: Anticipated Start Date: 2026-09-28 Anticipated End Date:"
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/Workday-Human-Resources-Lead_JR0000963": {
      "title": "Workday Human Resources Lead",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-28\nPosted: Posted 8 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nDepartment: University Human Resources Special Instructions to Applicants: To ensure full consideration, applications must be received by September 7, 2026. Applications will continue to be accepted after the full consideration date, until a sufficient applicant pool has been achieved or the position is filled. The closing date is subject to change without notice to applicants. When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents:   1) A resume/CV; and   2) A cover letter indicating how your qualifications and experience have prepared you for this position.   Starting salary will be commensurate with skills, education, and experience.   For additional information please contact: Meg Kilgore at meg.kilgore@oregonstate.edu Job Summary: University Human Resources is seeking a Workday Human Resources Lead. This is a full-time (1.00   FTE ), 12-month, professional faculty position. The Workday Human Resource Lead is responsible for guiding and sustaining the effective and efficient operation of Workday Human Resources modules at Oregon State University. This position plays a critical role in supporting Workday at OSU through strategic problem-solving, coordinating daily system activities, leading cross-functional teams, and serving as a liaison between functional and technical colleagues and partners. This position is responsible for leading a team of HR Analysts in Human Resources system configuration, testing, deploying business process changes, maintaining data integrity, aligning system capabilities with business needs, conducting training, and producing and maintaining documentation. This role partners closely with Research, Finance, IT, faculty and administrative units, and external vendors to optimize Workday functionality, strengthen data integrity, advance operational excellence, and deliver seamless user experience. The Lead plays a pivotal role in governance, system performance, continuous improvement, and roadmap planning, balancing day-to-day operations with long-range strategic initiatives. Key Responsibilities: Serve as a strong consultative partner to business and functional leaders to continuously improve Workday service delivery, user experience, and operational efficiency. Define and communicate a clear vision for the Workday Human Resources support team members that aligns with institutional goals and best practices. Lead governance processes for Human Resources functions, including decision-making frameworks, change control, prioritization, and resource planning. Oversee data integrity efforts, including routine audits of data, system integrations, and reports. Ensure Human Resources integrations are operational and establish a daily monitoring plan. Drive roadmap development for ongoing Workday improvements, new features, and cross-functional projects. Lead and mentor support team members, foster collaboration, accountability, and continuous learning. Qualifications: Required Qualifications: Bachelor’s degree in Business, Finance, Information Systems, or a related field; or an equivalent combination of education and progressively responsible experience that demonstrate the required knowledge, skills and abilities. 5 years of experience working with Workday Human Resources or similar ERP systems. Experience working in a highly regulated industry with demonstrated ability to implement internal controls to fulfill regulatory requirements. Knowledge of reporting, data governance, integrations, and security concepts related to ERP environments. Working knowledge of accounting concepts. Experience supervising or managing staff or providing lead work. Experience coordinating cross-functional teams or projects in HR, Finance, or IT environments.​ Experience leading projects, coordinating stakeholders, managing timelines, and documenting decisions. Exceptional written and verbal communication skills with proven ability to translate technical concepts into user-friendly language for diverse audiences. Customer-focused mindset with a commitment to timely, clear, and solution-oriented interactions. This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a criminal history check and be determined to be position qualified as per University Standard: 05-010 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in youth programs may have additional criminal history checks every 24 months. Preferred Qualifications: Process improvement, project management, or change management experience desirable (Lean, Six Sigma, PMP, or PROSCI). Ability to define KPIs, analyze performance data, and report operational metrics to leadership. Strong attention to detail in managing data integrity, compliance, and system documentation. Ability to manage ambiguity, prioritize competing tasks, and adapt to organizational change. MBA degree. Working Conditions/Schedule: This role requires the regular use of a computer and standard office equipment and may involve extended periods of sitting or standing. Occasional work outside of standard business hours may be required to meet deadlines or support projects. This position may also involve occasional travel for meetings, conferences, or other professional engagements. Salary Range: $110,000 - $150,000 Anticipated Start Date: 2026-09-21 Anticipated End Date: We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status. OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire. OSU is a fair chance employer committed to inclusive hiring. We encourage applications from candidates who bring a wide range of lived experience including involvement with the justice system. If this position has “critical or security-sensitive” responsibilities and/or driving duties and you are selected as a finalist, your initial job offer will be contingent upon the results of a job-related pre-employment check (such as a background check, motor vehicle history check, sexual misconduct reference check, etc.). Background check results do not automatically disqualify a candidate. Take a look at our Background Checks website including the for candidates section for more details. If you have questions or concerns about the pre-employment check, please contact OSU’s Employee and Labor Relations team at employee.relations@oregonstate.edu ."
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/BRS-Accounting-Student-Assistant_JR0000911": {
      "title": "BRS Accounting Student Assistant",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-28\nPosted: Posted 8 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nDepartment: Billing & Receivables Services Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents: Resume Cover Letter Please provide your major and projected graduation date. Are you available to work through the first week of July, 2027? For additional information please contact: Toni Cleland at Toni.cleland@oregonstate.edu We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status. This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a Criminal History Check and be determined to be position qualified as per OSU Standard 576-055-0000 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in Youth Programs may have additional Criminal History Checks every 2 years. OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire.   Note: All job offers are contingent upon Human Resources final approval Job Description: This recruitment will be used to fill 2 part-time (a maximum of 24 hours per week) Accounting Assistant positions for the Billing & Receivables Services (BRS) unit at Oregon State University (OSU). The successful candidate will contribute to the operational success of the BRS unit by processing Customer Invoices, Internal Service Deliveries, Customer Creations, creating cash deposits and applying payments, responding to emails, picking up and dropping off mail, taking deposits to the Cashier’s Office, and other general accounting or office duties. This position would be ideal for an accounting student looking to gain practical experience to supplement their education.   Position Duties Enter Internal Service Deliveries in Workday. Process check payments by creating and applying customer payments and creating customer deposits in Workday. Enter Customer Invoices in Workday. Create new customers in Workday. Pick up and drop off BRS mail from various mailbox locations. Deliver daily deposits to the Cashier’s Office. Other tasks as assigned by supervisor. Transferable Skill Development OSU is committed to ensuring students are prepared for success after graduation through intentional skill development as student employees. The competencies students develop while working at OSU are defined by the National Association of Colleges and Employers (NACE) as the essential skills employers seek in their hires. In this position, students learn and build vital career-readiness skills in the primary areas below:   Communication, Teamwork, Technology, Career & Self Development Qualifications: Full Employment Eligibility Requirements can be found here:   https://hr.oregonstate.edu/student-employment-manual/500-employment-eligibility-requirements Must be academically enrolled in a high school, community college, or university and pursuing a program or course of study Must meet Academic Standing Requirements; students on academic suspension are not eligible for employment Must meet the applicable minimal enrollment standard High School student: Regularly enrolled in a high school or participating in a home-schooling program Undergraduate and post-baccalaureate student: 6 credit hours per term Undergraduate international student: 12 credit hours per term* Graduate student officially admitted to Graduate School: 5 credit hours per term Graduate international student officially admitted to Graduate School: 9 credit hours per term* *International students may be allowed to carry fewer hours than specified above and still be considered “full-time” by the United States Citizenship and Immigration Services ( USCIS ). A reduced course load is approved by the   Office of International Services   ( OIS ), and must be provided to the Student Employment Center. Required Available to work through the first week of July. Maintain the ability to be reliable and responsible, including adhering to assigned schedule and effectively communicating your education and/or scheduling needs in a timely manner to your direct supervisor. Ability to understand instructions and ask questions when clarification is needed. Ability to complete assigned tasks in a timely and accurate manner. Demonstrated attention to detail and strong organizational skills. Willingness and ability to provide excellent customer service to all internal and external customers. This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a Criminal History Check and be determined to be position qualified as per OSU Standard 576-055-0000 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in Youth Programs may have additional Criminal History Checks every 2 years. Preferred Current Accounting student or accounting work experience. Working Conditions/Schedule: - Office setting. - Flexible work schedule M-F 8am-5pm. Salary Range: Anticipated Start Date: 2026-09-15 Anticipated End Date:"
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/FAST-Engine-Chief-Executive-Officer_JR0000688": {
      "title": "FAST Engine Chief Executive Officer",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-28\nPosted: Posted 8 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nDepartment: Division of Research and Innovation Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents:    1) A resume/CV; and     2) A cover letter indicating how your qualifications and experience have prepared you for this position.  Starting salary will be commensurate with skills, education, and experience.  For additional information please contact:   Konnie Handschuch/Sara Begley Konnie.handschuch@oregonstate.edu/sara.begley@oregonstate.edu Job Summary: The Division of Research and Innovation is seeking a Frontiers of Advanced Semiconductor Technology (FAST) Engine Chief Executive Officer. This is a full-time (1.00   FTE), 12-month, professional faculty position.  The Frontiers of Advanced Semiconductor Technology (FAST) NSF Regional Innovation Engine is seeking an entrepreneurial and visionary Chief Executive Officer to lead one of the nation’s premier place-based innovation initiatives. Backed by the National Science Foundation and a coalition of more than 95 partners, FAST is advancing U.S. leadership in AI-enabled semiconductor manufacturing while driving innovation, workforce development, and economic growth across Oregon. Reporting to the Associate Vice President for Economic Development and Industry Relations at Oregon State University and working closely with the FAST Strategic Governing Council, the CEO will lead a complex, multi-institutional organization spanning research universities, community colleges, industry, government, workforce organizations, investors, and community partners. The CEO will be responsible for achieving ambitious technical, workforce, commercialization, and regional economic development outcomes; successfully advancing the Engine through NSF stage-gate reviews; and building FAST into a nationally recognized, financially sustainable innovation enterprise. About FAST Frontiers of Advanced Semiconductor Technology (FAST) https://fast-engine.org/ is a National Science Foundation (NSF) designated Regional Innovation Engine https://www.nsf.gov/funding/initiatives/regional-innovation-engines/about-nsf-engines advancing U.S. leadership in semiconductor manufacturing through the development and deployment of AI-enabled Advanced Process Control (AI-APC) systems. FAST is a consortium of more than 95 industry, academic, government, workforce, and community partners aligned to catalyze innovation, workforce development, and inclusive economic growth across Oregon’s Cascade-Willamette Innovation Corridor (CWIC). FAST is one of the National Science Foundation’s Regional Innovation Engines, designed to strengthen U.S. economic competitiveness through long-term regional innovation ecosystems that integrate research, commercialization, workforce development, entrepreneurship, and industry engagement. FAST integrates use-inspired research, translation of innovation to practice, entrepreneurship, workforce development, and regional engagement to create a globally competitive semiconductor ecosystem with national economic and security impact. The Engine is funded by NSF for up to 10 years, with substantial state, industry, and philanthropic co-investment. Key Responsibilities: Serve as the chief executive and public face of FAST, articulating and advancing a compelling long-term vision for the Engine’s impact and sustainability. Direct a complex statewide partnership of universities, community colleges, industry, government, workforce organizations, and investors. Deliver measurable outcomes in semiconductor innovation, commercialization, workforce development, and regional economic competitiveness. Lead FAST through successful NSF stage-gate reviews following the initial two-year award period by ensuring the Engine achieves its milestone-based performance objectives, demonstrates measurable commercialization and regional economic impact, and secures subsequent phases of NSF funding. Build executive-level relationships with federal agencies, industry leaders, investors, elected officials, and academic partners. Expand FAST’s long-term financial sustainability through strategic partnerships and diversified public and private investment. Lead and develop FAST’s executive leadership team, providing strategic direction, performance management, and organizational leadership across a complex, multi-institutional organization. Partner closely with FAST Directors and Co-Directors to align research, workforce, commercialization, and partnership activities in support of the Engine’s strategic goals and NSF performance milestones. Serve as the public face and national spokesperson for FAST and Oregon’s semiconductor innovation ecosystem. Qualifications: Required Qualifications: Bachelor’s degree in engineering, physical sciences, business, or a related field. Experience in semiconductors, advanced manufacturing, AI, or closely related technology sectors. Minimum of 10 years of senior leadership experience with demonstrated responsibility for complex, multi-stakeholder organizations or initiatives. Proven ability to lead at the intersection of technology, industry, and economic development. Demonstrated success securing and managing large-scale public and private funding. Strong record of building partnerships across industry, academia, government, and communities. Exceptional communication skills and executive presence, with a proven ability to align diverse stakeholders around shared goals. Commitment to inclusive excellence and inclusive regional prosperity. This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a criminal history check and be determined to be position qualified as per University Standard: 05-010 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in youth programs may have additional criminal history checks every   24 months .   Final candidates for this position must complete a sexual misconduct reference check, per University Policy 05-010. An offer of employment will be contingent upon satisfactory results from the sexual misconduct reference check. Preferred Qualifications: Advanced degree (PhD, MBA, JD, or equivalent) in engineering, physical sciences, business, or a related field. Experience leading or scaling innovation ecosystems, research consortia, technology hubs, or startup platforms. Familiarity with federal research funding, cooperative agreements, and technology commercialization. Experience operating within or alongside research universities while building independent, sustainable organizations. Experience with intellectual property contracts between industry and academia. Demonstrated success managing a complex, multi-organizational budget. Experience leading a large federally funded initiative or center with milestone-based performance requirements and external oversight. Experience building and leading statewide or national innovation, workforce, or economic development partnerships.   Working Conditions/Schedule: Salary Range: Salary to commensurate with education, training and experience Anticipated Start Date: 2026-10-01 Anticipated End Date: We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status. OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire. OSU is a fair chance employer committed to inclusive hiring. We encourage applications from candidates who bring a wide range of lived experience including involvement with the justice system. If this position has “critical or security-sensitive” responsibilities and/or driving duties and you are selected as a finalist, your initial job offer will be contingent upon the results of a job-related pre-employment check (such as a background check, motor vehicle history check, sexual misconduct reference check, etc.). Background check results do not automatically disqualify a candidate. Take a look at our Background Checks website including the for candidates section for more details. If you have questions or concerns about the pre-employment check, please contact OSU’s Employee and Labor Relations team at employee.relations@oregonstate.edu ."
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Bend/Admissions-Advisor--Bilingual---Spanish-_JR0000737": {
      "title": "Admissions Advisor (Bilingual – Spanish)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-28\nPosted: Posted 8 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nDepartment: OSU Cascades Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents:   1) A resume/CV; and   2) A cover letter indicating how your qualifications and experience have prepared you for this position. 3) Transcripts: Transcripts must be submitted for all required and/or related courses. All courses must be from accredited colleges, universities, or private vocational schools. The online application system will allow you to attach your transcripts if the PDF file is 5MB or less. Transcripts must be received by the closing date.  If you have trouble uploading your transcripts, reach out to the contact listed. The salary range listed reflects the full-time salary range and will be commensurate with the candidate’s skills, education, and experience. For additional information please contact: Johana Hernandez johana.hernandez@osucascades.edu Job Summary: OSU Cascades is seeking an Admissions Advisor -Bilingual (Spanish). This is a full-time, (1.00 FTE), 12-month, professional faculty position. Join Oregon State University-Cascades as an Admissions Advisor and help students achieve their academic and career goals. You’ll provide personalized support, guiding prospective students through the admissions process with clarity and confidence. Your efforts will foster a welcoming and diverse community where students can thrive. In this role, you will demonstrate a deep commitment to expanding access to education in Central Oregon while supporting the growth of our local community. Reporting to the Assistant Director of Admissions, the Admissions Advisor represents OSU-Cascades through recruitment activities aimed at supporting the campus enrollment goals and strategic plan. The Admissions Advisor provides the first point of contact for prospective students and families as they navigate the application process. The position serves as a member of the recruitment team that is responsible for the planning and execution of undergraduate, domestic recruitment strategies. The Admissions Advisor must be willing to travel and represent OSU-Cascades and Oregon State University to a diverse population of students, counselors, family members and community partners. The successful candidate will be a detail-oriented individual, excited to work in a fast-paced environment, who values strong time management skills and is flexible, having the ability to adapt to rapid change. The Admissions Advisor will have the opportunity to support university goals and strategic plan and will play a critical role in assisting in the recruitment of talented, diverse students to the university. KEY RESPONSIBILITIES: Recruitment Counsel and Advise Prospective Students Recruitment Events Planning, Development, and Management of Recruitment Plan Holistic Admissions Review Service and Other Qualifications: REQUIRED QUALIFICATIONS: Bachelor’s Degree from a regionally accredited college or university in discipline of choice. Bachelor’s Degree must be completed by start date. Bilingual in Spanish. Experience working with college or high school students in an advisory or recruitment capacity. Excellent written and oral communication skills in English and Spanish. Ability to communicate effectively verbally in front of small and large groups and to provide workshops, trainings and outreach programs. Technological proficiency utilizing computers and working with programs similar to Microsoft Office applications including Word, Excel, Outlook and Power Point. Ability to think clearly under pressure and to work with individuals from various cultures, including the ability to handle difficult inter-personal situations with tact and diplomacy. Must be able and willing to independently travel for extended periods and work during irregular hours extensively, including late nights, early mornings, evenings and weekends for a minimum of seventeen (17) weeks per year on a continuous and ongoing basis. Must be able to manage frequent travel and fatigue related stress. Demonstrable commitment to promoting and enhancing inclusive excellence, ability to work with other individuals whose culture and/or beliefs may differ from your own. This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a criminal history check and be determined to be position qualified as per University Standard: 05-010 et seq. Incumbents are required to self report convictions and those in youth programs may have additional criminal history checks every 24 months. This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a criminal history check and be determined to be position qualified as per University Standard: 05-010 et seq. Incumbents are required to self report convictions and those in youth programs may have additional criminal history checks every 24 months. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS: 1-2 years of full-time professional experience in college admissions or college counseling or 1-3 years of full-time and formal work experience as an admissions, financial aid, housing, college outreach or college ambassador position that involves regular, frequent and direct work with prospective high school and/or transfer students and their families. Intermediate proficiency with spreadsheets and databases, including experience with CRM software (Slate experience preferred). Use of Social Networking media i.e. Instagram, Facebook and Twitter in professional context. Customer service and event planning experience. Strong attention to detail. Ability to be flexible and operate in a fast-paced and continuously changing environment. Working Conditions/Schedule: Admission Advisors independently travel for extended periods and work during irregular hours extensively, including late nights, early mornings, evenings, and weekends for a minimum of seventeen (17) weeks per year on a continuous and ongoing basis. Must be able to manage frequent travel and fatigue related stress. Must be able to lift 50 pounds or more. Must be able to drive in all-weather conditions. Must be able to fly on a commercial aircraft. Salary Range: $46,500-$50,500 Anticipated Start Date: 2026-09-30 Anticipated End Date: We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status. OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire. OSU is a fair chance employer committed to inclusive hiring. We encourage applications from candidates who bring a wide range of lived experience including involvement with the justice system. If this position has “critical or security-sensitive” responsibilities and/or driving duties and you are selected as a finalist, your initial job offer will be contingent upon the results of a job-related pre-employment check (such as a background check, motor vehicle history check, sexual misconduct reference check, etc.). Background check results do not automatically disqualify a candidate. Take a look at our Background Checks website including the for candidates section for more details. If you have questions or concerns about the pre-employment check, please contact OSU’s Employee and Labor Relations team at employee.relations@oregonstate.edu ."
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/Construction-Contracts-Officer_JR0000693": {
      "title": "Construction Contracts Officer",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-28\nPosted: Posted 8 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nDepartment: Procurement, Payment and Travel Special Instructions to Applicants: Special Instructions to Applicants: To ensure full consideration, applications must be received by September 09, 2026. Applications will continue to be accepted after the full consideration date, until a sufficient applicant pool has been achieved or the position is filled. The closing date is subject to change without notice to applicants.  When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents:    1) A resume 2) A cover letter indicating how your qualifications and experience have prepared you for this position.    Starting salary will be commensurate with skills, education, and experience.  For additional information please contact: Christine Atwood Christine.Atwood@oregonstate.edu Job Summary: The Office of Procurement, Payment and Travel is seeking a Construction Contracts Officer. This is a full-time (1.00 FTE), 12-month, professional faculty position. Join Oregon State University's Construction Contracts Administration team in a challenging and rewarding role that supports the delivery of facilities and infrastructure projects across OSU's statewide enterprise. From new buildings and major renovations to infrastructure improvements and specialized construction projects, the work of this position directly contributes to creating and maintaining the spaces where students learn, researchers innovate, and the university's mission comes to life. Construction Contracts Administration is part of the Office of Procurement, Payment and Travel (OPPT), a highly collaborative organization that partners with departments across the university to deliver responsive, transparent, and customer-focused services. OPPT values teamwork, relationship-building, continuous improvement, and innovative problem-solving, recognizing that strong partnerships are essential to supporting the University's teaching, research, and outreach mission. As a member of the Construction Contracts Administration team, the Construction Contracts Officer serves as a trusted advisor and subject matter expert in construction procurement and contract administration. Working closely with project managers, architects, engineers, contractors, consultants, and university stakeholders, this position leads complex construction and design procurements and supports projects throughout the project lifecycle. The role offers opportunities to solve unique challenges, navigate complex contracting issues, and contribute to projects that have a lasting impact on the OSU community. We are seeking a collaborative professional who enjoys analyzing complex situations, building strong working relationships, and developing practical solutions while balancing compliance requirements, risk management considerations, and project objectives. The ideal candidate will bring experience in construction contracting, sound professional judgment, strong communication skills, and a commitment to customer service and continuous improvement. This position offers the opportunity to work on a diverse portfolio of projects, expand expertise in public construction procurement and contract administration, and contribute to a highly collaborative team dedicated to supporting the University's strategic goals through effective procurement and contracting practices. Key Responsibilities:   Lead and administer procurement processes for construction and design services, including development of solicitations, evaluation support, contract execution, and project closeout. Develop, negotiate, and manage construction-related contracts, amendments, change orders, and other contract documents throughout the project lifecycle. Provide guidance and subject matter expertise regarding public construction contracting requirements, procurement strategies, contract administration, and regulatory compliance. Collaborate with project managers, architects, engineers, contractors, consultants, and university stakeholders to support successful delivery of capital improvement and infrastructure projects. Ensure compliance with applicable laws, regulations, policies, and industry standards, including prevailing wage requirements, public contracting requirements, and contract risk management practices. Review and administer construction-related insurance, bonding, and other contractual risk management requirements. Contribute to the continuous improvement of construction procurement and contract administration practices, templates, procedures, and customer service initiatives. Qualifications: Required Qualifications: Bachelor's degree in Business Administration, Public Administration, Construction Management, Engineering, Architecture, Law, or a field related to the duties of the position and three (3) years of professional experience in construction procurement, construction contracting, construction contract administration, capital project delivery, design and construction services procurement, public construction contracting, or a closely related field; OR An equivalent combination of education and experience that includes six (6) years of professional experience in construction procurement, construction contracting, construction contract administration, capital project delivery, design and construction services procurement, public construction contracting, or a closely related field. Experience drafting, administering, and managing construction-related solicitations, contracts, amendments, change orders, or other procurement and contract documents. Experience interpreting and applying laws, administrative rules, policies, procedures, and contractual requirements in support of procurement and contracting activities. Experience simultaneously managing multiple procurements, contracts, projects, or assignments with competing priorities and deadlines. Demonstrated ability to analyze complex information and communicate effectively with diverse stakeholders through both written and verbal communication. Experience using Microsoft Office applications and the ability to quickly learn and utilize specialized procurement, contract management, and enterprise systems. This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a criminal history check and be determined to be position qualified as per University Standard: 05-010 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in youth programs may have additional criminal history checks every 24 months. Preferred Qualifications: The ideal candidate will possess substantial experience in public construction procurement and construction contract administration and be capable of independently managing complex construction acquisitions with minimal training or oversight. Preference will be given to candidates who demonstrate advanced knowledge of construction-specific contracting practices, including project delivery methods, change order administration, prevailing wage compliance, bonding and insurance requirements, construction contract risk management, and public sector construction contracting requirements. Preference will be given to candidates with one or more of the following: Experience performing procurement, contracting, or contract administration functions for public construction projects within higher education, state government, local government, or other public sector organizations. Experience administering public improvement or capital construction contracts, including change orders, claims, contract modifications, project closeout, and contractor compliance requirements. Experience with Oregon public contracting requirements, including Bureau of Labor and Industries (BOLI) Prevailing Wage Law compliance. Experience with federally funded construction projects and applicable federal procurement and prevailing wage requirements, including Davis-Bacon Act compliance. Experience evaluating construction-related insurance, bonding, and risk management requirements. Experience working with construction contract law, project delivery methods, and industry-standard construction contract documents. Professional certification related to procurement, contract management, construction management, or project management, such as CPPO, CPPB, CPCM, CCM, PMP, or equivalent. Master's degree in Business Administration, Public Administration, Construction Management, or a related field. Demonstrated commitment to advancing inclusive procurement practices and supporting supplier diversity initiatives. Working Conditions/Schedule: Salary Range: $85,000-$100,000 Anticipated Start Date: 2026-10-01 Anticipated End Date: We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status. OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire. OSU is a fair chance employer committed to inclusive hiring. We encourage applications from candidates who bring a wide range of lived experience including involvement with the justice system. If this position has “critical or security-sensitive” responsibilities and/or driving duties and you are selected as a finalist, your initial job offer will be contingent upon the results of a job-related pre-employment check (such as a background check, motor vehicle history check, sexual misconduct reference check, etc.). Background check results do not automatically disqualify a candidate. Take a look at our Background Checks website including the for candidates section for more details. If you have questions or concerns about the pre-employment check, please contact OSU’s Employee and Labor Relations team at employee.relations@oregonstate.edu ."
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Tangent/Field-Crops-Undergraduate-Research-Assistant_JR0000952-1": {
      "title": "Field Crops Undergraduate Research Assistant",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-28\nPosted: Posted 8 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nDepartment: Linn County Extension Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents: ●        A Resume ●        A Cover Letter For additional information please contact: Christy Tanner at Christy.tanner@oregonstate.edu We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status. This position requires driving a University vehicle or a personal vehicle on behalf of the University; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a Motor Vehicle History Check , possess and maintain a current, valid driver's license in their state of residence, be determined to be position qualified and self-report convictions (as per Voluntary and Compulsory Driver Standards OSU Standard 125-155-0200) as per OSU Standard 576-056-0000 et seq. OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire.   Note: All job offers are contingent upon Human Resources final approval Job Description: This recruitment will be used to fill 1 part-time (a maximum of 24 hours per week) undergraduate research assistant for the Field Crops Extension Program at Oregon State University (OSU). Position Duties This position will assist with a variety of projects for the South Willamette Valley Field Crops Extension and Research Program. This program conducts research on seed and cereal crop production systems including grass seed, clover seed, cereals and other field crops. Current projects include pest management (slugs, voles, insects), soil health, drone aerial imagery, and plant growth regulators. The duties of this position will include set up, sampling and data collection for on-farm field trials. Indoor work will include sample processing, data entry and data analysis. Transferable Skill Development OSU is committed to ensuring students are prepared for success after graduation through intentional skill development as student employees. The competencies students develop while working at OSU are defined by the National Association of Colleges and Employers (NACE) as the essential skills employers seek in their hires. In this position, students learn and build vital career-readiness skills in the primary areas below:   Communication, Critical Thinking, Technology, Professionalism Qualifications: Full Employment Eligibility Requirements can be found here:   https://hr.oregonstate.edu/student-employment-manual/500-employment-eligibility-requirements Must be academically enrolled in a high school, community college, or university and pursuing a program or course of study Must meet Academic Standing Requirements; students on academic suspension are not eligible for employment Must meet the applicable minimal enrollment standard High School student: Regularly enrolled in a high school or participating in a home-schooling program Undergraduate and post-baccalaureate student: 6 credit hours per term Undergraduate international student: 12 credit hours per term* Graduate student officially admitted to Graduate School: 5 credit hours per term Graduate international student officially admitted to Graduate School: 9 credit hours per term* *International students may be allowed to carry fewer hours than specified above and still be considered “full-time” by the United States Citizenship and Immigration Services ( USCIS ). A reduced course load is approved by the   Office of International Services   ( OIS ), and must be provided to the Student Employment Center. Required This position requires driving a University vehicle or a personal vehicle on behalf of the University; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a Motor Vehicle History Check , possess and maintain a current, valid driver's license in their state of residence, be determined to be position qualified and self-report convictions (as per Voluntary and Compulsory Driver Standards OSU Standard 125-155-0200) as per OSU Standard 576-056-0000 et seq. Preferred Majoring in, or previous course work in agricultural sciences or related fields (e.g.soil science, crop science, horticulture, environmental science, biology, etc.). Experience with GIS. Experience with programming in R, Python or other languages. Working Conditions/Schedule: - The position will be based at the Linn County Extension Office in Tangent, about 15 minutes from campus. - Available hours will be up to the maximum for student employees during the academic year, and up to 40 hours per week during the summer. Salary Range: Anticipated Start Date: 2026-09-07 Anticipated End Date:"
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/TRIO-Student-Peer-Tutor_JR0000936": {
      "title": "TRIO Student Peer Tutor",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-27\nPosted: Posted 9 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nDepartment: TRIO Student Support Services Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents: ●       Transcripts ●        A Cover Letter For additional information please contact: Alondra Garcia Martinez at alondra.garciamartinez@oregonstate.edu We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status. This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a criminal history check and be determined to be position qualified as per OSU Standard 576-055-0000 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in youth programs may have additional criminal history checks every 24 months. OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire.   Note: All job offers are contingent upon Human Resources final approval Job Description: This recruitment will be used to fill numerous part-time (a maximum of 24 hours per week) Peer Tutor positions for the TRIO Student Support Services Program at Oregon State University (OSU). SSS Peer Tutors work individually and with small groups of SSS students over the course of the term (week 2 through week 10) to help them better understand course material and prepare for assignments and exams. In addition to helping students with course content, Peer Tutors share study strategies and demonstrate methods for how students can find the answers for themselves, with the goal that through tutoring. TRIO SSS students build the skills that will allow them to excel in their classes. Tutors must become familiar with tutoring strategies that equip students with active learning skills. TRIO SSS tutors need to have successfully completed any courses that they tutor with a B or higher (in some cases they must currently be taking the course). Training will be provided to become acquainted with the tutoring format of the program. Anticipated Hourly Wage: $15.55 Both Undergraduate and Graduate students are welcome/encouraged to apply! Position Duties Tutor students individually or in small groups Ongoing training and professional development Coordinating meetings directly with students and maintaining documentation Group and individual meetings with supervisor Assisting at quarterly study sessions Transferable Skill Development OSU is committed to ensuring students are prepared for success after graduation through intentional skill development as student employees. The competencies students develop while working at OSU are defined by the National Association of Colleges and Employers (NACE) as the essential skills employers seek in their hires. In this position, students learn and build vital career-readiness skills in the primary areas below:   Communication, Critical Thinking, Leadership, Professionalism, Equity & Inclusion Qualifications: Full Employment Eligibility Requirements can be found here:   https://hr.oregonstate.edu/student-employment-manual/500-employment-eligibility-requirements Must be academically enrolled in a high school, community college, or university and pursuing a program or course of study Must meet Academic Standing Requirements; students on academic suspension are not eligible for employment Must meet the applicable minimal enrollment standard High School student: Regularly enrolled in a high school or participating in a home-schooling program Undergraduate and post-baccalaureate student: 6 credit hours per term Undergraduate international student: 12 credit hours per term* Graduate student officially admitted to Graduate School: 5 credit hours per term Graduate international student officially admitted to Graduate School: 9 credit hours per term* *International students may be allowed to carry fewer hours than specified above and still be considered “full-time” by the United States Citizenship and Immigration Services ( USCIS ). A reduced course load is approved by the   Office of International Services   ( OIS ), and must be provided to the Student Employment Center. Required An interest in helping others be successful in college Experience interacting with people from diverse backgrounds Previous success in some of the most tutored courses (MTH 111-252, PH series, ECON, ST, etc.) The ability to arrange a tutoring schedule directly with students and manage one’s own schedule This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a criminal history check and be determined to be position qualified as per OSU Standard 576-055-0000 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in youth programs may have additional criminal history checks every 24 months. Preferred 3.0 GPA or above Work-study preferred Experience tutoring others (paid or unpaid) Current or past TRIO, CAMP, or EOP enrollment/involvement An understanding of the SSS student population (first-generation college students, low-income students, and students with disabilities) Working Conditions/Schedule: • 3-7 hours a week • Flexible scheduling: weekdays, evenings and weekends; subject to change every term Salary Range: Anticipated Start Date: 2026-09-01 Anticipated End Date:"
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/Ecampus-Student-Project-Assistant_JR0000917": {
      "title": "Ecampus Student Project Assistant",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-27\nPosted: Posted 9 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nDepartment: Division of Ecampus, Corporate & Workforce Education Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents: A Resume A Cover Letter explaining how your education and experience will help you succeed in this role. For additional information please contact: Michelle McAllaster at Michelle.mcallaster@oregonstate.edu We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status.   OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire.   Note: All job offers are contingent upon Human Resources final approval Job Description: This recruitment will be used to fill one part-time (a maximum of 24 hours per week) Ecampus Student Project Assistant for the Division of Ecampus, Corporate & Workforce Education at Oregon State University (OSU). Reporting to the Associate Director of Corporate and Workforce Education, this position supports the optimal operation of all corporate and workforce education collaborations, which increases access and affordability for working adult learners to online education at Oregon State. Do you enjoy working with data, solving problems, and collaborating with people? Are you self‑motivated, reliable, and curious about how universities collaborate with industry to make education more accessible? Join us as a Corporate and Workforce Education Operations Assistant and help make a positive impact on the student experience at Oregon State. In this role, you’ll use artificial intelligence (AI), Microsoft Excel, and other analytical tools to organize, interpret, and summarize data that informs process improvement. You’ll complete a range of defined, task‑based assignments—such as data preparation and analysis, information gathering, documentation, and summary reporting—that contribute to our decision‑making processes. Position Duties: Communication: Help with routine internal communications with division teams and campus partners Meeting Support: Assist with meeting prep and revision of meeting notes and storage. Assist with follow-up tasks as needed Collaboration Verifications: Assist with verification requests, associated tracking of verifications, and follow-up with students Data Analysis and Quality Assurance: Perform regular detailed review and analysis of student and corporate collaborator data aimed at proactive identification of issues that impact the student experience Event Coordination Assistance: Support the organization of recruitment fairs and events, both in-person and virtually. Monitor and report on marketing collateral inventory Documentation: Document and map out processes for managing corporate and workforce agreements, and keep the documents and processes updated Research: Support research efforts that contribute to executive briefs and regular collaborator summaries/updates Transferable Skill Development OSU is committed to ensuring students are prepared for success after graduation through intentional skill development as student employees. The competencies students develop while working at OSU are defined by the National Association of Colleges and Employers (NACE) as the essential skills employers seek in their hires. In this position, students learn and build vital career-readiness skills in the primary areas below:   Communication, Critical Thinking, Technology, Professionalism Qualifications: Full Employment Eligibility Requirements can be found here:   https://hr.oregonstate.edu/student-employment-manual/500-employment-eligibility-requirements Must be academically enrolled in a high school, community college, or university and pursuing a program or course of study Must meet Academic Standing Requirements; students on academic suspension are not eligible for employment Must meet the applicable minimal enrollment standard High School student: Regularly enrolled in a high school or participating in a home-schooling program Undergraduate and post-baccalaureate student: 6 credit hours per term Undergraduate international student: 12 credit hours per term* Graduate student officially admitted to Graduate School: 5 credit hours per term Graduate international student officially admitted to Graduate School: 9 credit hours per term* *International students may be allowed to carry fewer hours than specified above and still be considered “full-time” by the United States Citizenship and Immigration Services ( USCIS ). A reduced course load is approved by the   Office of International Services   ( OIS ), and must be provided to the Student Employment Center. Required Qualifications: Current enrollment as an Oregon State University student; applicants who expect to remain enrolled and employed in this position for at least the next 18 months will receive priority consideration. Clear written communication skills, including professional email habits and timely communication of questions or concerns. Comfortable learning and using new technology and software tools. Reliability in meeting deadlines and responding to feedback. Strong attention to detail, organizational skills, and the ability to manage multiple structured tasks accurately while prioritizing competing deadlines under high expectations. Preferred Qualifications: Experience with data analysis tools (e.g., experience using R and Python programming languages is a plus). Familiarity with project management tools (e.g., Asana, Trello) or CRM platforms such as Salesforce. Familiarity with Microsoft Office tools, especially Word, Outlook, SharePoint, and Teams. Experience with Microsoft Excel, including sorting, formatting, and simple formulas. Academic background or coursework in areas such as data analytics, business analytics, computer science, engineering, information systems, or related fields is helpful. Students from any major are encouraged to apply—relevant skills, experience, and interest in data‑informed work are more important than program of study. Interest in corporate education, workforce development, or higher education operations. Experience in roles that required managing detailed tasks or working from established procedures. Prior experience working independently while staying accountable to a supervisor or team. Curiosity, initiative, and a willingness to learn from feedback and examples. Working Conditions/Schedule: On site work is based in the Valley Library during business hours (8 am and 5 pm) on Tuesdays, Wednesdays, and Thursdays. Salary Range: 16.70 Anticipated Start Date: 2026-09-14 Anticipated End Date:"
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/BA-270-Teaching-Assistant_JR0000751": {
      "title": "BA 270 Teaching Assistant",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-27\nPosted: Posted 9 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nDepartment: College of Business School of Accounting, Finance, and Information Systems Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents: A Resume A Cover Letter For additional information please contact: Venkataramani Raja at VT.Raja@bus.oregonstate.edu We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status.   This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a Criminal History Check and be determined to be position qualified as per OSU Standard 576-055-0000 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in Youth Programs may have additional Criminal History Checks every 2 years.   OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire.   Note: All job offers are contingent upon Human Resources final approval Job Description: This recruitment will be used to fill 1 part-time (a maximum of 24 hours per week) Teaching Assistant position for the College of Business at Oregon State University (OSU). This is a part-time Teaching Assistant (TA) position for some sections of the BA270 course. The TA will have access to confidential data (e.g., Personally Identifiable Information).  Hence, they must have high integrity, strong work ethics, and must abide by OSU and FERPA rules. Depending on their availability, candidate can work for a minimum of five hours per week, and a maximum of 20 hours per week. A majority of the TA work can be done remotely. Depending on candidate’s availability and instructor/student needs, the TA work may involve lab demos, meeting with instructor, conducting help sessions, grading, editing content on the course Canvas site, communicating with instructor/students, and exam proctoring. Pay rate for this position is $16.55 per hour. Position Duties: The successful candidate will meet with instructor as needed and help with some activities such as: Reviewing and editing content on Canvas course site Grading and data entry Transcribing audio recordings of lectures Conducting IT lab help sessions (e.g., basic Microsoft Visio) The successful candidate is expected to: Work one-on-one with students seeking academic help relating to BA270 (this may involve in-person and virtual meetings with students) Have good interpersonal, listening, verbal and written communication skills Be organized and have good time management skills Identify and help process any academic integrity violations that may occur in the BA270 course. Transferable Skill Development OSU is committed to ensuring students are prepared for success after graduation through intentional skill development as student employees. The competencies students develop while working at OSU are defined by the National Association of Colleges and Employers (NACE) as the essential skills employers seek in their hires. In this position, students learn and build vital career-readiness skills in the primary areas below:   Communication, Critical Thinking, Professionalism Qualifications: Full Employment Eligibility Requirements can be found here:   https://hr.oregonstate.edu/student-employment-manual/500-employment-eligibility-requirements Must be academically enrolled in a high school, community college, or university and pursuing a program or course of study Must meet Academic Standing Requirements; students on academic suspension are not eligible for employment Must meet the applicable minimal enrollment standard High School student: Regularly enrolled in a high school or participating in a home-schooling program Undergraduate and post-baccalaureate student: 6 credit hours per term Undergraduate international student: 12 credit hours per term* Graduate student officially admitted to Graduate School: 5 credit hours per term Graduate international student officially admitted to Graduate School: 9 credit hours per term* *International students may be allowed to carry fewer hours than specified above and still be considered “full-time” by the United States Citizenship and Immigration Services ( USCIS ). A reduced course load is approved by the   Office of International Services   ( OIS) and must be provided to the Student Employment Center. Required Qualifications: Applicants must have successfully completed BA 270 (Business Process Management) course at Oregon State University Applicants must be comfortable using and teaching MS Visio, MS Dynamics and MS Office software as they relate to BA 270 Applicants must be interested in helping students with BA 270 related content. Additional Required Qualifications: This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a Criminal History Check and be determined to be position qualified as per OSU Standard 576-055-0000 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in Youth Programs may have additional Criminal History Checks every 2 years. Preferred Qualifications: Preference will be given to applicants with a service mindset who are genuinely interested in helping students with BA 270 related content Professionalism would be a preferred key attribute Applicants must have successfully completed BA270 (Business Process Management) course at Oregon State University with a grade of B+ or higher Working Conditions/Schedule: As the course may be an e-campus course, the job may require TAs to work and help students outside regular business hours. However, there is ample flexibility in selecting the hours during which the TA would prefer to work each week. The position allows for much variation in the hours selected each week. Typically, access to training by outgoing TA who is graduating is available for newly hired TA. Salary Range: Anticipated Start Date: 2026-09-17 Anticipated End Date:"
    },
