    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/Student-Teaching-Assistant-for-BA275---Foundations-of-Statistical-Inference_JR0000933": {
      "title": "Student Teaching Assistant for BA275 – Foundations of Statistical Inference",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-27\nPosted: Posted 9 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nDepartment: College of Business – School of Management, Entrepreneurship & Supply Chain Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents: ●        A Resume ●        A Brief Cover Letter For additional information please contact: Katie Jager at katie.jager@oregonstate.edu We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status.   This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a Criminal History Check and be determined to be position qualified as per OSU Standard 576-055-0000 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in Youth Programs may have additional Criminal History Checks every 2 years. OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire.   Note: All job offers are contingent upon Human Resources final approval Job Description: This recruitment will be used to fill 1 part-time (a maximum of 24 hours per week) student job for the College of Business at Oregon State University (OSU). Join us in empowering students to excel in their understanding of statistics for business! We are seeking a dedicated and knowledgeable Teaching Assistant (TA) for BA275  - Foundations of Statistical Inference for the upcoming academic year (2024-2025). As a TA, you will play a vital role in supporting students' learning and comprehension of statistical concepts. This position offers an excellent opportunity to enhance your leadership and communication skills, deepen your understanding of quantitative methods used in business, and contribute to the academic success of students in the College of Business. Pay rate for this position is $16.55 per hour. Position Duties The following duties are expected to take about 4 – 8 hours per week. Assist the instructor in proctoring exams on campus. Hold regular office hours to provide additional support and guidance to students. Grade weekly assignments and/or discussion boards online with feedback. Foster a positive and inclusive learning environment for all students. Respond promptly to student inquiries and concerns via email or in-person. Transferable Skill Development OSU is committed to ensuring students are prepared for success after graduation through intentional skill development as student employees. The competencies students develop while working at OSU are defined by the National Association of Colleges and Employers (NACE) as the essential skills employers seek in their hires. In this position, students learn and build vital career-readiness skills in the primary areas below:   Communication, Critical Thinking, Professionalism Qualifications: Full Employment Eligibility Requirements can be found here:   https://hr.oregonstate.edu/student-employment-manual/500-employment-eligibility-requirements Must be academically enrolled in a high school, community college, or university and pursuing a program or course of study Must meet Academic Standing Requirements; students on academic suspension are not eligible for employment Must meet the applicable minimal enrollment standard High School student: Regularly enrolled in a high school or participating in a home-schooling program Undergraduate and post-baccalaureate student: 6 credit hours per term Undergraduate international student: 12 credit hours per term* Graduate student officially admitted to Graduate School: 5 credit hours per term Graduate international student officially admitted to Graduate School: 9 credit hours per term* *International students may be allowed to carry fewer hours than specified above and still be considered “full-time” by the United States Citizenship and Immigration Services ( USCIS ). A reduced course load is approved by the   Office of International Services   ( OIS ), and must be provided to the Student Employment Center. Required: Must have completed at least one class in statistics with a minimum grade of B+. Demonstrated enthusiasm for statistics and a genuine desire to help others learn. Strong communication and interpersonal skills. Ability to complete tasks in a timely and organized manner. Perform basic functions in excel. This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a Criminal History Check and be determined to be position qualified as per OSU Standard 576-055-0000 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in Youth Programs may have additional Criminal History Checks every 2 years. Preferred: Taken BA275 with Katie Jager and has earned and A- or above. Currently a Business Analytics major or is earning a statistics minor Previous teaching or tutoring experience is preferred but not required. Working Conditions/Schedule: The TA position is for the duration of the 2026-2027 academic year starting Fall Term (some training may start prior to the end of spring term). Applicants must be available to work on campus during this time. Working schedule is 4-8 hours per week. Salary Range: Anticipated Start Date: 2026-09-21 Anticipated End Date:"
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Newport-Campus/Cashier-1--Seasonal-_JR0000826": {
      "title": "Cashier 1 (Seasonal)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-27\nPosted: Posted 9 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nDepartment: Hatfield Marine Science Center Special Instructions to Applicants: To ensure full consideration, applications must be received by September 2, 2026. Applications will continue to be accepted after the full consideration date, until a sufficient applicant pool has been achieved or the position is filled. The closing date is subject to change without notice to applicants. When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents:   1) A resume/CV; and   2) A cover letter indicating how your qualifications and experience have prepared you for this position.   3) Answer the following questions and upload as a separate document. Please tell us how your background and experience have prepared you for this position. What experience have you had working with and providing service to those whose backgrounds are different from your own, and how will you utilize that experience to create a welcoming environment in this position? Starting salary will be commensurate with skills, education, and experience. For additional information please contact: Stacia Carpenter at Stacia.carpenter@oregonstate.edu or 541-867-0226. Job Summary: This recruitment will be used to fill three part-time (approximately 20 hours per week), seasonal (September – May) Cashier 1 positions for the Hatfield Marine Science Visitor Center at Oregon State University (OSU). This position is located in Newport, Oregon. This position greets and orients visitors to the Hatfield Marine Science Center Visitors Center (HMSC VC), confirms online reservation(s) and receives money and makes change as needed in exchange for entrance to the VC and may receive donations to support the VC. The HMSC VC is in Newport, OR and is operated and managed by Oregon Sea Grant. The Hatfield Visitor Center attracts more than 70,000 visitors and upwards of 10,000 students annually to engage in lifelong learning and discovery about marine science and marine and coastal ecosystems. The 15,000 square foot facility is filled with family-friendly interactive exhibits, live animals and touch tanks, and state of the art technology.     This position will work two to three days a week and report to the Visitor Center Coordinator. The position requires a general knowledge of cash handling policies and procedures. Additional duties include professionally providing information about the Visitor Center, HMSC campus, Oregon Sea Grant program, and the local area to visitors; assisting volunteers with interpreting the exhibits within areas surrounding the Visitor Center entrance; answering visitors’ questions, assisting with exhibit trouble shooting and maintenance when needed, and light housekeeping.  Key Responsibilities: Receives money and assists with credit card purchases for entry to the Hatfield Visitor Center. Sells posters by cash and credit card and records sales. Welcomes and orients visitors to the Visitor Center. Interprets exhibits, answers visitor questions, and encourages donations. Performs light housekeeping and general office duties at the VC front desk. Enters visitor counts, posters sold, and cash/credit card totals for the day into a tracking spreadsheet. Qualifications: Required Qualifications: Six months of cashiering experience exchanging money for purchases and making change. Training or experience should have included operating a calculator or ten-key adding machine by touch, and using a computer and keyboard. The position requires a general knowledge of cash handling policies and procedures. This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a criminal history check and be determined to be position qualified as per University Standard: 05-010 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in youth programs may have additional criminal history checks every 24 months. Working Conditions/Schedule: Salary Range: $18.06-$20.98 Anticipated Start Date: 2026-09-07 Anticipated End Date: 2027-05-31 We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status. OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire. OSU is a fair chance employer committed to inclusive hiring. We encourage applications from candidates who bring a wide range of lived experience including involvement with the justice system. If this position has “critical or security-sensitive” responsibilities and/or driving duties and you are selected as a finalist, your initial job offer will be contingent upon the results of a job-related pre-employment check (such as a background check, motor vehicle history check, sexual misconduct reference check, etc.). Background check results do not automatically disqualify a candidate. Take a look at our Background Checks website including the for candidates section for more details. If you have questions or concerns about the pre-employment check, please contact OSU’s Employee and Labor Relations team at employee.relations@oregonstate.edu ."
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/Student-Technical-Assistant---Tomasino-Lab_JR0000918": {
      "title": "Student Technical Assistant – Tomasino Lab",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-27\nPosted: Posted 9 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nDepartment: Food Science & Technology Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents: ●        A Resume ●        A Cover Letter For additional information please contact: Elizabeth Tomasino at Elizabeth.tomasino@oregonstate.edu We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status. This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a Criminal History Check and be determined to be position qualified as per OSU Standard 576-055-0000 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in Youth Programs may have additional Criminal History Checks every 2 years. OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire.   Note: All job offers are contingent upon Human Resources final approval Job Description: This recruitment will be used to fill 1 part-time (maximum of 24 hours per week) Student Technical Assistant position for the Department of Food Science and Technology at Oregon State University (OSU). Performs research in support of academic faculty Elizabeth Tomasino’s program. Position Duties Duties to be determined by the hiring unit and may include cleaning the lab, routine to complex technical tasks in support of Dr. Tomasino’s research. The research will be related to vineyard and field work, data gathering, chemical analysis, data analysis, smoke trials, winemaking, sensory analysis and assisting with sample preparation. The work may include laboratory, data collection, or simple analysis and involve assisting Primary Investigator in writing research reports and manuscripts. Other duties as assigned. Cleaning lab Searching research papers related to the projects, performing experiments, and data analysis using bioinformatics tools Assist with sensory panels Laboratory organization/design to increase the laboratory’s efficiency for experiments Transferable Skill Development OSU is committed to ensuring students are prepared for success after graduation through intentional skill development as student employees. The competencies students develop while working at OSU are defined by the National Association of Colleges and Employers (NACE) as the essential skills employers seek in their hires. In this position, students learn and build vital career-readiness skills in the primary areas below:   Communication, Critical Thinking, Teamwork, Technology Qualifications: Full Employment Eligibility Requirements can be found here:   https://hr.oregonstate.edu/student-employment-manual/500-employment-eligibility-requirements Must be academically enrolled in a high school, community college, or university and pursuing a program or course of study Must meet Academic Standing Requirements; students on academic suspension are not eligible for employment Must meet the applicable minimal enrollment standard High School student: Regularly enrolled in a high school or participating in a home-schooling program Undergraduate and post-baccalaureate student: 6 credit hours per term Undergraduate international student: 12 credit hours per term* Graduate student officially admitted to Graduate School: 5 credit hours per term Graduate international student officially admitted to Graduate School: 9 credit hours per term* *International students may be allowed to carry fewer hours than specified above and still be considered “full-time” by the United States Citizenship and Immigration Services ( USCIS ). A reduced course load is approved by the   Office of International Services   ( OIS ), and must be provided to the Student Employment Center. Required Enrolled in high school, community college or university and pursuing a program or course of study. Meet academic standing requirements and applicable minimal enrollment standard. The candidate must be able to communicate effectively in both written and oral English. This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a Criminal History Check and be determined to be position qualified as per OSU Standard 576-055-0000 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in Youth Programs may have additional Criminal History Checks every 2 years. Preferred Must be able-bodied and sufficiently familiar with the equipment to be used Interest in enology Demonstratable commitment to promoting and enhancing diversity Working Conditions/Schedule: • May include evening and weekend hours. • May include work outdoors in all types of weather. • May require walking, standing, bending and stooping for extended periods. • May require working with or around chemicals, caustic compounds, or hazardous materials used in research. Salary Range: Anticipated Start Date: 2026-09-08 Anticipated End Date:"
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/Student-Intramural-Sports-Official--Volleyball-_JR0000542-1": {
      "title": "Student Intramurals Official (Volleyball)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-27\nPosted: Posted 9 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nDepartment: Recreational Sports Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents: Resume Cover Letter For additional information please contact Matt Macer at matt.macer@oregonstate.edu. We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status. OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire. Note: All job offers are contingent upon Human Resources final approval. Job Description: This recruitment will be used to fill multiple part-time (a maximum of 24 hours per week) Intramural Sports Official (Volleyball) positions for Recreational Sports at Oregon State University ( OSU ). The Intramural Sports Official reports to the Sport Programs professional staff and plays a key role in delivering high-quality recreational sport experiences. Officials are responsible for officiating a variety of Intramural Sports leagues and events, demonstrating sport-specific knowledge, sound judgment, and consistent rule enforcement. This position supports the Sport Programs Mission Statement: to engage the university community in diverse sport and program opportunities that enrich the student learning experience through involvement, leadership, and physical activity. Anticipated Hourly Rate: $ 17.96 Common sports needing officials include: Fall Term: Flag Football, Volleyball, Wheelchair Basketball, Soccer Winter Term: Basketball, Water Polo, Inner Tube Water Polo, Flag Football, Soccer Spring Term: Softball, Kickball, Soccer Employee Expectations Sport Programs staff are expected to model professionalism, leadership, and accountability while contributing to a welcoming and inclusive recreational environment. Professionalism & Policy Knowledge: Learn, model, promote, and consistently enforce Department of Recreational Sports policies, demonstrating reliability, ethical decision-making, and attention to detail. Effective Communication: Communicate professionally, proactively, and respectfully with participants, teammates, supervisors, and administrative staff using clear verbal and written communication. Career & Skill Development: Maintain required certifications, attend orientations, staff meetings, and actively participate in training opportunities to support continuous learning and self-development. Critical Thinking & Problem Solving: Provide high-quality customer service by assessing situations, managing challenges, and utilizing conflict resolution skills that align with departmental policies and mission. Equity, Safety & Inclusion: Promote a safe, fun, and inclusive environment that respects individual differences and supports positive experiences for all participants and staff. Leadership & Growth Mindset: Recognize and reflect on feedback and experiences to be able to articulate transferable skills such as leadership, teamwork, adaptability, and professionalism. We create engaging environments for student growth and success. We inspire healthy living by providing quality recreational and educational opportunities for the Oregon State University community. Our Values in Action As a student employee, you play a vital role in bringing our mission and values to life. We expect every team member to embody the following values in their work: We Care About People : Treat all patrons and colleagues with kindness, courtesy and inclusion. We Are Solution-Oriented : Identify and implement solutions that enhance the member and employee experience. Address conflict in a positive, calm and solution-focused manner. We Are Stewards : Use resources responsibly and ethically while working efficiently. We Are Innovative : Pioneer programs, services and facilities that serve as catalysts for student success. Create space to encourage, share and evolve new ideas. We Are Educators : Foster environments where learning and personal growth thrive for both participants and staff. Grant others the same good intentions you grant yourself. We Extend Respect & Build Trust : Communicate with clarity, transparency and integrity, fostering trust across our community. Every action rooted in our mission and values strengthens our team and makes Recreational Sports a place where everyone belongs, grows and thrives. Position Duties Demonstrate comprehensive knowledge of sport rules and Intramural Sports policies, applying them consistently and fairly. Enforce and interpret sport rules while exercising sound judgment and professionalism in dynamic environments. Utilize critical thinking and conflict management skills to respond to challenging situations and de-escalate disputes effectively. Manage participants and teams in collaboration with Sport Program Associates, demonstrating teamwork and leadership, including: Ensuring completion of team rosters and waivers Monitoring and maintaining crowd control Facilitating directions and court/field assignments for officials, teams, and participants Communicate clearly and respectfully with Intramural Sports participants regarding rules, policies, procedures, and program information. Complete required documentation and paperwork accurately and efficiently using department systems and technology. Assist with facility preparation and clean-up to ensure safe and functional activity spaces. Communicate injuries, safety concerns, suggestions, and operational issues to appropriate staff in a timely and professional manner. Perform additional duties as assigned to support program operations and team success. Transferable Skill Development OSU   is committed to ensuring students are prepared for success after graduation   through intentional skill development as student employees. The competencies students develop while working at   OSU   are defined by the   National Association of Colleges and Employers ( NACE )   as the essential skills employers seek in their hires. In this position, students learn and build vital career-readiness skills in the primary areas below: Communication, Critical Thinking, Leadership Qualifications: Full Employment Eligibility Requirements can be found here:   https://hr.oregonstate.edu/student-employment-manual/500-employment-eligibility-requirements   Must be academically enrolled in a high school, community college, or university and pursuing a program or course of study Must meet Academic Standing Requirements; students on academic suspension are not eligible for employment Must meet the applicable minimal enrollment standard High School student: Regularly enrolled in a high school or participating in a home-schooling program Undergraduate and post-baccalaureate student: 6 credit hours per term Undergraduate international student: 12 credit hours per term* Graduate student officially admitted to Graduate School: 5 credit hours per term Graduate international student officially admitted to Graduate School: 9 credit hours per term* *International students may be allowed to carry fewer hours than specified above and still be considered “full-time” by the United States Citizenship and Immigration Services ( USCIS ). A reduced course load is approved by the   Office of International Services   ( OIS ), and must be provided to the Student Employment Center. Preferred Qualifications Demonstrated knowledge of Sport Programs offerings with the ability to officiate or prior officiating experience. Ability to provide positive, consistent customer service through strong interpersonal and communication skills. Demonstrated problem-solving, conflict management, and critical thinking abilities, including effective oral and written communication. Awareness of safety considerations, risk management practices, and emergency response procedures. Commitment to equity, inclusion, and respect for individual differences, with an interest in student development and leadership. Ability to work both independently and collaboratively while upholding Department of Recreational Sports standards of conduct, professionalism, and accountability. Working Conditions/Schedule: Period of Employment: One academic year (continuing employment is contingent upon satisfactory completion of a 30 day probationary period and satisfactory job performance as evaluated by immediate supervisor). 8-15 hours per week. Hours will vary between evenings 3:30pm-Midnight and weekends 9:00am-Midnight Compensation: Student employees are paid an hourly wage consistent with the OSU Student Employment Pay Rate Guidelines. The Department of Recreational Sports identifies pay ranges base upon job duties, level of responsibility and complexity of the work to be performed Salary Range: Anticipated Start Date: 2026-10-06 Anticipated End Date:"
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/Marketing-and-Communications-Project-Coordinator_JR0000788": {
      "title": "Marketing and Communications Project Coordinator",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-26\nPosted: Posted 10 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nDepartment: Division of Extension and Engagement Special Instructions to Applicants: To ensure full consideration, applications must be received by September 7, 2026. Applications will continue to be accepted after the full consideration date, until a sufficient applicant pool has been achieved or the position is filled. The closing date is subject to change without notice to applicants.  When applying you will be required to attach the following digital documents: 1) Resume: Finalists will participate in a meet and greet with the team and key collaborators which will include the distribution of a short biography 2) Cover letter that addresses each required qualification 3)  Responses to two (2) supplemental questions (upload as a separate document) Tell us about a project that depended on people who didn't report to you, and who had different priorities than yours. How did you keep it moving? (limit of 500 words) Tell us about a time you had to tell someone their request couldn't happen the way they'd hoped. What did you actually say, and how did they take it? (limit of 500 words) Starting salary will be commensurate with skills, education, and experience. For additional information please contact:   Jessi Hibsman jessi.hibsman@oregonstate.edu Job Summary: The Division of Extension and Engagement is seeking a Marketing and Communications Project Coordinator for its Communications and Marketing Unit. This is a full-time (1.00 FTE), 12-month, professional faculty position. The mission of the OSU Division of Extension and Engagement (division) is to work with communities throughout Oregon to support lifelong learning and co-create innovative solutions to address local challenges. Together, we envision a future where Oregon’s people, communities and ecosystems prosper. This Marketing and Communications Project Coordinator serves as a central point of coordination for communications and marketing requests within OSU Extension and Engagement. This position manages project intake, timelines and workflows that help teams develop and deliver high-quality, accessible, and brand-aligned communications and marketing. Working closely with unit leadership, colleagues, and stakeholders across the division, this coordinator provides responsive customer service, project guidance, and communication support, helping align priorities, coordinate resources, and move projects efficiently from start to finish. The position also supports internal communications efforts and public-facing content for OSU’s Extension and Engagement work. Key Responsibilities Manage project intake, workflows, timelines, and stakeholder communication to help ensure projects are completed efficiently and on schedule. Coordinate assigned communications and marketing projects, campaigns, reports, and internal communications initiatives, partnering with unit leadership and project stakeholders to support successful execution. Serve as a primary point of contact for communications and marketing requests, providing responsive customer service, project guidance, branding consultation, and resource referrals. Maintain and update digital content across websites, intranet platforms, and content management systems, including copyediting, proofing, accessibility review, and backup support for web publishing and digital asset management. Collaborate with colleagues to ensure content aligns with accessibility requirements and university brand and style standards and reflects inclusive communication practices. Support division internal communications, including employee meetings, newsletters, intranet content, and related communication initiatives. Recommend and implement process improvements to enhance efficiency, effectiveness, and user experience Qualifications: Required Qualifications Bachelor’s degree in marketing, communications, business, or field related to the assignments of this position. Professional project management experience. Strong communication and customer service skills, including the ability to communicate effectively with a variety of audiences. Strong organizational, time-management, and attention-to-detail skills, with the ability to manage multiple priorities and deadlines. Ability to work independently and collaboratively while building effective professional relationships. Experience editing and managing content on digital platforms, including familiarity with accessibility, branding, and editorial standards. Proficiency with office, collaboration, and digital communication software and an ability to adapt to changes in an evolving technology environment. A commitment to creating safe, respectful, inclusive, and welcoming learning and work environments. Preferred Qualifications: Professional experience, training, or certification in project management. Experience with Asana or a similar project management platform. Experience editing and reviewing content for editorial, brand, and design standards. Experience creating accessible digital content. Experience with digital asset management systems. Experience publishing web content in Drupal 10 or later. Experience working in or with a marketing or communications team. Bilingual and/or biliterate, particularly in Spanish and English. Working Conditions/Schedule: The primary work location for this position is the OSU Division and Extension and Engagement’s Communications and Marketing unit on the OSU’s campus in Corvallis, Oregon. However, flexible work may be established via an OSU Flexible Work Arrangement Agreement, with pre-determined periodic reviews of ongoing work for continuation of the agreement. This position is expected to work onsite in Corvallis at least two days per week with in-person attendance at other meetings and events as needed. Infrequent out-of-state travel may be necessary for professional development and service. Work is performed in an office environment. Typical schedule is 8-5pm. Salary Range: $57,473 - $74,533 Anticipated Start Date: 2026-09-21 Anticipated End Date: We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status. OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire. OSU is a fair chance employer committed to inclusive hiring. We encourage applications from candidates who bring a wide range of lived experience including involvement with the justice system. If this position has “critical or security-sensitive” responsibilities and/or driving duties and you are selected as a finalist, your initial job offer will be contingent upon the results of a job-related pre-employment check (such as a background check, motor vehicle history check, sexual misconduct reference check, etc.). Background check results do not automatically disqualify a candidate. Take a look at our Background Checks website including the for candidates section for more details. If you have questions or concerns about the pre-employment check, please contact OSU’s Employee and Labor Relations team at employee.relations@oregonstate.edu ."
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/MB-230-Teaching-Student-Asst-Fall-2026_JR0000894": {
      "title": "MB 230 Teaching Student Asst Fall 2026",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-26\nPosted: Posted 10 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nDepartment: Microbiology Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents: A Cover Letter For additional information please contact: Linda Bruslind at Linda.Bruslind@oregonstate.edu We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status.   This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a Criminal History Check and be determined to be position qualified as per OSU Standard 576-055-0000 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in Youth Programs may have additional Criminal History Checks every 2 years.   OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire.   Note: All job offers are contingent upon Human Resources final approval Job Description: This recruitment will be used to fill three (3) part-time (a maximum of 24 hours per week) Teaching Student Assistant positions for the Department of Microbiology at Oregon State University (OSU). This position is for undergraduate teaching assistants for MB 230: Introductory Microbiology. https://microbiology.oregonstate.edu/ Position Duties: TAs will be doing set-up and clean-up of lab, supervising students during lab, checking off in-class tasks, and helping with grading of class assignments, after FERPA training. Transferable Skill Development OSU is committed to ensuring students are prepared for success after graduation through intentional skill development as student employees. The competencies students develop while working at OSU are defined by the National Association of Colleges and Employers (NACE) as the essential skills employers seek in their hires. In this position, students learn and build vital career-readiness skills in the primary areas below:   Communication, Teamwork, Leadership, Professionalism Qualifications: Full Employment Eligibility Requirements can be found here:   https://hr.oregonstate.edu/student-employment-manual/500-employment-eligibility-requirements Must be academically enrolled in a high school, community college, or university and pursuing a program or course of study Must meet Academic Standing Requirements; students on academic suspension are not eligible for employment Must meet the applicable minimal enrollment standard High School student: Regularly enrolled in a high school or participating in a home-schooling program Undergraduate and post-baccalaureate student: 6 credit hours per term Undergraduate international student: 12 credit hours per term* Graduate student officially admitted to Graduate School: 5 credit hours per term Graduate international student officially admitted to Graduate School: 9 credit hours per term* *International students may be allowed to carry fewer hours than specified above and still be considered “full-time” by the United States Citizenship and Immigration Services ( USCIS ). A reduced course load is approved by the   Office of International Services   ( OIS ), and must be provided to the Student Employment Center. Required Qualifications: Must have completed MB 230 or MB 303 with a C- or higher. Additional Required Qualifications: This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a Criminal History Check and be determined to be position qualified as per OSU Standard 576-055-0000 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in Youth Programs may have additional Criminal History Checks every 2 years. Preferred Qualifications: Completed MB 230 or MB 303 with a B- or higher. MB or BHS major. Current enrollment in MB 406. Working Conditions/Schedule: Teaching laboratory environment Salary Range: Anticipated Start Date: 2026-09-16 Anticipated End Date:"
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Newport-Campus/Motorboat-Operator--Training-Course-Instructor-Pool--Short-Term----2026-2027_JR0000813": {
      "title": "Motorboat Operator -Training Course Instructor Pool (Short Term) - 2026/2027",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-26\nPosted: Posted 10 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nDepartment: Marine Operations Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents:   1) A resume/CV; and   2) A cover letter indicating how your qualifications and experience have prepared you for this position. Starting salary will be commensurate with skills, education, and experience. For additional information please contact: Dan Battaglia at Daniel.Battaglia@oregonstate.edu Job Summary: Oregon State University’s Marine Operations unit is seeking hourly, short-term Motorboat Operator Training Course Instructors. These are full-time (1.00 FTE), 12-month positions. These positions are expected to last up to one year from appointment date. These positions are located in Newport, Oregon. Under the supervision of the Boating Safety Officer (BSO), the Motorboat Operator Training Instructor contributes to the delivery and coordination of OSU’s Motorboat Operator Training Course (MOTC)—a required safety certification for faculty, staff, students, and research collaborators who operate small boats in support of education and research. This intermittent position is part of a qualified instructional pool that helps ensure training compliance and student-to-instructor ratio requirements are met during MOTC sessions. The Motorboat Operator Training Instructor plays a critical role in implementing hands-on, competency-based learning in classroom, field, and on-water settings. Working independently and in collaboration with the Small Boat Program (SBP) team, this position helps ensure the successful delivery, review, and improvement of watercraft safety instruction across diverse instructional sites and audiences. As one of only three Land, Sea, Space and Sun Grant universities in the nation, OSU serves Oregon and the world through our campuses in Corvallis and Bend, our marine research center in Newport, field station in Port Orford, and our award-winning E-campus. With over $400 million in competitive research awards per year, OSU continues to lead the way with practical, impact-driven research that improves lives, protects natural resources, and generates economic growth to transform our future. This posting will be used to fill multiple openings. Key Responsibilities: Deliver established curriculum for the Motorboat Operator Training Course in classroom, dockside, and on-water environments. Tailor instructional approaches to meet the needs of adult learners and OSU-affiliated research personnel. Help create and revise teaching aids, checklists, and supplementary learning materials. Collaborate with the Boating Safety Officer and other instructors to ensure consistency and alignment with university safety policies. Assist in maintaining accurate instructional logs and compliance records for audit purposes. Qualifications: Required Qualifications: Completion of the Motorboat Operator Instructor Training Course (MOITC) or equivalent. Documented experience participating in the instruction of at least one MOTC or equivalent course within the past 3 years, and one within the past 5 years. Demonstrated skill in teaching, training, or instructional delivery for adult learners. Demonstrated experience operating small boats in a range of environments. Strong interpersonal and communication skills, with the ability to work effectively with a diverse range of learners and colleagues. Familiarity with the maintenance and safe operation of small boats and equipment (e.g., outboard motors, trailers). Competence in using computers for communication, recordkeeping, and data entry. Ability to foster an inclusive learning environment and embrace a diverse set of ideas, perspectives and backgrounds. This position requires driving a university vehicle or a personal vehicle on behalf of the university; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a motor vehicle history check, possess and maintain a current, valid driver's license in their state of residence, be determined to be position qualified and self-report convictions as per University Policy 05-030. Preferred Qualifications: Certification as a “Lead Instructor” or “Course Administrator” from the Scientific Boating Safety Association (SBSA) or the U.S. Department of the Interior Watercraft Safety Program. Authorization to teach advanced MOTC modules (e.g., Open Water, River, or Non-Motorized Modules). Experience coordinating instructional activities with multiple partners or stakeholders. Demonstrated commitment to inclusive education and safety practices. Experience assisting with program evaluation, training material development, or peer instructional review. Working Conditions/Schedule: This is an on call, as needed position. Working dates and hours will be arranged in advance. Regular outdoor work in a variety of field settings including rivers, bays, and offshore marine environments. Exposure to inclement weather and cold-water conditions. Frequent lifting, loading, and transport of equipment weighing up to 50 lbs. Physical ability to assist in rescue simulations and extraction scenarios. Occasional weekend work and overnight travel may be required. Must be eligible to drive OSU Motor Pool vehicles, including full-size pickups with trailers. Salary Range: Salary is commensurate with skills, education and experience. Anticipated Start Date: 2026-08-21 Anticipated End Date: 2027-08-20 We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status. OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire. OSU is a fair chance employer committed to inclusive hiring. We encourage applications from candidates who bring a wide range of lived experience including involvement with the justice system. If this position has “critical or security-sensitive” responsibilities and/or driving duties and you are selected as a finalist, your initial job offer will be contingent upon the results of a job-related pre-employment check (such as a background check, motor vehicle history check, sexual misconduct reference check, etc.). Background check results do not automatically disqualify a candidate. Take a look at our Background Checks website including the for candidates section for more details. If you have questions or concerns about the pre-employment check, please contact OSU’s Employee and Labor Relations team at employee.relations@oregonstate.edu ."
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/Education-Student-Teaching-Assistant_JR0000848": {
      "title": "Education Student Teaching Assistant",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-25\nPosted: Posted 11 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nDepartment: College of Education - DEPR Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents: ●        A Resume ●        A Cover Letter For additional information please contact: Karen Thompson at karen.thompson@oregonstate.edu We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status. This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a Criminal History Check and be determined to be position qualified as per OSU Standard 576-055-0000 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in Youth Programs may have additional Criminal History Checks every 2 years. OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire.   Note: All job offers are contingent upon Human Resources final approval Job Description: This recruitment will be used to fill multiple (a maximum of 10 hours per week per course assigned) Education Teaching Assistant for the Education & Teaching Programs within the College of Education at Oregon State University (OSU). This position provides excellent student and customer service support for the staff, faculty, visitors, students, and administration of the College of Education while working in a busy office with frequent distractions and interruptions. The College of Education contributes to major national dialogues concerning research and the promotion of social justice in (1) the success of English language learners of all ages; (2) developmentally appropriate pedagogy for all ages; (3) learning in science, technology, engineering, and mathematics inside and outside of school; (4) the preparation of mental health and school counselors; (5) adult and higher education leadership. We develop change agents in the form of formal and informal educators, counselors, and leaders. These change agents identify issues and act towards social justice and the full development of human potential and society. As a part of the Division of Arts and Sciences, we support the division’s campus-wide role in providing a well-rounded, compelling educational experience. Our work seeks to enhance and extend the historic democratic mission of public education: to prepare all citizens, across their lifetime for full participation in a culturally diverse and technical world. This position, as with all positions within the College of Education, is directly responsible for advancing the mission of the College Position Duties Successful candidate will meet with instructor as needed and assist with activities such as: Reviewing and editing content on Canvas course site Grading and data entry Transcribing audio recordings of lectures Designing or editing related assignments and conducting help sessions Work one-on-one with students seeking academic support. Identify and help process any academic integrity violations that may occur in the course. General office work, reception, or other projects (as needed) Transferable Skill Development OSU is committed to ensuring students are prepared for success after graduation through intentional skill development as student employees. The competencies students develop while working at OSU are defined by the National Association of Colleges and Employers (NACE) as the essential skills employers seek in their hires. In this position, students learn and build vital career-readiness skills in the primary areas below:   Communication, Critical Thinking, Teamwork, Professionalism Qualifications: Full Employment Eligibility Requirements can be found here:   https://hr.oregonstate.edu/student-employment-manual/500-employment-eligibility-requirements Must be academically enrolled in a high school, community college, or university and pursuing a program or course of study Must meet Academic Standing Requirements; students on academic suspension are not eligible for employment Must meet the applicable minimal enrollment standard High School student: Regularly enrolled in a high school or participating in a home-schooling program Undergraduate and post-baccalaureate student: 6 credit hours per term Undergraduate international student: 12 credit hours per term* Graduate student officially admitted to Graduate School: 5 credit hours per term Graduate international student officially admitted to Graduate School: 9 credit hours per term* *International students may be allowed to carry fewer hours than specified above and still be considered “full-time” by the United States Citizenship and Immigration Services ( USCIS ). A reduced course load is approved by the   Office of International Services   ( OIS ), and must be provided to the Student Employment Center. Required Must have successfully passed ED 216 and ED 219 and be academically enrolled. Experience with data entry and/or database applications. Experience organizing paper and digital files. Demonstrated successful work in a team environment. Demonstrated self-motivation in project management. Demonstrated organizational skills. Demonstrated ability and willingness to do detailed repetitive tasks with consistent high quality. Excellent verbal and written communication. Attention to detail. Ability to use Microsoft Office suite. Must meet Academic Standing Requirements; students on academic suspension are not eligible or employment This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a Criminal History Check and be determined to be position qualified as per OSU Standard 576-055-0000 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in Youth Programs may have additional Criminal History Checks every 2 years. Preferred Interest in learning about the College of Education and representing the College Demonstrated positive and proactive attitude Able to lift/carry 20 lbs Bilingual/Multilingual or experience with bilingual/multilingual students, employees, or co-workers Demonstrated commitment to enhancing diversity and social justice Pursuing a program or course of study in one of the following areas: EDU B.S. – College of Education “Double Degree” student EDU M.S. – Education Math or Science Education option Workstudy Eligible Working Conditions/Schedule: Typical classroom and campus environment with in-person work and occasional remote work as needed. Up to 10 hours per week per course. Actual work hours will be determined by the supervisor based on scheduling needs. Salary Range: Anticipated Start Date: 2026-09-01 Anticipated End Date:"
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/Student-Teaching-Laboratory-Assistant_JR0000909": {
      "title": "Student Teaching Laboratory Assistant",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-25\nPosted: Posted 11 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nDepartment: Special Instructions to Applicants: Job Description: The Student Teaching Lab assistant provides continuous daily cleaning of the Carlson College of Veterinary Medicine (CCVM) Student Teaching Lab facilities, individual patient kennels, maintenance of equipment, laundry, and animal transportation. Provide care, restraint and handling of small animals (cats and dogs) within a veterinary teaching college. Qualifications: Full Employment Eligibility Requirements can be found here:   https://hr.oregonstate.edu/student-employment-manual/500-employment-eligibility-requirements   Must be academically enrolled in a high school, community college, or university and pursuing a program or course of study Must meet Academic Standing Requirements; students on academic suspension are not eligible for employment Must meet the applicable minimal enrollment standard High School student: Regularly enrolled in a high school or participating in a home-schooling program Undergraduate and post-baccalaureate student: 6 credit hours per term Undergraduate international student: 12 credit hours per term* Graduate student officially admitted to Graduate School: 5 credit hours per term Graduate international student officially admitted to Graduate School: 9 credit hours per term* *International students may be allowed to carry fewer hours than specified above and still be considered “full-time” by the United States Citizenship and Immigration Services ( USCIS ). A reduced course load is approved by the   Office of International Services   ( OIS ), and must be provided to the Student Employment Center. Working Conditions/Schedule: Must be available to work during the hours of 7:30am-11:30am, Tuesday- Friday. If funding permits, employment may be extended through the upcoming school year. Salary Range: Anticipated Start Date: 2026-09-28 Anticipated End Date:"
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Milton-Freewater/Assistant-Professor--Practice---Horticulture_JR0000861-1": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor (Practice): Horticulture",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-25\nPosted: Posted 11 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nDepartment: Division of Extension and Engagement and the Extension Agriculture & Natural Resources Program Special Instructions to Applicants: To ensure full consideration, applications must be received by September 16, 2026 . Applications will continue to be accepted after the full consideration date, until a sufficient applicant pool has been achieved or the position is filled. The closing date is subject to change without notice to applicants.  When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents: 1) Curriculum Vitae: Finalists will be required to give an open presentation which may include the distribution of their redacted curriculum vitae and a short biography. 2)  Cover letter that addresses each required qualification. Starting salary will be commensurate with skills, education, and experience. For additional information please contact:   Vaughn Walton vaughn.walton@oregonstate.edu Job Summary: The Division of Extension and Engagement invites applications for a full-time (1.00 FTE), 12-month, fixed-term Assistant Professor of Practice position with the Extension Agriculture and Natural Resources Program and serves Umatilla County, Oregon. Reappointment is at the discretion of the supervisory team which includes the regional director, program leader, and department head. This position is located in Milton-Freewater, Oregon. The mission of the OSU Division of Extension and Engagement (division) is to work with communities throughout Oregon to support lifelong learning and co-create innovative solutions to address local challenges.  Together, we envision a future where Oregon’s people, communities and ecosystems prosper. This Assistant Professor (Practice) position is with the division’s Extension Agriculture and Natural Resources program and will have the academic home of the Department of Horticulture in the OSU College of Agricultural Sciences .  This academic faculty member will have principal responsibility for needs assessment; for development, delivery, and evaluation of Extension non-credit program and educational material; and for conducting applied research to support Extension activities. This Assistant Professor (Practice) provides leadership in the development, implementation, synthesis, and evaluation of Extension and applied research in commercial horticulture in Umatilla County . Specific goals are to work with producers (largely wine grape, tree fruit, and annual crop producers), processors, and packinghouses. This position requires working cooperatively with landowners, state and local agencies, organizations, and the general public to maintain agricultural management systems that meet producers’ goals and objectives while complementing the goals of the local communities. Key Responsibilities: Utilize Oregon State University’s Quality Teaching Framework – which emphasizes inclusivity (audience consideration), evidence-based teaching practices (planning, engagement, teaching, assessment), and learner mentorship (reflection and learner support) – to deliver high-quality,  non-credit educational experiences for Extension audiences.  Conduct on-going needs-assessment activities to determine the educational needs specific to this position’s broad and diverse audiences. Develop networks of formal and informal interest groups in subject matter areas where appropriate. Utilize partnerships with diverse interest groups to provide advisory support and to enhance program quality and delivery. This will likely include appropriate commodities and/or producer organizations. Determine Extension program needs and priorities through use of local advisory groups, commodity commissions and other stakeholder groups, and meetings with applicable industry leaders. Evaluate effectiveness and impact of Extension non-credit education programs and refine to meet changing needs. Conduct applied research activities that support Extension programming as well as demonstration trials to develop or confirm management practices in areas of specific need and clientele interest. Develop annual plans of work that address educational needs as well as evaluate the quality and impact of Extension non-credit educational and applied research programs materials and digital communications. Assigned duties for this position are expected to result in scholarly outcomes. Qualifications: Required Qualifications: Master of Science degree in horticulture, agronomy, crop science, soil science, or another discipline that addresses the duties of the position. Degree must be completed by application date. Demonstrated experience providing adult education. Demonstrated evidence of teaching, specifically non-credit education, in commercial horticulture. Experience conducting applied research related to the assignments of this position. Must have or be able to obtain an Oregon Department of Agriculture Pesticide Consultant License within six months of employment and maintain it throughout employment in this position. A commitment to collaborative and respectful learning and working environments that are safe, inclusive, and welcoming.   Ability to work as a team member with other professionals and volunteers. Ability to incorporate multiple cultural perspectives in educational design and delivery. Demonstrated ability to cultivate and maintain collaborations that include individuals with a diversity of expertise, skills, and backgrounds. Strong verbal and written communication skills, with ability to successfully engage with broad and diverse audiences in a culturally responsible manner. General knowledge and ability to use computers, including proficiency with information technology, professional office software and collaborative work platforms. Ability to learn and adapt to using multiple forms of digital communications tools and systems, including websites, email newsletters, social media, and other tools in a changing technological environment.  Ability to work independently with minimal supervision and evidence of good time management skills.​ This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a criminal history check and be determined to be position qualified as per University Standard: 05-010 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in youth programs may have additional criminal history checks every 24 months. This position requires driving a university vehicle or a personal vehicle on behalf of the university; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a motor vehicle history check, possess and maintain a current, valid driver's license in their state of residence, be determined to be position qualified and self-report convictions as per University Policy 05-030. Preferred Qualifications: Experience with Extension or other informal education delivery methods. Experience or training in modern precision agriculture tools and management techniques. Pest management training or experience and familiarity with pesticide registration procedures. Experience applying agricultural pesticides and/or fertilizers. Experience with digital engagement strategies, including use of online content, multimedia, email campaigns, customer relationship management systems or marketing methods. Experience working with program advisory committees. Ability to conduct data analysis with statistical software (e.g., SAS, R, Statistix). Ability to secure external funding to support applied research and educational programs. Working Conditions/Schedule: • Extension teaching and applied research activities may take place outdoors and other settings such as field conditions, including inclement weather. • Flexibility to perform work in a variety of office and work settings. Mornings, evenings and weekends may be required based on responsibilities, events and other non-credit educational programming needs. • The position requires travel/driving to deliver non-credit educational programming and services throughout the assigned county, as necessary. Salary Range: $60,996 - $78,000 Anticipated Start Date: 2026-10-05 Anticipated End Date: 2027-06-30 We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status. OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire. OSU is a fair chance employer committed to inclusive hiring. We encourage applications from candidates who bring a wide range of lived experience including involvement with the justice system. If this position has “critical or security-sensitive” responsibilities and/or driving duties and you are selected as a finalist, your initial job offer will be contingent upon the results of a job-related pre-employment check (such as a background check, motor vehicle history check, sexual misconduct reference check, etc.). Background check results do not automatically disqualify a candidate. Take a look at our Background Checks website including the for candidates section for more details. If you have questions or concerns about the pre-employment check, please contact OSU’s Employee and Labor Relations team at employee.relations@oregonstate.edu ."
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/BA270-SCLM350-BA514-Student-Teaching-Assistant_JR0000887": {
      "title": "BA270/SCLM350/BA514 Student Teaching Assistant",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-25\nPosted: Posted 11 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nDepartment: College of Business – School of Management, Entrepreneurship, and Supply Chain Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents: ●        A Resume For additional information please contact: Gary Micheau at Gary.Micheau@oregonstate.edu We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status. This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a Criminal History Check and be determined to be position qualified as per OSU Standard 576-055-0000 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in Youth Programs may have additional Criminal History Checks every 2 years. OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire.   Note: All job offers are contingent upon Human Resources final approval Job Description: This recruitment will be used to fill 1 part-time (a maximum of 24 hours per week) student job for the College of Business at Oregon State University (OSU). Assists in content preparation, grading, and delivery of student assignments under the direction of faculty. Pay rate for this position is $16.55 per hour. Position Duties Produces class content and student assignment copies. Advises and assists students on class assignments. Grades assignments, enters grades into the LMS, and answers student grading inquiries. Transferable Skill Development OSU is committed to ensuring students are prepared for success after graduation through intentional skill development as student employees. The competencies students develop while working at OSU are defined by the National Association of Colleges and Employers (NACE) as the essential skills employers seek in their hires. In this position, students learn and build vital career-readiness skills in the primary areas below:   Communication, Critical Thinking, Professionalism Qualifications: Full Employment Eligibility Requirements can be found here:   https://hr.oregonstate.edu/student-employment-manual/500-employment-eligibility-requirements Must be academically enrolled in a high school, community college, or university and pursuing a program or course of study Must meet Academic Standing Requirements; students on academic suspension are not eligible for employment Must meet the applicable minimal enrollment standard High School student: Regularly enrolled in a high school or participating in a home-schooling program Undergraduate and post-baccalaureate student: 6 credit hours per term Undergraduate international student: 12 credit hours per term* Graduate student officially admitted to Graduate School: 5 credit hours per term Graduate international student officially admitted to Graduate School: 9 credit hours per term* *International students may be allowed to carry fewer hours than specified above and still be considered “full-time” by the United States Citizenship and Immigration Services ( USCIS ). A reduced course load is approved by the   Office of International Services   ( OIS ), and must be provided to the Student Employment Center. Required: Successfully completed BA270, BA357, and SCLM350. Working knowledge of  Canvas LMS. This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a Criminal History Check and be determined to be position qualified as per OSU Standard 576-055-0000 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in Youth Programs may have additional Criminal History Checks every 2 years. Preferred: Supply Chain Major. Working Conditions/Schedule: Working up to 5 hours per week Salary Range: Anticipated Start Date: 2026-09-14 Anticipated End Date:"
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/Podcast-Production-Student-Assistant_JR0000808": {
      "title": "Podcast Production Student Assistant",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-25\nPosted: Posted 11 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nDepartment: Forestry and Natural Resources Extension Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents: A Resume Supplemental Questions Supplemental Questions: 1. Why are you interested in working on a podcast? 2. How have your work and life experiences, so far, prepared you to work as a podcast production assistant? For additional information please contact: Lauren Grand at Lauren.Grand@oregonstate.edu We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status.   OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire.   Note: All job offers are contingent upon Human Resources final approval Job Description: This recruitment will be used to fill one (1) part-time (a maximum of 24 hours per week) Podcast Production Student Assistant for the Forestry and Natural Resources Extension at Oregon State University (OSU). The Forestry & Natural Resources Extension Podcast Team is seeking a part-time production assistant for our Podcast, In the Woods ( inthewoodspodcast.com ). “In the Woods” is a podcast series brought to you by the Oregon State University Forestry & Natural Resources Extension program. This podcast brings the forest to listeners by sharing the stories and interviews of forest scientists, land managers, and enthusiastic members of the public. Each month, it provides research- and science-based information on a new topic related to forests, offering insight into what we know and are still learning about forest science and management. The program assistant will help edit, post, and advertise the In the Woods podcast. This position is in the Forestry and Natural Resources Extension Program in Oregon State University’s College of Forestry. For more information on the OSU Forestry and Natural Resources Extension program, visit: https://www.forestry.oregonstate.edu/extension Position Duties: Audio recording and editing Posting podcasts to various RSS feeds and YouTube. Editing closed captions.   Creating simple graphics for social media. OSU Extension and Blog website editing.   Transferable Skill Development OSU is committed to ensuring students are prepared for success after graduation through intentional skill development as student employees. The competencies students develop while working at OSU are defined by the National Association of Colleges and Employers (NACE) as the essential skills employers seek in their hires. In this position, students learn and build vital career-readiness skills in the primary areas below:   Communication, Technology, Professionalism, Career & Self Development Qualifications: Full Employment Eligibility Requirements can be found here:   https://hr.oregonstate.edu/student-employment-manual/500-employment-eligibility-requirements Must be academically enrolled in a high school, community college, or university and pursuing a program or course of study Must meet Academic Standing Requirements; students on academic suspension are not eligible for employment Must meet the applicable minimal enrollment standard High School student: Regularly enrolled in a high school or participating in a home-schooling program Undergraduate and post-baccalaureate student: 6 credit hours per term Undergraduate international student: 12 credit hours per term* Graduate student officially admitted to Graduate School: 5 credit hours per term Graduate international student officially admitted to Graduate School: 9 credit hours per term* *International students may be allowed to carry fewer hours than specified above and still be considered “full-time” by the United States Citizenship and Immigration Services ( USCIS ). A reduced course load is approved by the   Office of International Services   ( OIS ), and must be provided to the Student Employment Center. Required Qualifications: Demonstrated experience editing audio and closed captions Demonstrated experience editing graphics Demonstrated experience with podcasts Preferred Qualifications: Experience with OSU Branding Experience using Dscript audio editing Experience with YouTube and advertising on social media. Working Conditions/Schedule: • This will be a remote work position. • This position can begin as early as October 1, 2026. • Students will work about 5 hours per week, up to 20 hours per month. Salary Range: Anticipated Start Date: 2026-10-01 Anticipated End Date:"
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Bend/Campus-Safety-Officer_JR0000815": {
      "title": "Campus Safety Officer",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-25\nPosted: Posted 11 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nDepartment: Department of Public Safety - Cascades Special Instructions to Applicants: To ensure full consideration, applications must be received by 9/8/2026. Applications will continue to be accepted after the full consideration date, until a sufficient applicant pool has been achieved or the position is filled. The closing date is subject to change without notice to applicants. When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents:   1) A resume/CV; and   2) A cover letter indicating how your qualifications and experience have prepared you for this position. Starting salary will be commensurate with skills, education, and experience. For additional information please contact: Johana Hernandez at johana.hernandez@osucascades.edu Job Summary: Oregon State University- Cascades is seeking a Campus Safety Officer. This is a full time (1.00 FTE), 12-month, professional faculty position. This position is located in Bend, Oregon. The mission of OSU-Cascades Campus Safety is to: Ensure the safety and security of all who come to OSU-Cascades to learn, work, or visit. Protect the property of OSU-Cascades and the personal property of students, faculty, staff, and visitors on campus. Promote student success by maintaining a welcoming environment conducive to the educational process. The successful candidate will have the opportunity to be a part of a small team of Campus Safety professionals who promote the safety and well-being of all individuals on the OSU-Cascades campus. The Campus Safety Officer conducts roving security patrols, provides routine assistance to the campus community, and serves in crisis situations as initial first responder and liaison with emergency services. OSU-Cascades Campus Safety Officer may be designated special campus security officers, as defined in ORS352.118, through a separate written designation from the University. Such written designation as special campus security officer would describe the additional authority being granted and any limits that apply. OSU-Cascades, located in Bend, Oregon, is a branch campus of Oregon State University. The campus currently enrolls more than 1,200 students in undergraduate and graduate programs. Unlike the main campus in Corvallis, where OSU operations its own police department, the Cascades campus uses a Campus Safety model focused on crisis response, relying on Bend Police Department for law enforcement services. Key Responsibilities: Patrol: Provide vehicular, bicycle, and foot patrol of University grounds in an effort to protect life and property and promote safe campus operations. Ensure all buildings are secured as required and assist with parking enforcement. Perform regularly scheduled fire drills and documents results. Performs regularly scheduled inspections of patrol vehicle and safety equipment (AED & Fire Extinguishers) and documents results. Promote safety and security at special events, including but not limited to providing traffic control, crowd control and security screening. Provide information and general assistance to the campus community and the general public. Provide walking safety escort for members of the campus community. In a courteous and professional manner, determine authorization of persons in buildings; document and report suspicious persons or vehicles, criminal activity, hazards, fire, and property damage and/or loss. Issue Exclusion (Trespass) Notices as required to protect people and property on campus. Respond to calls for emergency medical aid and coordinate with emergency services to expedite their response. Observe and report potentially hazardous physical conditions; may remedy condition when appropriate (e.g., fallen limbs, debris, damage to streets or sidewalks, etc.); respond to situations representing a threat to persons or property (e.g., break-ins, fights, illicit drug activity, etc.); may intervene to prevent injury, and call for police assistance as necessary; secure scene and may obtain information from involved parties to assist local law enforcement officials. Perform courtesy unlocks of buildings and assist motorists with jump start or contacting roadside assistance as appropriate. Office Duties: Maintain the shift log for on-duty activities. Compose and submit reports for on-duty activities, noting date, time, location and circumstances of all incidents. Report unusual conditions or occurrences to supervisor. Provide information or testimony to law enforcement agencies or courts as required. Observe and maintain confidentiality regarding all persons and incidents, as required to protect the rights of individuals and the integrity of investigations in accordance with law, rule, policy and procedure. Manage the University lost and found property program while on duty. Complete assigned departmental projects which could include the development of campus or officer educational training programs, policy or Standard Operating Procedure (SOP) development, uniform or equipment acquisition research, scheduling of training, or other supervisor assigned projects Enforce University Rules and Regulations: Enforce guidelines and limitations established by management including but not limited to: Skateboard Policy, Speech Policy, Alcoholic Beverage Policy, parking enforcement and Bicycle Regulations. Refer violators to the student conduct process, University management or local law enforcement as necessary and appropriate to the person’s status. Assist Resident Assistants and other University Residential Education and Housing professional staff in the performance of their duties; to include the enforcement of University Residence Hall regulations and policies on Alcohol and Marijuana possession and confiscation. Assist Facilities Services: Assist, as needed, with snow removal, de-icing, office moves and other services as required. Issue identification cards to staff, faculty, students, and contractors; issue physical keys for building access to students, employees, and contractors; input electronic access permissions and attributes for system users; set building electronic door locks. Outreach & Education: Assist in the development and delivery of campus crime prevention, medical (1st Aid/CPR/AED), Resident Assistant and emergency management training for the University. Other Duties as Assigned: Perform other duties as assigned by supervisor, as related to position. Qualifications: Required Qualifications Be at least twenty-one years of age; Possess and maintain a current, valid Oregon driver’s license within 30 days of appointment to the position; Possess and maintain first aid,   AED   and   CPR   certification, or obtain within 30 days of appointment to the position; Possess and maintain certification for Law Enforcement Data Systems ( LEDS ), or obtain within 90 days of appointment to the position; Possess a high school diploma or equivalent; Must successfully pass a comprehensive background assessment that includes: criminal history check; driving record review; medical examination; psychological evaluation; drug screening; and an inclusive appraisal of work history. Understand and demonstrate knowledge of   OSU - DPS   administrative policies, procedures, rules and guidelines within 6-month trial service period; Computer/software literacy; training in or experience with e-mail, Internet, word processing and spreadsheets, preferable with MS Office Suite products. One year of full-time (or full-time equivalent) experience in a security, military or similar position in which contemporary knowledge and techniques in security and property protection were applied. Demonstrated ability to promote and enhance inclusive excellence through equitable and respectful engagement with individuals from diverse backgrounds in a campus safety or public service setting, including during patrols, crisis response, and community outreach. This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a criminal history check and be determined to be position qualified as per University Standard: 05-010 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in youth programs may have additional criminal history checks every 24 months.  This position requires driving a university vehicle or a personal vehicle on behalf of the university; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a motor vehicle history check, possess and maintain a current, valid driver’s license in their state of residence, be determined to be position qualified and self-report convictions as per University Policy 05-030.   Preferred Qualifications Possess and maintain OC Spray, and Handcuffing certification, or obtain within 30 days of appointment to the position. 1 year of prior experience in campus public safety, security, law enforcement, or military police with general patrol functions such as: emergency response, incident report writing, incident investigation, contact with violators, enforcement of rules, policies or laws. Working Conditions/Schedule: Working Conditions/Schedule: Position hiring for is expected to work midnight/third shift. Working conditions include working outside in various weather conditions to perform vehicle patrol, foot patrol, and bicycle patrol. Must be available to work all shifts: day, swing, and night. Working Schedule: 4/10-hour days, or 5/8-hour days plus any emergency and on-call responses. Shift and/or work assignment may be changed based on operational needs. Campus Safety is a 7-day/week operation; as a result, weekends and holiday work may be required. Employee carries a cell phone during all working hours. Work in an all-hazards environment that includes, but is not limited to, loud noises, bright lights, smoke, noxious odors, heights, confined spaces, hazardous materials such as chemicals and body fluids, hostile confrontations, physical arrests of persons, serious injuries or death, and the keeping and maintaining of confidential information. Salary Range: $27.32 - $37.41 per hour Anticipated Start Date: 2026-10-05 Anticipated End Date: We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status. OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire. OSU is a fair chance employer committed to inclusive hiring. We encourage applications from candidates who bring a wide range of lived experience including involvement with the justice system. If this position has “critical or security-sensitive” responsibilities and/or driving duties and you are selected as a finalist, your initial job offer will be contingent upon the results of a job-related pre-employment check (such as a background check, motor vehicle history check, sexual misconduct reference check, etc.). Background check results do not automatically disqualify a candidate. Take a look at our Background Checks website including the for candidates section for more details. If you have questions or concerns about the pre-employment check, please contact OSU’s Employee and Labor Relations team at employee.relations@oregonstate.edu ."
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/Editorial-and-Content-Student-Intern_JR0000656": {
      "title": "Editorial and Content Student Intern",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-25\nPosted: Posted 11 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nDepartment: College of Science Dean's Office Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents: A Resume A Cover Letter Writing Sample: Please submit one writing sample that demonstrates your ability to communicate clearly and engage a reader. This may be a class assignment, essay, article, blog post, news or feature story, or other piece of writing. Professional or published work is not required, and the sample does not need to be related to science or marketing. Suggested length: 500–1,500 words For additional information please contact: Hannah Ashton at ashtonh@oregonstate.ed u We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status.   OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire.   Note: All job offers are contingent upon Human Resources final approval   Job Description: This recruitment will be used to fill one part-time (a maximum of 20 hours per week) Student Information Technology position for a current Oregon State University student who will work in the College of Science (COS) Dean's Office at OSU. The College of Science is looking for a talented and creative journalism/digital media current OSU student who is highly motivated, enthusiastic and skilled in the art of storytelling and crafting thought-provoking communications. In this position, you will produce content for print, digital and social media channels and gain hands-on experience.   You will write about a wide range of topics such as science student activities, faculty and student profiles, academic programs, College news, groundbreaking research (in biology, zoology, biochemistry, microbiology, mathematics, physics and chemistry), events, scholarships and anything that will engage readers while raising the visibility and impact of science.   The Editorial and Content Intern will work in our cool marketing space in Kidder Hall and learn from our award-winning creative team of designers and writers in the dean's office.   We are looking for a currently enrolled OSU student who is self-motivated and takes initiative to craft story ideas. The anticipated hourly pay for this position is $18.00. Position Duties Write and research interesting stories for our website and newsletters. Identify photos and graphics to complement stories. Write copy and help with flyers for lectures and other events. Learn how to post content using Drupal, our content management system. Use and perfect your social media skills to help increase awareness among our key audiences. Proofread stories for grammatical errors and OSU/Associated Press Style. Adhere to OSU's brand, tone, voice and style as well as best practices. Consult with our team to assess copy needs, audiences and objectives. Transferable Skill Development OSU is committed to ensuring students are prepared for success after graduation through intentional skill development as student employees. The competencies students develop while working at OSU are defined by the National Association of Colleges and Employers (NACE) as the essential skills employers seek in their hires. In this position, students learn and build vital career-readiness skills in the primary areas below:   Communication, Critical Thinking, Technology, Career & Self Development Qualifications: Full Employment Eligibility Requirements can be found here: https://hr.oregonstate.edu/student-employment-manual/500-employment-eligibility-requirements Must be academically enrolled in a high school, community college, or university and pursuing a program or course of study Must meet Academic Standing Requirements; students on academic suspension are not eligible for employment Must meet the applicable minimal enrollment standard High School student: Regularly enrolled in a high school or participating in a home-schooling program Undergraduate and post-baccalaureate student: 6 credit hours per term Undergraduate international student: 12 credit hours per term* Graduate student officially admitted to Graduate School: 5 credit hours per term Graduate international student officially admitted to Graduate School: 9 credit hours per term* *International students may be allowed to carry fewer hours than specified above and still be considered “full-time” by the United States Citizenship and Immigration Services (USCIS). A reduced course load is approved by the Office of International Services (OIS) and must be provided to the Student Employment Center. Required Must be a currently enrolled OSU student. Able to manage your own projects. Learn team-building and collaboration skills. Participate in weekly creative team meetings as your schedule permits. Preferred Excellent writing and editing skills. Strong communication skills. Good problem-solving skills. A strong understanding of journalism and news writing. Strong understanding of the Internet and social media marketing best practices. Experience using multiple social media platforms. Detail oriented, consistently meet deadlines and produce high-quality work. Highly organized with the ability to multitask. Interest in supporting the ambassador program is preferred. Responsibilities may include greeting tour attendees, accompanying campus tours and, if interested, leading tours. Working Conditions/Schedule: Part time, on-campus Corvallis, 15-20 hours a week. Salary Range: Anticipated Start Date: 2026-09-23 Anticipated End Date:"
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/Project-Director--Short-Term-_JR0000618": {
      "title": "Project Director (Short-Term)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-25\nPosted: Posted 11 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nDepartment: College of Engineering Special Instructions to Applicants: To ensure full consideration, applications must be received by 9/9/2026. Applications will continue to be accepted after the full consideration date, until a sufficient applicant pool has been achieved or the position is filled. The closing date is subject to change without notice to applicants. When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents:   1) A Resume/CV; and   2) A Cover letter describing your experience, qualifications, and achievements, with specific examples of how they address the minimum qualifications and, if applicable, the preferred qualifications for this position.   Starting salary will be commensurate with skills, education, and experience.   For additional information please contact: Dong Lin Dong.Lin@oregonstate.edu Job Summary: The College of Engineering, School of Mechanical, Industrial, & Manufacturing Engineering is seeking a short-term, Project Director. This is a full-time (1.00 FTE), 12-month, position.  This position is expected to last approximately 12 months. The Project Director will manage daily operations to ensure that outcomes meet approved budget and milestone plan in support of the mission of the $5M contract from the Small Business Administration (SBA) for the project titled “Pacific Northwest Institute for Advanced Manufacturing (PACNW-IAM)” to strengthen the capabilities of small and medium manufacturers in Oregon and across the Pacific Northwest. Key Responsibilities: Manage daily operations. Schedule instructors and students into workshops. Coordinate intake of participating firms. Maintain the master workplan, milestone dashboard, and project document repository. Prepare quarterly reports on project milestone completion. Review monthly spending against the approved budget and milestone plan. Serve as the point of contact for site leads. Qualifications: Required Qualifications: Bachelor’s degree in Engineering, Business, or other related field. Minimum of 3 years’ experience managing one or more projects with a cumulative budget of over $1M and more than 10 project staff Demonstrated experience with financial management Proficiency with Microsoft Office productivity tools Proficiency with project management software Professional written and verbal communication ​   This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a criminal history check and be determined to be position qualified as per University Standard: 05-010 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in youth programs may have additional criminal history checks every 24 months. Preferred Qualifications: Project Management Professional certification Demonstrated experience managing federally funded projects Knowledge of the manufacturing sector Working Conditions/Schedule: Normal business hours. Option for hybrid or remote work with occasional in-person meetings in Corvallis required. Some travel to locations in Oregon, Washington, and Idaho may be required. Salary Range: $81,000 - $90,000 Anticipated Start Date: 2026-09-30 Anticipated End Date: 2027-09-30 We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status. OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire. OSU is a fair chance employer committed to inclusive hiring. We encourage applications from candidates who bring a wide range of lived experience including involvement with the justice system. If this position has “critical or security-sensitive” responsibilities and/or driving duties and you are selected as a finalist, your initial job offer will be contingent upon the results of a job-related pre-employment check (such as a background check, motor vehicle history check, sexual misconduct reference check, etc.). Background check results do not automatically disqualify a candidate. Take a look at our Background Checks website including the for candidates section for more details. If you have questions or concerns about the pre-employment check, please contact OSU’s Employee and Labor Relations team at employee.relations@oregonstate.edu ."
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/Advanced-Manufacturing-Training-Consultant--Short-Term-_JR0000619": {
      "title": "Advanced Manufacturing Training Consultant (Short-Term)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-25\nPosted: Posted 11 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nDepartment: College of Engineering Special Instructions to Applicants: To ensure full consideration, applications must be received by 9/9/2026. Applications will continue to be accepted after the full consideration date, until a sufficient applicant pool has been achieved or the position is filled. The closing date is subject to change without notice to applicants.   When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents:   1) A Resume/CV; and   2) A Cover letter indicating how your qualifications and experience have prepared you for this position.   3) A Statement of Teaching: please explain your technical expertise in one or more of the following topics: Machining and machining dynamics, machining dynamics, machining process optimization, and chatter/vibration analysis Additive manufacturing including material-specific process-parameter selection and support-structure design for printability and post-processing Metrology including GD&T (geometric dimensioning and tolerancing) and advanced dimensional and structural metrology Smart manufacturing including IIoT (Industrial Internet of Things) deployment and AI-assisted in-process quality inspection Manufacturing systems including workplace safety, lean manufacturing, and energy efficiency Federal contract readiness including capability statements, proposal pricing, capture, and supply-chain qualification 4) Please provide samples of relevant teaching materials, if available, including course syllabi, assessments, and evidence of teaching effectiveness such as student or peer evaluations. upload as a separate document.   Starting salary will be commensurate with skills, education, and experience.   For additional information please contact: Dong Lin Dong.Lin@oregonstate.edu   Job Summary: The College of Engineering, School of Mechanical, Industrial, & Manufacturing Engineering, is seeking four short-term Advanced Manufacturing Training Consultants . These are full-time (1.00 FTE), 12-month, positions.  These positions are expected to last approximately 12 months. The position supports the mission of a $5M contract from the Small Business Administration (SBA) for the project titled “Pacific Northwest Institute for Advanced Manufacturing (PACNW-IAM)” to strengthen the skills of small and medium manufacturers in Oregon and across the Pacific Northwest. As a subject‑matter expert, the position will provide technical consulting services to community colleges and manufacturing firms by developing and leading advanced manufacturing training workshops in Precision & Advanced Manufacturing Skills and Smart Manufacturing & Reshoring Readiness. Key Responsibilities: Engage partners to assess the state of workforce readiness in manufacturing firms and manufacturing education in community colleges in relation to Precision & Advanced Manufacturing Skills and Smart Manufacturing & Reshoring. Provide recommendations and technical advice to community colleges and manufacturing firms on necessary training in advanced manufacturing to close knowledge, skills, and capabilities gaps in their students and workforce, respectively. Develop and lead workshops in Precision & Advanced Manufacturing Skills or Smart Manufacturing & Reshoring Readiness. Respond to emails from partners and workshop participants regarding workshop logistics and content. Contribute to the evaluation of program implementation and training outcomes. Attend and participate in group meetings as directed. Qualifications: Required Qualifications: MS/MEng in industrial engineering, manufacturing engineering, or mechanical engineering by the start date of employment OR 10 or more years of professional experience in industrial engineering, manufacturing engineering, or mechanical engineering Demonstrated subject matter expertise in one or more of the following areas: Machining and machining dynamics, machining dynamics, machining process optimization, and chatter/vibration analysis Additive manufacturing including material-specific process-parameter selection and support-structure design for printability and post-processing Metrology including GD&T (geometric dimensioning and tolerancing) and advanced dimensional and structural metrology Smart manufacturing including IIoT (Industrial Internet of Things) deployment and AI-assisted in-process quality inspection Manufacturing systems including workplace safety, lean manufacturing, and energy efficiency Federal contract readiness including capability statements, proposal pricing, capture, and supply-chain qualification Demonstrated experience teaching at the tertiary education level or executive/corporate education Demonstrated experience working with clients to develop and execute proposals that meet customer needs and enable successful outcomes Ability to travel up to 50% to partner sites in Oregon for project-related duties Professional verbal and written communication skills This position requires driving a university vehicle or a personal vehicle on behalf of the university; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a motor vehicle history check, possess and maintain a current, valid driver's license in their state of residence, be determined to be position qualified and self-report convictions as per University Policy 05-030. Preferred Qualifications: Industry experience in aerospace, automotive, metal parts, or industrial automation industry sectors Knowledge of commercial advanced manufacturing products and applications Experience working with third-party suppliers to provide additional products/services Experience in a client-facing role, either engineering or executing technical projects Working Conditions/Schedule: Normal business hours. Consulting and workshops will take place in-person and may take place in Corvallis or at partner locations during normal business hours. As such, occasional travel within Oregon may be required. Option for remote or hybrid work otherwise. Occasional evening or weekend training sessions may be required. Occasional in-person meetings in Corvallis with the project team. Salary Range: $78,731-$103,554 Anticipated Start Date: 2026-09-30 Anticipated End Date: 2027-09-30 We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status. OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire. OSU is a fair chance employer committed to inclusive hiring. We encourage applications from candidates who bring a wide range of lived experience including involvement with the justice system. If this position has “critical or security-sensitive” responsibilities and/or driving duties and you are selected as a finalist, your initial job offer will be contingent upon the results of a job-related pre-employment check (such as a background check, motor vehicle history check, sexual misconduct reference check, etc.). Background check results do not automatically disqualify a candidate. Take a look at our Background Checks website including the for candidates section for more details. If you have questions or concerns about the pre-employment check, please contact OSU’s Employee and Labor Relations team at employee.relations@oregonstate.edu ."
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/Instructor-Pool---2026-2027--Graphic-Design_JR0000801": {
      "title": "Instructor Pool - 2026/2027: Graphic Design",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-25\nPosted: Posted 11 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nDepartment: College of Liberal Arts Special Instructions to Applicants: Applications will be reviewed and considered as opportunities arise throughout the 2026/2027 academic year. When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents:   1) A resume/CV; and   2) A cover letter indicating how your qualifications and experience have prepared you for this position. Starting salary will be commensurate with skills, education, and experience.   For additional information please contact: Coleen Keedah Coleen.keedah@oregonstate.edu 541-737-5003 Job Summary: The School of Visual, Performing and Design Arts (SVPDA), Graphic Design program, invites applications for one or more fixed-term, non-tenure-track full/part-time Instructor positions to teach on a term-by-term basis for the 2026/2027 academic year. Some of these appointments may be reviewed for renewal or transition to an instructional position on an annual basis at the discretion of the Director. The primary mission of the Graphic Design program at OSU is undergraduate education. Students may seek a Bachelor of Fine Arts degree in graphic design. Key Responsibilities: Teach undergraduate courses on-campus or through Ecampus. Possible courses include; typography, design tools & techniques, interaction design, design thinking, information design, branding systems, package design, professional development, design theory, graphic design history, etc. Qualifications: Required Qualifications: Bachelor’s degree with significant industry experience. Record of and/or demonstrated interest in engaging, educating, and mentoring a diverse group of learners and enhancing inclusive excellence through teaching, outreach, or programming. Ability to work collegially with other faculty, staff, and students. Preferred Qualifications: Master’s degree Experience teaching at the college level. Evident commitment to educational equity. A demonstrable commitment to promoting and enhancing inclusive excellence. History of success in program/curriculum development. Working Conditions/Schedule: Work schedule may vary depending upon student needs and engagement in campus and/or program activities. Salary Range: Salary commensurate with skill, education and experience. Anticipated Start Date: 2026-09-16 Anticipated End Date: 2027-06-15 We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status. OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire. OSU is a fair chance employer committed to inclusive hiring. We encourage applications from candidates who bring a wide range of lived experience including involvement with the justice system. If this position has “critical or security-sensitive” responsibilities and/or driving duties and you are selected as a finalist, your initial job offer will be contingent upon the results of a job-related pre-employment check (such as a background check, motor vehicle history check, sexual misconduct reference check, etc.). Background check results do not automatically disqualify a candidate. Take a look at our Background Checks website including the for candidates section for more details. If you have questions or concerns about the pre-employment check, please contact OSU’s Employee and Labor Relations team at employee.relations@oregonstate.edu ."
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/Impact-Storytelling-Lead_JR0000576": {
      "title": "Impact Storytelling Lead",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-24\nPosted: Posted 12 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nDepartment: College of Liberal Arts Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents:   1) A Resume/CV; and   2) A Cover letter indicating how your qualifications and experience have prepared you for this position.   3) A Portfolio   Starting salary will be commensurate with skills, education, and experience. For additional information please contact: Tim Jensen tim.jensen@oregonstate.edu 541-737-1634 Job Summary: The College of Liberal Arts, School of Writing, Literature & Film, is seeking an Impact Storytelling Lead. This is a full-time (1.00 FTE), 12-month, professional faculty position. The OSU Story Lab advances research impact through compelling, richly told stories. We partner with researchers at OSU and beyond, as well as nonprofits, Tribal partners and government agencies, creating powerful communications that achieve outreach goals while training the next generation of science communicators. The Impact Storytelling Lead position collaborates with Story Lab leadership to ensure projects are optimized for impact. From client intake to publication and audience engagement, this position will help build a sustainable, mission-driven program. Key Responsibilities: Integrate impact considerations throughout the creative process, from project selection and story development through distribution and audience engagement. Develop systems, templates, and best practices that strengthen Story Lab's creative and operational processes. Provide editorial feedback throughout the storytelling process, with an emphasis on audience, impact, and narrative effectiveness. Assess projects for publication opportunities, audience engagement, and overall impact potential. Develop strategies that help Story Lab projects reach intended audiences and maximize public engagement. Qualifications: Required Qualifications: Graduate degree in field relevant to science communication, science methodologies, and/or storytelling. 5+ years of professional experience in the field of impact storytelling Strong record of impact storytelling development, evidenced by a portfolio of distributed/published media work Evident commitment to working with and building teams of diverse, inclusive, and high-performing media professionals This position requires driving a university vehicle or a personal vehicle on behalf of the university; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a motor vehicle history check, possess and maintain a current, valid driver's license in their state of residence, be determined to be position qualified and self-report convictions as per University Policy 05-030.   Preferred Qualifications: Experience working with diverse clients and subjects, especially in remote settings or challenging conditions Experience with science communication and research storytelling, translating specialized research into compelling narratives for non-specialist audiences Evidence of dedication to effective mentorship of aspiring storytellers Working Conditions/Schedule: This position requires routine on-campus presence. Periodically, the incumbent will be expected to participate in Story Lab projects conducted in diverse and often remote areas, both on land and water. The Impact Storytelling Lead may be expected to help transport media equipment, at times weighing more than 50 lbs., and may be expected to work extended periods outdoors, including in inclement weather and/or challenging conditions (e.g., filming on watercraft). Schedule is flexible and aligned with Story Lab needs and priorities. Salary Range: $58,000-$75,000 Anticipated Start Date: 2026-09-01 Anticipated End Date: We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status. OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire. OSU is a fair chance employer committed to inclusive hiring. We encourage applications from candidates who bring a wide range of lived experience including involvement with the justice system. If this position has “critical or security-sensitive” responsibilities and/or driving duties and you are selected as a finalist, your initial job offer will be contingent upon the results of a job-related pre-employment check (such as a background check, motor vehicle history check, sexual misconduct reference check, etc.). Background check results do not automatically disqualify a candidate. Take a look at our Background Checks website including the for candidates section for more details. If you have questions or concerns about the pre-employment check, please contact OSU’s Employee and Labor Relations team at employee.relations@oregonstate.edu ."
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/DAS-Lab-Student-Assist_JR0000838": {
      "title": "DAS Lab Student Assist",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-24\nPosted: Posted 12 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nDepartment: Disability Access Services Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents: A Resume A Cover Letter A supplemental document answering the following question: What specific experience do you have with high level technical skills and expertise with SOUND ANALYSIS for MARINE SCIENCE? For additional information please contact: Pam Hoene at pamela.hoene@oregonstate.edu We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status.   This position requires driving a University vehicle or a personal vehicle on behalf of the University; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a Motor Vehicle History Chec k, possess and maintain a current, valid driver's license in their state of residence, be determined to be position qualified and self-report convictions (as per Voluntary and Compulsory Driver Standards OSU Standard 125-155-0200) as per OSU Standard 576-056-0000 et seq.   OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire.   Note: All job offers are contingent upon Human Resources final approval Job Description: This recruitment will be used to fill 1 part-time (a maximum of 24 hours per week) AA position for Disability Services at Oregon State University (OSU).   Position Duties Assistance with High level technical skills and expertise with SOUND ANALYSIS for MARINE SCIENCE AAs, under the direction of the DAS student, may assist with the manipulation of lab materials and with the completion of in-class assignments. AAs may also assist the student with navigating the classroom/lab environment. All information about the actual course and lab processes and procedures are the student's responsibility. Tasks performed are to assist a blind student in navigating. Transferable Skill Development OSU is committed to ensuring students are prepared for success after graduation through intentional skill development as student employees. The competencies students develop while working at OSU are defined by the National Association of Colleges and Employers (NACE) as the essential skills employers seek in their hires. In this position, students learn and build vital career-readiness skills in the primary areas below:   Communication, Teamwork, Equity & Inclusion, Career & Self Development     Qualifications: Full Employment Eligibility Requirements can be found here:   https://hr.oregonstate.edu/student-employment-manual/500-employment-eligibility-requirements Must be academically enrolled in a high school, community college, or university and pursuing a program or course of study Must meet Academic Standing Requirements; students on academic suspension are not eligible for employment Must meet the applicable minimal enrollment standard High School student: Regularly enrolled in a high school or participating in a home-schooling program Undergraduate and post-baccalaureate student: 6 credit hours per term Undergraduate international student: 12 credit hours per term* Graduate student officially admitted to Graduate School: 5 credit hours per term Graduate international student officially admitted to Graduate School: 9 credit hours per term* *International students may be allowed to carry fewer hours than specified above and still be considered “full-time” by the United States Citizenship and Immigration Services ( USCIS ). A reduced course load is approved by the   Office of International Services   ( OIS ), and must be provided to the Student Employment Center. Required High level technical skills and expertise with SOUND ANALYSIS for MARINE SCIENCE Content Knowledge for FW 507.800 - MARINE SCIENCE (N) (10520) Ability to commute to Newport on Thursdays - R at 3:30 PM - 4:20 PM in Newport (HMSC)   Session: 9/23/26 - 12/04/26   This position requires driving a University vehicle or a personal vehicle on behalf of the University; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a Motor Vehicle History Check , possess and maintain a current, valid driver's license in their state of residence, be determined to be position qualified and self-report convictions (as per Voluntary and Compulsory Driver Standards OSU Standard 125-155-0200) as per OSU Standard 576-056-0000 et seq Preferred Prior experience working with individuals who are low vision/Blind or individuals with limited fine motor skills. The ability to assist with Research for FW 603.036 Working Conditions/Schedule: R at 3:30 PM - 4:20 PM in Newport (HMSC) Session: 9/23/26 - 12/04/26 Ability to commute to Newport on Thursdays Salary Range: Anticipated Start Date: 2026-09-14 Anticipated End Date:"
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/Gymnastics-PAC---KidSpirit_JR0000643": {
      "title": "Gymnastics PAC - KidSpirit",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-24\nPosted: Posted 12 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nDepartment: KidSpirit Extension and Engagement Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic document: A Resume In addition to applying to the OSU job website portal please go to KidSpirit website to join our team and apply to KidSpirit. https://kidspirit.oregonstate.edu/employment For additional information please contact: kidspirit@oregonstate.edu We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status. This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a Criminal History Check and be determined to be position qualified as per OSU Standard 576-055-0000 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in Youth Programs may have additional Criminal History Checks every 2 years.   OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire. Note: All job offers are contingent upon Human Resources final approval Job Description: This recruitment will be used to fill 30 part-time (a maximum of 24 hours per week) Gymnastics Instructor for the KidSpirit Youth Organization at Oregon State University (OSU). The Youth Program Instructor is responsible for delivering high‑quality instruction to youth while fostering a safe, supportive, and growth‑oriented environment. The position requires strong communication, critical thinking, teamwork, and a commitment to ongoing professional development. Responsibilities include lesson planning, safety response, equipment management, program promotion, and effective collaboration with staff, families, and supervisors. Join Our Team | OSU–KidSpirit | Extension and Engagement | Oregon State University Position Duties Create and maintain an instructional environment that prioritizes skill mastery, emotional safety, and positive youth development. Plan, prepare, and implement age-appropriate lessons and activities that support participant growth and engagement. Use observation, critical thinking, and feedback strategies to evaluate participants and support staff performance. Maintain accountability for all youth in assigned areas, ensuring consistent supervision and responsive care. Experience in gymnastics, dance, tumbling. Monitor participant safety at all times and administer first aid when necessary. Attend required orientation sessions, program meetings, and in-service training. Transferable Skill Development OSU is committed to ensuring students are prepared for success after graduation through intentional skill development as student employees. The competencies students develop while working at OSU are defined by the National Association of Colleges and Employers (NACE) as the essential skills employers seek in their hires. In this position, students learn and build vital career-readiness skills in the primary areas below:   Communication, Critical Thinking, Teamwork, Professionalism Qualifications: Full Employment Eligibility Requirements can be found here:   https://hr.oregonstate.edu/student-employment-manual/500-employment-eligibility-requirements Must be academically enrolled in a high school, community college, or university and pursuing a program or course of study Must meet Academic Standing Requirements; students on academic suspension are not eligible for employment Must meet the applicable minimal enrollment standard High School student: Regularly enrolled in a high school or participating in a home-schooling program Undergraduate and post-baccalaureate student: 6 credit hours per term Undergraduate international student: 12 credit hours per term* Graduate student officially admitted to Graduate School: 5 credit hours per term Graduate international student officially admitted to Graduate School: 9 credit hours per term* *International students may be allowed to carry fewer hours than specified above and still be considered “full-time” by the United States Citizenship and Immigration Services ( USCIS ). A reduced course load is approved by the   Office of International Services   ( OIS ), and must be provided to the Student Employment Center. Required Current, valid First Aid certification by the start of programming. This can be done during this hiring process. Recognizing and Reporting Child Abuse and Neglect (RRCAN) certification by start of programming. Introduction to Child Care Health & Safety certification by start of programming. Fundamentals of Learning OSU Youth Safety and Compliance requirements, standards of behavior and mandatory reporting training. This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a Criminal History Check and be determined to be position qualified as per OSU Standard 576-055-0000 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in Youth Programs may have additional Criminal History Checks every 24 months. Preferred Extensive Experience in Gymnastics, Tumbling, Dance. Demonstrates the professionalism to complete CANVAS tasks. Working Conditions/Schedule: On campus at Langton Hall, may instruct gymnastics at community events, OSU campus, Albany, Corvallis, Siletz communities and / or throughout the state of Oregon. Facilitate Gymnastic parties. Afternoons / Afterschool programming, and every Saturday. Community events may happen any day of the week and anytime during the day. Salary Range: N/A Anticipated Start Date: 2026-08-31 Anticipated End Date:"
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/KidSpirit-Special-Events-School-Year-2026-2027_JR0000647": {
      "title": "KidSpirit Special Events School Year 2026-2027",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-24\nPosted: Posted 12 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nDepartment: KidSpirit Extension and Engagement Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic document: A Resume In addition to applying to the OSU job website portal please go to KidSpirit website to join our team and apply to KidSpirit. https://kidspirit.oregonstate.edu/employment For additional information please contact: kidspirit@oregonstate.edu We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status. This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a Criminal History Chec k and be determined to be position qualified as per OSU Standard 576-055-0000 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in Youth Programs may have additional Criminal History Checks every 2 years. OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire. Note: All job offers are contingent upon Human Resources final approval Job Description: This recruitment will be used to fill 60 part-time (a maximum of 24 hours per week) Special Event positions for the KidSpirit Youth Organization at Oregon State University (OSU). Typically, this position is incorporated with staff that are hired for KidSpirit primary school year gymnastics and swimming positions. There may be only 2 people that are hired for special events only throughout the school year. The Youth Program Instructor is responsible for delivering high‑quality instruction to youth while fostering a safe, supportive, and growth‑oriented environment. The position requires strong communication, critical thinking, teamwork, and a commitment to ongoing professional development. Responsibilities include lesson planning, safety response, equipment management, program promotion, and effective collaboration with staff, families, and supervisors. This position facilitates community events for KidSpirit, it includes non-school day camps birthday, marketing duties, archery, gymnastics, art activities and much more. OSU–KidSpirit | Extension and Engagement | Oregon State University   Position Duties Responsible for leading specialized activities for children during but not limited to: birthday parties, event childcare and ACES (Activities Create Excitement on non-School days) while promoting skill development, preparing and implementing lesson plans, providing feedback, maintaining safe learning environment, and assisting other instructors and camp counselors. At times this will require the student employee to go to different campuses, community events, community centers and / or travel to venues. Support the head instructor in maintaining an instructional environment focused on mastery/success of sport/activity, emotional safety, and providing a positive and caring environment for youth or head instruct activities. Plan, prepare, and implement appropriate age lessons and activities that support participant growth and engagement. Maintain accountability for all youth in assigned areas, ensuring consistent supervision and responsive care. Monitor participant safety always and administer first aid when necessary. Attend required orientation sessions, program meetings, and in-service training. Transferable Skill Development OSU is committed to ensuring students are prepared for success after graduation through intentional skill development as student employees. The competencies students develop while working at OSU are defined by the National Association of Colleges and Employers (NACE) as the essential skills employers seek in their hires. In this position, students learn and build vital career-readiness skills in the primary areas below:   Communication, Critical Thinking, Teamwork, Professionalism Qualifications: Full Employment Eligibility Requirements can be found here: http://fa.oregonstate.edu/stu-manual/500-employment-eligibility-requirements Must be academically enrolled in a high school, community college, or university and pursuing a program or course of study Must meet Academic Standing Requirements; students on academic suspension are not eligible for employment Must meet the applicable minimal enrollment standard High School student: Regularly enrolled in a high school or participating in a home-schooling program Undergraduate and post-baccalaureate student: 6 credit hours per term Undergraduate international student: 12 credit hours per term* Graduate student officially admitted to Graduate School: 5 credit hours per term Graduate international student officially admitted to Graduate School: 9 credit hours per term* *International students may be allowed to carry fewer hours than specified above and still be considered “full-time” by the United States Citizenship and Immigration Services (USCIS). A reduced course load is approved by the Office of International Services (OIS) and must be provided to the Student Employment Center. Required Current, valid First Aid certification by the start of programming.  This can be done during this hiring process. Recognizing and Reporting Child Abuse and Neglect (RRCAN) certification by start of programming. Introduction to Child Care Health & Safety certification by start of programming. Fundamentals of Learning OSU Youth Safety and Compliance requirements, standards of behavior and mandatory reporting training. This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a Criminal History Check and be determined to be position qualified as per OSU Standard 576-055-0000 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in Youth Programs may have additional Criminal History Checks every 2 years. Preferred Experience working with children, event planning, gymnastics, creativity, organizing and facilitating activities. Working Conditions/Schedule: Most of the programming takes place in Langton Hall on OSU campus. This position does go to community events throughout Corvallis, Albany and the State of Oregon. Work schedule varies, can be non-school days (during the day), afternoons, evenings and primarily weekends. Salary Range: N/A Anticipated Start Date: 2026-08-31 Anticipated End Date:"
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/KidSpirit-Gymnastics-School-Year-2026-2027_JR0000610": {
