    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/Student-FST-Research---Ma-AI4Food-Lab_JR0000723": {
      "title": "Student FST Research - Ma AI4Food Lab",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-13\nPosted: Posted 23 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nDepartment: Food Science & Technology Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents: ●        A Resume ●        A Cover Letter For additional information please contact: Luyao Ma at luyao.ma@oregonstate.edu We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status.   This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a Criminal History Check and be determined to be position qualified as per OSU Standard 576-055-0000 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in Youth Programs may have additional Criminal History Checks every 2 years.   OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire.   Note: All job offers are contingent upon Human Resources final approval Job Description: This recruitment is to fill (1) part-time (fewer than 15 hours per week) Senior Undergraduate Student or Graduate Student Project Assistant Position in Ma’s AI4Food Lab in the Department of Food Science and Technology at Oregon State University (OSU). Working conditions may require working around chemicals, caustic compounds and hazardous materials. Position Duties 70% machine learning model development and programming 20% general operation related, such as assisting with data collection and statistical analysis 10% writing reports Transferable Skill Development OSU is committed to ensuring students are prepared for success after graduation through intentional skill development as student employees. The competencies students develop while working at OSU are defined by the National Association of Colleges and Employers (NACE) as the essential skills employers seek in their hires. In this position, students learn and build vital career-readiness skills in the primary areas below:   Communication, Critical Thinking, Technology Qualifications: Full Employment Eligibility Requirements can be found here:   https://hr.oregonstate.edu/student-employment-manual/500-employment-eligibility-requirements Must be academically enrolled in a high school, community college, or university and pursuing a program or course of study Must meet Academic Standing Requirements; students on academic suspension are not eligible for employment Must meet the applicable minimal enrollment standard High School student: Regularly enrolled in a high school or participating in a home-schooling program Undergraduate and post-baccalaureate student: 6 credit hours per term Undergraduate international student: 12 credit hours per term* Graduate student officially admitted to Graduate School: 5 credit hours per term Graduate international student officially admitted to Graduate School: 9 credit hours per term* *International students may be allowed to carry fewer hours than specified above and still be considered “full-time” by the United States Citizenship and Immigration Services ( USCIS ). A reduced course load is approved by the   Office of International Services   ( OIS ), and must be provided to the Student Employment Center. Required Proficient in Python programming and machine learning Applicant must be organized and dependable Applicant must be willing to work independently and within a team environment The candidate must be able to follow written and oral instructions This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a Criminal History Check and be determined to be position qualified as per OSU Standard 576-055-0000 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in Youth Programs may have additional Criminal History Checks every 2 years. Preferred Demonstratable commitment to promoting and enhancing diversity Working Conditions/Schedule: • May require working with chemicals, caustic compounds and hazardous materials • Anticipated hours per week: 15 hours Salary Range: Anticipated Start Date: 2026-08-24 Anticipated End Date:"
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/VPR-Project-Assistant_JR0000645": {
      "title": "VPR Project Assistant",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-13\nPosted: Posted 23 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nDepartment: Division of Research and Innovation Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents: A Resume A Cover Letter For additional information please contact: Konnie Handschuch at Konnie.handschuch@oregonstate.edu and/or Sara Begley at sara.begley@oregonstate.edu We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status.   This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a Criminal History Check and be determined to be position qualified as per OSU Standard 576-055-0000 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in Youth Programs may have additional Criminal History Checks every 2 years.   OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire.   Note: All job offers are contingent upon Human Resources final approval Job Description: This recruitment will be used to fill one part-time (maximum of 20 hours per week) Student Project Assistant position for the Office of VP for Research at Oregon State University (OSU). This position will assist the Operations Manager and the Administrations, Finance and Operations team with administrative duties, special projects, and operational tasks. The Project Assistant will be a crucial role to support the operations of the Division of Research and Innovation. Position Duties: 40% Administrative Support Support and update office standard operating procedures and manuals Work with cross functional teams to update and maintain Onboarding and offboarding processes Collaborate with leadership team and HR teams to communicate updates and opportunities for Employee and supervisor training Streamline the new employee search processes to ensure all candidates are communicated with Participate in office improvement work groups Maintain specific Division of Research and Innovation webpages Update employee communication expectations as well as other division wide procedures Plan employee appreciation events and activities Collaborate with leadership team to host events in person and remotely to increase staff engagement Assist with calendar organization and scheduling for shared spaces and the Operations Manager 40% Operational Support Assist with the receipt, documentation, and processing of financial checks to ensure accurate and timely deposits in accordance with university financial procedures Support Buyer with invoice reconciliation and other duties Assist with office updates (office moves, office remodels, organization, facilities updates) Assist with projects as needed 10% Office Improvement Projects Assist the Operations Manager in continued improvement to the Division of Research and Innovation 10% Other Duties as Assigned Transferable Skill Development OSU is committed to ensuring students are prepared for success after graduation through intentional skill development as student employees. The competencies students develop while working at OSU are defined by the National Association of Colleges and Employers (NACE) as the essential skills employers seek in their hires. In this position, students learn and build vital career-readiness skills in the primary areas below:   Communication, Critical Thinking, Teamwork, Technology Qualifications: Full Employment Eligibility Requirements can be found here:   https://hr.oregonstate.edu/student-employment-manual/500-employment-eligibility-requirements Must be academically enrolled in a high school, community college, or university and pursuing a program or course of study Must meet Academic Standing Requirements; students on academic suspension are not eligible for employment Must meet the applicable minimal enrollment standard High School student: Regularly enrolled in a high school or participating in a home-schooling program Undergraduate and post-baccalaureate student: 6 credit hours per term Undergraduate international student: 12 credit hours per term* Graduate student officially admitted to Graduate School: 5 credit hours per term Graduate international student officially admitted to Graduate School: 9 credit hours per term* *International students may be allowed to carry fewer hours than specified above and still be considered “full-time” by the United States Citizenship and Immigration Services ( USCIS ). A reduced course load is approved by the   Office of International Services   ( OIS ), and must be provided to the Student Employment Center. Required Qualifications: Strong attention to detail and organization Demonstrable ability to work independently and in a team environment Excellent verbal and written communication skills Flexible, able to take on projects that are unfamiliar Proficient in Microsoft Suite Applications (Word, Excel, Outlook, Visio) Additional Required Qualifications: This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a criminal history check and be determined to be position qualified as per OSU Standard 576-055-0000 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in youth programs may have additional criminal history check. Preferred Qualifications Freshman or Sophomore standing Prior experience providing excellent customer service Prior experience with visual software applications (Canva, Visio, etc.) Goes above and beyond in their work A demonstrable commitment to promoting and enhancing diversity Working Conditions/Schedule: This position is expected to work between 10 and 24 hours per week in the office within standard business hours Monday through Friday. The specific schedule will be agreed upon between the employee and the supervisor based on the employee’s class schedule. Salary Range: N/A Anticipated Start Date: Anticipated End Date:"
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/ASOSU-Graduate-Senator_JR0000534": {
      "title": "ASOSU Graduate Senator",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-13\nPosted: Posted 23 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nDepartment: Student Affairs - ASOSU Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents: A Resume A Cover Letter Supplemental Questions Supplemental Questions: (Yes/No) Will you be available September 14-18, 2026, for training? For additional information please contact: Keri Simonet at simonetk@oregonstate.edu We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status.   OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire.   Note: All job offers are contingent upon Human Resources final approval Job Description: This recruitment will be used to fill two (2) part-time (a maximum of 24 hours per week) ASOSU Graduate Senator for the ASOSU at Oregon State University (OSU). The ASOSU Student Senator serves as an elected representative of the student body and is responsible for advocating on behalf of students through legislation, policy development, fiscal oversight, and constituent engagement. Senators work collaboratively to identify student needs, improve student experience, and ensure that student perspectives are represented in university governance and decision-making. The Senate serves as the legislative branch of the ASOSU Student Government and is responsible for establishing official ASOSU policies, positions, and priorities. Given the evolving priorities of the student body and Oregon State University, these responsibilities (including those provided below) may be amended or vary depending on ongoing priorities, legislative initiatives, or emergent issues. Student Senators work collaboratively with fellow Senators, the ASOSU Executive Branch, Judicial Council, university administration, campus partners, and members of the student body to fulfill the mission of ASOSU. Please note: ASOSU Senator seats are filled through an appointment committee consisting of the ASOSU President, ASOSU Vice President, President Pro Tempore, and Judicial Council Chair. You must be either a current graduate student or accepted for the 2026-2027 academic year as a graduate student. The Undergraduate Student Support Services Worker performs a variety of work related to the support and enhancement of the quality of student life at the university. Position Duties: Represent the interests of the student body and serve as an accessible resource for students regarding ASOSU and university concerns. Engage in legislative discussion and decision-making on issues affecting Oregon State University students. Research, draft, sponsor, and deliberate legislation, resolutions, and policy initiatives that improve the student experience. Participate in oversight of ASOSU fiscal matters, including review and recommendation of budgets, expenditures, and incidental fees as required. Participate in Senate confirmation processes for appointments as required by the Constitution. Review and vote on legislation, appointments, resolutions, and other matters brought before the Senate. Maintain regular communication with constituents to better understand student concerns, priorities, and emerging issues. Conduct outreach to students through conversations, events, tabling, classroom visits, surveys, or other engagement opportunities. Collaborate with university departments, student organizations, and campus partners to identify opportunities to improve campus life and student success. Serve on at least one ASOSU Standing Committee or an approved university committee and actively participate in committee meetings and assigned work. Provide committee reports and updates during Senate meetings when applicable. Promote transparency, accountability, and ethical governance in all ASOSU activities. Uphold the ASOSU Constitution, Statutes, Standing Rules, university policies, and applicable state laws, including Oregon Public Meetings Law. Foster an inclusive environment that encourages student participation and democratic engagement. Support initiatives that promote academic success, community wellbeing, civic engagement, and student advocacy consistent with the mission of ASOSU. Transferable Skill Development OSU is committed to ensuring students are prepared for success after graduation through intentional skill development as student employees. The competencies students develop while working at OSU are defined by the National Association of Colleges and Employers (NACE) as the essential skills employers seek in their hires. In this position, students learn and build vital career-readiness skills in the primary areas below:   Communication, Teamwork, Leadership, Equity & Inclusion Qualifications: Full Employment Eligibility Requirements can be found here:   https://hr.oregonstate.edu/student-employment-manual/500-employment-eligibility-requirements Must be academically enrolled in a high school, community college, or university and pursuing a program or course of study Must meet Academic Standing Requirements; students on academic suspension are not eligible for employment Must meet the applicable minimal enrollment standard High School student: Regularly enrolled in a high school or participating in a home-schooling program Undergraduate and post-baccalaureate student: 6 credit hours per term Undergraduate international student: 12 credit hours per term* Graduate student officially admitted to Graduate School: 5 credit hours per term Graduate international student officially admitted to Graduate School: 9 credit hours per term* *International students may be allowed to carry fewer hours than specified above and still be considered “full-time” by the United States Citizenship and Immigration Services ( USCIS ). A reduced course load is approved by the   Office of International Services   ( OIS ), and must be provided to the Student Employment Center. Required Qualifications:  For this particular position, student must be a graduate student. Preferred Qualifications: Must be a Corvallis campus-based student. Maintenance of good academic and conduct standing and a cumulative and prior term 2.0 GPA. Commitment to promoting and advancing diversity and creating an atmosphere of inclusion for all students and community members. Communication skills in areas including but not limited to interpersonal, public speaking, digital, verbal, and written communications. Organizational skills, including managing time and working accurately with interruptions to meet a deadline; delegating tasks, when applicable. Working well under pressure, exercising ﬂexibility, initiative, good judgment, and discretion. Working Conditions/Schedule: Salary Range: Anticipated Start Date: 2026-09-01 Anticipated End Date: 2027-06-01"
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/Instructor-Pool---2026-2027--Nuclear-Science-and-Engineering_JR0000247": {
      "title": "Instructor Pool - 2026/2027: Nuclear Science and Engineering",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-13\nPosted: Posted 23 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nDepartment: College of Engineering Special Instructions to Applicants: Applications will be considered throughout the 2026–2027 Academic Year, as opportunities become available. When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents:   1) A Curriculum Vitae   2) A Cover Letter of Interest; indicating how your qualifications and experience have prepared you for this position and how you meet each of the required qualifications and any preferred qualifications if applicable. 3) A Statement of Teaching 4) Publications 5) Transcripts Letters of Reference will be requested for those advanced to the reference stage. At that time, you will be asked to provide contact information for your referees who will be sent a secure quicklink that will allow them to upload the requested letters of reference on your behalf. For additional information please contact: Lizz Koch lizz.koch@oregonstate.edu 541-737-7063   Job Summary: The School of Nuclear Science and Engineering invites applications for part-time (varied   FTE ), 9-month, fixed-term Instructor positions to teach on a term-by-term basis for the 2026-2027 academic year. Some of these appointments may be reviewed for renewal or transition to an instructional position on an annual basis at the discretion of the Head of School. The School of Nuclear Science & Engineering within the College of Engineering seeks temporary instructors to teach courses in the following areas: nuclear engineering, radiation health physics, radiochemistry or engineering science. May also include course/curriculum development. With a mission to provide world-class education that produces industry, academic, and policy leaders in the field of nuclear science, NSE boasts a robust graduate program with over 200 students. The curriculum covers various aspects of nuclear and radiation sciences, engineering, and technology. In addition, the School offers an acclaimed online Radiation Health Physics program, which ranks among the best in the nation. NSE's tenure/tenure-track faculty members specialize in a wide range of areas, such as nuclear reactor physics, thermal hydraulics, computational methods, radiation detection and measurement, nuclear security and nonproliferation, nuclear materials, health physics, and radiochemistry. Key Responsibilities: Instruct courses by using appropriate and various teaching strategies. Acknowledge differences in learning styles.   Communicates effectively, meets classes as scheduled, and submits grading in a timely manner.   Complete ABET accreditation course assessment.   Participates in departmental and college governance as well as any professional activities.  Qualifications: Required Qualifications: Masters degree in nuclear engineering, radiation health physics, radiochemistry, or closely related field Good written and oral communication skills Preferred Qualifications: PhD in nuclear engineering, radiation health physics, radiochemistry, or closely related field Industrial research experience in nuclear engineering, radiation health physics, thermodynamics, thermal fluids, radiochemistry, or engineering science Teaching experience at the college or university level. A commitment to fostering environments that value different perspectives and experiences Working Conditions/Schedule: Office and classroom Salary Range: Salary is commensurate with skills, knowledge, and experience. Anticipated Start Date: 2026-09-16 Anticipated End Date: 2027-06-30 We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status. OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire. OSU is a fair chance employer committed to inclusive hiring. We encourage applications from candidates who bring a wide range of lived experience including involvement with the justice system. If this position has “critical or security-sensitive” responsibilities and/or driving duties and you are selected as a finalist, your initial job offer will be contingent upon the results of a job-related pre-employment check (such as a background check, motor vehicle history check, sexual misconduct reference check, etc.). Background check results do not automatically disqualify a candidate. Take a look at our Background Checks website including the for candidates section for more details. If you have questions or concerns about the pre-employment check, please contact OSU’s Employee and Labor Relations team at employee.relations@oregonstate.edu ."
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/Undergraduate-Learning-Assistant---School-of-MIME_JR0000188": {
      "title": "Undergraduate Learning Assistant - School of MIME",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-13\nPosted: Posted 23 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nDepartment: School of MIME Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents: ●        A Resume ●        A Cover Letter For additional information please contact: Rex Bamba at rex.bamba@oregonstate.edu We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status. Job Description: This recruitment will be used to hire 75 part-time (a maximum of 20 hours per week) undergraduate learning assistant/grader positions for the School of Mechanical, Industrial, and Manufacturing Engineering (MIME) at Oregon State University (OSU). 100%: Duties include holding office hours, grading papers, copying materials for courses, helping with 30-person lab sections, tutoring, leading study sessions, attending weekly meetings, and other tasks as needed by the supervising instructor. This position will also assist with checking off work with direct supervision from the supervisor via a grading rubric. Breakdown will vary by course. Transferable Skill Development OSU is committed to ensuring students are prepared for success after graduation through intentional skill development as student employees. The competencies students develop while working at OSU are defined by the National Association of Colleges and Employers (NACE) as the essential skills employers seek in their hires. In this position, students learn and build vital career-readiness skills in the primary areas below:   Critical Thinking, Teamwork, Equity & Inclusion, Career & Self Development Qualifications: Full Employment Eligibility Requirements can be found here:   https://hr.oregonstate.edu/student-employment-manual/500-employment-eligibility-requirements   Minimum Qualifications: Must be academically enrolled in a high school, community college, or university and pursuing a program or course of study Must meet Academic Standing Requirements; students on academic suspension are not eligible for employment Must meet the applicable minimal enrollment standard High School student: Regularly enrolled in a high school or participating in a home-schooling program Undergraduate and post-baccalaureate student: 6 credit hours per term Undergraduate international student: 12 credit hours per term* Graduate student officially admitted to Graduate School: 5 credit hours per term Graduate international student officially admitted to Graduate School: 9 credit hours per term* *International students may be allowed to carry fewer hours than specified above and still be considered “full-time” by the United States Citizenship and Immigration Services ( USCIS ). A reduced course load is approved by the   Office of International Services   ( OIS ), and must be provided to the Student Employment Center. Required Qualifications: Engineering background. Depending on the course, successful completion of the course they are supporting may be required. Working Conditions/Schedule: Regular working hours as assigned by the instructor/s Salary Range: Anticipated Start Date: Anticipated End Date:"
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/Instructor-Pool---2026-2027--Environmental-Sciences_JR0000624": {
      "title": "Instructor Pool - 2026/2027: Environmental Sciences",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-12\nPosted: Posted 24 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nDepartment: Office of Graduate Education Special Instructions to Applicants: Applications will be considered throughout the 2026-2027 Academic Year. When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents:    1) A resume/CV; and     2) A cover letter indicating how your qualifications and experience have prepared you for this position. 3) A Statement of Teaching- Upload as separate document. Starting salary will be commensurate with skills, education, and experience.  For additional information please contact: Mo Childers at Mo.Childers@oregonstate.edu or 541-737-2033. Job Summary: The Office of Graduate Education at OSU in Corvallis, Oregon, invites applications for one or more fixed-term, non-tenure-track part-time Instructors to teach on a term-by-term basis for the 2026-2027 academic year.   Some of these appointments may be reviewed for renewal or transition to an instructional position on an annual basis at the discretion of the Dean of Academic Affairs. Applications submitted to this recruitment pool are reviewed on an as needed basis. Qualified candidates will be contacte d  when positions become available.   Instructors may be needed to teach Corvallis campus and Ecampus courses in the following areas: graduate courses in Environmental Sciences (ENSC). The primary mission of the Environmental Sciences Graduate Program is to provide graduate education in Environmental Sciences. The curriculum encompasses most standard fields of the discipline. Key Responsibility: Administer and teach graduate courses in Environmental Sciences. Qualifications: Required Qualifications: Master’s degree in discipline appropriate to the field of teaching/instructional tasks. Teaching or assistant experience at the college/university level. Evident commitment to educational equity. Preferred Qualifications: A PhD in a discipline appropriate to the field of teaching. Working Conditions/Schedule: Will be working in a classroom (physical or virtual) teaching Environmental Sciences graduate courses. Schedule will vary based upon course schedules and classroom availability. Salary Range: Salary is commensurate with skills, education and experience. Anticipated Start Date: 2026-09-16 Anticipated End Date: 2027-06-15 We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status. OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire. OSU is a fair chance employer committed to inclusive hiring. We encourage applications from candidates who bring a wide range of lived experience including involvement with the justice system. If this position has “critical or security-sensitive” responsibilities and/or driving duties and you are selected as a finalist, your initial job offer will be contingent upon the results of a job-related pre-employment check (such as a background check, motor vehicle history check, sexual misconduct reference check, etc.). Background check results do not automatically disqualify a candidate. Take a look at our Background Checks website including the for candidates section for more details. If you have questions or concerns about the pre-employment check, please contact OSU’s Employee and Labor Relations team at employee.relations@oregonstate.edu ."
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/CBEE-Lab-Worker_JR0000141": {
      "title": "CBEE Lab Worker",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-12\nPosted: Posted 24 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nDepartment: ECB Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents: 1. A Resume/Vita 2. A cover letter indicating how your qualifications and experience have prepared you for this position   For additional information please contact: Jennifer Moore at jennifer.moore@oregonstate.edu   We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status. Job Description: Duties to be determined by supervisor and may include book reviews and publication research, experimentation in a laboratory setting, laboratory set-up and maintenance, data collection and analysis, and other related tasks. Other duties are performed specific to the funding and research topic. Duties may include, but are not limited to: Work in a chemical laboratory performing chemical reactions, chemical analyses, instrument operation, general cleanup duties, chemical purchasing, report and publication writing and presentations to various audiences depending on the project. Computer modeling Work with hazardous chemicals and/or materials as needed, depending on the project. Prepare samples for testing. Manage data and experiment analysis Use all manner of common lab equipment, such as microscopes, scales, measurement devices, testing machines, power saws, and data collection systems to address project goals. Transferable Skill Development OSU is committed to ensuring students are prepared for success after graduation through intentional skill development as student employees. The competencies students develop while working at OSU are defined by the National Association of Colleges and Employers (NACE) as the essential skills employers seek in their hires.  In this position, students learn and build vital career-readiness skills in the primary areas below: Communication, Critical Thinking, Teamwork, and Technology Qualifications: Full Employment Eligibility Requirements can be found here:   https://hr.oregonstate.edu/student-employment-manual/500-employment-eligibility-requirements   Must be academically enrolled in a high school, community college, or university and pursuing a program or course of study Must meet Academic Standing Requirements; students on academic suspension are not eligible for employment Must meet the applicable minimal enrollment standard High School student: Regularly enrolled in a high school or participating in a home-schooling program Undergraduate and post-baccalaureate student: 6 credit hours per term Undergraduate international student: 12 credit hours per term* Graduate student officially admitted to Graduate School: 5 credit hours per term Graduate international student officially admitted to Graduate School: 9 credit hours per term* *International students may be allowed to carry fewer hours than specified above and still be considered “full-time” by the United States Citizenship and Immigration Services ( USCIS ). A reduced course load is approved by the   Office of International Services   ( OIS ), and must be provided to the Student Employment Center. Required Qualifications: Complete safety training. Protection gear is necessary and provided by the laboratory. College of Engineering major Strong written and oral communication skills Desire to be involved in hands-on research Team player Preferred Qualifications: Environmental Engineering, Chemical Engineering or Bioengineering major with a Chemistry background. Experience working with hand tools. This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a criminal history check and be determined to be position qualified as per OSU Standard 576-055-0000 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in youth programs may have additional criminal history checks every 24 months. Note: All job offers are contingent upon Human Resources final approval. OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire. Working Conditions/Schedule: This recruitment will be used to fill multiple part-time (a maximum of 24 hours per week) Student Technical Assistant positions for the School of Chemical, Biological, and Environmental Engineering (CBEE) at Oregon State University (OSU). These positions are based on lab group needs and vary from 3-20 hours per week, depending on the workload and assignment. Information on the various research groups and their area(s) of focus can be found from our faculty directory, located here: https://engineering.oregonstate.edu/CBEE/About/People. Salary Range: Anticipated Start Date: 2026-07-13 Anticipated End Date: 2027-06-30"
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/School-of-CCE---Undergraduate-Lab-Position_JR0000087": {
      "title": "School of CCE - Undergraduate Lab Position",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-12\nPosted: Posted 24 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nDepartment: Sch of Civil/Constr Engr (ECC) Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents:  A Resume  A Cover Letter  For additional information please contact: Nicole Thompson at nicole.thompson@oregonstate.edu We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status.  Job Description: This recruitment will be used to fill multiple part-time (a maximum of 24 hours per week) Student Technical Assistant positions for the School of Civil & Construction Engineering at Oregon State University (OSU) to work in one of our research labs. Duties to be determined by supervisor. Duties may include, but are not limited to: experimentation in a lab setting, laboratory set-up and maintenance, data collection and analysis, assist in keeping the lab clean, working closely with other students, and and related tasks. May be using all manner of common wood and metal shop tools, such as wrenches, screwdrivers, measuring tapes, bolts, and hammers. May require sitting or standing for longer periods of time. Transferable Skill Development   OSU is committed to ensuring students are prepared for success after graduation   through intentional skill development   as   student employees. The competencies students develop while working at OSU are defined by the National Association of Colleges and Employers (NACE) as the essential skills employers   seek   in their hires. In this position, students learn and build vital career-readiness skills in the primary areas below:     Communication, Critical Thinking, Teamwork Qualifications: Full Employment Eligibility Requirements can be found here:   https://hr.oregonstate.edu/student-employment-manual/500-employment-eligibility-requirements   Must be academically enrolled in a high school, community college, or university and pursuing a program or course of study Must meet Academic Standing Requirements; students on academic suspension are not eligible for employment Must meet the applicable minimal enrollment standard High School student: Regularly enrolled in a high school or participating in a home-schooling program Undergraduate and post-baccalaureate student: 6 credit hours per term Undergraduate international student: 12 credit hours per term* Graduate student officially admitted to Graduate School: 5 credit hours per term Graduate international student officially admitted to Graduate School: 9 credit hours per term* *International students may be allowed to carry fewer hours than specified above and still be considered “full-time” by the United States Citizenship and Immigration Services ( USCIS ). A reduced course load is approved by the   Office of International Services   ( OIS ), and must be provided to the Student Employment Center. Must be able to lift 25 kg. Complete safety training. Protection gear is necessary and provided by the laboratory. College of Engineering major Strong written and oral communication skills Desire to be involved in hands-on research Team player Preferred Qualifications: The student enrolled as a CCE student (CE, ARE, or CEM) in good standing. Experience working with hand tools. Working Conditions/Schedule: Lab environment, work may be in a semi-open environment subject to colder temperatures during the winter, warm and humid conditions in the summer. Salary Range: Anticipated Start Date: 2026-07-10 Anticipated End Date:"
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/NSE-234-Undergraduate-Learning-Assistant_JR0000690-1": {
      "title": "NSE 234 Undergraduate Learning Assistant",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-11\nPosted: Posted 25 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nDepartment: School of Nuclear Science & Engineering Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents: Resume clearly indicating your declared major and work experience Current unofficial transcript For additional information please contact: Lizz Koch at lizz.koch@oregonstate.edu We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status.   This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a Criminal History Check and be determined to be position qualified as per OSU Standard 576-055-0000 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in Youth Programs may have additional Criminal History Checks every 2 years.   OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire.   Note: All job offers are contingent upon Human Resources final approval   Job Description: This recruitment will be used to fill up to three part-time (a maximum of 24 hours per week) Undergraduate Learning Assistant (ULA) positions for the School of Nuclear Science & Engineering (NSE) at Oregon State University (OSU). This position recruitment is for NSE 234, Fall 2026. This position performs a variety of duties in support of NSE course instruction. Responsibilities may include working with the instructor to assist with classroom and lab activities, holding office hours, and helping with grading. These duties will vary depending on the specific course requirements. Position Duties Assist with in-class activities and/or labs Tutor students during regular office hours and/or in the classroom Grade homework, quizzes and/or exams under instructor guidance May assist with managing course materials on Canvas Attend weekly meetings with the instructor of record Transferable Skill Development OSU is committed to ensuring students are prepared for success after graduation through intentional skill development as student employees. The competencies students develop while working at OSU are defined by the National Association of Colleges and Employers (NACE) as the essential skills employers seek in their hires. In this position, students learn and build vital career-readiness skills in the primary areas below:   Communication, Critical Thinking, Teamwork, Leadership Qualifications: Full Employment Eligibility Requirements can be found here:   https://hr.oregonstate.edu/student-employment-manual/500-employment-eligibility-requirements Must be academically enrolled in a high school, community college, or university and pursuing a program or course of study Must meet Academic Standing Requirements; students on academic suspension are not eligible for employment Must meet the applicable minimal enrollment standard High School student: Regularly enrolled in a high school or participating in a home-schooling program Undergraduate and post-baccalaureate student: 6 credit hours per term Undergraduate international student: 12 credit hours per term* Graduate student officially admitted to Graduate School: 5 credit hours per term Graduate international student officially admitted to Graduate School: 9 credit hours per term* *International students may be allowed to carry fewer hours than specified above and still be considered “full-time” by the United States Citizenship and Immigration Services ( USCIS ). A reduced course load is approved by the   Office of International Services   ( OIS ), and must be provided to the Student Employment Center. Required Undergraduate Engineering Major GPA greater than 3.25 Completion of course with a B or higher Good communication skills Students must complete  FERPA  training prior to starting position. This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a Criminal History Check and be determined to be position qualified as per OSU Standard 576-055-0000 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in Youth Programs may have additional Criminal History Checks every 2 years. Preferred Major or Minor in Nuclear Science & Engineering Previous undergraduate learning assistant experience Knowledge of Canvas Working Conditions/Schedule: - Work may be performed in classrooms, labs, or office spaces. - Must be able to work between 5 – 20 hours per week. Salary Range: Anticipated Start Date: 2026-09-23 Anticipated End Date:"
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Portland-Campus/OSU-Food-Innovation-Center-Student-Sensory-Technician_JR0000686": {
      "title": "OSU Food Innovation Center Student Sensory Technician",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-11\nPosted: Posted 25 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nDepartment: Food Innovation Center Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents: A Resume/Vita A cover letter indicating how your qualifications and experience have prepared you for this position. A supplemental document answering the following question: This position is located in Portland, OR. Would you be able to commute if you are located outside of Portland?(Open Ended Question)   For additional information please contact: Ann Colonna at Sensory.testing@oregonstate.edu We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status.   This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a Criminal History Check and be determined to be position qualified as per OSU Standard 576-055-0000 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in Youth Programs may have additional Criminal History Checks every 2 years.   OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire.   Note: All job offers are contingent upon Human Resources final approval Job Description: This recruitment will be used to fill ten (10) part-time (a maximum of 24 hours per week) Student Sensory Program Technician positions for the Food Innovation Center in Portland, Oregon, part of Oregon State University (OSU). This is a temporary position for part-time work to assist with conducing large consumer taste tests/sensory evaluation. Tests dates are variable and may be on weekends, but Tuesdays, Wednesdays and Thursdays from 8:30 am to 5 pm are preferred test days/times. Position Duties The Student Worker will be assisting the Sensory Program staff with the scheduling of participants, preparation of food and serving of samples for consumer taste tests. Duties will include: Scheduling participants Sending confirmation emails Making copies Coding serving materials Food sample preparation/serving Clean up Consumer tests are conducted both at the Oregon State University Food Innovation Center laboratory complex in downtown Portland, OR and off-site such as grocery stores or food festivals. The laboratory complex includes a reception area, 10 taste test booths and a staging area, a focus group room and a commercial kitchen. Transferable Skill Development OSU is committed to ensuring students are prepared for success after graduation through intentional skill development as student employees. The competencies students develop while working at OSU are defined by the National Association of Colleges and Employers (NACE) as the essential skills employers seek in their hires. In this position, students learn and build vital career-readiness skills in the primary areas below:   Communication, Teamwork, Professionalism, Career & Self Development Qualifications: Full Employment Eligibility Requirements can be found here:   https://hr.oregonstate.edu/student-employment-manual/500-employment-eligibility-requirements Must be academically enrolled in a high school, community college, or university and pursuing a program or course of study Must meet Academic Standing Requirements; students on academic suspension are not eligible for employment Must meet the applicable minimal enrollment standard High School student: Regularly enrolled in a high school or participating in a home-schooling program Undergraduate and post-baccalaureate student: 6 credit hours per term Undergraduate international student: 12 credit hours per term* Graduate student officially admitted to Graduate School: 5 credit hours per term Graduate international student officially admitted to Graduate School: 9 credit hours per term* *International students may be allowed to carry fewer hours than specified above and still be considered “full-time” by the United States Citizenship and Immigration Services ( USCIS ). A reduced course load is approved by the   Office of International Services   ( OIS ), and must be provided to the Student Employment Center. Required This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a Criminal History Check and be determined to be position qualified as per OSU Standard 576-055-0000 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in Youth Programs may have additional Criminal History Checks every 2 years. Preferred Preferred student candidates will be available for a minimum of 6 months Have a current Food Handlers License Have solid knowledge of Microsoft Office (Word, Excel, Access and Outlook) Working Conditions/Schedule: - Sensory testing work will take place on Tuesdays, Wednesdays and Thursdays from 8am to 5pm. - Could be office work for recruitment and labeling or serving and preparing food samples in the sensory laboratory, which would require being on your feet for up to 8 hours per shift. Salary Range: Anticipated Start Date: 2026-09-04 Anticipated End Date:"
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/Video-Reporter_JR0000115": {
      "title": "Video Reporter",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-11\nPosted: Posted 25 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nDepartment: Orange Media Network Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents: A Resume/Vita A cover letter indicating how your qualifications and experience have prepared you for this position If available, please include a link to a portfolio of recent video work. For additional information please contact: Jen Moody at jennifer.moody@oregonstate.edu We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status.    OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire.   Note: All job offers are contingent upon Human Resources final approval Job Description: This recruitment will be used to fill up to 2 part-time (a maximum of 24 hours per week) Video Reporters for the Orenge Media Network at Oregon State University (OSU). The Video reporter is responsible for pitching, researching, and writing/creating multimedia stories for OMN’s news teams, primarily for The Daily Barometer and Beaver’s Digest, as required by the editor-in-chief of the publications.  The Undergraduate Student Clerical Worker performs general office work such as typing, copying, filing, sorting, indexing, reception duties, and accounting tasks. Position Duties: Pitches, accepts and develops story ideas that have news value for the OSU community. Conducts research and necessary interviews to produce thorough video news stories that include multiple relevant perspectives. Remains objective and provides fair and accurate coverage. Pursues news in a timely fashion. Cover breaking news as needed. Video reporter is responsible for conducting interviews with sources, shooting and editing video, writing scripts and providing narration for video stories. Responsible for editing together news videos between 60 - 1:30, under direction of TV news producer and/or publication editors-in-chief. Prepare information for on-screen graphics. Shares stories on social media to reach audience. May be asked to present their story live in studio. May be asked to create horizontal and vertical versions of stories. Attends all staff and team meetings, including weekly reporter meeting. Participates in critique sessions with producer to make any necessary revisions in style or content. Able to act as mentors to new hires. Other duties as assigned. Communication   Respond to emails, messages, team chats and phone calls the same day, professionally representing OMN. Ensuring you are using, checking and responding to project management platform (Basecamp, Discord, etc.). Proactively communicates work status with editor(s). Notify adviser immediately should any ethical, legal, personnel or controversial problems occur. Leadership   Follow all OMN policies and procedures including professionalism and deadlines.  Ensure designs and content are centered on equitable representation and inclusion, with attention to impact on historically underrepresented and marginalized communities and constituencies. Transferable Skill Development: OSU is committed to ensuring students are prepared for success after graduation through intentional skill development as student employees. The competencies students develop while working at OSU are defined by the National Association of Colleges and Employers (NACE) as the essential skills employers seek in their hires. In this position, students learn and build vital career-readiness skills in the primary areas below:   Communication, Critical Thinking, Professionalism Qualifications: Full Employment Eligibility Requirements can be found here:   https://hr.oregonstate.edu/student-employment-manual/500-employment-eligibility-requirements Must be academically enrolled in a high school, community college, or university and pursuing a program or course of study Must meet Academic Standing Requirements; students on academic suspension are not eligible for employment Must meet the applicable minimal enrollment standard High School student: Regularly enrolled in a high school or participating in a home-schooling program Undergraduate and post-baccalaureate student: 6 credit hours per term Undergraduate international student: 12 credit hours per term* Graduate student officially admitted to Graduate School: 5 credit hours per term Graduate international student officially admitted to Graduate School: 9 credit hours per term* *International students may be allowed to carry fewer hours than specified above and still be considered “full-time” by the United States Citizenship and Immigration Services ( USCIS ). A reduced course load is approved by the   Office of International Services   ( OIS ), and must be provided to the Student Employment Center. Required Qualifications: Is knowledgeable of or willing to learn news gathering and fact checking strategies  Beginner experience shooting or editing video  Willing to learn AP style and work within the SPJ Code of Ethics  Able to work flexible hours including nights, weekends and academic breaks  Able to meet deadlines  Is a strong writer, communicator and comfortable with public speaking  Is organized and reliable  Is experienced with giving and receiving feedback  Working Conditions/Schedule: Employment is through the academic year. May require standing or sitting for extended periods and occasional lifting. Hours are flexible and may require working nights or weekends depending on events and news. Salary Range: Anticipated Start Date: 2026-07-16 Anticipated End Date:"
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/Photographer_JR0000101": {
      "title": "Photographer",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-11\nPosted: Posted 25 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nDepartment: Orange Media Network Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents: A Resume A Cover Letter If available, please include a link to a portfolio of recent photo work For additional information please contact: Jennifer Moody at Moodyjen@oregonstate.edu We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status.   OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire.   Note: All job offers are contingent upon Human Resources final approval Job Description: This recruitment will be used to fill five part-time (a maximum of 24 hours per week) Photographer positions for Orange Media Network at Oregon State University (OSU). The Photographer is responsible for pitching, researching and photographing stories for The Daily Barometer, Beavers Digest, or DAMchic, including print and online, as required by the editor in-chief of the publications or the OMN Photo Chief. The Undergraduate Student Support Services Worker performs a variety of work related to the support and enhancement of the quality of student life at the university. Position Duties: Pitches, accepts and delivers photos that either stand alone or accompany content for OMN Works with OMN writers to accurately and fully reflect stories of news value to the OSU community Provides fair and accurate coverage that represents the story and the people involved Suggests story, photo and/or video coverage to enhance story presentation online Write accurate captions in AP style Attends all staff and team meetings as required by editor(s)-in-chief Transferable Skill Development OSU is committed to ensuring students are prepared for success after graduation through intentional skill development as student employees. The competencies students develop while working at OSU are defined by the National Association of Colleges and Employers (NACE) as the essential skills employers seek in their hires. In this position, students learn and build vital career-readiness skills in the primary areas below:   Communication, Critical Thinking, Professionalism Qualifications: Full Employment Eligibility Requirements can be found here:   https://hr.oregonstate.edu/student-employment-manual/500-employment-eligibility-requirements Must be academically enrolled in a high school, community college, or university and pursuing a program or course of study Must meet Academic Standing Requirements; students on academic suspension are not eligible for employment Must meet the applicable minimal enrollment standard High School student: Regularly enrolled in a high school or participating in a home-schooling program Undergraduate and post-baccalaureate student: 6 credit hours per term Undergraduate international student: 12 credit hours per term* Graduate student officially admitted to Graduate School: 5 credit hours per term Graduate international student officially admitted to Graduate School: 9 credit hours per term* *International students may be allowed to carry fewer hours than specified above and still be considered “full-time” by the United States Citizenship and Immigration Services ( USCIS ). A reduced course load is approved by the   Office of International Services   ( OIS ), and must be provided to the Student Employment Center. Required Qualifications: Is knowledgeable in photo news gathering and fact-checking strategies Able to work flexible hours including nights, weekends and academic breaks Able to meet deadlines Is knowledgeable and experienced in building and maintaining relationships with sources, stakeholders, partners, collaborators and the community Preferred Qualifications: Willing to learn AP Style and work within the SPJ Code of Ethics Is a strong communicator and comfortable with public speaking Is organized and reliable Is comfortable giving and receiving feedback Able to remain professional and calm when working with difficult individuals and situations Working Conditions/Schedule: The Photographer employment dates: Sept. 8, 2026 – June 15, 2027 Able to work flexible hours including nights, weekends and academic breaks. Cover breaking news as needed. Salary Range: Anticipated Start Date: 2026-07-16 Anticipated End Date:"
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/Reporter_JR0000102": {
      "title": "Reporter",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-11\nPosted: Posted 25 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nDepartment: Experiential Learning & Activities Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents: A Resume A Cover Letter with indication of desired start medium (news, lifestyle, sports, fashion) For additional information please contact: Jen Moody at jennifer.moody@oregonstate.edu We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status.   OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire.   Note: All job offers are contingent upon Human Resources final approval Job Description: This recruitment will be used to fill up to 20 part-time (a maximum of 24 hours per week) Reporters for the Orange Media Network at Oregon State University (OSU). The Reporter is responsible for pitching, researching, and writing/creating multimedia stories for The Daily Barometer, Beaver’s Digest, or DAMchic, including print and online, as required by the editor-in-chief of the publications. The Undergraduate Student Clerical Worker performs general office work such as typing, copying, filing, sorting, indexing, reception duties, and accounting tasks. Position Duties: Print Contributor/Reporter/Multimedia Reporter Responsibilities  Pitches, accepts as assigned and develops story ideas that have news value for the OSU community. Conducts research and necessary interviews to produce full stories with relevant sources of at least 400 words. Remains objective and provides fair and accurate coverage. Pursues news in a timely fashion. Cover breaking news as needed. Suggests photo and/or video coverage to enhance story presentation online. Shares stories on social media to reach audience. Attends all staff and team meetings as required by editor(s)-in-chief. Participates in readouts with editors to make any necessary revisions in style or content. Able to act as mentors to new hires   Communication   Respond to emails, messages, team chats and phone calls the same day, professionally representing OMN. Ensuring you are using, checking and responding to project management platform (Basecamp, Discord, etc.). Proactively communicates work status with editor(s). Notify adviser immediately should any ethical, legal, personnel or controversial problems occur. Leadership   Follow all OMN policies and procedures including professionalism and deadlines.  Ensure designs and content are centered on equitable representation and inclusion, with attention to impact on historically underrepresented and marginalized communities and constituencies.  Transferable Skill Development: OSU is committed to ensuring students are prepared for success after graduation through intentional skill development as student employees. The competencies students develop while working at OSU are defined by the National Association of Colleges and Employers (NACE) as the essential skills employers seek in their hires. In this position, students learn and build vital career-readiness skills in the primary areas below:   Communication, Critical Thinking, Professionalism Qualifications: Full Employment Eligibility Requirements can be found here:   https://hr.oregonstate.edu/student-employment-manual/500-employment-eligibility-requirements Must be academically enrolled in a high school, community college, or university and pursuing a program or course of study Must meet Academic Standing Requirements; students on academic suspension are not eligible for employment Must meet the applicable minimal enrollment standard High School student: Regularly enrolled in a high school or participating in a home-schooling program Undergraduate and post-baccalaureate student: 6 credit hours per term Undergraduate international student: 12 credit hours per term* Graduate student officially admitted to Graduate School: 5 credit hours per term Graduate international student officially admitted to Graduate School: 9 credit hours per term* *International students may be allowed to carry fewer hours than specified above and still be considered “full-time” by the United States Citizenship and Immigration Services ( USCIS ). A reduced course load is approved by the   Office of International Services   ( OIS ), and must be provided to the Student Employment Center. Required Qualifications:  Is knowledgeable of or willing to learn news gathering and fact checking strategies  Willing to learn AP style and work within the SPJ Code of Ethics  Able to work flexible hours including nights, weekends and academic breaks  Able to meet deadlines  Is a strong writer, communicator and comfortable with public speaking  Is organized and reliable  Is experienced with giving and receiving feedback  Able to remain professional and calm when working with difficult individuals and situations  Must complete all elements of onboarding curriculum  Working Conditions/Schedule: Employment is Sept. 8, 2026 through the academic year. May require standing or sitting for extended periods and occasional lifting. Hours are flexible and may require working nights or weekends depending on events and news. Salary Range: Anticipated Start Date: 2026-07-16 Anticipated End Date:"
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/Instructor-Pool---2026-2027--Student-Success_JR0000575": {
      "title": "Instructor Pool - 2026/2027: Student Success",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-10\nPosted: Posted 26 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nDepartment: College of Business Special Instructions to Applicants: Applications will be considered throughout the 2026-2027 Academic Year. When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents: 1) A Curriculum Vitae; and 2) Cover Letter describing; your experience, qualifications and interests that have prepared you to teach at the university level in the listed discipline(s); including which discipline(s) you are qualified to teach and the course modalities you are prepared to teach (online, in-person, and/or both). Starting salary will be commensurate with skills, education, and experience. For additional information, please contact: Sandra Neubaum sandy.neubaum@bus.oregonstate.edu (541) 713 8046 Job Summary: The College of Business invites applications for fixed-term, non-tenure track, full/part-time Instructors to teach on a term-by-term basis for the 2026-2027 academic year. Some of these appointments may be reviewed for renewal or transition to an instructional position on an annual basis at the discretion of the Dean . This position is responsible for the instruction, curriculum development, classroom management, and overseeing academic projects as part of the Student Success curriculum. This position will participate in strategic planning efforts that support the mission, vision, and goals for the College of Business as it pertains to the Student Success program. Key Responsibilities: Teach Student Success courses using inclusive, student-centered instructional practices that promote academic success and engagement. Develop course materials, curriculum, assessments, and classroom activities while supporting the delivery of Student Success programming. Plan and implement student success initiatives, including orientation, welcome week, and experiential learning opportunities that support student success. Provide individualized mentoring and holistic support to students while fostering an inclusive and welcoming learning environment. Participate in service activities that support the College, University, profession, and initiatives that advance diversity, equity, inclusion, and student success. Qualifications: Required Qualifications: Master’s degree in Business, Education, College Student Services and Administration, or a closely related field from an accredited university. Experience with supporting students in their personal, professional and academic pursuits in a higher education setting. Demonstrated commitment to educational equity and a commitment to advancing the participation of diverse groups and supporting diverse perspectives. Demonstrated record of effective teaching at a high school level or accredited university. Excellent written and verbal communication skills, utilizing inclusive language and the ability to communicate with tact and diplomacy across diverse cultural boundaries. Excellent teamwork and collaboration skills. High computer proficiency; required qualifications include: strong MS office experience with Word, PowerPoint, Excel and working knowledge of Outlook and Windows/Mac. Ability to maintain professional decorum, as well as know or can demonstrate ability to learn and work within OSU system. This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a criminal history check and be determined to be position qualified as per University Standard: 05-010 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in youth programs may have additional criminal history checks every 24 months. Preferred Qualifications: Experience in utilizing best practices in retaining first year students in a higher education setting. An ability and willingness to learn new programs and applications quickly. Ability to juggle multiple tasks and responsibilities, prioritize appropriately, pay careful attention to details, and meet deadlines. Ability to work with ambiguity. Working Conditions/Schedule: The incumbent is responsible for maintaining a flexible work schedule. Some evenings and weekend work is required. Teaching assignments may include course delivery in any format – site-based in-person, asynchronous online, or hybrid combinations of online, site-based in-person, and/or remote instruction. Salary Range: Salary is commensurate with skills, education and experience. Anticipated Start Date: 2026-12-16 Anticipated End Date: 2027-06-15 We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status. OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire. OSU is a fair chance employer committed to inclusive hiring. We encourage applications from candidates who bring a wide range of lived experience including involvement with the justice system. If this position has “critical or security-sensitive” responsibilities and/or driving duties and you are selected as a finalist, your initial job offer will be contingent upon the results of a job-related pre-employment check (such as a background check, motor vehicle history check, sexual misconduct reference check, etc.). Background check results do not automatically disqualify a candidate. Take a look at our Background Checks website including the for candidates section for more details. If you have questions or concerns about the pre-employment check, please contact OSU’s Employee and Labor Relations team at employee.relations@oregonstate.edu ."
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/Instructor-Pool---2026-2027--Spanish_JR0000523": {
      "title": "Instructor Pool - 2026/2027: Spanish",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-10\nPosted: Posted 26 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nDepartment: World Languages and Cultures Program Special Instructions to Applicants: Applications will be considered throughout the 2026-2027 Academic Year. When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents: 1) A Curriculum Vitae; and 2) Cover Letter indicating how your qualifications and experience have prepared you for this position; and 3) Statement of Teaching For additional information, please contact: Lynn Roylance lynn.roylance@oregonstate.edu 541-737-3847 Job Summary: The World Languages & Cultures Program invites applications for one or more fixed-term, non-tenure-track, full/part-time Spanish Instructor positions on a term-by-term basis for the 2026-2027 academic year. Some of these appointments may be reviewed for renewal or transition to an instructional position on an annual basis at the discretion of the Director. The World Languages and Cultures program offers both in-person and online undergraduate majors and minors in Spanish, French, and German, as well as a dual degree in Global Studies, minors in Linguistics and Japanese, and additional language instruction in ASL, Chinese, Italian, and Korean. It is part of the School of Language, Culture, and Society, which also includes undergraduate and graduate programs in Anthropology & Women, Gender, and Sexuality Studies; an undergraduate program and graduate minor in Ethnic Studies; minors in Indigenous Studies, Latinx Studies, Social Justice, Queer Studies; and a certificate in Food in Culture and Social Justice. Our School values interdisciplinary collaboration, community-centered research, and socially transformative learning.   Key Responsibilities: Instruction Qualifications: Required Qualifications: Master’s degree in   Spanish   or related field.   Evident commitment to educational equity. Preferred Qualifications: PhD in   Spanish   or related field. Demonstrated ability to design,   teach   and assess courses that support inclusive learning principles and promote   equitable   teaching practices, to the   benefit of all learners.   Working Conditions/Schedule: Salary Range: $48,000 - $51,000 Anticipated Start Date: 2026-09-16 Anticipated End Date: 2027-06-15 We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status. OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire. OSU is a fair chance employer committed to inclusive hiring. We encourage applications from candidates who bring a wide range of lived experience including involvement with the justice system. If this position has “critical or security-sensitive” responsibilities and/or driving duties and you are selected as a finalist, your initial job offer will be contingent upon the results of a job-related pre-employment check (such as a background check, motor vehicle history check, sexual misconduct reference check, etc.). Background check results do not automatically disqualify a candidate. Take a look at our Background Checks website including the for candidates section for more details. If you have questions or concerns about the pre-employment check, please contact OSU’s Employee and Labor Relations team at employee.relations@oregonstate.edu ."
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/Assistant-Professor--Socio-Ecological-Systems-of-Forested-Landscapes_JR0000394": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor: Socio-Ecological Systems of Forested Landscapes",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-10\nPosted: Posted 26 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nDepartment: Forest Ecosystems and Society Special Instructions to Applicants: To ensure full consideration, applications must be received by September 24, 2026. Applications will continue to be accepted after the full consideration date, until a sufficient applicant pool has been achieved or the position is filled. The closing date is subject to change without notice to applicants. When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents:   1) A Curriculum Vitae; and   2) A cover letter indicating how your qualifications and experience have prepared you for this position. 3) Statement of Research - please attach as separate document 4) Statement of Teaching - please attach as separate document 5) Publications - please attach as separate document   Starting salary will be commensurate with skills, education, and experience. For additional information please contact: Dana Warren dana.warren@oregonstate.edu Job Summary: The Department of Forest Ecosystems and Society invites applications for a full-time (1.00 FTE), 9-month, tenure-track, Assistant Professor (Socio-Ecological Systems of Forested Landscape) position. The College of Forestry at Oregon State University is home to one of the top-ranked forestry programs in the world, with strengths spanning forest ecosystems, conservation science, recreation and tourism management, natural resources, wood science, and sustainable land management. The Department of Forest Ecosystems and Society is a collaborative and interdisciplinary unit where faculty work across traditional disciplinary boundaries to address complex environmental and societal challenges. Faculty benefit from strong partnerships with public agencies, Tribal Nations, industry, non-governmental organizations, and communities, as well as other units across campus. We seek a scholar whose research and teaching examine interactions among people, institutions, and forested ecosystems, including areas such as social, behavioral, economic, organizational, conservation, recreation, climate solutions, or other human dimensions of natural resource management. We also welcome candidates who bring interdisciplinary perspectives and innovative approaches to understanding and addressing contemporary environmental challenges through the integration of social and biophysical sciences. KEY RESPONSIBILITIES: Develop and m aintain an active, externally funded, nationally recognized research program on socio-ecological systems of forested landscapes and related human-environment interactions. Conduct and publish innovative scholarship that advances understanding of social, behavioral, economic, governance, and ecological dimensions of natural resource and environmental challenges. Teach undergraduate and graduate courses in areas aligned with the candidate’s expertise and departmental needs, including for both on-campus and E-campus instruction. Recruit, advise, mentor, and support the professional development of undergraduate researchers, graduate students, and/or postdoctoral scholars. Collaborate with interdisciplinary teams in the department, college, and across campus, as well as with Tribal, governmental, non-governmental, community, and industry partners. Support departmental, college, and university goals to incorporate diverse perspectives and foster a diverse, equitable, and inclusive community in the department, college, and university. Qualifications: REQUIRED QUALIFICATIONS: A Ph.D. or equivalent in sociology, psychology, geography, forestry, environmental sciences, natural   resources   or closely related   field , earned by the time of appointment.   Demonstrated potential to conduct effective research and scholarly work   in   area   of disciplinary   expertise .   Demonstrated potential to effectively teach undergraduate and graduate level courses (online and in-person) in a manner that is consistent with the principles   identified   in the OSU Quality Teaching   Framework.   Evidence of effective interpersonal and communication skills (written and verba l).     This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a criminal history check and be determined to be position qualified as per University Standard: 05-010 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in youth programs may have additional criminal history checks every 24 months. This position requires driving a university vehicle or personal vehicle on behalf of the university; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a motor vehicle history check, possess and maintain current, valid driver's license in their state of residence, be determined to be position qualified and self-report convictions as per University Policy 05-030.   PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS: Demonstrated potential to integrate social, ecological, and natural resource fields to address environmental challenges in forested landscapes. Demonstrated potential to work effectively in collaborative interdisciplinary teams. Expertise in qualitative and quantitative social science approaches. Experience teaching undergraduate and graduate level courses (online and in-person). Experience with curriculum and/or course development. Evidence of success obtaining external research funding. Evidence of success mentoring undergraduate and/or graduate students. Working Conditions/Schedule: Travel may be required for research, teaching, and outreach Field research may require working outdoors in uneven and rugged terrain to collect field data or samples Work schedule and location are flexible to meet the requirements of active research projects Salary Range: $89.000 - $99,000 Anticipated Start Date: 2027-01-01 Anticipated End Date: We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status. OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire. OSU is a fair chance employer committed to inclusive hiring. We encourage applications from candidates who bring a wide range of lived experience including involvement with the justice system. If this position has “critical or security-sensitive” responsibilities and/or driving duties and you are selected as a finalist, your initial job offer will be contingent upon the results of a job-related pre-employment check (such as a background check, motor vehicle history check, sexual misconduct reference check, etc.). Background check results do not automatically disqualify a candidate. Take a look at our Background Checks website including the for candidates section for more details. If you have questions or concerns about the pre-employment check, please contact OSU’s Employee and Labor Relations team at employee.relations@oregonstate.edu ."
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/Research-Technical-Assistant_JR0000544": {
      "title": "Research Technical Assistant",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-10\nPosted: Posted 26 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nDepartment: Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents: A CV A Research Statement For additional information please contact: Laurent Deluc at delucl@oregonstate.edu We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status.   OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire.   Note: All job offers are contingent upon Human Resources final approval Job Description: This recruitment will be used to fill two (2) undergraduate student part-time (a maximum of 15 hours per week) in Plant Molecular and Genetic Engineering for the Department of Horticulture at Oregon State University (OSU). The two Research Technical Assistants will join a Research Group in the Department of Horticulture at OSU working on functional genomics and gene editing technology in winegrapes. Under the direct supervision of a Research Associate and a PhD student, the two assistants will develop a series of transgenic cassettes via Golden Gate cloning for Agrobacterium tumefaciens-mediated transformation, select transgenic plants, maintain plant tissue culture, and assist with genotyping and phenotyping transformants. The Undergraduate Student Technical Assistant performs routine to complex technical tasks in support of research or administrative work which includes laboratory, green house, field plot, data collection, simple analysis, or the care and maintenance of animals. Position Duties: Perform Golden Gate cloning of transgenic cassette Perform Real-Time PCR Propagate plant tissue material in tissue culture conditions Prepare culture media for plants and bacteria Contribute to common tasks in the lab Transferable Skill Development OSU is committed to ensuring students are prepared for success after graduation through intentional skill development as student employees. The competencies students develop while working at OSU are defined by the National Association of Colleges and Employers (NACE) as the essential skills employers seek in their hires. In this position, students learn and build vital career-readiness skills in the primary areas below:   Communication, Critical Thinking, Teamwork, Technology Qualifications: Full Employment Eligibility Requirements can be found here:   https://hr.oregonstate.edu/student-employment-manual/500-employment-eligibility-requirements Must be academically enrolled in a high school, community college, or university and pursuing a program or course of study Must meet Academic Standing Requirements; students on academic suspension are not eligible for employment Must meet the applicable minimal enrollment standard High School student: Regularly enrolled in a high school or participating in a home-schooling program Undergraduate and post-baccalaureate student: 6 credit hours per term Undergraduate international student: 12 credit hours per term* Graduate student officially admitted to Graduate School: 5 credit hours per term Graduate international student officially admitted to Graduate School: 9 credit hours per term* *International students may be allowed to carry fewer hours than specified above and still be considered “full-time” by the United States Citizenship and Immigration Services ( USCIS ). A reduced course load is approved by the   Office of International Services   ( OIS ), and must be provided to the Student Employment Center. Required Qualifications: Plant Molecular Biology Experience with cloning techniques experience with basic plant molecular biology Preferred Qualifications: Plant Biology Plant tissue propagation trained to work in sterile conditions Working Conditions/Schedule: A minimum of 10 hours per week Salary Range: Anticipated Start Date: 2026-08-24 Anticipated End Date: 2027-09-01"
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/Instructor-Pool---2026-2027--Women--Gender--and-Sexuality-Studies_JR0000522": {
      "title": "Instructor Pool - 2026/2027: Women, Gender, and Sexuality Studies",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-07\nPosted: Posted 29 Days Ago\n\nDepartment: Women, Gender, and Sexuality Studies Special Instructions to Applicants: Applications will be considered throughout the 2026-2027 Academic Year. When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents:   1) A resume/CV; and   2) A cover letter indicating how your qualifications and experience have prepared you for this position and how you meet each of the required qualifications and any preferred qualifications if applicable. 3) Statement of Teaching (upload as a separate document).   For additional information please contact: Lynn Roylance at Lynn.Roylance@oregonstate.edu or 541-737-3847. Job Summary: The  Women, Gender, and Sexuality Studies program  in the School of Language, Culture, and Society invites applications for one or more fixed-term, non-tenure-track, full/part-time Instructor positions on a term-by-term basis for the 2026-2027 academic year. Some of these appointments may be reviewed for renewal or transition to an instructional position on an annual basis at the discretion of the Director. Women, Gender, and Sexuality Studies place women’s concerns, gender analysis, and/or LGBTQ experience at the center of academic study. We recognize that gender and sexuality intersect with other categories of identity and experience, including race, ethnicity, social class, ability, and age. Our courses examine a wide range of topics from multidisciplinary perspectives. We encourage community involvement and activism, and our majors, minors, and graduate students complete internships with social change agencies and organizations. It is part of the School of Language, Culture, and Society, which also includes undergraduate and graduate programs in Anthropology; an undergraduate program and graduate minor in Ethnic Studies; minors in Indigenous Studies, Latinx Studies, Social Justice, Queer Studies; undergraduate majors and minors the World Languages & Cultures Program and a certificate in Food in Culture and Social Justice. Our School values interdisciplinary collaboration, community-centered research, and socially transformative learning. Key Responsibilities: Instruction Qualifications: Required Qualifications: Master’s degree in WGSS or related field. Evident commitment to educational equity. Preferred Qualifications: PhD in WGSS or related field. Demonstrated ability to design, teach and assess courses that support inclusive learning principles and promote equitable teaching practices, to the benefit of all learners. Working Conditions/Schedule: Salary Range: $48,000 - $51,000 Anticipated Start Date: 2026-09-16 Anticipated End Date: 2027-06-15 We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status. OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire. OSU is a fair chance employer committed to inclusive hiring. We encourage applications from candidates who bring a wide range of lived experience including involvement with the justice system. If this position has “critical or security-sensitive” responsibilities and/or driving duties and you are selected as a finalist, your initial job offer will be contingent upon the results of a job-related pre-employment check (such as a background check, motor vehicle history check, sexual misconduct reference check, etc.). Background check results do not automatically disqualify a candidate. Take a look at our Background Checks website including the for candidates section for more details. If you have questions or concerns about the pre-employment check, please contact OSU’s Employee and Labor Relations team at employee.relations@oregonstate.edu ."
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/Integrative-Biology-Academic-Assistant-26-27_JR0000537-1": {
      "title": "Integrative Biology Academic Assistant 26-27",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-07\nPosted: Posted 29 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nDepartment: IB Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents: A Resume A Cover Letter For additional information please contact: Melissa Platt at melissa.platt@oregonstate.edu We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status.   OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire.   Note: All job offers are contingent upon Human Resources final approval Job Description: This recruitment will be used to fill up to 20 part-time (a maximum of 24 hours per week) positions for the Department of Integrative Biology at Oregon State University (OSU). This recruitment is meant to serve as a job pool for faculty as needs arise. The Undergraduate Student Technical Assistant performs routine to complex technical tasks in support of research or administrative work which includes laboratory, green house, field plot, data collection, simple analysis, or the care and maintenance of animals. Position Duties: Duties may include but are not limited to: Proctoring for major field testing Grading lab work, projects, homework, quizzes, exams. Set up and taken down teaching laboratory materials including chemical preparation, equipment checking, glassware cleaning and conduct inventory of lab equipment. Cleaning and preparation in teaching labs Inventorying and preparing order requests for coordinator, cleaning labs, putting away equipment for summer sessions, maintaining facilities and other duties needed by coordinator Light office work including photocopying, printing, laminating, etc. Prepare dissection tools, perform dissections, follow disposal protocols for tissue waste. Accessing controlled rooms that house sensitive materials (cadavers, equipment) Transferable Skill Development OSU is committed to ensuring students are prepared for success after graduation through intentional skill development as student employees. The competencies students develop while working at OSU are defined by the National Association of Colleges and Employers (NACE) as the essential skills employers seek in their hires. In this position, students learn and build vital career-readiness skills in the primary areas below:   Communication, Critical Thinking, Teamwork, Technology, Leadership, Professionalism, Equity & Inclusion, Career & Self Development Qualifications: Full Employment Eligibility Requirements can be found here:   https://hr.oregonstate.edu/student-employment-manual/500-employment-eligibility-requirements Must be academically enrolled in a high school, community college, or university and pursuing a program or course of study Must meet Academic Standing Requirements; students on academic suspension are not eligible for employment Must meet the applicable minimal enrollment standard High School student: Regularly enrolled in a high school or participating in a home-schooling program Undergraduate and post-baccalaureate student: 6 credit hours per term Undergraduate international student: 12 credit hours per term* Graduate student officially admitted to Graduate School: 5 credit hours per term Graduate international student officially admitted to Graduate School: 9 credit hours per term* *International students may be allowed to carry fewer hours than specified above and still be considered “full-time” by the United States Citizenship and Immigration Services ( USCIS ). A reduced course load is approved by the   Office of International Services   ( OIS ), and must be provided to the Student Employment Center. Working Conditions/Schedule: Salary Range: N/A Anticipated Start Date: Anticipated End Date:"
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/Student-Intramural-Sports-Official--Flag-Football-_JR0000540": {
      "title": "Student Intramurals Official (Flag Football)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-07\nPosted: Posted 29 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nDepartment: Recreational Sports Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents: Resume Cover Letter For additional information please contact Matt Macer at matt.macer@oregonstate.edu. We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status. OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire. Note: All job offers are contingent upon Human Resources final approval. Job Description: This recruitment will be used to fill multiple part-time (a maximum of 24 hours per week) Intramural Sports Official (Flag Football) positions for Recreational Sports at Oregon State University ( OSU ). The Intramural Sports Official reports to the Sport Programs professional staff and plays a key role in delivering high-quality recreational sport experiences. Officials are responsible for officiating a variety of Intramural Sports leagues and events, demonstrating sport-specific knowledge, sound judgment, and consistent rule enforcement. This position supports the Sport Programs Mission Statement: to engage the university community in diverse sport and program opportunities that enrich the student learning experience through involvement, leadership, and physical activity. Anticipated Hourly Rate: $ 17.96 Common sports needing officials include: Fall Term: Flag Football, Volleyball, Wheelchair Basketball, Soccer Winter Term: Basketball, Water Polo, Inner Tube Water Polo, Flag Football, Soccer Spring Term: Softball, Kickball, Soccer Employee Expectations Sport Programs staff are expected to model professionalism, leadership, and accountability while contributing to a welcoming and inclusive recreational environment. Professionalism & Policy Knowledge: Learn, model, promote, and consistently enforce Department of Recreational Sports policies, demonstrating reliability, ethical decision-making, and attention to detail. Effective Communication: Communicate professionally, proactively, and respectfully with participants, teammates, supervisors, and administrative staff using clear verbal and written communication. Career & Skill Development: Maintain required certifications, attend orientations, staff meetings, and actively participate in training opportunities to support continuous learning and self-development. Critical Thinking & Problem Solving: Provide high-quality customer service by assessing situations, managing challenges, and utilizing conflict resolution skills that align with departmental policies and mission. Equity, Safety & Inclusion: Promote a safe, fun, and inclusive environment that respects individual differences and supports positive experiences for all participants and staff. Leadership & Growth Mindset: Recognize and reflect on feedback and experiences to be able to articulate transferable skills such as leadership, teamwork, adaptability, and professionalism. We create engaging environments for student growth and success. We inspire healthy living by providing quality recreational and educational opportunities for the Oregon State University community. Our Values in Action As a student employee, you play a vital role in bringing our mission and values to life. We expect every team member to embody the following values in their work: We Care About People : Treat all patrons and colleagues with kindness, courtesy and inclusion. We Are Solution-Oriented : Identify and implement solutions that enhance the member and employee experience. Address conflict in a positive, calm and solution-focused manner. We Are Stewards : Use resources responsibly and ethically while working efficiently. We Are Innovative : Pioneer programs, services and facilities that serve as catalysts for student success. Create space to encourage, share and evolve new ideas. We Are Educators : Foster environments where learning and personal growth thrive for both participants and staff. Grant others the same good intentions you grant yourself. We Extend Respect & Build Trust : Communicate with clarity, transparency and integrity, fostering trust across our community. Every action rooted in our mission and values strengthens our team and makes Recreational Sports a place where everyone belongs, grows and thrives. Position Duties Demonstrate comprehensive knowledge of sport rules and Intramural Sports policies, applying them consistently and fairly. Enforce and interpret sport rules while exercising sound judgment and professionalism in dynamic environments. Utilize critical thinking and conflict management skills to respond to challenging situations and de-escalate disputes effectively. Manage participants and teams in collaboration with Sport Program Associates, demonstrating teamwork and leadership, including: Ensuring completion of team rosters and waivers Monitoring and maintaining crowd control Facilitating directions and court/field assignments for officials, teams, and participants Communicate clearly and respectfully with Intramural Sports participants regarding rules, policies, procedures, and program information. Complete required documentation and paperwork accurately and efficiently using department systems and technology. Assist with facility preparation and clean-up to ensure safe and functional activity spaces. Communicate injuries, safety concerns, suggestions, and operational issues to appropriate staff in a timely and professional manner. Perform additional duties as assigned to support program operations and team success. Transferable Skill Development OSU   is committed to ensuring students are prepared for success after graduation   through intentional skill development as student employees. The competencies students develop while working at   OSU   are defined by the   National Association of Colleges and Employers ( NACE )   as the essential skills employers seek in their hires. In this position, students learn and build vital career-readiness skills in the primary areas below: Communication, Critical Thinking, Leadership Qualifications: Full Employment Eligibility Requirements can be found here:   https://hr.oregonstate.edu/student-employment-manual/500-employment-eligibility-requirements   Must be academically enrolled in a high school, community college, or university and pursuing a program or course of study Must meet Academic Standing Requirements; students on academic suspension are not eligible for employment Must meet the applicable minimal enrollment standard High School student: Regularly enrolled in a high school or participating in a home-schooling program Undergraduate and post-baccalaureate student: 6 credit hours per term Undergraduate international student: 12 credit hours per term* Graduate student officially admitted to Graduate School: 5 credit hours per term Graduate international student officially admitted to Graduate School: 9 credit hours per term* *International students may be allowed to carry fewer hours than specified above and still be considered “full-time” by the United States Citizenship and Immigration Services ( USCIS ). A reduced course load is approved by the   Office of International Services   ( OIS ), and must be provided to the Student Employment Center. Preferred Qualifications Demonstrated knowledge of Sport Programs offerings with the ability to officiate or prior officiating experience. Ability to provide positive, consistent customer service through strong interpersonal and communication skills. Demonstrated problem-solving, conflict management, and critical thinking abilities, including effective oral and written communication. Awareness of safety considerations, risk management practices, and emergency response procedures. Commitment to equity, inclusion, and respect for individual differences, with an interest in student development and leadership. Ability to work both independently and collaboratively while upholding Department of Recreational Sports standards of conduct, professionalism, and accountability. Working Conditions/Schedule: Period of Employment: One academic year (continuing employment is contingent upon satisfactory completion of a 30 day probationary period and satisfactory job performance as evaluated by immediate supervisor). 8-15 hours per week. Hours will vary between evenings 3:30pm-Midnight and weekends 9:00am-Midnight Compensation: Student employees are paid an hourly wage consistent with the OSU Student Employment Pay Rate Guidelines. The Department of Recreational Sports identifies pay ranges base upon job duties, level of responsibility and complexity of the work to be performed Salary Range: Anticipated Start Date: 2026-10-06 Anticipated End Date:"
    },
