    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/CoE-ENG--ULA_JR0000182": {
      "title": "CoE ENG+ ULA",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-07-16\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nPart time\n\nDepartment: College of Engineering Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents: A Resume A Cover Letter For additional information please contact: Paula de Szoeke at paula.deszoeke@oregonstate.edu We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status.   This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a Criminal History Check and be determined to be position qualified as per OSU Standard 576-055-0000 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in Youth Programs may have additional Criminal History Checks every 2 years.   OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire.   Note: All job offers are contingent upon Human Resources final approval Job Description: This recruitment will be used to fill 50 part-time (a maximum of 24 hours per week) for an ENG+ Undergraduate Learning Assistants (ULAs) at Oregon State University (OSU) in the College of Engineering (CoE). This application is one step in the application process, which also includes responding to a survey sent to engineering undergraduate students May 18, 2026, asking for information such as past experiences, interests, and availability. The Undergraduate Student Support Services Worker performs a variety of work related to the support and enhancement of the quality of student life at the university. Position Duties: Complete trainings Holding office hours Grading Assisting in 30-person lab sessions Leading study sessions Tutoring Attending weekly meetings Copying materials for the course Entering scores into Canvas Mentoring and supporting other students Other tasks as needed by the supervising Instructor Transferable Skill Development OSU is committed to ensuring students are prepared for success after graduation through intentional skill development as student employees. The competencies students develop while working at OSU are defined by the National Association of Colleges and Employers (NACE) as the essential skills employers seek in their hires. In this position, students learn and build vital career-readiness skills in the primary areas below:   Communication, Teamwork, Professionalism, Equity & Inclusion Qualifications: Full Employment Eligibility Requirements can be found here:   https://hr.oregonstate.edu/student-employment-manual/500-employment-eligibility-requirements Must be academically enrolled in a high school, community college, or university and pursuing a program or course of study Must meet Academic Standing Requirements; students on academic suspension are not eligible for employment Must meet the applicable minimal enrollment standard High School student: Regularly enrolled in a high school or participating in a home-schooling program Undergraduate and post-baccalaureate student: 6 credit hours per term Undergraduate international student: 12 credit hours per term* Graduate student officially admitted to Graduate School: 5 credit hours per term Graduate international student officially admitted to Graduate School: 9 credit hours per term* *International students may be allowed to carry fewer hours than specified above and still be considered “full-time” by the United States Citizenship and Immigration Services ( USCIS ). A reduced course load is approved by the   Office of International Services   ( OIS) and must be provided to the Student Employment Center. Required Qualifications: Engaging background Depending on the course, successful completion may require support from the course Student in good standing, including maintaining a strong academic integrity record within CoE Additional Required Qualifications: This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a Criminal History Check and be determined to be position qualified as per OSU Standard 576-055-0000 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in Youth Programs may have additional Criminal History Checks every 2 years. Working Conditions/Schedule: • Work may be performed in classrooms, labs, or other office locations. • Expect a minimum of 8 hours per week, and on average 10 hours per week. Salary Range: Anticipated Start Date: 2026-08-24 Anticipated End Date: 2026-10-01"
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/College-of-Engineering-Peer-Tutor_JR0000197": {
      "title": "College of Engineering Peer Tutor",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-07-16\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nPart time\n\nDepartment: College of Engineering Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents: A Resume/Vita Unofficial Transcript For additional information please contact: Brittany Altamirano at Brittany.altamirano@oregonstate.edu We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status. This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a Criminal History Check and be determined to be position qualified as per OSU Standard 576-055-0000 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in Youth Programs may have additional Criminal History Checks every 2 years.   OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire.   Note: All job offers are contingent upon Human Resources final approval Job Description: This recruitment will be used to fill 10 part-time (a maximum of 24 hours per week) for the College of Engineering at Oregon State University (OSU). Appointments are expected to begin the week prior to the start of Fall Term 2026 to allow for onboarding and training. Peer Tutors serve as student leaders and learning partners, helping fellow engineering students better understand specific subject areas, deepen course understanding, and develop strategies for academic success. They offer one-on-one in key engineering courses and foster an inclusive, welcoming environment that supports academic development and improvement In addition to tutoring, Peer Tutors are also a part of a vibrant peer educator community, attending and engaging in skill-building opportunities and collaborative reflection activities throughout the academic year. The COE Peer Tutoring Program is a core part of the College of Engineering’s student success ecosystem. Peer Tutors play an essential role in building a supportive community of learners and a sense of belonging for all COE students- in Corvallis, Ecampus, and Cascades campuses alike. The Undergraduate Student Support Services Worker performs a variety of work related to the support and enhancement of the quality of student life at the university. Position Duties: Provide peer-to-peer tutoring support in assigned subject areas, in both in-person and online formats. Offer academic support through scheduled sessions and drop-ins. Welcome and support all students seeking tutoring, fostering a respectful and inclusive environment. Help students learn study habits and strategies. Serve as a resource and role model for student success in engineering. Attend and engage with mandatory orientations, team meetings, professional development opportunities and workshops. Regularly engage in community-building activities and reflective practices with fellow tutors. Use tutor tracking systems to manage appointments, log student contact, and report data. Communicate proactively with the COE Peer Tutoring Coordinator about scheduling conflicts or student concerns. Qualifications: Full Employment Eligibility Requirements can be found here:   https://hr.oregonstate.edu/student-employment-manual/500-employment-eligibility-requirements Must be academically enrolled in a high school, community college, or university and pursuing a program or course of study Must meet Academic Standing Requirements; students on academic suspension are not eligible for employment Must meet the applicable minimal enrollment standard High School student: Regularly enrolled in a high school or participating in a home-schooling program Undergraduate and post-baccalaureate student: 6 credit hours per term Undergraduate international student: 12 credit hours per term* Graduate student officially admitted to Graduate School: 5 credit hours per term Graduate international student officially admitted to Graduate School: 9 credit hours per term* *International students may be allowed to carry fewer hours than specified above and still be considered “full-time” by the United States Citizenship and Immigration Services ( USCIS ). A reduced course load is approved by the   Office of International Services   ( OIS ), and must be provided to the Student Employment Center. Additional Required Qualifications: This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a Criminal History Check and be determined to be position qualified as per OSU Standard 576-055-0000 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in Youth Programs may have additional Criminal History Checks every 2 years. Preferred Qualifications: Minimum of two completed terms at OSU (one term for transfer students). Minimum cumulative GPA of 3.25. Prior experience tutoring, mentoring, or supporting others (formal or informal). Experience or comfort with asking questions, building relationships, and helping peers to problem-solve. Strong listening and communication skills. Experience in service or leadership roles. Familiarity with the engineering curriculum and academic support resources at OSU. Students with Federal Work Study and meet the minimum qualifications will receive priority. Transferable Skill Development OSU is committed to ensuring students are prepared for success after graduation through intentional skill development as student employees. The competencies students develop while working at OSU are defined by the National Association of Colleges and Employers (NACE) as the essential skills employers seek in their hires. In this position, students learn and build vital career-readiness skills in the primary areas below:   Communication, Teamwork, Professionalism, Equity & Inclusion Working Conditions/Schedule: The student worker may be scheduled up to a maximum of 24 hours per week, depending on tutoring demand and student employee availability. Work will be conducted in-person at a location in one of the College of Engineering buildings at OSU, with some limited remote tutoring opportunities available. Salary Range: Anticipated Start Date: 2026-09-01 Anticipated End Date:"
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/Horse-Center-Crew-Member_JR0000242-1": {
      "title": "Horse Center Crew Member",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-07-16\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nPart time\n\nDepartment: College of Agricultural Sciences | Animal and Rangeland Sciences Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents: A Resume A Cover Letter highlighting experience working with horses For additional information please contact: Dawn Sherwood at dawn.sherwood@oregonstate.edu We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status. Criminal History Check Statement (CHC): This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a Criminal History Check and be determined to be position qualified as per OSU Standard 576-055-0000 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in Youth Programs may have additional Criminal History Checks every 2 years.   Motor Vehicle History Check Statement (MVHC): This position requires driving a University vehicle or a personal vehicle on behalf of the University; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a Motor Vehicle History Check, possess and maintain a current, valid driver's license in their state of residence, be determined to be position qualified and self-report convictions (as per Voluntary and Compulsory Driver Standards OSU Standard 125-155-0200) as per OSU Standard 576-056-0000 et seq.   OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire.   Note: All job offers are contingent upon Human Resources final approval Job Description: This recruitment will be used to create a pool of part-time (a maximum of 20 hours per week during the school year; higher during holidays and summer break) Horse Center Crew Member positions for the Department of Animal and Rangeland Sciences at Oregon State University (OSU). This position is for the care and maintenance of horses. Typical duties include cleaning stalls, feeding, watering, and handling of horses. The Undergraduate Student Technical Assistant performs routine to complex technical tasks in support of research or administrative work which includes laboratory, green house, field plot, data collection, simple analysis, or the care and maintenance of animals. Position Duties: Cleaning stalls, paddocks, and aisles in barns. Maintaining horse stall bedding. Following feed schedule and guidelines. Assisting with putting out horses from barns to pastures or bringing them back in at night. Assisting with maintaining the facility. Other duties as assigned. Transferable Skill Development OSU is committed to ensuring students are prepared for success after graduation through intentional skill development as student employees. The competencies students develop while working at OSU are defined by the National Association of Colleges and Employers (NACE) as the essential skills employers seek in their hires. In this position, students learn and build vital career-readiness skills in the primary areas below:   Communication, Teamwork, Professionalism, Career & Self Development Qualifications: Full Employment Eligibility Requirements can be found here:   https://hr.oregonstate.edu/student-employment-manual/500-employment-eligibility-requirements Must be academically enrolled in a high school, community college, or university and pursuing a program or course of study Must meet Academic Standing Requirements; students on academic suspension are not eligible for employment Must meet the applicable minimal enrollment standard High School student: Regularly enrolled in a high school or participating in a home-schooling program Undergraduate and post-baccalaureate student: 6 credit hours per term Undergraduate international student: 12 credit hours per term* Graduate student officially admitted to Graduate School: 5 credit hours per term Graduate international student officially admitted to Graduate School: 9 credit hours per term* *International students may be allowed to carry fewer hours than specified above and still be considered “full-time” by the United States Citizenship and Immigration Services ( USCIS ). A reduced course load is approved by the   Office of International Services   ( OIS ), and must be provided to the Student Employment Center. Required Qualifications: Must be dependable, possess a strong work ethic, and be able to work without supervision in a team setting. Must possess strong communication skills and the ability to follow directions. Have a willingness to learn and complete any and all assigned tasks. Because the work location is off-campus, employees must have a reliable form of motorized transportation to get to the horse center facilities. Additional Required Qualifications: Criminal History Check Statement (CHC): This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a Criminal History Check and be determined to be position qualified as per OSU Standard 576-055-0000 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in Youth Programs may have additional Criminal History Checks every 2 years. Motor Vehicle History Check Statement (MVHC): This position requires driving a University vehicle or a personal vehicle on behalf of the University; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a Motor Vehicle History Check, possess and maintain a current, valid driver's license in their state of residence, be determined to be position qualified and self-report convictions (as per Voluntary and Compulsory Driver Standards OSU Standard 125-155-0200) as per OSU Standard 576-056-0000 et seq. Preferred Qualifications: Preference will be given to student applicants who have previous experience working with horses including: The ability to recognize illness, colic, lameness, or other ailments. Experience handling a wide variety of horses including young stock. Aptitude to recognize behavior changes. Knowledge of feed identification and nutrition basics. Basic equine first aid skills. Working Conditions/Schedule: • Typical duties include cleaning stalls, feeding, watering, and handling of horses. The employee will perform weekly tasks and projects around the Horse Center, as needed. • Employees are expected to work every-other-weekend and partial holidays. • Employee must be available to work weekdays between 5:30am -8am. • Work is performed in barns and outdoors at the OSU Horse Center located on NW Walnut Dr. in Corvallis, OR. Salary Range: Anticipated Start Date: 2026-07-30 Anticipated End Date:"
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/CBEE-Office-Support-AY26-27_JR0000180": {
      "title": "CBEE Office Support AY26-27",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-07-14\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nPart time\n\nDepartment: ECB Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents: 1. A Resume/Vita 2. A cover letter indicating how your qualifications and experience have prepared you for this position   For additional information please contact: Jennifer Moore at jennifer.moore@oregonstate.edu   We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status.   This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a criminal history check and be determined to be position qualified as per OSU Standard 576-055-0000 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in youth programs may have additional criminal history checks every 24 months.   Note: All job offers are contingent upon Human Resources final approval.   OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire. Job Description: This recruitment will be used to fill multiple part-time (a maximum of 24 hours per week) Office Support positions for the School of Chemical, Biological, and Environmental Engineering (CBEE) at Oregon State University (OSU). This position serves as an important ambassador for CBEE, supporting both office operations and instructional laboratory activities. The student employee assists the Office Manager by providing professional, welcoming front-desk reception and customer service to faculty, staff, students, and visitors. The position also supports the Development Engineer by assisting with equipment organization, maintenance, inventory management, and preparation of teaching laboratories for instructional use. Students in this role are expected to work effectively with administrators, faculty, staff, students, and visitors while representing CBEE in a courteous, professional, and customer-focused manner. 45% Office and Clerical Support: Serves as the first point of contact for the school by answering phone calls, greeting visitors, and providing front-desk reception services. Delivers courteous, professional customer service to faculty, staff, students, and visitors. Receives incoming and outgoing mail and packages. Assists with document preparation, confidential data entry, and information gathering. Supports the coordination and follow-up of work orders and maintenance or repair requests for office spaces and equipment. 40% Teaching Lab support: Assists with the setup and teardown of laboratory class experiments. Assists with troubleshooting equipment, maintenance, and basic repairs. Supports laboratory operations and other activities as needed. Assists with inventory organization and preparation of laboratory materials and equipment. Supports laboratory safety and cleanliness by following established procedures and maintaining organized workspaces. 10% Facilities support: Assists with setup and teardown for school events and attends events as schedules permit. Performs light maintenance and organizational tasks. Assists with the installation, relocation, and movement of equipment and furnishings. 5%: Other duties as assigned based on school need. Transferable Skill Development: OSU is committed to ensuring students are prepared for success after graduation through intentional skill development as student employees. The competencies students develop while working at OSU are defined by the National Association of Colleges and Employers (NACE) as the essential skills employers seek in their hires.    In this position, students learn and build vital career-readiness skills in the primary areas below: Communication, Critical Thinking, Teamwork, and Professionalism his position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a criminal history check and be determined to be position qualified as per OSU Standard 576-055-0000 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in youth programs may have additional criminal history checks every 24 months. Qualifications: Full Employment Eligibility Requirements can be found here:   https://hr.oregonstate.edu/student-employment-manual/500-employment-eligibility-requirements   Must be academically enrolled in a high school, community college, or university and pursuing a program or course of study Must meet Academic Standing Requirements; students on academic suspension are not eligible for employment Must meet the applicable minimal enrollment standard High School student: Regularly enrolled in a high school or participating in a home-schooling program Undergraduate and post-baccalaureate student: 6 credit hours per term Undergraduate international student: 12 credit hours per term* Graduate student officially admitted to Graduate School: 5 credit hours per term Graduate international student officially admitted to Graduate School: 9 credit hours per term* *International students may be allowed to carry fewer hours than specified above and still be considered “full-time” by the United States Citizenship and Immigration Services ( USCIS ). A reduced course load is approved by the   Office of International Services   ( OIS ), and must be provided to the Student Employment Center. Required Qualifications : Engineering undergraduate major Strong attention to detail and organizational skills. Strong interpersonal and communication skills, with the ability to interact professionally and effectively with individuals from diverse backgrounds and experiences in a busy office environment. Experience using hand tools, laboratory equipment, or technical instrumentation. Proficiency with Microsoft Office applications, and using online collaboration and file management tools, or the ability to learn software programs quickly. Demonstrated ability to work independently with minimal supervision and collaboratively as part of a team. Ability to maintain confidentiality and exercise discretion when handling sensitive information. Preferred Qualifications : Major in Environmental Engineering, Chemical Engineering, or Bioengineering. Ability to learn the operation, maintenance, and troubleshooting of laboratory equipment. Familiarity with laboratory safety practices and procedures. Previous customer service, reception, or office experience. Strong interpersonal and customer service skills. Demonstrated reliability, professionalism, initiative, and accountability. Experience supporting laboratory, instructional, or technical environments. Ability to use technology and office equipment, including mobile devices, phones, and digital cameras. Willingness to receive coaching and feedback and apply it to customer service, communication, and workplace interactions. Working Conditions/Schedule: Work may be performed in classrooms, labs, or copy/supply rooms. Must be able to work between 5 – 20 hours per week. Salary Range: Anticipated Start Date: 2026-07-13 Anticipated End Date: 2027-06-30"
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/CBEE-Undergrad-Learning-Assistant--AY26-27-_JR0000148": {
      "title": "CBEE Undergrad Learning Assistant (AY26-27)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-07-14\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nPart time\n\nDepartment: ECB Special Instructions to Applicants: For additional information please contact: Jennifer Moore at jennifer.moore@oregonstate.edu   We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status. This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a criminal history check and be determined to be position qualified as per OSU Standard 576-055-0000 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in youth programs may have additional criminal history checks every 24 months.   Note: All job offers are contingent upon Human Resources final approval.   OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire.   Job Description: This recruitment will be used to fill multiple part-time (a maximum of 24 hours per week) Undergraduate Learning Assistant (ULA) positions for the School of Chemical, Biological, and Environmental Engineering (CBEE) at Oregon State University (OSU). This position performs a wide variety of work that supports the quality of education in CBEE. This work includes mentoring, checking off work, assisting with holding office hours, assisting with labs, and attending TA meetings. These duties will vary depending on the specific course support needs, and vary from 3-20 hours per week, depending on the workload and assignment. 90% Course Support : Duties include helping with 30-person studio sections, tutoring, leading study sessions, attending weekly meetings, holding office hours, copying materials for courses, and other tasks as needed by the supervising instructor. This position may also assist with grading via a grading rubric. Breakdown will vary by course. 10% Training : Attend and complete a pedagogy training course. Transferable Skill Development OSU is committed to ensuring students are prepared for success after graduation through intentional skill development as student employees. The competencies students develop while working at OSU are defined by the National Association of Colleges and Employers (NACE) as the essential skills employers seek in their hires.    In this position, students learn and build vital career-readiness skills in the primary areas below: Communication, Critical Thinking, Teamwork, and Leadership Qualifications: Full Employment Eligibility Requirements can be found here:   https://hr.oregonstate.edu/student-employment-manual/500-employment-eligibility-requirements   Must be academically enrolled in a high school, community college, or university and pursuing a program or course of study Must meet Academic Standing Requirements; students on academic suspension are not eligible for employment Must meet the applicable minimal enrollment standard High School student: Regularly enrolled in a high school or participating in a home-schooling program Undergraduate and post-baccalaureate student: 6 credit hours per term Undergraduate international student: 12 credit hours per term* Graduate student officially admitted to Graduate School: 5 credit hours per term Graduate international student officially admitted to Graduate School: 9 credit hours per term* *International students may be allowed to carry fewer hours than specified above and still be considered “full-time” by the United States Citizenship and Immigration Services ( USCIS ). A reduced course load is approved by the   Office of International Services   ( OIS ), and must be provided to the Student Employment Center. Required Qualifications : Engineering undergraduate major Sophomore or above Successful completion of the course they are supporting with a B or higher. Overall GPA greater than 2.9/4.0 Knowledge of Canvas Strong interpersonal skills and interacting with people from diverse backgrounds Attention to detail and organization Demonstrable ability to work independently and in a team environment Preferred Qualifications : Environmental Engineering, Chemical Engineering or Bioengineering major Junior or above Successful completion of the course they are supporting with a B+ or higher Overall GPA greater than 3.0/Pre-core GPA greater than 3.2 Comfortable leading sections of a class with 25-30 students each Team player Students must complete  FERPA  training prior to beginning work. This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a Criminal History Check and be determined to be position qualified as per OSU Standard 576-055-0000 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in Youth Programs may have additional Criminal History Checks every 2 years. Working Conditions/Schedule: Work may be performed in classrooms, labs, or copy/supply rooms. Must be able to work between 5 – 20 hours per week. Salary Range: Anticipated Start Date: 2026-07-13 Anticipated End Date: 2027-06-30"
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/Assistant-Professor_JR0000039": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-07-13\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nFull time\n\nDepartment: Special Instructions to Applicants: To ensure full consideration, applications must be received by 10/1/26. Applications will continue to be accepted after the full consideration date, until a sufficient applicant pool has been achieved or the position is filled. The closing date is subject to change without notice to applicants. When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents:   1) A Resume/CV; including in bold the top five most relevant publications (please provide DOI when available). In-review or in-preparation (completed projects only) papers may be included as a citation in the CV list for early-career candidates if the projects and its findings are also referenced in your statement of research. The CV should clearly indicate experiences or accomplishments that support the minimum and preferred qualifications.   2) A Cover letter; not to exceed 2 pages, that includes an executive summary of your research and teaching statements that addresses why your research and teaching is a strong fit for an integrative biology department that is seeking to bolster its expertise in organismal biology. 3) A Statement of Research; not to exceed 2 pages, that summarizes your research interests and vision. 4) A Statement of Teaching; not to exceed 1 page, that summarizes your teaching and mentorship experience, philosophy and inclusive, evidence-based practices.   Starting salary will be commensurate with skills, education, and experience. Letters of Reference will be requested for those advanced to the reference stage. At that time, you will be asked to provide contact information for your referees who will be sent a secure quicklink that will allow them to upload the requested letters of reference on your behalf.   For additional information please contact: Tara Bevandich Tara.bevandich@oregonstate.edu 541-737-5336 Job Summary: The Department of Integrative Biology at Oregon State University (OSU) invites applications for three full-time (1.00 FTE), 9-month, tenure-track Assistant Professor positions in the field of integrative biology. Oregon State University is a public R1 research university and the largest university in the state of Oregon, with an enrollment of over 35,000 students and a sponsored project research budget of over 400 million dollars in 2025. Within Oregon State, the College of Science is dedicated to research and education in exact and life sciences. The College is made of seven departments, deeply committed to upholding the values of inclusive excellence. Through our rich collection of people, ideas and perspectives, we engage in a healthy, constructive dialogue to learn, inform our research, and make discoveries. Successful candidates are expected to develop vigorous, externally funded research programs with a primary focus on understanding processes within organisms that drive performance. Organismal-level research that is integrated with and/or has clear implications for ecological or evolutionary processes is preferred. Research may include theoretical, experimental, comparative and/or observational approaches in laboratory and/or field settings. The candidates will also contribute to a vibrant, inclusive and collaborative department, including through mentorship of graduate and undergraduate students, teaching within the biology and zoology degree programs, and service to the university and profession. More information about the department is available at http://ib.oregonstate.edu . Key Responsibilities: Scholarship and Creative Activity Teaching, Advising and Other Assigned Duties Service Qualifications: Required Qualifications: Ph.D. (or foreign equivalent) in Biology or related field. Research with a primary focus on understanding processes within organisms that drive performance and may affect processes at ecological or evolutionary scales. Demonstrated ability or potential to establish an extramurally funded research program of high impact and longevity. Demonstrated ability or potential to instruct effectively using evidence-based practices at the undergraduate or graduate level Demonstrated ability or potential to mentor undergraduate and graduate students. Demonstrated ability to collaborate with and engage with colleagues in a department, research or community group. This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a criminal history check and be determined to be position qualified as per University Standard: 05-010 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in youth programs may have additional criminal history checks every 24 months. Preferred (Special) Qualifications: Relevant post-doctoral experience, including academic or professional positions, and especially training that afford the candidate a unique, integrative perspective. Research that spans multiple biological levels or that utilizes multi-pronged approaches (e.g., field, laboratory, theory).     Experience in interdisciplinary or transdisciplinary collaboration. Demonstrable commitment to inclusive teaching and mentorship practices. Experience teaching in multiple modalities, including traditional classroom, online and experiential settings. Working Conditions/Schedule: Salary Range: $85,000-$100,000 Anticipated Start Date: 2027-09-16 Anticipated End Date: We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status. OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire. OSU is a fair chance employer committed to inclusive hiring. We encourage applications from candidates who bring a wide range of lived experience including involvement with the justice system. If this position has “critical or security-sensitive” responsibilities and/or driving duties and you are selected as a finalist, your initial job offer will be contingent upon the results of a job-related pre-employment check (such as a background check, motor vehicle history check, sexual misconduct reference check, etc.). Background check results do not automatically disqualify a candidate. Take a look at our Background Checks website including the for candidates section for more details. If you have questions or concerns about the pre-employment check, please contact OSU’s Employee and Labor Relations team at employee.relations@oregonstate.edu ."
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/Undergraduate-Student-Technical-Assistant---Erdem-Coleri_JR0000040": {
      "title": "Undergraduate Student Technical Assistant - Erdem Coleri",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-07-09\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nPart time\n\nDepartment: ECC Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents: 1. A Resume/Vita 2. A cover letter indicating how your qualifications and experience have prepared you for this position   For additional information please contact: Erdem Coleri at erdem.coleri@oregonstate.edu   We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status. Note: All job offers are contingent upon Human Resources final approval.   OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire. Job Description: This recruitment will be used to fill several part-time (a maximum of 24 hours per week) Undergraduate Student Technical Assistant positions for the School of Civil and Construction Engineering at Oregon State University (OSU). Work will be performed in the Asphalt Materials and Pavements lab in the preparation, instrumentation, testing and cleanup of the testing of asphalt concrete and asphalt materials. Students will perform job duties in the Asphalt Materials and Pavements Lab located on campus. Transferable Skill Development OSU is committed to ensuring students are prepared for success after graduation through intentional skill development as student employees. The competencies students develop while working at OSU are defined by the National Association of Colleges and Employers (NACE) as the essential skills employers seek in their hires.  In this position, students learn and build vital career-readiness skills in the primary areas below: Communication, Critical Thinking, Teamwork, and Professionalism Qualifications: Full Employment Eligibility Requirements can be found here:   https://hr.oregonstate.edu/student-employment-manual/500-employment-eligibility-requirements   Must be academically enrolled in a high school, community college, or university and pursuing a program or course of study Must meet Academic Standing Requirements; students on academic suspension are not eligible for employment Must meet the applicable minimal enrollment standard High School student: Regularly enrolled in a high school or participating in a home-schooling program Undergraduate and post-baccalaureate student: 6 credit hours per term Undergraduate international student: 12 credit hours per term* Graduate student officially admitted to Graduate School: 5 credit hours per term Graduate international student officially admitted to Graduate School: 9 credit hours per term* *International students may be allowed to carry fewer hours than specified above and still be considered “full-time” by the United States Citizenship and Immigration Services ( USCIS ). A reduced course load is approved by the   Office of International Services   ( OIS ), and must be provided to the Student Employment Center. Complete safety training. Protection gear is necessary and provided by the Laboratory. Must be able to lift 25 kg. Working Conditions/Schedule: Lab environment that is semi-open environment subject to colder temperatures in winter, warm and humid conditions in summer Salary Range: Anticipated Start Date: 2026-07-08 Anticipated End Date:"
    },
