    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/OSU_Careers_Site/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/Construction-Contracts-Officer_JR0000693": {
      "title": "Construction Contracts Officer",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-28\nPosted: Posted 18 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nDepartment: Procurement, Payment and Travel Special Instructions to Applicants: Special Instructions to Applicants: To ensure full consideration, applications must be received by September 09, 2026. Applications will continue to be accepted after the full consideration date, until a sufficient applicant pool has been achieved or the position is filled. The closing date is subject to change without notice to applicants.  When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents:    1) A resume 2) A cover letter indicating how your qualifications and experience have prepared you for this position.    Starting salary will be commensurate with skills, education, and experience.  For additional information please contact: Christine Atwood Christine.Atwood@oregonstate.edu Job Summary: The Office of Procurement, Payment and Travel is seeking a Construction Contracts Officer. This is a full-time (1.00 FTE), 12-month, professional faculty position. Join Oregon State University's Construction Contracts Administration team in a challenging and rewarding role that supports the delivery of facilities and infrastructure projects across OSU's statewide enterprise. From new buildings and major renovations to infrastructure improvements and specialized construction projects, the work of this position directly contributes to creating and maintaining the spaces where students learn, researchers innovate, and the university's mission comes to life. Construction Contracts Administration is part of the Office of Procurement, Payment and Travel (OPPT), a highly collaborative organization that partners with departments across the university to deliver responsive, transparent, and customer-focused services. OPPT values teamwork, relationship-building, continuous improvement, and innovative problem-solving, recognizing that strong partnerships are essential to supporting the University's teaching, research, and outreach mission. As a member of the Construction Contracts Administration team, the Construction Contracts Officer serves as a trusted advisor and subject matter expert in construction procurement and contract administration. Working closely with project managers, architects, engineers, contractors, consultants, and university stakeholders, this position leads complex construction and design procurements and supports projects throughout the project lifecycle. The role offers opportunities to solve unique challenges, navigate complex contracting issues, and contribute to projects that have a lasting impact on the OSU community. We are seeking a collaborative professional who enjoys analyzing complex situations, building strong working relationships, and developing practical solutions while balancing compliance requirements, risk management considerations, and project objectives. The ideal candidate will bring experience in construction contracting, sound professional judgment, strong communication skills, and a commitment to customer service and continuous improvement. This position offers the opportunity to work on a diverse portfolio of projects, expand expertise in public construction procurement and contract administration, and contribute to a highly collaborative team dedicated to supporting the University's strategic goals through effective procurement and contracting practices. Key Responsibilities:   Lead and administer procurement processes for construction and design services, including development of solicitations, evaluation support, contract execution, and project closeout. Develop, negotiate, and manage construction-related contracts, amendments, change orders, and other contract documents throughout the project lifecycle. Provide guidance and subject matter expertise regarding public construction contracting requirements, procurement strategies, contract administration, and regulatory compliance. Collaborate with project managers, architects, engineers, contractors, consultants, and university stakeholders to support successful delivery of capital improvement and infrastructure projects. Ensure compliance with applicable laws, regulations, policies, and industry standards, including prevailing wage requirements, public contracting requirements, and contract risk management practices. Review and administer construction-related insurance, bonding, and other contractual risk management requirements. Contribute to the continuous improvement of construction procurement and contract administration practices, templates, procedures, and customer service initiatives. Qualifications: Required Qualifications: Bachelor's degree in Business Administration, Public Administration, Construction Management, Engineering, Architecture, Law, or a field related to the duties of the position and three (3) years of professional experience in construction procurement, construction contracting, construction contract administration, capital project delivery, design and construction services procurement, public construction contracting, or a closely related field; OR An equivalent combination of education and experience that includes six (6) years of professional experience in construction procurement, construction contracting, construction contract administration, capital project delivery, design and construction services procurement, public construction contracting, or a closely related field. Experience drafting, administering, and managing construction-related solicitations, contracts, amendments, change orders, or other procurement and contract documents. Experience interpreting and applying laws, administrative rules, policies, procedures, and contractual requirements in support of procurement and contracting activities. Experience simultaneously managing multiple procurements, contracts, projects, or assignments with competing priorities and deadlines. Demonstrated ability to analyze complex information and communicate effectively with diverse stakeholders through both written and verbal communication. Experience using Microsoft Office applications and the ability to quickly learn and utilize specialized procurement, contract management, and enterprise systems. This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a criminal history check and be determined to be position qualified as per University Standard: 05-010 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in youth programs may have additional criminal history checks every 24 months. Preferred Qualifications: The ideal candidate will possess substantial experience in public construction procurement and construction contract administration and be capable of independently managing complex construction acquisitions with minimal training or oversight. Preference will be given to candidates who demonstrate advanced knowledge of construction-specific contracting practices, including project delivery methods, change order administration, prevailing wage compliance, bonding and insurance requirements, construction contract risk management, and public sector construction contracting requirements. Preference will be given to candidates with one or more of the following: Experience performing procurement, contracting, or contract administration functions for public construction projects within higher education, state government, local government, or other public sector organizations. Experience administering public improvement or capital construction contracts, including change orders, claims, contract modifications, project closeout, and contractor compliance requirements. Experience with Oregon public contracting requirements, including Bureau of Labor and Industries (BOLI) Prevailing Wage Law compliance. Experience with federally funded construction projects and applicable federal procurement and prevailing wage requirements, including Davis-Bacon Act compliance. Experience evaluating construction-related insurance, bonding, and risk management requirements. Experience working with construction contract law, project delivery methods, and industry-standard construction contract documents. Professional certification related to procurement, contract management, construction management, or project management, such as CPPO, CPPB, CPCM, CCM, PMP, or equivalent. Master's degree in Business Administration, Public Administration, Construction Management, or a related field. Demonstrated commitment to advancing inclusive procurement practices and supporting supplier diversity initiatives. Working Conditions/Schedule: Salary Range: $85,000-$100,000 Anticipated Start Date: 2026-10-01 Anticipated End Date: We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status. OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire. OSU is a fair chance employer committed to inclusive hiring. We encourage applications from candidates who bring a wide range of lived experience including involvement with the justice system. If this position has “critical or security-sensitive” responsibilities and/or driving duties and you are selected as a finalist, your initial job offer will be contingent upon the results of a job-related pre-employment check (such as a background check, motor vehicle history check, sexual misconduct reference check, etc.). Background check results do not automatically disqualify a candidate. Take a look at our Background Checks website including the for candidates section for more details. If you have questions or concerns about the pre-employment check, please contact OSU’s Employee and Labor Relations team at employee.relations@oregonstate.edu ."
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/OSU_Careers_Site/job/Tangent/Field-Crops-Undergraduate-Research-Assistant_JR0000952-1": {
      "title": "Field Crops Undergraduate Research Assistant",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-28\nPosted: Posted 18 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nDepartment: Linn County Extension Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents: ●        A Resume ●        A Cover Letter For additional information please contact: Christy Tanner at Christy.tanner@oregonstate.edu We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status. This position requires driving a University vehicle or a personal vehicle on behalf of the University; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a Motor Vehicle History Check , possess and maintain a current, valid driver's license in their state of residence, be determined to be position qualified and self-report convictions (as per Voluntary and Compulsory Driver Standards OSU Standard 125-155-0200) as per OSU Standard 576-056-0000 et seq. OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire.   Note: All job offers are contingent upon Human Resources final approval Job Description: This recruitment will be used to fill 1 part-time (a maximum of 24 hours per week) undergraduate research assistant for the Field Crops Extension Program at Oregon State University (OSU). Position Duties This position will assist with a variety of projects for the South Willamette Valley Field Crops Extension and Research Program. This program conducts research on seed and cereal crop production systems including grass seed, clover seed, cereals and other field crops. Current projects include pest management (slugs, voles, insects), soil health, drone aerial imagery, and plant growth regulators. The duties of this position will include set up, sampling and data collection for on-farm field trials. Indoor work will include sample processing, data entry and data analysis. Transferable Skill Development OSU is committed to ensuring students are prepared for success after graduation through intentional skill development as student employees. The competencies students develop while working at OSU are defined by the National Association of Colleges and Employers (NACE) as the essential skills employers seek in their hires. In this position, students learn and build vital career-readiness skills in the primary areas below:   Communication, Critical Thinking, Technology, Professionalism Qualifications: Full Employment Eligibility Requirements can be found here:   https://hr.oregonstate.edu/student-employment-manual/500-employment-eligibility-requirements Must be academically enrolled in a high school, community college, or university and pursuing a program or course of study Must meet Academic Standing Requirements; students on academic suspension are not eligible for employment Must meet the applicable minimal enrollment standard High School student: Regularly enrolled in a high school or participating in a home-schooling program Undergraduate and post-baccalaureate student: 6 credit hours per term Undergraduate international student: 12 credit hours per term* Graduate student officially admitted to Graduate School: 5 credit hours per term Graduate international student officially admitted to Graduate School: 9 credit hours per term* *International students may be allowed to carry fewer hours than specified above and still be considered “full-time” by the United States Citizenship and Immigration Services ( USCIS ). A reduced course load is approved by the   Office of International Services   ( OIS ), and must be provided to the Student Employment Center. Required This position requires driving a University vehicle or a personal vehicle on behalf of the University; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a Motor Vehicle History Check , possess and maintain a current, valid driver's license in their state of residence, be determined to be position qualified and self-report convictions (as per Voluntary and Compulsory Driver Standards OSU Standard 125-155-0200) as per OSU Standard 576-056-0000 et seq. Preferred Majoring in, or previous course work in agricultural sciences or related fields (e.g.soil science, crop science, horticulture, environmental science, biology, etc.). Experience with GIS. Experience with programming in R, Python or other languages. Working Conditions/Schedule: - The position will be based at the Linn County Extension Office in Tangent, about 15 minutes from campus. - Available hours will be up to the maximum for student employees during the academic year, and up to 40 hours per week during the summer. Salary Range: Anticipated Start Date: 2026-09-07 Anticipated End Date:"
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/OSU_Careers_Site/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/TRIO-Student-Peer-Tutor_JR0000936": {
      "title": "TRIO Student Peer Tutor",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-27\nPosted: Posted 19 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nDepartment: TRIO Student Support Services Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents: ●       Transcripts ●        A Cover Letter For additional information please contact: Alondra Garcia Martinez at alondra.garciamartinez@oregonstate.edu We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status. This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a criminal history check and be determined to be position qualified as per OSU Standard 576-055-0000 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in youth programs may have additional criminal history checks every 24 months. OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire.   Note: All job offers are contingent upon Human Resources final approval Job Description: This recruitment will be used to fill numerous part-time (a maximum of 24 hours per week) Peer Tutor positions for the TRIO Student Support Services Program at Oregon State University (OSU). SSS Peer Tutors work individually and with small groups of SSS students over the course of the term (week 2 through week 10) to help them better understand course material and prepare for assignments and exams. In addition to helping students with course content, Peer Tutors share study strategies and demonstrate methods for how students can find the answers for themselves, with the goal that through tutoring. TRIO SSS students build the skills that will allow them to excel in their classes. Tutors must become familiar with tutoring strategies that equip students with active learning skills. TRIO SSS tutors need to have successfully completed any courses that they tutor with a B or higher (in some cases they must currently be taking the course). Training will be provided to become acquainted with the tutoring format of the program. Anticipated Hourly Wage: $15.55 Both Undergraduate and Graduate students are welcome/encouraged to apply! Position Duties Tutor students individually or in small groups Ongoing training and professional development Coordinating meetings directly with students and maintaining documentation Group and individual meetings with supervisor Assisting at quarterly study sessions Transferable Skill Development OSU is committed to ensuring students are prepared for success after graduation through intentional skill development as student employees. The competencies students develop while working at OSU are defined by the National Association of Colleges and Employers (NACE) as the essential skills employers seek in their hires. In this position, students learn and build vital career-readiness skills in the primary areas below:   Communication, Critical Thinking, Leadership, Professionalism, Equity & Inclusion Qualifications: Full Employment Eligibility Requirements can be found here:   https://hr.oregonstate.edu/student-employment-manual/500-employment-eligibility-requirements Must be academically enrolled in a high school, community college, or university and pursuing a program or course of study Must meet Academic Standing Requirements; students on academic suspension are not eligible for employment Must meet the applicable minimal enrollment standard High School student: Regularly enrolled in a high school or participating in a home-schooling program Undergraduate and post-baccalaureate student: 6 credit hours per term Undergraduate international student: 12 credit hours per term* Graduate student officially admitted to Graduate School: 5 credit hours per term Graduate international student officially admitted to Graduate School: 9 credit hours per term* *International students may be allowed to carry fewer hours than specified above and still be considered “full-time” by the United States Citizenship and Immigration Services ( USCIS ). A reduced course load is approved by the   Office of International Services   ( OIS ), and must be provided to the Student Employment Center. Required An interest in helping others be successful in college Experience interacting with people from diverse backgrounds Previous success in some of the most tutored courses (MTH 111-252, PH series, ECON, ST, etc.) The ability to arrange a tutoring schedule directly with students and manage one’s own schedule This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a criminal history check and be determined to be position qualified as per OSU Standard 576-055-0000 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in youth programs may have additional criminal history checks every 24 months. Preferred 3.0 GPA or above Work-study preferred Experience tutoring others (paid or unpaid) Current or past TRIO, CAMP, or EOP enrollment/involvement An understanding of the SSS student population (first-generation college students, low-income students, and students with disabilities) Working Conditions/Schedule: • 3-7 hours a week • Flexible scheduling: weekdays, evenings and weekends; subject to change every term Salary Range: Anticipated Start Date: 2026-09-01 Anticipated End Date:"
    },
