    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/OSU_Careers_Site/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/COH-Learning-Assistant-Level-1--2026-2027-Pool-_JR0000284": {
      "title": "COH Learning Assistant Level 1 (2026-2027 Pool)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-07-30\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nPart time\n\nDepartment: College of Health Special Instructions to Applicants: The College of Health is hiring undergraduate Learning Assistants   to help deliver CORE Transitions , a course designed to engage students with practical skills, tools, and knowledge for success at OSU. We need motivated students to help make this course a success.   When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents: ●        A Resume ●        A Cover Letter For additional information please contact: Sean Newsom at Sean.Newsom@oregonstate.edu Job Description: Learning Assistants assist instructors in College of Health courses by facilitating student group work. 85% – Working with students in group learning settings with LA faculty present (i.e., classrooms, help sessions, office hours, online) 15% – Preparing for facilitation by meeting with course faculty, reviewing/completing course materials, training for being an LA (one-time orientation) All Learning Assistants attend a weekly preparatory meeting with the faculty leader of the course and work respectfully with students in the course to facilitate learning in lecture, recitation, and/or Ecampus settings. Assignments and total working hours are dependent upon alignment with course schedules. Qualifications: Full Employment Eligibility Requirements can be found here:   https://hr.oregonstate.edu/student-employment-manual/500-employment-eligibility-requirements   Must be academically enrolled in a high school, community college, or university and pursuing a program or course of study Must meet Academic Standing Requirements; students on academic suspension are not eligible for employment Must meet the applicable minimal enrollment standard High School student: Regularly enrolled in a high school or participating in a home-schooling program Undergraduate and post-baccalaureate student: 6 credit hours per term Undergraduate international student: 12 credit hours per term* Graduate student officially admitted to Graduate School: 5 credit hours per term Graduate international student officially admitted to Graduate School: 9 credit hours per term* *International students may be allowed to carry fewer hours than specified above and still be considered “full-time” by the United States Citizenship and Immigration Services ( USCIS ). A reduced course load is approved by the   Office of International Services   ( OIS ), and must be provided to the Student Employment Center. Working Conditions/Schedule: Salary Range: Anticipated hourly pay for this position is $15.55. Anticipated Start Date: 2026-08-01 Anticipated End Date:"
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/OSU_Careers_Site/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/IB-Curator-Assistant-26-27_JR0000275": {
      "title": "IB Curator Assistant 26-27",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-07-30\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nPart time\n\nDepartment: Integrative Biology (SZO) Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents: A Resume A Cover Letter For additional information please contact: Melissa Platt at  melissa.platt@oregonstate.edu We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status. OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire. Note: All job offers are contingent upon Human Resources final approval Job Description: This recruitment will be used to fill part-time (a maximum of 24 hours per week) Curator Assistant positions for the Department of Integrative Biology at Oregon State University (OSU). Applications submitted to this recruitment are reviewed on an as needed basis. Qualified applicants will be contacted when positions become available.  The approximate hours worked per week will vary depending on the needs of the projects but will not exceed 24 hours per week during academic terms.  These positions are eligible to students with federal work study awards; work study students are preferred. The Undergraduate Student Technical Assistant performs routine to complex technical tasks in support of research or administrative work which includes laboratory, green house, field plot, data collection, simple analysis, or the care and maintenance of animals. Position Duties: May include any of the following duties: Physical curation, care, preparation and imaging of specimens Data basing and cataloguing Filing and clerical work Library research Assist with laboratory organization and cleaning Sample preparation for analysis DNA extraction of specimens, PCR, and processing of DNA sequence data Transferable Skill Development OSU is committed to ensuring students are prepared for success after graduation through intentional skill development as student employees. The competencies students develop while working at OSU are defined by the National Association of Colleges and Employers (NACE) as the essential skills employers seek in their hires. In this position, students learn and build vital career-readiness skills in the primary areas below:   Critical Thinking, Teamwork, Career & Self Development Qualifications: Full Employment Eligibility Requirements can be found here:   https://hr.oregonstate.edu/student-employment-manual/500-employment-eligibility-requirements   Minimum Qualifications: Must be academically enrolled in a high school, community college, or university and pursuing a program or course of study Must meet Academic Standing Requirements; students on academic suspension are not eligible for employment Must meet the applicable minimal enrollment standard High School student: Regularly enrolled in a high school or participating in a home-schooling program Undergraduate and post-baccalaureate student: 6 credit hours per term Undergraduate international student: 12 credit hours per term* Graduate student officially admitted to Graduate School: 5 credit hours per term Graduate international student officially admitted to Graduate School: 9 credit hours per term* *International students may be allowed to carry fewer hours than specified above and still be considered “full-time” by the United States Citizenship and Immigration Services ( USCIS ). A reduced course load is approved by the   Office of International Services   ( OIS ), and must be provided to the Student Employment Center. Required Qualifications: Students with career goals related to biological sciences or related field Strong and demonstrable work ethic Preferred Qualifications: 3.0 GPA Students in good standing A demonstrable commitment to promoting and enhancing diversity Working Conditions/Schedule: Salary Range: Anticipated Start Date: Anticipated End Date:"
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/OSU_Careers_Site/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/Beaver-Classic-Creamery---Production_JR0000201-1": {
      "title": "Beaver Classic Creamery - Production",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-07-29\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nPart time\n\nDepartment: Food Science & Technology Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents: A Resume A Cover Letter For additional information please contact: Brandon Riesgaard at brandon.riesgaard@oregonstate.edu We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status.   Criminal History Check Statement (CHC): This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a Criminal History Check and be determined to be position qualified as per OSU Standard 576-055-0000 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in Youth Programs may have additional Criminal History Checks every 2 years.   Motor Vehicle History Check Statement (MVHC): This position requires driving a University vehicle or a personal vehicle on behalf of the University; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a Motor Vehicle History Check, possess and maintain a current, valid driver's license in their state of residence, be determined to be position qualified and self-report convictions (as per Voluntary and Compulsory Driver Standards OSU Standard 125-155-0200) as per OSU Standard 576-056-0000 et seq.   OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire.   Note: All job offers are contingent upon Human Resources final approval Job Description: This recruitment will be used to fill 10 part-time (maximum of 24 hours per week) Student Technical Assistant positions for the Department of Food Science and Technology at Oregon State University (OSU) in the OSU Creamery. The Undergraduate Student Technical Assistant performs routine to complex technical tasks in support of research or administrative work which includes laboratory, green house, field plot, data collection, simple analysis, or the care and maintenance of animals. Position Duties: Position duties to be determined by the hiring unit, but may include cleaning, production and operations routine to the OSU Creamery. Cleaning and sanitizing dairy plant and equipment Assist in ice cream and cheese production, including milk hauling, pasteurizing, documentation and other dairy production tasks Transferable Skill Development OSU is committed to ensuring students are prepared for success after graduation through intentional skill development as student employees. The competencies students develop while working at OSU are defined by the National Association of Colleges and Employers (NACE) as the essential skills employers seek in their hires. In this position, students learn and build vital career-readiness skills in the primary areas below:   Communication, Teamwork, Technology Qualifications: Full Employment Eligibility Requirements can be found here:   https://hr.oregonstate.edu/student-employment-manual/500-employment-eligibility-requirements Must be academically enrolled in a high school, community college, or university and pursuing a program or course of study Must meet Academic Standing Requirements; students on academic suspension are not eligible for employment Must meet the applicable minimal enrollment standard High School student: Regularly enrolled in a high school or participating in a home-schooling program Undergraduate and post-baccalaureate student: 6 credit hours per term Undergraduate international student: 12 credit hours per term* Graduate student officially admitted to Graduate School: 5 credit hours per term Graduate international student officially admitted to Graduate School: 9 credit hours per term* *International students may be allowed to carry fewer hours than specified above and still be considered “full-time” by the United States Citizenship and Immigration Services ( USCIS ). A reduced course load is approved by the   Office of International Services   ( OIS) and must be provided to the Student Employment Center. Required Qualifications: Physically able to lift 50 lbs. repeatedly The candidate must be able to communicate effectively in both written and oral English Additional Required Qualifications: Criminal History Check Statement (CHC): This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a Criminal History Check and be determined to be position qualified as per OSU Standard 576-055-0000 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in Youth Programs may have additional Criminal History Checks every 2 years. Motor Vehicle History Check Statement (MVHC): This position requires driving a University vehicle or a personal vehicle on behalf of the University; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a Motor Vehicle History Check, possess and maintain a current, valid driver's license in their state of residence, be determined to be position qualified and self-report convictions (as per Voluntary and Compulsory Driver Standards OSU Standard 125-155-0200) as per OSU Standard 576-056-0000 et seq. Preferred Qualifications: Preference toward students interested in pursuing a career in dairy Demonstratable commitment to promoting and enhancing diversity Working Conditions/Schedule: • To be determined by hiring unit • May include evening and weekend hours • May include use of motor pool vehicles • May include work outdoors in all types of weather • May require walking, standing, bending and stooping for extended periods • May require maneuvering and transporting heavy objects • May require working with or around cleaning chemicals • Anticipated hours per week 20 hours Salary Range: Anticipated Start Date: 2026-07-16 Anticipated End Date: 2026-08-30"
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/OSU_Careers_Site/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/Social-Media-Manager_JR0000037": {
      "title": "Social Media Manager",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-07-29\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nPart time\n\nDepartment: College of Business – Marketing, Communications, and Events Team Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents: ●        A Resume ●        A Cover Letter For additional information please contact: Samantha Pinkerton at samantha.pinkerton@oregonstate.edu We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status.   This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a criminal history check and be determined to be position qualified as per OSU Standard 576-055-0000 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in youth programs may have additional criminal history checks every 24 months. OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire. Note: All job offers are contingent upon Human Resources final approval Job Description: This recruitment will be used to fill 1 part-time (a maximum of 24 hours per week) student job for the College of Business at Oregon State University (OSU). The College of Business is seeking a College of Business student to join the student social media team as a part of the Marketing Communications and Events team. The Social Media Manager, under the direction of the Assistant Director of Marketing, will co-lead the development and implementation of the social media strategy for the OSU College of Business alongside another Social Media Manager. This role requires collaboration with various stakeholders within the college to create a comprehensive content calendar that aligns with the goals of stakeholders, the college, and the university. To apply, please email  the Assistant Director of Marketing, samantha.pinkerton@oregonstate.edu with a video showcasing why you'd be a great fit to join the team. This job is Hybrid, starts this summer (flexible). Must be Corvallis based (during school year). Pay rate for this position is $17.00 Position Duties With the direction of the Assistant Director of Marketing, develop and execute a strategic social media plan that enhances the college’s brand and outreach.  Work with the Assistant Director of Marketing each term to create goals for the social media team and priority social platforms, Instagram and Tiktok. Collaborate with stakeholders to determine their goals and produce a content calendar that supports institutional objectives.  Work with the social media team to plan content, filming dates, and posting schedules, updating and tracking progress in Asana.   Manage the social media specialists to ensure timely, high-quality content creation and engagement. Ensure deadlines are met, review and suggest edits if needed for all content prior to posting.   Stay informed on current trends and best practices in social media to inform strategy and implementation.  Oversee and analyze the performance metrics of all social media platforms, including LinkedIn, Facebook, Instagram, and TikTok and create a recap each term to share with stakeholders.  Schedule and post content on social media platforms.  Co-lead in-person social media small group meetings  Attend weekly meetings (2-3 hours per week). OSU is committed to ensuring students are prepared for success after graduation through intentional skill development as student employees. The competencies students develop while working at OSU are defined by the National Association of Colleges and Employers (NACE) as the essential skills employers seek in their hires. In this position, students learn and build vital career-readiness skills in the primary areas below:   Communication, Technology, Professionalism, Career & Self Development Qualifications: Full Employment Eligibility Requirements can be found here:   https://hr.oregonstate.edu/student-employment-manual/500-employment-eligibility-requirements   Must be academically enrolled in a high school, community college, or university and pursuing a program or course of study Must meet Academic Standing Requirements; students on academic suspension are not eligible for employment Must meet the applicable minimal enrollment standard High School student: Regularly enrolled in a high school or participating in a home-schooling program Undergraduate and post-baccalaureate student: 6 credit hours per term Undergraduate international student: 12 credit hours per term* Graduate student officially admitted to Graduate School: 5 credit hours per term Graduate international student officially admitted to Graduate School: 9 credit hours per term* *International students may be allowed to carry fewer hours than specified above and still be considered “full-time” by the United States Citizenship and Immigration Services ( USCIS ). A reduced course load is approved by the   Office of International Services   ( OIS ), and must be provided to the Student Employment Center. Working Conditions/Schedule: This role is up to 20 hours per week. Attend weekly meetings (2-3 hours per week). Salary Range: Anticipated Start Date: 2026-07-14 Anticipated End Date:"
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/OSU_Careers_Site/job/Bend/Belonging-and-Community-Mentor_JR0000324": {
      "title": "Belonging and Community Mentor",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-07-28\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nPart time\n\nDepartment: OSU | Cascades Student Life Belonging and Equity (Lupita Gamez) Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents: ●        A Resume ●        A Cover Letter For additional information please contact: Lupita Gamez at lupita.gamez@osucascades.edu We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status. This position requires driving a University vehicle or a personal vehicle on behalf of the University; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a Motor Vehicle History Check, possess and maintain a current, valid driver's license in their state of residence, be determined to be position qualified and self-report convictions (as per Voluntary and Compulsory Driver Standards OSU Standard 125-155-0200) as per OSU Standard 576-056-0000 et seq.   Job Description: This recruitment will be used to fill 1 part-time (a maximum of 24 hours per week) Mentor position for the Belonging and Community Center at Oregon State University (OSU). The Belonging and Community Mentor engages in community building, education, and support on behalf of the Student Life and their inclusive excellence initiatives and programs. Students in this position will focus their support and address the needs of their community in one or more of the following areas: Academic Success Social Justice Leadership Identity Development Community & Belonging Well-being This position is located in Bend, Oregon. Position Duties: 1. Assist in daily operations of The Bridge and/or initiative. For example: giving tours, staffing the front desk, keeping attendance records, maintaining cleanliness and organization of The Bridge Student Center, materials, etc. 2. Engage oneself and others in mutual learning on identity development and social justice For example: staying current on issues impacting communities; interrupting and educating about bias, microaggressions and other issues impacting communities; raise awareness for underserved parts of the community, etc. 3. Engage in community-specific and collaborative event planning and leadership with support of the Belonging & Equity Coordinator. For example: history and heritage month celebrations; Cascades Leadership Institute; inclusive excellence workshops/trainings, etc. 4. Assist in community specific or collaborative development of resources, projects, and initiatives. For example: communication projects such as publications or videos, resource development, distributing promotional materials, educational campaigns, etc. 5. Assist in creating and maintaining a welcoming environment in The Bridge. For example: greeting guests, aiding the Belonging & Equity Coordinator in addressing community conflicts, identify ways to make the space more inclusive, etc. 6. Support individuals’ connections to community. For example: outreach to students, helping guests build relationships with each other, providing supportive listening, assisting guests in finding relevant resources, etc. 7. Contribute to a culture of respect and accountability. For example: engaging in and modeling self-reflection, learning, and personal and professional growth, etc. 8. Promote involvement opportunities connected with Student Life’s inclusive excellence programming. For example: promoting hiring and volunteer opportunities, involvement opportunities in affiliated student organizations, participation in programming, etc. 9. Engage with peers and connect them to relevant campus and community resources to support their student experience. For example: Academic Advising, Library services, Cascades Edge, student Clubs & Organizations, etc. 10. Act as a representative of the The Bridge. For example: building relationships with student / community organizations Tribal communities, and other OSU-Cascades departments/programs; serving on planning committees for campus events as needed. Other responsibilities as assigned by professional staff 1. Attend mandatory scheduled trainings Such as ongoing training sessions, weekly staff meetings, and other professional development. 2. Obtain FERPA certification and complete necessary trainings in a timely manner. 3. Complete daily center tasks & needs as identified by staff May also involve focusing efforts on specialized projects based on needs of the center and/or initiative. 4. May occasionally require evening and weekend hours. Transferable Skill Development OSU is committed to ensuring students are prepared for success after graduation through intentional skill development as student employees. The competencies students develop while working at OSU are defined by the National Association of Colleges and Employers (NACE) as the essential skills employers seek in their hires. In this position, students learn and build vital career-readiness skills in the primary areas below:   Communication, Critical Thinking, Leadership, Equity & Inclusion Qualifications: Full Employment Eligibility Requirements can be found here:   https://hr.oregonstate.edu/student-employment-manual/500-employment-eligibility-requirements   Must be academically enrolled in a high school, community college, or university and pursuing a program or course of study Must meet Academic Standing Requirements; students on academic suspension are not eligible for employment Must meet the applicable minimal enrollment standard High School student: Regularly enrolled in a high school or participating in a home-schooling program Undergraduate and post-baccalaureate student: 6 credit hours per term Undergraduate international student: 12 credit hours per term* Graduate student officially admitted to Graduate School: 5 credit hours per term Graduate international student officially admitted to Graduate School: 9 credit hours per term* *International students may be allowed to carry fewer hours than specified above and still be considered “full-time” by the United States Citizenship and Immigration Services ( USCIS ). A reduced course load is approved by the   Office of International Services   ( OIS ), and must be provided to the Student Employment Center. This position requires driving a University vehicle or a personal vehicle on behalf of the University; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a Motor Vehicle History Check, possess and maintain a current, valid driver's license in their state of residence, be determined to be position qualified and self-report convictions (as per Voluntary and Compulsory Driver Standards OSU Standard 125-155-0200) as per OSU Standard 576-056-0000 et seq. Working Conditions/Schedule: Salary Range: Anticipated Start Date: 2026-09-21 Anticipated End Date:"
