    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/OSU_Careers_Site/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/Student-Clerical--BEE-Front-Office-Support_JR0001064": {
      "title": "Student Clerical: BEE Front Office Support",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-08\nPosted: Posted 7 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nDepartment: Biological & Ecological Engineering Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents: A Resume A Cover Letter explaining how they meet the minimum requirements For additional information please contact: Jennifer Cohen at jennifer.cohen@oregonstate.edu We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status.   OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire.   Note: All job offers are contingent upon Human Resources final approval Job Description: This recruitment will be used to fill two part-time (a maximum of 20 hours per week) Student Clerical/Office Assistant position for the Department of Biological & Ecological Engineering at Oregon State University (OSU). The person in this position will perform general office work such as document creation, flyer development, meeting scheduling copying, reception coverage, and database management.  Position Duties: Provides coverage for the main office, based on availability, open and/or close the office, greet visitors, and answer incoming calls and emails and routing those appropriately Receives, sorts, and distributes mail and prepares out-going mail and shipments Creates/designs electronic and/or hard copy materials including letters, announcements, fliers and brochures Assist with donor relations events and Thank You letters Assist with building repairs, reporting as needed via the facilities ticketing system Submit Surplus pick-up requests as needed Maintains inventory of office and event supplies and submits orders to office manager Helps organize field trips, including travel and lodging arrangements Other office tasks as needed General policy research, as needed Transferable Skill Development OSU is committed to ensuring students are prepared for success after graduation through intentional skill development as student employees. The competencies students develop while working at OSU are defined by the National Association of Colleges and Employers (NACE) as the essential skills employers seek in their hires. In this position, students learn and build vital career-readiness skills in the primary areas below:   Communication, Teamwork, Professionalism, Equity & Inclusion Qualifications: Full Employment Eligibility Requirements can be found here:   https://hr.oregonstate.edu/student-employment-manual/500-employment-eligibility-requirements Must be academically enrolled in a high school, community college, or university and pursuing a program or course of study Must meet Academic Standing Requirements; students on academic suspension are not eligible for employment Must meet the applicable minimal enrollment standard High School student: Regularly enrolled in a high school or participating in a home-schooling program Undergraduate and post-baccalaureate student: 6 credit hours per term Undergraduate international student: 12 credit hours per term* Graduate student officially admitted to Graduate School: 5 credit hours per term Graduate international student officially admitted to Graduate School: 9 credit hours per term* *International students may be allowed to carry fewer hours than specified above and still be considered “full-time” by the United States Citizenship and Immigration Services ( USCIS ). A reduced course load is approved by the   Office of International Services   ( OIS ), and must be provided to the Student Employment Center. Required Qualifications: Must be available to work up to 20 hrs each week Previous experience with MS Office Suite Must be responsible and have a strong work ethic Must have good written/verbal communications skills and attention to detail Proficiency with professional email communications Preferred Qualifications: Previous experience with designing flyers and outreach materials including familiarity with Adobe, Canva, or similar Customer service experience Experience in an office administration setting Working Conditions/Schedule: • Office setting • Students will cover front office desk from 9 - 12 PM and 1 - 4 PM Salary Range: Anticipated Start Date: 2026-09-23 Anticipated End Date:"
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/OSU_Careers_Site/job/Bend/Student-Research-Assistant_JR0000781": {
      "title": "Student Research Assistant",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-14\nPosted: Posted Yesterday\nPart time\n\nDepartment: Laboratory for the American Conversation Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents: A Resume For additional information please contact: Elizabeth Marino at Elizabeth.Marino@osucascades.edu We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status.   OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire.   Note: All job offers are contingent upon Human Resources final approval Job Description: This recruitment will be used to fill one part-time (a maximum of 24 hours per week) Student Research Assistant for the Laboratory for the American Conversation at Oregon State University (OSU). The Laboratory for the American Conversation at Oregon State University–Cascades is seeking an undergraduate research assistant to join a nationally recognized team working at the intersection of psychology, anthropology, and public discourse. The Lab uses social theory, discourse analysis, and large-scale surveys to uncover what values communities express when they enter into public discourse, and what threats they are trying to avoid. Our work tackles some of the most divisive issues of our time, from climate change to public health, with the goal of building communication strategies and tools that foster respectful dialogue and forge new solutions. This is a opportunity to contribute to research with real public impact. Working alongside faculty and research staff, the successful candidate will gain hands-on experience across the full research lifecycle, from IRB-compliant study design through data collection, analysis, and write-up, while developing skills directly relevant to graduate study and careers in research, policy, and public service. You'll be part of a small, collaborative team on OSU-Cascades' Bend campus that promotes an intellectually and inter-culturally rich environment for engaging the critical issues confronting the American public. Learn more about the Lab and its work at https://osucascades.edu/lac . Position Duties: Assist with the design and execution of research projects, ensuring all activities comply with IRB protocols and university research policies. Conduct literature reviews and synthesize scholarly sources to support project development and framing. Collect, organize, and manage research data, maintaining accurate records and adhering to data-handling and confidentiality requirements. Contribute to the drafting, editing, and formatting of research write-ups, reports, and related materials. Support the preparation and submission of IRB documentation, including consent forms, protocol amendments, and required approvals. Attend regular lab meetings and collaborate with faculty and research staff to track project progress and deadlines. Perform administrative and logistical tasks related to research operations, such as scheduling, correspondence, and materials preparation. Transferable Skill Development OSU is committed to ensuring students are prepared for success after graduation through intentional skill development as student employees. The competencies students develop while working at OSU are defined by the National Association of Colleges and Employers (NACE) as the essential skills employers seek in their hires. In this position, students learn and build vital career-readiness skills in the primary areas below:   Communication, Critical Thinking, Career & Self Development Qualifications: Full Employment Eligibility Requirements can be found here:   https://hr.oregonstate.edu/student-employment-manual/500-employment-eligibility-requirements Must be academically enrolled in a high school, community college, or university and pursuing a program or course of study Must meet Academic Standing Requirements; students on academic suspension are not eligible for employment Must meet the applicable minimal enrollment standard High School student: Regularly enrolled in a high school or participating in a home-schooling program Undergraduate and post-baccalaureate student: 6 credit hours per term Undergraduate international student: 12 credit hours per term* Graduate student officially admitted to Graduate School: 5 credit hours per term Graduate international student officially admitted to Graduate School: 9 credit hours per term* *International students may be allowed to carry fewer hours than specified above and still be considered “full-time” by the United States Citizenship and Immigration Services ( USCIS ). A reduced course load is approved by the   Office of International Services   ( OIS ), and must be provided to the Student Employment Center. Preferred Qualifications: Experience in social science research: Qualitative research methods Quantitative research methods Data organization and analysis Working Conditions/Schedule: • The Laboratory for the American Conversation has a need for an undergraduate student researcher to help with a new data collection. • Might work up to 10-15 hours/week • Hybrid Salary Range: Anticipated Start Date: 2026-08-28 Anticipated End Date: 2026-09-01"
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/OSU_Careers_Site/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/Student-Web---Marketing-Assistant_JR0001120": {
      "title": "Student Web & Marketing Assistant",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-14\nPosted: Posted Yesterday\nPart time\n\nDepartment: Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents: ●        A Resume or CV ●        A Cover Letter outlining your qualifications and interest in the position For additional information please contact: Jennifer Rouse at Jennifer.rouse@oregonstate.edu We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status.   OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire.   Note: All job offers are contingent upon Human Resources final approval   Job Description: This recruitment will be used to fill one part-time (a maximum of 24 hours per week) Student Web & Marketing Assistant position for the Career and Development Center at Oregon State University (OSU). This position is for a student Web & Marketing Assistant who will work closely with the Director of Marketing & Communications to edit, update and enhance the Career Development Center website and promote awareness of career services, events, and programs. The Web & Marketing Assistant will work collaboratively as a member of the Career Development Center’s marketing team to create effective content that tells the story of the Career Development Center’s mission and impact on the Oregon State University community. Position Duties Assist with production, editing, posting, and maintenance of the Career Development Center website and online blog. Troubleshoot website problems with the Director of Marketing & Communications; identify solutions and implement corrections as needed. Work in consultation with the CDC Director of Marketing & Communications to respond to requests for website updates from members of the Career Development Center team. Post blog entries, videos, images, PDFs and other content as needed. Select and optimize photos for web. Assist with the creation of email newsletters to drive online traffic. Use Google Analytics to prepare monthly web analytics reports. Send requests to campus partners to include Career Development Center content in their outreach materials such as newsletters, emails, and digital slide rotations. Update Career Development Center events on the OSU online events calendar. Meet regularly and brainstorm with members of the Career Development team for ways to share the mission and impact of the Career Development Center. Additional duties Complete training required to satisfactorily complete assigned tasks. Assist with special events as needed. Depending on the skills and interests of the successful candidate, the position could also include writing articles for blogs and newsletters, photography/videography, and creation of marketing materials using Canva or the Adobe Creative Suite. Other duties as assigned. Transferable Skill Development OSU is committed to ensuring students are prepared for success after graduation through intentional skill development as student employees. The competencies students develop while working at OSU are defined by the National Association of Colleges and Employers (NACE) as the essential skills employers seek in their hires. In this position, students learn and build vital career-readiness skills in the primary areas below:   Communication, Critical Thinking, Technology, Professionalism Qualifications: Full Employment Eligibility Requirements can be found here:   https://hr.oregonstate.edu/student-employment-manual/500-employment-eligibility-requirements Must be academically enrolled in a high school, community college, or university and pursuing a program or course of study Must meet Academic Standing Requirements; students on academic suspension are not eligible for employment Must meet the applicable minimal enrollment standard High School student: Regularly enrolled in a high school or participating in a home-schooling program Undergraduate and post-baccalaureate student: 6 credit hours per term Undergraduate international student: 12 credit hours per term* Graduate student officially admitted to Graduate School: 5 credit hours per term Graduate international student officially admitted to Graduate School: 9 credit hours per term* *International students may be allowed to carry fewer hours than specified above and still be considered “full-time” by the United States Citizenship and Immigration Services ( USCIS ). A reduced course load is approved by the   Office of International Services   ( OIS ), and must be provided to the Student Employment Center. Required Demonstrated excellence in written communication Demonstrated ability and willingness to complete detailed tasks with consistent high quality Demonstrated ability to work independently with a high level of self-direction. Excellent time management skills Demonstrated ability to learn new skills and technologies Ability to work effectively and respectfully with individuals from a variety of backgrounds, experiences and perspectives. Preferred Experience with web content management systems and website design Knowledge of basic HTML Experience in photo editing and optimizing photos for websites Experience writing for publication Working Conditions/Schedule: • This position typically works about 10 hours a week and no more than 24 hours week. • Period of employment is one academic year, contingent upon employee maintaining satisfactory job performance as evaluated by supervisor. Work location • A hybrid or remote work arrangement may be available for successful candidates. Salary Range: Anticipated Start Date: 2026-10-12 Anticipated End Date:"
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/OSU_Careers_Site/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/Student-Marketing---MultiMedia-Designer_JR0000703": {
      "title": "Student Marketing & MultiMedia Designer",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-14\nPosted: Posted Yesterday\nPart time\n\nDepartment: Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents: A Resume or CV A Cover Letter Portfolio or work samples containing at least three examples of creative work. You may submit a PDF portfolio or a document that contains a link to an online portfolio. For additional information please contact: Jennifer Rouse at Jennifer.rouse@oregonstate.edu We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status.   OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire.   Note: All job offers are contingent upon Human Resources final approval Job Description: This recruitment will be used to fill one part-time (a maximum of 24 hours per week) Student Marketing & Multimedia Designer for the Integrated Support & Development at Oregon State University (OSU). This position is for a Student Marketing & Multimedia Designer who will create visual and digital content as part of the marketing team for the Integrated Support & Development portfolio of departments, including New Student Onboarding, Academics for Student Athletes, the Career Development Center, the Office of Academic Success, and Cross-Campus Strategic Initiatives. The Student Marketing & Multimedia Designer will create graphic design assets, marketing materials, photography, short-form video, and social media content for a variety of audiences across print and digital channels. The student will also assist with publishing social media content and reviewing basic engagement metrics to help improve future content. The Student Marketing & Multimedia Designer will work collaboratively with staff throughout the portfolio, with lead work provided by the Digital Communications Specialist. Other related duties may be assigned as needed. Through this position, students will develop core competencies that prepare them for careers after graduation, including communication, critical thinking, creativity, teamwork/collaboration, leadership, professionalism, intercultural fluency, and digital technology. Position Duties: Design print and digital marketing materials for a variety of audiences. Create visual assets such as posters, flyers, social media graphics, and digital advertisements. Create and publish content for social media and other digital communication channels, including writing captions and adapting content for different platforms. Capture and edit photography and video content. Assist with creation of short-form social media content, including Instagram Reels and TikTok-style videos. Use analytics tools to review social media content performance and adjust content plans accordingly. Adhere to OSU brand standards, accessibility requirements, and style guidelines. Collaborate with staff across the portfolio to identify and promote student stories, services, programs, and events. Assist with programs, outreach activities, and other events. Meet regularly with the Marketing & Communications team to brainstorm campaign ideas. Other duties as assigned. Transferable Skill Development OSU is committed to ensuring students are prepared for success after graduation through intentional skill development as student employees. The competencies students develop while working at OSU are defined by the National Association of Colleges and Employers (NACE) as the essential skills employers seek in their hires. In this position, students learn and build vital career-readiness skills in the primary areas below:   Communication, Critical Thinking, Technology, Professionalism Qualifications: Full Employment Eligibility Requirements can be found here:   https://hr.oregonstate.edu/student-employment-manual/500-employment-eligibility-requirements   Must be academically enrolled in a high school, community college, or university and pursuing a program or course of study Must meet Academic Standing Requirements; students on academic suspension are not eligible for employment Must meet the applicable minimal enrollment standard High School student: Regularly enrolled in a high school or participating in a home-schooling program Undergraduate and post-baccalaureate student: 6 credit hours per term Undergraduate international student: 12 credit hours per term* Graduate student officially admitted to Graduate School: 5 credit hours per term Graduate international student officially admitted to Graduate School: 9 credit hours per term* *International students may be allowed to carry fewer hours than specified above and still be considered “full-time” by the United States Citizenship and Immigration Services ( USCIS ). A reduced course load is approved by the   Office of International Services   ( OIS ), and must be provided to the Student Employment Center. Required Qualifications: Demonstrated graphic design experience and understanding of visual design principles. Demonstrated experience using Adobe Creative Suite, particularly Illustrator and Photoshop. Familiarity with social media platforms and current content formats. Ability to create compelling visual content for print and digital platforms. Excellent verbal and written communication skills. Strong attention to detail and commitment to producing high-quality work. Ability to work independently and collaboratively. Strong organizational and time management skills. Ability to work effectively and respectfully with individuals from a variety of backgrounds, experiences and perspectives Preferred Qualifications: Student pursuing a degree in Graphic Design, Digital Communication Arts, New Media Communications, Marketing, or a related field. Experience creating short-form video content such as Instagram Reels, TikTok videos, or other social media video formats. Experience with video editing software such as Adobe Premiere Pro, After Effects, or CapCut. Photography and photo editing experience. Experience managing or contributing to social media accounts. Experience monitoring engagement metrics and analytics. Project management and digital asset management experience. Working Conditions/Schedule: Typical working hours are 10-15 hours per week during the academic year and up to 40 hours during academic breaks. Employment is contingent upon satisfactory performance. A hybrid work arrangement may be available. Period of employment is one academic year, contingent upon employee maintaining satisfactory job performance as evaluated by supervisor. Salary Range: Anticipated Start Date: 2026-10-12 Anticipated End Date:"
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/OSU_Careers_Site/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/Undergraduate-Student-Research-Assistant_JR0001055-1": {
      "title": "Undergraduate Student Research Assistant",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-14\nPosted: Posted Yesterday\nPart time\n\nDepartment: Crop and Soil Science Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents: ●       A Resume ●       A short statement of interest (1 paragraph) For additional information please contact: Victor Ribeiro at victor.ribeiro@oregonstate.edu We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status.   This position requires driving a University vehicle or a personal vehicle on behalf of the University; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a Motor Vehicle History Check , possess and maintain a current, valid driver's license in their state of residence, be determined to be position qualified and self-report convictions (as per Voluntary and Compulsory Driver Standards OSU Standard 125-155-0200) as per OSU Standard 576-056-0000 et seq.   OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire.   Note: All job offers are contingent upon Human Resources final approval   Job Description: This recruitment will be used to fill one part-time undergraduate student position (a maximum of 24 hours per week) for the Field Crops Weed Science Program at Oregon State University (OSU). The student will assist with field, greenhouse, and laboratory research and gain hands-on experience in weed science, crop production, and agricultural research. Position Duties Assist with field and greenhouse research, including setup, maintenance, data collection, and harvest. Assist with laboratory work, sample processing, and data entry. Maintain clean and safe research areas and equipment. Follow research and safety protocols. Transferable Skill Development OSU is committed to ensuring students are prepared for success after graduation through intentional skill development as student employees. The competencies students develop while working at OSU are defined by the National Association of Colleges and Employers (NACE) as the essential skills employers seek in their hires. In this position, students learn and build vital career-readiness skills in the primary areas below:   Communication, Teamwork, Professionalism, Equity & Inclusion Qualifications: Full Employment Eligibility Requirements can be found here:   https://hr.oregonstate.edu/student-employment-manual/500-employment-eligibility-requirements Must be academically enrolled in a high school, community college, or university and pursuing a program or course of study Must meet Academic Standing Requirements; students on academic suspension are not eligible for employment Must meet the applicable minimal enrollment standard High School student: Regularly enrolled in a high school or participating in a home-schooling program Undergraduate and post-baccalaureate student: 6 credit hours per term Undergraduate international student: 12 credit hours per term* Graduate student officially admitted to Graduate School: 5 credit hours per term Graduate international student officially admitted to Graduate School: 9 credit hours per term* *International students may be allowed to carry fewer hours than specified above and still be considered “full-time” by the United States Citizenship and Immigration Services ( USCIS ). A reduced course load is approved by the   Office of International Services   ( OIS ), and must be provided to the Student Employment Center. Required Ability to work independently and as part of a team. Strong attention to detail and willingness to learn. Reliable and punctual. Ability to conduct field work. This position requires driving a University vehicle or a personal vehicle on behalf of the University; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a Motor Vehicle History Check , possess and maintain a current, valid driver's license in their state of residence, be determined to be position qualified and self-report convictions (as per Voluntary and Compulsory Driver Standards OSU Standard 125-155-0200) as per OSU Standard 576-056-0000 et seq. Preferred Interest or background in crop science, agronomy, weed science, biology, or related areas. Previous experience with fieldwork, greenhouse work, or lab research. Working Conditions/Schedule: Flexible schedule during the academic year (10-20 hours/week) with the possibility of increased hours during summer. Salary Range: Anticipated Start Date: 2026-09-21 Anticipated End Date:"
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/OSU_Careers_Site/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/Work-Study-Only--Undergraduate-Ecology-Research-Assistant_JR0001125": {
      "title": "Work Study Only: Undergraduate Ecology Research Assistant",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-14\nPosted: Posted Yesterday\nPart time\n\nDepartment: Botany and Plant Pathology Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents: Transcripts A brief cover letter detailing your experience in any of the qualification of the position and interest in the position (1-3 paragraphs). For additional information please contact: Bella Stiver at stiveri@oregonstate.edu We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status.   OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire.   Note: All job offers are contingent upon Human Resources final approval Job Description: This recruitment will be used to fill one Undergraduate Ecology Research Assistant part-time (a maximum of 24 hours per week) approx. 12 hours for the Dept of Botany and Plant Pathology (BPP) at Oregon State University (OSU). The Halliday Lab in the Botany and Plant Pathology department at Oregon State University is seeking an undergraduate research assistant to support an ecology literature synthesis and meta-analysis. The assistant will help organize and summarize scientific literature related to priority effects in host-associated systems. This project examines how the order in which organisms colonize a host can influence which species successfully establish and how host-associated communities develop. Using published ecological studies, we are conducting a systematic review and meta-analysis to understand how these “priority effects” shape interactions among microbes, parasites, and other host-associated organisms. The goal is to synthesize existing evidence to identify general patterns across different host-symbiont systems and ecological contexts. If you’re interested in ecology and want to learn more/get research experience, please apply! Position Duties: Extract and research covariates from selected papers (70%) Maintain structured spreadsheets of reviewed studies (15%) Participate in periodic meetings with supervisor to discuss progress and challenges (10%) Support preparation of figures, tables, or summary documents for reports or manuscripts (5%) Transferable Skill Development OSU is committed to ensuring students are prepared for success after graduation through intentional skill development as student employees. The competencies students develop while working at OSU are defined by the National Association of Colleges and Employers (NACE) as the essential skills employers seek in their hires. In this position, students learn and build vital career-readiness skills in the primary areas below:   Critical Thinking, Teamwork, Technology, Professionalism Qualifications: Full Employment Eligibility Requirements can be found here:   https://hr.oregonstate.edu/student-employment-manual/500-employment-eligibility-requirements Must be academically enrolled in a high school, community college, or university and pursuing a program or course of study Must meet Academic Standing Requirements; students on academic suspension are not eligible for employment Must meet the applicable minimal enrollment standard High School student: Regularly enrolled in a high school or participating in a home-schooling program Undergraduate and post-baccalaureate student: 6 credit hours per term Undergraduate international student: 12 credit hours per term* Graduate student officially admitted to Graduate School: 5 credit hours per term Graduate international student officially admitted to Graduate School: 9 credit hours per term* *International students may be allowed to carry fewer hours than specified above and still be considered “full-time” by the United States Citizenship and Immigration Services ( USCIS ). A reduced course load is approved by the   Office of International Services   ( OIS ), and must be provided to the Student Employment Center. Required Qualifications: Current undergraduate student in Ecology, Biology, Botany, or a related field Strong reading comprehension of scientific literature Attention to detail and ability to follow research protocols Excellent organizational and time-management skills Ability to work independently and meet deadlines Preferred Qualifications: Basic data management or spreadsheet skills (Excel, Google Sheets, or R) Coursework in ecology, botany/mycology, parasitology, or plant pathology Familiarity with scientific literature databases Working Conditions/Schedule: • Work will be done in Cordley Hall. • Work will be between 6-12 hours a week depending on availability of student and supervisor. • This position is open to work-study students only. Salary Range: Anticipated Start Date: 2026-09-30 Anticipated End Date:"
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/OSU_Careers_Site/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/Student-ALI-Field-Specialist_JR0001134": {
      "title": "Student ALI Field Specialist",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-12\nPosted: Posted 3 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nDepartment: Recreational Sports Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents: Resume Cover Letter Supplemental document addressing the following questions: Review the Core Theories of Adventure Leadership from LEAD 430/530 and reflect on your observations and experiences with ALI or elsewhere to answer the following questions. Feel free to use your class notes, readings, and assignments, or additional research as needed. Your responses do not need to be lengthy but should address all components of the questions. Tuckman’s Stages of Group Development:  Describe a time you observed or experienced Tuckman’s Stages of Group Development. Which stage(s) of group development did you observe? Apply your understanding of Tuckman’s theory to explain your reasoning.  Kolb’s Experiential Learning Cycle:  Describe a time in which you observed or experienced an instructor using this theory. How did their actions support student learning? Accident Potential:  Explain the categories a Field Staff uses to identify potential hazards. Describe a time when a leader used this model to minimize exposure prior to or during a field/program experience. Situational Leadership:  Explain the two components of follower readiness. Describe an instance in which you saw/experienced a field staff or instructor adjust their leadership style to support the person/group and explain why the change was warranted.  Certifications/coursework: Have you completed LEAD 430/530 Foundations of Adventure Leadership? Have you successfully achieved a Wilderness First Responder certification? If not, are you currently enrolled and in good standing? Prior to working as field staff, students must complete all their ALI coursework, including 3+ electives. What other courses have you completed with ALI? Please review this document for more information. For additional information please contact: Olivia Cameron at olivia.cameron@oregonstate.edu We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status. This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a Criminal History Check and be determined to be position qualified as per OSU Standard 576-055-0000 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in Youth Programs may have additional Criminal History Checks every 2 years. This position requires driving a University vehicle or a personal vehicle on behalf of the University; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a Motor Vehicle History Check, possess and maintain a current, valid driver’s license in their state of residence, be determined to be position qualified and self-report convictions (as per Voluntary and Compulsory Driver Standards OSU Standard 125-155-0200) as per OSU Standard 576-056-0000 et seq. Note: All job offers are contingent upon Human Resources final approval. Job Description: This recruitment will be used to fill multiple part-time (a maximum of 24 hours per week) ALI Field Specialist positions for Recreational Sports at Oregon State University (OSU). The Field Specialist reports to the Trips and Classes Coordinator of the Adventure Leadership Institute and is responsible for assisting with class outings, trips/activities, and programs. Field Specialists attend any required or necessary trainings or meetings that the Coordinator mandates. Field Specialists are responsible for maintaining their training and skills in the relevant activity to which they are guiding and are encouraged to seek continuing education to better themselves and the programs to which they assist. In this position, Field Specialists can expect to challenge themselves and gain ample opportunities for growth in their interpersonal and leadership skills through leading outdoor programming with ALI. Field Specialists also have the opportunity to assist in teaching PAC or LEAD classes per instructor approval. Employee Expectations Know Your Stuff : Learn, model, promote and consistently enforce policies for the Department of Recreational Sports. Communicate Like a Pro : Professionally and proactively communicate with your teammates, supervisors and administrative staff. Stay Sharp : Maintain required certifications and attend orientation sessions, staff meetings and trainings as directed. Seek Solutions : Provide quality customer service to all patrons and search for a “path to yes” when possible and aligned with mission. Deliver a Positive Experience : Maintain and promote a safe and fun environment for all participants and staff. We create engaging environments for student growth and success. We inspire healthy living by providing quality recreational and educational opportunities for the Oregon State University community. Our Values in Action As a student employee, you play a vital role in bringing our mission and values to life. We expect every team member to embody the following values in their work: We Care About People: Treat all patrons and colleagues with kindness, courtesy and inclusion. We Are Solution-Oriented: Identify and implement solutions that enhance the member and employee experience. Address conflict in a positive, calm and solution-focused manner. We Are Stewards: Use resources responsibly and ethically while working efficiently. We Are Innovative: Pioneer programs, services and facilities that serve as catalysts for student success. Create space to encourage, share and evolve new ideas. We Are Educators: Foster environments where learning and personal growth thrive for both participants and staff. Grant others the same good intentions you grant yourself. We Extend Respect & Build Trust: Communicate with clarity, transparency and integrity, fostering trust across our community. We create engaging environments for student growth and success. We inspire healthy living by providing quality recreational and educational opportunities for the Oregon State University community. Position Details, Duties and Responsibilities OSU is committed to ensuring students are prepared for success after graduation through intentional skill development as student employees. The competencies students develop while working at OSU are defined by the National Association of Colleges and Employers (NACE) as the essential skills employers seek in their hires. In this position, students learn and build vital career-readiness skills in the primary areas below: Facilitate PAC and LEAD class trips, non-credit Adventure outings, and other activities to provide learning and personal growth opportunities for OSU students and community. Coordinate logistics (including organizing, maintaining, and cleaning equipment) for PAC and LEAD classes in collaboration with ALI professional staff, co-student leaders, and ALI Operations staff. Attend and represent the ALI ™ at special events, such as welcome nights and tabling events. Execute all emergency procedures and provide First Aid and CPR assistance when necessary within the scope of ALI wilderness medicine protocols Evaluate trips process and provide recommendations for improvements on an ongoing basis Drive Motorpool vehicles for off-campus programs including credit course trips and non-credit Adventure outings Maintain department specific safety certifications, such as CPR, Wilderness First Aid or Wilderness First Responder. Provide support as needed to the ALI Operations Desk staff in the maintenance, inventory, and cleaning of trip equipment after each outing or program. Transferable Skill Development OSU is committed to ensuring students are prepared for success after graduation through intentional skill development as student employees. The competencies students develop while working at OSU are defined by the National Association of Colleges and Employers ( NACE ) as the essential skills employers seek in their hires. In this position, students learn and build vital career-readiness skills in the primary areas below: Communication, Professionalism, Critical Thinking, Career and Self Development  Qualifications: Full Employment Eligibility Requirements can be found here:   https://hr.oregonstate.edu/student-employment-manual/500-employment-eligibility-requirements Must be academically enrolled in a high school, community college, or university and pursuing a program or course of study Must meet Academic Standing Requirements; students on academic suspension are not eligible for employment Must meet the applicable minimal enrollment standard High School student: Regularly enrolled in a high school or participating in a home-schooling program Undergraduate and post-baccalaureate student: 6 credit hours per term Undergraduate international student: 12 credit hours per term* Graduate student officially admitted to Graduate School: 5 credit hours per term Graduate international student officially admitted to Graduate School: 9 credit hours per term* *International students may be allowed to carry fewer hours than specified above and still be considered “full-time” by the United States Citizenship and Immigration Services ( USCIS ). A reduced course load is approved by the   Office of International Services   ( OIS ), and must be provided to the Student Employment Center. Required Qualifications First Aid, CPR/AED for the Healthcare Provider and DRS Bloodborne Pathogens certifications is required before employment begins and will be provided by the department upon hiring Wilderness First Responder, Professional Rescuer CPR with AED, DRS Bloodborne Pathogens training. Demonstrated progress toward the Adventure Leadership Certificate with ALI including completion of LEAD 430/530 Foundations of Adventure Leadership and three ALI elective courses. The ALI Field Specialist and Field Associate positions are reserved for students who are actively pursuing their Adventure Leadership Certificate with ALI. All required coursework for the ALI Certificate must be completed before being hired as field staff: Students must complete  Wilderness First Responder , LEAD  430/530 Foundations of Adventure Leadership , and three ALI electives prior to being hired.  If you have not completed your coursework, you are not eligible for hiring at this time. You are encouraged to enroll in these courses and apply at a later date. Info on LEAD and PAC courses can be found in the course catalog. Non-credit WFR course info is found here: https://recsports.oregonstate.edu/adventure/leadership-education/classes/non-credit-classes . Please reach out to the ALI Trips and Classes Coordinator ( Olivia.cameron@oregonstate.edu ) for any questions related to this policy. This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a Criminal History Check and be determined to be position qualified as per OSU Standard 576-055-0000 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in Youth Programs may have additional Criminal History Checks every 2 years. This position requires driving a University vehicle or a personal vehicle on behalf of the University; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a Motor Vehicle History Check, possess and maintain a current, valid driver’s license in their state of residence, be determined to be position qualified and self-report convictions (as per Voluntary and Compulsory Driver Standards OSU Standard 125-155-0200) as per OSU Standard 576-056-0000 et seq. Preferred Work and Extracurricular Experience Strong role models, possess excellent conflict resolution skills and respond quickly and effectively in emergency situations. Commitment to student development and leadership. Awareness of safety concerns and risk management within an adventure education setting. Ability to provide positive customer service. Awareness of and appreciation for individual uniqueness and diversity. Excellent oral and written communication skills. Energetic and enjoy working with a variety of people and environments. Reliable, self-motivated, and work well with minimum supervision. Progress toward and/or completion of the Adventure Leadership Certificate with the Adventure Leadership Institute. Working Conditions/Schedule: Period of Employment: One academic year (continuing employment is contingent upon satisfactory completion of a 30-day probationary period and satisfactory job performance as evaluated by immediate supervisor). 2-4 hours per week on average. Field Specialists are typically assigned 1-2 weekend day or overnight trips per term. Compensation: Student employees are paid an hourly wage consistent with the OSU Student Employment Pay Rate Guidelines. The Department of Recreational Sports identifies pay ranges base upon job duties, level of responsibility and complexity of the work to be performed. Salary Range: Anticipated Start Date: 2026-11-23 Anticipated End Date:"
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/OSU_Careers_Site/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/Digital-Accessibility-Assistant--Student-_JR0000712-1": {
      "title": "Digital Accessibility Assistant (Student)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-11\nPosted: Posted 4 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nDepartment: University Information & Technology Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents: A Resume A Cover Letter Supplemental Questions Supplemental Questions: 1. Do you have any experience with assistive technology? 2. Do you have any experience with HTML, CSS or JavaScript? For additional information please contact: Tel Woolsey at woolseyt@oregonstate.edu We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status.   OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire.   Note: All job offers are contingent upon Human Resources final approval Job Description: This recruitment will be used to fill 2 part-time (a maximum of 20 hours per week) Digital Accessibility Assistant positions to support the UIT Digital Experience team. This student team will help evaluate and improve the accessibility of OSU’s digital footprint by testing university websites and digital content against recognized accessibility standards. Working primarily as accessibility auditors, team members will use web governance and accessibility testing tools such as Monsido, along with manual testing techniques, to identify barriers that affect people with disabilities and to document clear, actionable findings for website owners. Successful candidates will demonstrate strong attention to detail, a willingness to learn, and the ability to collaborate with peers to accomplish tasks. At Oregon State University (OSU), University Information and Technology (UIT) is at the forefront of the university's technological transformation. UIT is dedicated to empowering the entire university community by providing secure access to the right data, tools, and services needed to navigate and shape their unique paths to success. By protecting OSU’s technological assets and managing resources efficiently, we enable the university to thrive in an ever-evolving landscape.   Led by the Office of the Chief Information Officer (CIO), UIT manages IT operations for OSU’s three campuses, serving students in Oregon’s 36 counties and in more than 100 countries. UIT's leadership philosophy centers around a human-centric and fully architected technology landscape, leveraging enterprise solutions for common good services while empowering individual colleges and business units to innovate and invest in their missions and initiatives.   UIT designs and implements a seamless, interconnected digital ecosystem that aligns to the university’s strategic goals and maximizes impact. The division is comprised of trusted partners and consultants committed to igniting innovation and fostering a culture of continuous improvement by providing expert guidance and innovative solutions that support the university’s teaching, research, outreach and extension mission. Together, we are building a future where technology and education converge to create limitless possibilities.  Located within the Division of University Information and Technology, the Business Architecture domain bridges the gap between the OSU community and technology, ensuring alignment with strategic goals and operational needs. We collaborate to deliver tailored IT solutions that enhance capabilities, foster innovation, and drive organizational change. By understanding the needs of faculty, staff, and students and connecting them with the right resources, we help improve engagement, streamline processes, and enable seamless access to both administrative and academic technology services.   Please note all OSU IT team members are expected to meet the following commitments:         AI Statement:  OSU IT embraces the transformative potential of artificial intelligence (AI) to drive innovation, enhance efficiency, and create meaningful impact across our teaching, research, and administrative functions. As a member of the OSU IT community, the person in this position is expected to thoughtfully engage with AI tools and practices, champion their ethical and responsible use, and actively contribute to the development of AI-driven solutions that uphold our institutional values and advance the university’s mission.   Data Statement:  OSU IT commits to deliver data as a strategic working asset and to enable data informed decision making across OSU. OSU IT leaders and staff lead by example and use data to inform decision making at all levels in the organization. Persons in technology roles are also expected to design systems with data portability in mind and work within enterprise architecture and privacy guidelines.      DEIA Statement:  OSU IT commits to and believes in the strength and value of diversity, equity, inclusion, and access (DEIA) both throughout our team and as an intentional and active practice to advance the vision, mission, and strategic efforts of the entire university. As a member of the OSU IT community, the person in this position is expected to foster and promote the values of DEIA and demonstrate a commitment to inclusive excellence in their work.         Security Statement:  Proactively securing and protecting OSU’s digital assets and information systems is crucial to our missions of teaching and learning, research, and extension and engagement. All OSU IT professionals are directly responsible for providing high quality and secure IT systems and services. Persons in technology roles are expected to be responsive to security related actions and requirements, and to collaborate to find secure ways to support the OSU community.       Position Duties: Accessibility Testing & Auditing (50%) Conduct manual and automated accessibility evaluations of university websites and digital content, measuring them against WCAG 2.1 AA success criteria. Use web governance and accessibility tools (such as Monsido) along with browser-based testing tools to detect issues such as missing alternative text, improper heading structure, insufficient color contrast, keyboard navigation barriers, and inaccessible forms. Verify automated findings through manual review to confirm true issues and reduce false positives. Where assistive technologies are available, use tools such as screen readers to confirm the real-world impact of identified barriers. Retest content after remediation to confirm that reported issues have been resolved. Documentation & Reporting (20%) Record accessibility findings in a clear, consistent, and prioritized format, referencing the specific WCAG success criteria involved. Translate technical accessibility issues into plain-language explanations and actionable remediation recommendations for website owners and content authors. Collaborate with the Digital Experience team and website owners to prioritize identified issues based on severity and user impact. Maintain accurate records of audit progress and status across assigned sites and content. Contribute to shared documentation, checklists, and best-practice guides that support consistent testing across the team. Assist with preparing summary reports and supporting materials that help university staff understand accessibility findings and recommended next steps. Document Review and Remediation (20%) Review digital documents for accessibility issues and perform basic remediation to improve their structure, reading order, and usability for people with disabilities. Flag documents that require more advanced remediation for escalation to the Digital Experience team. General Duties (10%) Attend regular team meetings, participate in ongoing training, and collaborate with peers to accomplish tasks effectively. Exhibit a strong willingness to learn, problem solve, and adapt to changing project needs. Contribute to the development of new processes, tools, or strategies for enhancing the overall quality and efficiency of the Digital Experience team's work. Perform other duties as assigned to support the successful operation of the UIT Digital Experience team. These duties may be adjusted according to the specific skills and interests of the selected candidate. The position may also involve other duties as assigned to support the goals of the UIT Digital Experience team. Transferable Skill Development OSU is committed to ensuring students are prepared for success after graduation through intentional skill development as student employees. The competencies students develop while working at OSU are defined by the National Association of Colleges and Employers (NACE) as the essential skills employers seek in their hires. In this position, students learn and build vital career-readiness skills in the primary areas below:   Communication, Critical Thinking, Technology, Leadership, Professionalism Qualifications: Full Employment Eligibility Requirements can be found here:   https://hr.oregonstate.edu/student-employment-manual/500-employment-eligibility-requirements Must be academically enrolled in a high school, community college, or university and pursuing a program or course of study Must meet Academic Standing Requirements; students on academic suspension are not eligible for employment Must meet the applicable minimal enrollment standard High School student: Regularly enrolled in a high school or participating in a home-schooling program Undergraduate and post-baccalaureate student: 6 credit hours per term Undergraduate international student: 12 credit hours per term* Graduate student officially admitted to Graduate School: 5 credit hours per term Graduate international student officially admitted to Graduate School: 9 credit hours per term* *International students may be allowed to carry fewer hours than specified above and still be considered “full-time” by the United States Citizenship and Immigration Services ( USCIS ). A reduced course load is approved by the   Office of International Services   ( OIS ), and must be provided to the Student Employment Center. Preferred Qualifications: Interpersonal and Professional Skills Demonstrates dependability, professionalism, and conscientiousness; consistently follows through on commitments and displays an ethical approach to tasks and interactions. Possesses strong problem-solving skills, with the ability to approach complex issues logically and methodically. Shows initiative by proactively exploring problems, researching solutions, and persisting until the successful completion of tasks. Displays exceptional attention to detail, ensuring accuracy and consistency in all tasks undertaken. Exhibits excellent verbal and written communication skills, with a demonstrated ability to convey complex ideas clearly and effectively. Practices effective time management skills, balancing multiple tasks and priorities while maintaining high-quality output. Demonstrates strong customer service skills, focusing on understanding and addressing the needs of the end user. Openness to peer learning: willing to both share expertise and knowledge with peers, as well as learn from them. Technical Skills and Experience Familiarity with one or more of the following is helpful. None of these is required on its own, and we expect strong candidates to bring some, but not all, of these: A basic understanding of how web pages are built and structured, including HTML and CSS. Some familiarity with JavaScript is a plus but is not required. Awareness of WCAG 2.1 accessibility guidelines and general digital accessibility best practices is a plus. Content management systems: Some experience with web content management systems such as Drupal or WordPress is a plus. Exposure to accessibility or web governance tools (such as Monsido, WAVE, or axe) is a plus. Familiarity with assistive technologies such as screen readers (for example, JAWS, NVDA, or VoiceOver) is a strong plus but not expected. Working Conditions/Schedule: • Schedule: This position will work a typical Monday – Friday schedule and will be based on the Corvallis campus.    • Modality: This position is designated as hybrid, working both on-site and remotely. The exact amount of on-site and remote work and schedule will be discussed with the supervisor. Hybrid work is dependent on business needs.   • Physical Requirements: This position will experience prolonged periods sitting at a desk, using a computer and performing detailed tasks.    Salary Range: Anticipated Start Date: 2026-09-21 Anticipated End Date:"
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/OSU_Careers_Site/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/PAC-Academic---Recreational-SCUBA-Leader_JR0000359": {
      "title": "PAC Academic & Recreational SCUBA Leader",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-11\nPosted: Posted 4 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nDepartment: College of Health, Physical Activity Courses Special Instructions to Applicants: To ensure full consideration, applications must be received by August 28, 2026. Applications will continue to be accepted after the full consideration date, until a sufficient applicant pool has been achieved or the position is filled. The closing date is subject to change without notice to applicants. When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents:   1) A resume/CV; and   2) A cover letter indicating how your qualifications and experience have prepared you for this position.   3) License/Certifications: Please attach all licenses/ certification as appropriate. 4) Supplemental Questions: Please attach as a separate document. a) Please list your total number of dives, the number of dives in a professional supervisory position, and the number of dives in cold water environments? b) What is your philosophy in teaching beginners to dive? c) After reviewing the OSU Mission statement at ( https://leadership.oregonstate.edu/trustees/about-board/oregon-state-university-mission-statement ) please state how your background and experience has prepared you to support inclusive learning principles and promote equitable teaching practices. For additional information, please contact: Name: Katelyn Kirk Email: Katelyn.Kirk@oregonstate.edu Phone: 503-737-3222 Job Summary: The Physical Activities Course (PAC) in the College of Health at Oregon State University ( OSU ) invites applications for a full-time (1.00   FTE ), 9-month, combination position as PAC Academic & Recreational SCUBA Program Manager & PAC SCUBA Instructor reflecting duties in both academic and administrative areas. We passionately believe that health is for all people. Our world-leading faculty and committed students work to prevent disease and injury, identify factors that affect health and well-being, inform policy, and shape environments that improve lives and help everyone, from children to seniors, thrive. We serve others, enjoy meaningful careers and lives, innovate, approach health and well-being through a lens of equity, and co-create knowledge and solutions to pressing health challenges in the far corners of Oregon, the nation and world. Physical Activity Courses provide an interactive and engaging experience that allows student to learn through active participation in a variety of physical activities. Recognizing individual interests and abilities, the program provides courses that cater to all levels and abilities. The Office of Physical Activity and Health Programs provides all Oregon State University with the knowledge and experience to make intelligent decisions regarding present and future physically active lifestyles. The program provides quality instruction in safe, adequately equipped facilities and creates a positive, enjoyable environment for learning. KEY RESPONSIBILITIES: Management of the PAC Academic & Recreational Dive Center, PAC Academic & Recreational PADI SCUBA Diving Program, The Dive Locker Rental System, PADI SCUBA Divemaster Program and SCUBA Dive Locker Facility Management of SCUBA personnel, including SCUBA Certification Evaluator staff, Instructors, equipment management, student workers, and volunteers & Divemaster candidates Manage the DiveLocker Rental and Inventory System Collaborate with the PAC Director to manage SCUBA budget Plan and organize Instructor that maximizes Student Learning Establish meaningful student learning outcomes for courses/program Demonstrate a thorough and accurate knowledge of the their field or discipline Qualifications: REQUIRED QUALIFICATIONS: Bachelor's degree in any field 150+ dives demonstrated by an active dive log with experience in cold water environments Current and valid certification as a PADI Master SCUBA Diver Instructor & Trainer Current and valid driver’s license 1+ year experience in the operation of a dive shop, dive program, or equivalent. 1+ years experience instructing SCUBA courses Previous supervisory experience Current, valid Adult CPR, AED, and First Aid certification (must be completed prior to start of instruction; higher level certification accepted). Current, valid Emergency O2 Certification by the start of classes Demonstrated experience, knowledge and ability to instruct SCUBA courses Experience and/or certification in equipment tuning, service and maintenance Concussion training by the start of classes Evident commitment to educational equity.  Demonstrated ability to design, teach and assess courses that support inclusive learning principles and promote equitable teaching practices, to the benefit of all learners. Final candidates for this position must complete a sexual misconduct reference check, per University Policy 05-010. An offer of employment will be contingent upon satisfactory results from the sexual misconduct reference check. This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a criminal history check and be determined to be position qualified as per University Standard: 05-010 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in youth programs may have additional criminal history checks every 24 months. This position requires driving a university vehicle or a personal vehicle on behalf of the university; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a motor vehicle history check, possess and maintain a current, valid driver's license in their state of residence, be determined to be position qualified and self-report convictions as per University Policy 05-030. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS: Current and valid certification as a PADI Course Director 3+ years supervisory experience 5+ year experience in the operation of a dive shop, dive program, or equivalent. Experience mentoring and certifying Dive Masters Master's degree in any field Successful teaching experience in a higher education setting, or equivalent Experience assessing student learning outcomes and curriculum development Master's degree in any field Current and valid certification as a PADI Master SCUBA Diver Trainer, and/or Course Director 5+ years experience instructing SCUBA courses Current SCUBA cylinder inspector (PSI preferred) and experience and/or certification in SCUBA equipment formal annual service and general maintenance. Emergency O2 Certification CPR, FA, AED, and Emergency O2 Instructor Experience mentoring and certifying Dive Masters Successful teaching experience in a higher education setting, or equivalent Demonstrated ability to design, teach and assess courses that support inclusive learning principles and promote equitable teaching practices, to the benefit of all learners. Previous experience assessing student learning outcomes and curriculum development Working Conditions/Schedule: · In the performance of the duties of the position, incumbents will be required to lead physical activity courses and demonstrate activity-specific movements for a minimum duration of 50-90 minutes per scheduled class. · Ability to lift/carry SCUBA cylinders and full SCUBA rigs (50-80lbs.) and to demonstrate and teach dive rescue of adult victims · Ability to safely operate university Motor Pool vehicles, including ¾ ton pickup trucks and passenger vans · Requires working nights and/or weekends. Travel with students to open water sites and spending the night near dive sites is required. · This position requires working in water. Some work is required to be performed in an pool and/or outdoor environment, which may involve extreme temperature and weather conditions. Salary Range: $38,000 -$40,000 Anticipated Start Date: 2026-09-16 Anticipated End Date: We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status. OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire. OSU is a fair chance employer committed to inclusive hiring. We encourage applications from candidates who bring a wide range of lived experience including involvement with the justice system. If this position has “critical or security-sensitive” responsibilities and/or driving duties and you are selected as a finalist, your initial job offer will be contingent upon the results of a job-related pre-employment check (such as a background check, motor vehicle history check, sexual misconduct reference check, etc.). Background check results do not automatically disqualify a candidate. Take a look at our Background Checks website including the for candidates section for more details. If you have questions or concerns about the pre-employment check, please contact OSU’s Employee and Labor Relations team at employee.relations@oregonstate.edu ."
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/OSU_Careers_Site/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/NSE-Undergraduate-Research-Assistant_JR0001127": {
      "title": "NSE Undergraduate Research Assistant",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-11\nPosted: Posted 4 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nDepartment: NSE Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents: ●        A Resume ●        An Unofficial Transcript For additional information please contact: Lizz Koch at lizz.koch@oregonstate.edu We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status. This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a Criminal History Check and be determined to be position qualified as per OSU Standard 576-055-0000 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in Youth Programs may have additional Criminal History Checks every 2 years. OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire.   Note: All job offers are contingent upon Human Resources final approval   Job Description: This recruitment will be used to fill multiple part-time (a maximum of 24 hours per week) Undergraduate Research Assistant (URA) positions for the School of Nuclear Science & Engineering (NSE) at Oregon State University (OSU).  This position performs a variety of duties including routine to complex technical or specialized tasks in support of research, clinical or administrative work. Positions may include laboratory research and data collection. Position Duties Perform laboratory experiments, tests, or simulations under supervision. Set up, calibrate, and maintain research equipment; monitor supply inventory and assist with ordering materials as needed. Accurately document experimental results, observations, and measurements in lab notebooks or electronic databases; maintain organized records for reproducibility. Use software tools (e.g., MATLAB, Python, Excel) to analyze collected data, generate graphs/charts, and summarize findings for review by senior researchers. Follow all safety guidelines and institutional compliance standards; participate in safety training as required. Transferable Skill Development OSU is committed to ensuring students are prepared for success after graduation through intentional skill development as student employees. The competencies students develop while working at OSU are defined by the National Association of Colleges and Employers (NACE) as the essential skills employers seek in their hires. In this position, students learn and build vital career-readiness skills in the primary areas below:   Communication, Critical Thinking, Teamwork, Technology Qualifications: Full Employment Eligibility Requirements can be found here:   https://hr.oregonstate.edu/student-employment-manual/500-employment-eligibility-requirements   Must be academically enrolled in a high school, community college, or university and pursuing a program or course of study Must meet Academic Standing Requirements; students on academic suspension are not eligible for employment Must meet the applicable minimal enrollment standard High School student: Regularly enrolled in a high school or participating in a home-schooling program Undergraduate and post-baccalaureate student: 6 credit hours per term Undergraduate international student: 12 credit hours per term* Graduate student officially admitted to Graduate School: 5 credit hours per term Graduate international student officially admitted to Graduate School: 9 credit hours per term* *International students may be allowed to carry fewer hours than specified above and still be considered “full-time” by the United States Citizenship and Immigration Services ( USCIS ). A reduced course load is approved by the   Office of International Services   ( OIS ), and must be provided to the Student Employment Center. Required: This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a Criminal History Check and be determined to be position qualified as per OSU Standard 576-055-0000 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in Youth Programs may have additional Criminal History Checks every 2 years. Preferred: Undergraduate Engineering Major GPA greater than 3.00 Major or Minor in Nuclear Science & Engineering Working Conditions/Schedule: Work may be performed in labs or office spaces. Must be able to work between 5 – 24 hours per week. Salary Range: Anticipated Start Date: 2026-09-14 Anticipated End Date:"
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/OSU_Careers_Site/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/Assistant-Registrar--Enrollment---Term-Operations_JR0001065": {
      "title": "Assistant Registrar: Enrollment & Term Operations",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-11\nPosted: Posted 4 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nDepartment: Office of the Registrar Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents:     1) A resume/CV; and      2) A cover letter indicating how your qualifications and experience have prepared you for this position.     Starting salary will be commensurate with skills, education, and experience.     For additional information please contact:    Jennifer Ajeto jennifer.ajeto@oregonstate.edu Job Summary: The Office of the Registrar is seeking an Assistant Registrar for Enrollment & Term Operations. This is a full-time (1.00 FTE), 12-month, professional faculty position. Academic Operations (Office of the Registrar, Curriculum Management & University Accreditation) is a mission-critical Oregon State University unit that supports student success from admission through graduation and beyond. We safeguard institutional integrity by managing academic records, enrollment and instructional services, accreditation, and academic policies. As a trusted institutional partner, we provide expertise, resources, and systems solutions for students, faculty, and staff while upholding regulatory and accreditation standards. We foster a welcoming, inclusive, and collaborative environment where all employees are valued for their contributions to student success. The Assistant Registrar for Enrollment & Term Operations position in the Office of the Registrar is a member of the Operations Team, reporting to the Senior Associate Registrar. This position is responsible for enrollment status compliance and external reporting to the National Student Clearinghouse (NSC), academic term setup and related end of term processing, integrity of data in the student information system (SIS), with application of procedures and regulations. Independent judgements are frequently necessary for policy exceptions and internal operations decisions. The Assistant Registrar is responsible for the supervision of one employee. Key Responsibilities Serve as the institutional subject matter expert for National Student Clearinghouse (NSC) enrollment reporting , ensuring accurate, timely compliance reporting to the NSC and National Student Loan Data System (NSLDS) in accordance with federal financial aid regulations and Department of Education requirements. Manage and enhance enrollment reporting operations , including error resolution, data discrepancy investigations, reporting schedules, compliance monitoring, audit responses, and implementation of best practices to mitigate reporting risks and ensure regulatory compliance. Administration and configuration of term setup , including registration rules, holds, priority registration controls, special population processes, academic calendar deadlines, and testing of system upgrades and new functionality. Oversee critical academic records and end-of-term processes , including grade submission, prerequisite enforcement, and student record updates related to administrative actions and academic regulations. Collaborate with campus partners and external stakeholders , including the academic departments, and federal and state agencies to support student records and enrollment compliance initiatives. Qualifications: Required Qualifications Bachelor's degree. A minimum of two years' experience in a registrar's office or similar professional environment. Experience with data management, error resolution, and compliance reporting to the external agencies. Experience analyzing multifaceted challenges using an equity lens, evaluating options, predicting outcomes and disparities, forecast consequences, and driving actionable, equitable solutions. Experience providing exceptional customer service, with the confidence to act when critical situations arise. Experience with a student information system (e.g., Banner, Colleague, Jenzabar) Ability to analyze complex issues and situations, identify alternative solutions, forecast of consequences and recommend an effective course of action. Excellent written and verbal communication skills, including ability to communicate with administrative management and academic constituencies. Experience interpreting and communicating complex policies and procedures to stakeholders. Experience working as a team member on projects and implementation of new initiatives. Ability to promote policy and regulation equality and integrity in all work, ensuring diverse needs of OSU's students are appropriately addressed. This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a criminal history check and be determined to be position qualified as per University Standard: 05-010 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in youth programs may have additional criminal history checks every   24 months .   Preferred Qualifications A minimum of one year of supervision experience. Experience training or providing instructions to staff, students or stakeholders as a functional user and content expert. Ability to work within an environment where ambiguity and the ability to pivot quickly are commonplace. Experience coordinating all aspects of enrollment data reporting process with the National Student Clearinghouse. Familiarity with the academic regulations and policies that pertain to student records and registration in higher education. Working Conditions/Schedule: Typical office environment, 8 am-5 pm, Monday through Friday. Salary Range: $60,700 -$67,000 Anticipated Start Date: 2026-10-26 Anticipated End Date: We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status. OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire. OSU is a fair chance employer committed to inclusive hiring. We encourage applications from candidates who bring a wide range of lived experience including involvement with the justice system. If this position has “critical or security-sensitive” responsibilities and/or driving duties and you are selected as a finalist, your initial job offer will be contingent upon the results of a job-related pre-employment check (such as a background check, motor vehicle history check, sexual misconduct reference check, etc.). Background check results do not automatically disqualify a candidate. Take a look at our Background Checks website including the for candidates section for more details. If you have questions or concerns about the pre-employment check, please contact OSU’s Employee and Labor Relations team at employee.relations@oregonstate.edu ."
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/OSU_Careers_Site/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/FRC-ECE-Aide---Student-Early-Care-and-Education-Aide_JR0001073": {
      "title": "FRC ECE Aide - Student Early Care and Education Aide",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-11\nPosted: Posted 4 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nDepartment: Family Resource Center Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents: A Resume A Cover Letter For additional information please contact: Shanon Rice at Shanon.rice@oregonstate.edu We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status.   This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a Criminal History Check and be determined to be position qualified as per OSU Standard 576-055-0000 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in Youth Programs may have additional Criminal History Checks every 2 years.   OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire.   Note: All job offers are contingent upon Human Resources final approval Job Description: This recruitment will be used to fill 10 part-time (a maximum of 24 hours per week) FRC ECE Aide - Student Early Care and Education for the Family Resource Center at Oregon State University (OSU). The Student Early Care and Education Aide (Aide) supports the lead and assistant teachers at any of the Family Resource Center's Early Care and Education (ECE) centers on the Oregon State University Corvallis campus. The Aides will be an on-call position to fill in during staff absences and vacations. Campus ECE centers are committed to continually discovering, creating, and sustaining a teaching and learning environment that supports inquiry, diversity, and equity. Aides are responsible (as outlined below) for the areas to which they are assigned and the young children in their care. This position supports the full-time teacher(s), the Center Directors, helps with other Center wide duties. Aides report to the lead and assistant teachers of their respective classrooms. This position requires knowledge of child development, excellent communication skills and tremendous patience to provide a safe, emotionally secure, and nurturing environment for the healthy development of young children. The primary duties will be to assist with day-to-day classroom operations. Position Duties: Assist in a licensed early care and education center classroom. Specific duties include, but are not limited to: Assist in the implementation of a developmentally appropriate, play-based, culturally responsive, and equity-oriented educational program with an emergent curriculum for an assigned program and age range of children.  Prepare and maintain a safe, attractive, neat, and functional environment, both indoors and outdoors, and utilize the environment as a teaching tool. Support lead and assistant teachers with daily documentation. Assist program staff with lunch and break time schedules. Assist with bottle preparation and preparing snacks. Form a positive, supportive team atmosphere with other program teachers and staff members. Transferable Skill Development OSU is committed to ensuring students are prepared for success after graduation through intentional skill development as student employees. The competencies students develop while working at OSU are defined by the National Association of Colleges and Employers (NACE) as the essential skills employers seek in their hires. In this position, students learn and build vital career-readiness skills in the primary areas below:   Communication, Critical Thinking, Teamwork, Career & Self Development Qualifications: Full Employment Eligibility Requirements can be found here:   https://hr.oregonstate.edu/student-employment-manual/500-employment-eligibility-requirements Must be academically enrolled in a high school, community college, or university and pursuing a program or course of study Must meet Academic Standing Requirements; students on academic suspension are not eligible for employment Must meet the applicable minimal enrollment standard High School student: Regularly enrolled in a high school or participating in a home-schooling program Undergraduate and post-baccalaureate student: 6 credit hours per term Undergraduate international student: 12 credit hours per term* Graduate student officially admitted to Graduate School: 5 credit hours per term Graduate international student officially admitted to Graduate School: 9 credit hours per term* *International students may be allowed to carry fewer hours than specified above and still be considered “full-time” by the United States Citizenship and Immigration Services ( USCIS ). A reduced course load is approved by the   Office of International Services   ( OIS ), and must be provided to the Student Employment Center. Required Qualifications: Experience caring for children 0-5 years old Demonstrable contributions to promoting and enhancing diversity Food Handler’s Certification Oregon ELC Background Clearance- Criminal Background Registry (CBR) Adult & Pediatric CPR, First Aid, AED within 90 day of employment OSU Background Clearance Additional Required Qualifications: This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a Criminal History Check and be determined to be position qualified as per OSU Standard 576-055-0000 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in Youth Programs may have additional Criminal History Checks every 2 years. Preferred Qualifications: Experience in a licensed early care and education center Previous or current college courses in Early Childhood Education, Early Education, Human Development and Family Sciences, Special Education, or related field. Working Conditions/Schedule: • Supervise and interact daily with children both indoors and outdoors (for extended periods in varied weather conditions) by maintaining mental and physical alertness and an appropriate level of energy throughout the day • Repeatedly lift, reach (overhead, at and below shoulder level), squat, crouch, climb, sit, bend, stretch, twist, grasp/move small objects, push and pull, and engage in all other physical movements needed to fully participate in supervision and curriculum and care delivery activities, which take place while standing, sitting (including on floor or low chairs), and walking • Stand for extended periods of time • Ability to respond immediately and appropriately to multiple or unexpected situations or emergencies, which may include the necessity to take rapid physical actions to assist children and/or evacuate Salary Range: Anticipated Start Date: 2026-09-23 Anticipated End Date:"
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/OSU_Careers_Site/job/CEOAS-Instructor-Pool--2026-2027_JR0001015": {
      "title": "CEOAS Instructor Pool- 2026/2027",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-10\nPosted: Posted 5 Days Ago\n\nDepartment: College of Earth, Ocean and Atmospheric Sciences Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents:   1) A resume/CV; and   2) A cover letter indicating how your qualifications and experience have prepared you for this position. 3) A Statement of Teaching. 4) Upload your response to the supplemental question as a separate document: Please describe your prior teaching experience in higher education, including institution and courses taught, mode of delivery (in person, online, lab, field, etc), number of students enrolled, and your role in the course (instructor of record, teaching assistant, etc). Starting salary will be commensurate with skills, education, and experience. For additional information please contact: Kaplan Yalcin at Kaplan.Yalcin@oregonstate.edu or 541-737-1230. Job Summary: The College of Earth, Ocean and Atmospheric Sciences in Corvallis, Oregon, invites applications for one or more fixed-term, non-tenure-track full/part-time Instructor/Senior Instructor I positions to teach on a term-by-term basis for the 2026-2027 academic year. Appointment at the Instructor rank is anticipated; however, appointment at a promoted rank may be considered depending upon the qualifications of the successful candidate. Some of these appointments may be reviewed for renewal or transition to an instructional position on an annual basis at the discretion of the Assistant Dean for Undergraduate Programs. Applications submitted to this recruitment pool are reviewed on an as needed basis. Qualified candidates will be contacted when positions become available. The primary purpose of these positions is to instruct on a term-by-term basis. Instructors may be needed to teach courses, either on-campus or Ecampus, in the following areas: Earth Sciences, Environmental Sciences, Climate Science, Geography, Geology, Geographic Information Sciences, Marine Resource Management, and Ocean and Atmospheric Sciences. Applications may be considered throughout the 2026-27 academic year. Key Responsibilities: D eliver instruction Assess student learning Assign student grades Collaborate with other faculty and program directors Manage graduate teaching assistants and/or undergraduate learning assistants Qualifications: Required Qualifications: Instructor Rank: Master of Arts (MA)/Master of Science (MS) in discipline central to Earth or Environmental Sciences such as Atmospheric Sciences, Environmental Sciences, Geology, Geography, Oceanography of fields relevant to the course being taught. Evidence of or commitment to excellence in inclusive teaching and student success.   Senior Instructor I Rank: PhD in Geology, Geography, Geospatial Science, Oceanography, Atmospheric Sciences, Environmental sciences, or a closely-related field. Four years (full time equivalent) of professional experience teaching at the college or university level. Sustained record of exceptional teaching at the college or university level as evidenced by student evaluations of teaching. Preferred Qualifications: All Ranks: Demonstrated ability to teach across modalities, including online courses, and proficiency with online teaching platforms such as Canvas. Instructor Rank: Vision for incorporating high impact pedagogical practices into course design and teaching, including but not limited to experiential learning, community-engaged teaching, inquiry-based learning, and career integration. Senior Instructor I Rank: Demonstrated experience incorporating high impact pedagogical practices course design and teaching, including but not limited to experiential learning, community-engaged teaching, inquiry-based learning, and career integration. Working Conditions/Schedule: This position is located onsite in Corvallis, OR. The incumbent may work remotely as agreed upon with the supervisor, but is expected to be available for on-campus (face to face) teaching assignments and is required to hold on-campus office hours when teaching on-campus classes. Salary Range: Starting salary will be commensurate with skills, education, and experience. Anticipated Start Date: 2026-09-16 Anticipated End Date: 2027-06-15 We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status. OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire. OSU is a fair chance employer committed to inclusive hiring. We encourage applications from candidates who bring a wide range of lived experience including involvement with the justice system. If this position has “critical or security-sensitive” responsibilities and/or driving duties and you are selected as a finalist, your initial job offer will be contingent upon the results of a job-related pre-employment check (such as a background check, motor vehicle history check, sexual misconduct reference check, etc.). Background check results do not automatically disqualify a candidate. Take a look at our Background Checks website including the for candidates section for more details. If you have questions or concerns about the pre-employment check, please contact OSU’s Employee and Labor Relations team at employee.relations@oregonstate.edu ."
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/OSU_Careers_Site/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/BA-223-Student-Teaching-Assistant_JR0001101": {
      "title": "BA 223 Student Teaching Assistant",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-10\nPosted: Posted 5 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nDepartment: College of Business – School of Marketing, Analytics, and Design Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents: ●        A Resume ●        A Cover Letter For additional information please contact: Franzi Gibbons at franziska.gibbons@oregonstate.edu We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status. This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a Criminal History Check and be determined to be position qualified as per OSU Standard 576-055-0000 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in Youth Programs may have additional Criminal History Checks every 2 years. OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire.   Note: All job offers are contingent upon Human Resources final approval Job Description: This recruitment will be used to fill 1 part-time (a maximum of 24 hours per week) student job for the College of Business at Oregon State University (OSU). You will be a teaching assistant for the class BA 223 and will support instructors with various tasks such as grading, email replies, proctoring, and monitoring the course site. Pay rate for this position is $16.55 per hour. Position Duties Grade assignments Proctor exams Communicate with students Support in the classroom and on course site OSU is committed to ensuring students are prepared for success after graduation through intentional skill development as student employees. The competencies students develop while working at OSU are defined by the National Association of Colleges and Employers (NACE) as the essential skills employers seek in their hires. In this position, students learn and build vital career-readiness skills in the primary areas below:   Communication, Teamwork, Professionalism Qualifications: Full Employment Eligibility Requirements can be found here:   https://hr.oregonstate.edu/student-employment-manual/500-employment-eligibility-requirements Must be academically enrolled in a high school, community college, or university and pursuing a program or course of study Must meet Academic Standing Requirements; students on academic suspension are not eligible for employment Must meet the applicable minimal enrollment standard High School student: Regularly enrolled in a high school or participating in a home-schooling program Undergraduate and post-baccalaureate student: 6 credit hours per term Undergraduate international student: 12 credit hours per term* Graduate student officially admitted to Graduate School: 5 credit hours per term Graduate international student officially admitted to Graduate School: 9 credit hours per term* *International students may be allowed to carry fewer hours than specified above and still be considered “full-time” by the United States Citizenship and Immigration Services ( USCIS ). A reduced course load is approved by the   Office of International Services   ( OIS ), and must be provided to the Student Employment Center. Required: This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a Criminal History Check and be determined to be position qualified as per OSU Standard 576-055-0000 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in Youth Programs may have additional Criminal History Checks every 2 years. Preferred: Have taken BA 223 in the past College of Business student Working Conditions/Schedule: Average of 5 hours of work per week. Salary Range: Anticipated Start Date: 2026-09-21 Anticipated End Date:"
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/OSU_Careers_Site/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/Work-Study-Only--MUS-Vocal-Opera-Student-Assistant_JR0001097": {
      "title": "Work Study Only: MUS Vocal/Opera Student Assistant",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-10\nPosted: Posted 5 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nDepartment: SVPDA Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents: Brief statement (no more than 300 words) as to why you would like the job and why you feel you are qualified for it. Please tell us about your characteristics and experience that would make you a good VSOP One-page resume indicating: Basic information: who are you and how do we get in touch with you? Education: high school and college, what year are you in school, what degree(s) are you pursuing, educational experiences outside of regular OSU courses; Performing experience: ensembles, solo performances, opera roles, at OSU and outside; Previous work experience and any other information relevant to this position (academic or performance awards, special skills, etc.) For additional information please contact: Nicholas Larson at Nicholas.larson@oregonstate.edu We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status.   OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire.   Note: All job offers are contingent upon Human Resources final approval Job Description: This recruitment will be used to fill one part-time (a maximum of 24 hours per week) MUS Vocal/Opera Student Assistant for the School of VPDA at Oregon State University (OSU). The Vocal Studies/Opera Intern (VSOP), a work-study position, plays an essential role in the Vocal Studies program. The student occupying this position has a high degree of responsibility and is involved in nearly all aspects of Vocal Studies. The Vocal Studies/Opera Intern position is allotted twenty billable hours per week. Position Duties: General Responsibilities may include, but are not limited to: Serving as a liaison between the Director of Vocal Studies and students, organizing and monitoring voice juries each term Assisting with Vocal/Choral auditions at the beginning of the year Maintaining student files via Google Drive Participating when appropriate in Voice area faculty meetings Overseeing logistics and publicity for visiting master class teachers For the Opera Workshop, the VSOP’s responsibilities could include but are not limited to: Serving as the assistant to the Director of the Opera Workshop Organizing and overseeing Opera Workshop auditions Producing the weekly rehearsal schedule Participating in all production meetings Serving as the costume master and often designer Procuring and transporting all set pieces Serving as the props master Serving as liaison with all performance and rehearsal venues Assisting in generating publicity, including via social media Overseeing house management and stage management for performances Serving as stage director for at least one scene Handling logistics relating to load-in, strike, and load-out Transferable Skill Development OSU is committed to ensuring students are prepared for success after graduation through intentional skill development as student employees. The competencies students develop while working at OSU are defined by the National Association of Colleges and Employers (NACE) as the essential skills employers seek in their hires. In this position, students learn and build vital career-readiness skills in the primary areas below:   Communication, Teamwork, Leadership, Professionalism Qualifications: Full Employment Eligibility Requirements can be found here:   https://hr.oregonstate.edu/student-employment-manual/500-employment-eligibility-requirements Must be academically enrolled in a high school, community college, or university and pursuing a program or course of study Must meet Academic Standing Requirements; students on academic suspension are not eligible for employment Must meet the applicable minimal enrollment standard High School student: Regularly enrolled in a high school or participating in a home-schooling program Undergraduate and post-baccalaureate student: 6 credit hours per term Undergraduate international student: 12 credit hours per term* Graduate student officially admitted to Graduate School: 5 credit hours per term Graduate international student officially admitted to Graduate School: 9 credit hours per term* *International students may be allowed to carry fewer hours than specified above and still be considered “full-time” by the United States Citizenship and Immigration Services ( USCIS ). A reduced course load is approved by the   Office of International Services   ( OIS ), and must be provided to the Student Employment Center. Preferred Qualifications: A Vocal Performance option The ability to serve as VSOP for two years Working Conditions/Schedule: Salary Range: Anticipated Start Date: 2026-09-16 Anticipated End Date:"
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/OSU_Careers_Site/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/Operations-Manager_JR0000991": {
      "title": "Operations Manager",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-10\nPosted: Posted 5 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nDepartment: College of Business Special Instructions to Applicants: To ensure full consideration, applications must be received by 10/1/26. Applications will continue to be accepted after the full consideration date, until a sufficient applicant pool has been achieved or the position is filled. The closing date is subject to change without notice to applicants. When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents:   1) A resume/CV; and   2) A cover letter indicating how your qualifications and experience have prepared you for this position.   Starting salary will be commensurate with skills, education, and experience. For additional information please contact: Marilyn Pentecost marilyn.pentecost@oregonstate.edu 1-541- 737-4033 Job Summary: The College of Business is seeking an Operations Manager . This is a full-time (1.00 FTE), 12-month, professional faculty position . The Operations Manager provides leadership and oversight for the day-to-day operations, facilities, and academic support services of the College of Business. This position manages College space and facilities, oversees Faculty Services, supervises staff and student employees, manages operational resources and budgets, and leads projects that support the College’s instructional, research, and administrative missions. Working collaboratively with College leadership, university partners, vendors, faculty, staff, and external stakeholders, the Operations Manager uses independent judgment to anticipate needs, resolve complex operational issues, and ensure effective and equitable use of College resources. The College of Business is a dynamic and collaborative environment committed to academic excellence, student success, innovation, and meaningful engagement with the business community. The Operations Manager plays a critical role in creating an effective environment for faculty, staff, and students by strengthening operational systems, supporting high-quality academic services, managing facilities and resources, and leading initiatives that improve the College’s spaces and operations. This position offers an opportunity to make a broad, visible impact on how the College operates and serves its community. Learn more about the College of Business at: https://business.oregonstate.edu/ Key Responsibilities: Lead College facilities and space management, including planning, allocation, maintenance, emergency preparedness, and operational improvements across multiple locations. Manage and oversee Faculty Services operations, ensuring timely, high-quality support for faculty, visitors, academic activities, and College events. Supervise, develop, and support Faculty Services staff and student employees, fostering an inclusive environment and opportunities for professional growth. Manage operational resources and budgets, including facilities, supplies, space, equipment, and other College operational needs. Lead and coordinate facilities, operations, and academic services projects, working with College leadership, university partners, contractors, and other stakeholders to achieve project goals. Oversee operational systems, processes, and data that support effective College operations, including space, facilities, inventory, access, and institutional reporting. Serve as a key operational partner to College leadership, using independent judgment to anticipate needs, resolve complex issues, and improve the efficiency and effectiveness of College operations. Qualifications: Required Qualifications: Bachelor’s degree in Business Administration, Business Management/Operations, Human Resource Management or related field with five or more years of supervisory and management experience with budget responsibilities in an organization with diverse elements. Experience as manager includes responsibilities for providing operational excellence and delivering quality customer service. Experience coordinating with other support services (e.g., marketing, HR, IT). Ability to work in a team setting as a leader for a responsive and diverse set of professionals. Demonstrated ability to work independently and resolve problems. Self-motivated/directed and willing to take on any tasks that might arise. Excellent customer service and communication skills (both written and verbal); ability to utilize inclusive language and communicate across cultural boundaries. Exceptional organizational skills. High computer proficiency in MS office applications and database applications; an ability and willingness to learn new programs & applications quickly. Experience managing data collection and input, generating reports and analyzing results through an inclusive lens. Demonstrated project management experience with responsibility for meeting budget, schedule and scope parameters. Demonstrated success in improving/implementing processes and services; ability to review processes and services for improvement to ensure accessibility and inclusivity. Ability to juggle multiple tasks & responsibilities, prioritize appropriately, pay careful attention to details, and meet deadlines. Ability to maintain professional decorum and show a high level of diplomacy with all clients served, regardless of background. This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a criminal history check and be determined to be position qualified as per University Standard: 05-010 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in youth programs may have additional criminal history checks every 24 months. Preferred Qualifications: Experience with HR processes and systems Experience with building management Knowledge of Salesforce, Workday, Concur, Qualtrics, Bluebeam Working Conditions/Schedule: Normal work schedule is 8 a.m. to 5 p.m., Monday-Friday, but evening/weekend hours will be needed intermittently to support facilities work, projects and/or high-volume work periods. Occasional travel may be required as needed to manage facilities and projects. Salary Range: $79,600-$87,900 Anticipated Start Date: 2026-10-26 Anticipated End Date: We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status. OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire. OSU is a fair chance employer committed to inclusive hiring. We encourage applications from candidates who bring a wide range of lived experience including involvement with the justice system. If this position has “critical or security-sensitive” responsibilities and/or driving duties and you are selected as a finalist, your initial job offer will be contingent upon the results of a job-related pre-employment check (such as a background check, motor vehicle history check, sexual misconduct reference check, etc.). Background check results do not automatically disqualify a candidate. Take a look at our Background Checks website including the for candidates section for more details. If you have questions or concerns about the pre-employment check, please contact OSU’s Employee and Labor Relations team at employee.relations@oregonstate.edu ."
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/OSU_Careers_Site/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/PRAx-Livestreaming-Student-Associate_JR0001098": {
      "title": "PRAx Livestreaming Student Associate",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-10\nPosted: Posted 5 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nDepartment: PRAx Production Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents: A Resume A Cover Letter For additional information please contact: Alden Micklavzina at alden.micklavzina@oregonstate.edu We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status   OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire.   Note: All job offers are contingent upon Human Resources final approval Job Description: This recruitment will be used to fill 15 part-time (a maximum of 24 hours per week) PRAx Livestreaming Student Associate for PRAx Production at Oregon State University (OSU). This position is responsible for operating the livestreaming equipment at PRAx, including PTZ Cameras, video switcher, audio mixer, and livestream dashboard, primarily during the Student Arts Season. Employees are responsible for ensuring the stream looks professional and is a pleasant viewing and listening experience for viewers nationwide. In this position, employees will learn how to direct a broadcast, and operate industry standard equipment. In addition, this position offers the opportunity to learn technical and communication skills that are relevant across the broadcast industry. Position Duties: Livestream Setup: Assist with setting up cameras, connecting cables or setting up other video equipment Livestream support during shifts may include: Operating and switching cameras at direction of PRAx staff and director to get the necessary shots Directing livestream to select the necessary shots and give direction to camera operator(s) Ensuring livestream is successfully being sent to YouTube, including successful delivery of audio and video without issue Respond to minor streaming issues or escalate concerns to PRAx staff Post-Event Duties: Put away all livestream equipment Notify PRAx staff of any equipment malfunction or damage Additional Tasks: Maintain compliance with OSU policies and safety guidelines Transferable Skill Development OSU is committed to ensuring students are prepared for success after graduation through intentional skill development as student employees. The competencies students develop while working at OSU are defined by the National Association of Colleges and Employers (NACE) as the essential skills employers seek in their hires. In this position, students learn and build vital career-readiness skills in the primary areas below:   Critical Thinking, Teamwork, Technology, Professionalism Qualifications: Full Employment Eligibility Requirements can be found here:   https://hr.oregonstate.edu/student-employment-manual/500-employment-eligibility-requirements Must be academically enrolled in a high school, community college, or university and pursuing a program or course of study Must meet Academic Standing Requirements; students on academic suspension are not eligible for employment Must meet the applicable minimal enrollment standard High School student: Regularly enrolled in a high school or participating in a home-schooling program Undergraduate and post-baccalaureate student: 6 credit hours per term Undergraduate international student: 12 credit hours per term* Graduate student officially admitted to Graduate School: 5 credit hours per term Graduate international student officially admitted to Graduate School: 9 credit hours per term* *International students may be allowed to carry fewer hours than specified above and still be considered “full-time” by the United States Citizenship and Immigration Services ( USCIS ). A reduced course load is approved by the   Office of International Services   ( OIS ), and must be provided to the Student Employment Center. Required Qualifications:  Timely and punctual arrival to shift Ability to think creatively and innovatively Is comfortable giving and receiving feedback Is adaptable to a changing environment Ability to follow multiple sets of simultaneous verbal direction Willingness to learn and be trained on unfamiliar AV systems Preferred Qualifications: Experience in livestreaming and/or broadcast Experience with cameras and/or recording equipment Willingness to independently research equipment and techniques Working Conditions/Schedule: • Hours are based on the needs of events. Therefore, the schedule is variable and there is not a set number of hours guaranteed each week. • Must be able to work flexible hours, including weekdays, evenings, and weekends, depending on event needs. • Work is performed in theatres, studios, and other performance venues. • May involve standing for long periods, working in dim lighting and/or noisy environments, and working in confined and/or crowded areas. • Possible exposure to weather or outdoor conditions. Salary Range: Anticipated Start Date: 2026-09-21 Anticipated End Date:"
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/OSU_Careers_Site/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/KidSpirit-Youth-Program-Admin_JR0001047": {
      "title": "KidSpirit Youth Program Admin",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-15\nPosted: Posted Today\nPart time\n\nDepartment: KidSpirit Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic document: ● A Resume In addition to applying to the OSU job website portal please go to KidSpirit website to join our team and apply to KidSpirit. https://kidspirit.oregonstate.edu/employment For additional information please contact: kidspirit@oregonstate.edu We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status. OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire. Note: All job offers are contingent upon Human Resources final approval Job Description: This recruitment will be used to fill four part-time (a maximum of 24 hours per week) student positions for the KidSpirit Youth Program Admin at Oregon State University (OSU). POSITION DUTIES: Assist with the creation, editing, updating and maintenance of program projects and reports; maintain a variety of schedules. Attending orientations, meetings, and in-service training. Completing and submitting appropriate documents as needed (incident/accident reports, monthly timesheets, lesson plans, etc.) Using information technology to assist with maintaining systems or database security. Double checking attendance and entering it into the information system, double checking all processes are being done in WRIKE, monitoring emails, radios and answering phones. Perform data entry functions which may include personal information. Processing financial transactions (cash, check and credit card payments) and making cash deposits. Maintain equipment and supplies inventory. Administer first aid and activate emergency action steps if medical attention is required. May care for sick/injured students including handling medications. Follow emergency action procedures and documentation tasks (phone, radio, in person). Transferable Skill Development OSU is committed to ensuring students are prepared for success after graduation through intentional skill development as student employees. The competencies students develop while working at OSU are defined by the National Association of Colleges and Employers (NACE) as the essential skills employers seek in their hires. In this position, students learn and build vital career-readiness skills in the primary areas below:   Teamwork, Leadership, Professionalism, Career & Self Development Qualifications: Full Employment Eligibility Requirements can be found here:   https://hr.oregonstate.edu/student-employment-manual/500-employment-eligibility-requirements   Must be academically enrolled in a high school, community college, or university and pursuing a program or course of study Must meet Academic Standing Requirements; students on academic suspension are not eligible for employment Must meet the applicable minimal enrollment standard High School student: Regularly enrolled in a high school or participating in a home-schooling program Undergraduate and post-baccalaureate student: 6 credit hours per term Undergraduate international student: 12 credit hours per term* Graduate student officially admitted to Graduate School: 5 credit hours per term Graduate international student officially admitted to Graduate School: 9 credit hours per term* *International students may be allowed to carry fewer hours than specified above and still be considered “full-time” by the United States Citizenship and Immigration Services ( USCIS ). A reduced course load is approved by the   Office of International Services   ( OIS ), and must be provided to the Student Employment Center. REQUIRED QUALIFICATIONS: The onboarding will require these trainings. KidSpirit will communicate how to complete once hired. Current, valid First Aid certification by the start of programming. Recognizing and Reporting Child Abuse and Neglect (RRCAN) certification by start of programming. Introduction to Child Care Health & Safety certification by start of programming. Fundamentals of Learning This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a Criminal History Check and be determined to be position qualified as per OSU Standard 576-055-0000 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in Youth Programs may have additional Criminal History Checks every 24 months. OSU Youth Safety and Compliance requirements, standards of behavior and mandatory reporting training. Working Conditions/Schedule: Salary Range: 15.55 - 21.50 Anticipated Start Date: 2026-09-21 Anticipated End Date:"
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/OSU_Careers_Site/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/Administrative-Program-Specialist_JR0000731": {
      "title": "Administrative Program Specialist",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-09\nPosted: Posted 6 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nDepartment: Animal & Rangeland Science Special Instructions to Applicants: To ensure full consideration, applications must be received by September 23, 2026. Applications will continue to be accepted after the full consideration date, until a sufficient applicant pool has been achieved or the position is filled. The closing date is subject to change without notice to applicants. When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents:   1) A resume/CV; and   2) A cover letter indicating how you meet the required and preferred qualifications.   Starting salary will be commensurate with skills, education, and experience.   For additional information please contact: Tedd Huffman Tedd.huffman@oregonstate.edu 1-541-737-1619 Job Summary: This recruitment will be used to fill one full-time Administrative Program Specialist position for the Department of Animal & Rangeland Sciences at Oregon State University (OSU). The ADMINISTRATIVE PROGRAM SPECIALIST assists a program manager or administrator to ensure the smooth and efficient completion of all activities required for effective administrative support in the Department of Animal and Rangeland Sciences (AnRS) at Oregon State University. The primary function of the department is teaching, research, and Extension. AnRS has 41 faculty, 2 professional faculty, 7 support staff, 24 graduate students, and 429 undergraduate students. In addition, AnRS manages the James Oldfield Animal Teaching Facility and shares Withycombe Hall on the Corvallis campus and has 8 farm units in the Corvallis area. Work at this level requires a comprehensive knowledge of the program or operational area. Key Responsibilities: Graduate Program Coordinator for the Department of Animal and Rangeland Sciences. Web content coordination and communications. Department Compliance and coordination. Building Coordinator/Manager including office space coordination, key and building access management, facilities maintenance coordination, and custodial services coordination. Support to the Department’s Scholarship Committee. Department invoice and payment processing. Telecom coordination Process Courtesy Faculty applications. Backup for Department Office Manager. Qualifications: Required Qualifications: Two years of experience as an administrative specialist or executive support specialist which included administrative support for a project, program, or operation. Administrative support includes those duties beyond clerical/secretarial such as: interpretation of laws, rules, and regulations; administrative data collection and analysis; and evaluation of projects, processes, and operations; OR an equivalent combination of training and experience. ( One year of postsecondary education may be substituted for up to one year of the experience) Demonstrated excellent interpersonal communication skills, dependable and customer service oriented with evidence of working collaboratively with individuals and groups/other units/agencies, and keen attention to detail. Ability to communicate effectively in written form, such as in reports, websites,  presentations and outreach materials. Excellent time management skills to prioritize multiple projects. Commitment to inclusive excellence. This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a criminal history check and be determined to be position qualified as per University Standard: 05-010 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in youth programs may have additional criminal history checks every 24 months. Preferred Qualifications: Proficiency in a Windows environment (Word, Excel, PowerPoint, Outlook, Teams), Adobe, and Zoom. Experience with Workday, Slate, Canvas, Concur, Banner, and similar applications Knowledge of Oregon State University policies. Bachelor’s Degree. Higher Education experience. Public or non-profit experience. Working Conditions/Schedule: Full time office work in the Department’s main office. Coordinates facilities work through two buildings on campus and support to farm units. Regular business schedule, occasional work outside normal work hours once or twice a year Salary Range: $22.84 - $34.70 Anticipated Start Date: 2026-09-04 Anticipated End Date: We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status. OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire. OSU is a fair chance employer committed to inclusive hiring. We encourage applications from candidates who bring a wide range of lived experience including involvement with the justice system. If this position has “critical or security-sensitive” responsibilities and/or driving duties and you are selected as a finalist, your initial job offer will be contingent upon the results of a job-related pre-employment check (such as a background check, motor vehicle history check, sexual misconduct reference check, etc.). Background check results do not automatically disqualify a candidate. Take a look at our Background Checks website including the for candidates section for more details. If you have questions or concerns about the pre-employment check, please contact OSU’s Employee and Labor Relations team at employee.relations@oregonstate.edu ."
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/OSU_Careers_Site/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/OSU-KidSpirit---Archery-Youth-Program-Instructor---Coach---Event-Counselor_JR0000968": {
      "title": "OSU KidSpirit - Archery Youth Program Instructor / Coach / Event Counselor",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-09\nPosted: Posted 6 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nDepartment: KidSpirit Special Instructions to Applicants: Job Description: Instruction, Lesson Planning & Youth Accountability Head Instructor  (65%) Head Instructor Create and maintain an instructional environment that prioritizes skill mastery, emotional safety, and positive youth development. Plan, prepare, and implement age-appropriate lessons and activities that support participant growth and engagement. Use observation, critical thinking, and feedback strategies to evaluate participants and support staff performance. Maintain accountability for all youth in assigned areas, ensuring consistent supervision and responsive care. Experience in archery is preferred. Safety, First Aid & Documentation (10%) Monitor participant safety at all times and administer first aid when necessary. Initiate emergency action procedures appropriately and calmly. Accurately complete incident, accident, and behavioral reports as required. Use sound judgment to assess situations, respond appropriately, and communicate effectively with supervisors and families. Training, Meetings & Professional Development (10%) Attend required orientation sessions, program meetings, and in‑service training. Review and comply with all program policies, procedures, and activity guidelines. Actively pursue opportunities for career growth and skill development through training and feedback.   Equipment & Supplies Management (5%) Check out, request, and properly use equipment and supplies for activities. Maintain and organize the equipment area before clocking out each day. Report equipment needs, safety concerns, and supply issues to supervisors. Program Promotion & Family Engagement (5%) Communicate effectively with parents regarding participant progress and development. Promote KidSpirit programs including recreational classes, camps, ACES, and special events. Serve as a positive ambassador for the program, using strong interpersonal communication skills. Communication, Paperwork & Record Keeping (5%) Maintain clear, timely communication with coworkers and supervisors, including responding promptly to emails and phone calls. Complete necessary paperwork, attendance sheets, and documentation with accuracy and attention to detail. Collaborate with team members to support smooth program operations. Other Duties Carry out additional responsibilities as requested by supervisors to support program success and team functioning. May require lifting of 35lbs. Leadership For leadership positions: Train archery staff in administrative tasks and protocol. Maintain a archery program and staff schedule. Delegate and manage gymnastics projects. Revise SOP as needed. May need to lift 35 lbs. Qualifications: Full Employment Eligibility Requirements can be found here:   https://hr.oregonstate.edu/student-employment-manual/500-employment-eligibility-requirements   Must be academically enrolled in a high school, community college, or university and pursuing a program or course of study Must meet Academic Standing Requirements; students on academic suspension are not eligible for employment Must meet the applicable minimal enrollment standard High School student: Regularly enrolled in a high school or participating in a home-schooling program Undergraduate and post-baccalaureate student: 6 credit hours per term Undergraduate international student: 12 credit hours per term* Graduate student officially admitted to Graduate School: 5 credit hours per term Graduate international student officially admitted to Graduate School: 9 credit hours per term* *International students may be allowed to carry fewer hours than specified above and still be considered “full-time” by the United States Citizenship and Immigration Services ( USCIS ). A reduced course load is approved by the   Office of International Services   ( OIS ), and must be provided to the Student Employment Center. When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic document: ● A Resume In addition to applying to the OSU job website portal please go to KidSpirit website to join our team and apply to KidSpirit. https://kidspirit.oregonstate.edu/employment For additional information please contact: kidspirit@oregonstate.edu We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status. OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire. Note: All job offers are contingent upon Human Resources final approval Working Conditions/Schedule: Salary Range: Anticipated Start Date: 2026-09-07 Anticipated End Date:"
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/OSU_Careers_Site/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/VITA-IT-Support-Student-Worker_JR0001077": {
      "title": "VITA IT Support Student Worker",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-09\nPosted: Posted 6 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nDepartment: College of Business Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents: A Resume A Cover Letter For additional information please contact: Shelly Krehbiel at shelly.krehbiel@oregonstate.edu We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status. This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a Criminal History Check and be determined to be position qualified as per OSU Standard 576-055-0000 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in Youth Programs may have additional Criminal History Checks every 2 years.   OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire.   Note: All job offers are contingent upon Human Resources final approval Job Description: This recruitment will be used to fill one part-time (a maximum of 24 hours per week) VITA IT Support Student Worker position for the Volunteer Income Tax Assistance (VITA) Program in the College of Business at Oregon State University (OSU). OSU VITA prepares free federal and state tax returns for low- and moderate-income Oregonians. It is a partnership between the College of Business, OSU Extension, and the Internal Revenue Service, and it runs on a real IT footprint: laptops and Chromebooks deployed across multiple sites, secure networking, tax preparation software, volunteer account management, and a help desk that has to work on a Saturday morning in February when a family is waiting on their refund. This is a hands-on technical role on a small, collaborative team, and it is broader than a help desk job. You will own the seasonal build-out and upkeep of the systems the program runs on: laptops, Chromebooks, and printers deployed to sites across Oregon, the client appointment and intake platforms, the phone and hotspot systems, and the volunteer scheduling software. Our core systems are established and documented, so the work is configuration, testing, support, and improvement rather than design from scratch. You will leave the year with real experience administering business software platforms end to end, supporting users of every skill level, and working inside a regulated data environment — and with the knowledge that the technology you kept running put money back in the pockets of Oregon families. Learn more about OSU VITA at https://business.oregonstate.edu/volunteer-income-tax-assistance and about the College of Business at https://business.oregonstate.edu/ Position Duties: Prepare, update, and maintain laptops, Chromebooks, printers, and all supplemental hardware used across VITA sites, including imaging, enrollment, testing, and update cycles. Learn, configure, and manage the client appointment and intake software throughout the season — primarily Acuity and Qualtrics — including calendar setup, survey routing, and ongoing monitoring to confirm appointments flow correctly. Support the setup and technical administration of volunteer management and scheduling software and of telecom systems, including the program phone system, dial plan, and mobile hotspot. Assist staff, site coordinators, and volunteers with training, troubleshooting, and problem solving on hardware and non-tax software; provide this support to all VITA sites, both in person and remotely. Execute seasonal start-up and shutdown processes for hardware and software, working from established program checklists and documentation. Support the functional aspects of program reporting, and provide limited TaxSlayer administration support such as account setup, roles, and status changes. Maintain IT inventory and equipment records, and keep setup guides and troubleshooting documentation current. Transferable Skill Development OSU is committed to ensuring students are prepared for success after graduation through intentional skill development as student employees. The competencies students develop while working at OSU are defined by the National Association of Colleges and Employers (NACE) as the essential skills employers seek in their hires. In this position, students learn and build vital career-readiness skills in the primary areas below:   Communication, Critical Thinking, Technology, Professionalism Qualifications: Full Employment Eligibility Requirements can be found here:   https://hr.oregonstate.edu/student-employment-manual/500-employment-eligibility-requirements Must be academically enrolled in a high school, community college, or university and pursuing a program or course of study Must meet Academic Standing Requirements; students on academic suspension are not eligible for employment Must meet the applicable minimal enrollment standard High School student: Regularly enrolled in a high school or participating in a home-schooling program Undergraduate and post-baccalaureate student: 6 credit hours per term Undergraduate international student: 12 credit hours per term* Graduate student officially admitted to Graduate School: 5 credit hours per term Graduate international student officially admitted to Graduate School: 9 credit hours per term* *International students may be allowed to carry fewer hours than specified above and still be considered “full-time” by the United States Citizenship and Immigration Services ( USCIS ). A reduced course load is approved by the   Office of International Services   ( OIS ), and must be provided to the Student Employment Center. Required Qualifications: Demonstrated experience troubleshooting computer hardware and software (Windows and/or ChromeOS), gained through coursework, employment, volunteer work, or personal projects. Ability to explain technical concepts clearly and patiently to users with a wide range of technical experience. Interpersonal communication skills, including phone etiquette, and a professional, courteous manner with staff, volunteers, and community members. Ability to learn and administer web-based software platforms independently, working from written documentation. Ability to handle confidential taxpayer and volunteer information with discretion. Availability to work a consistent schedule across the full academic year, including scheduled evening and Saturday shifts during the primary tax season, late January through mid-April. Must have or be able to complete IRS VITA/TCE Volunteer Standards of Conduct – Ethics training within 30 days of hire and maintain certification throughout employment. Additional Required Qualifications: This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a Criminal History Check and be determined to be position qualified as per OSU Standard 576-055-0000 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in Youth Programs may have additional Criminal History Checks every 2 years.  Preferred Qualifications: Prior VITA, tax preparation, or other IRS volunteer experience. Coursework or experience in Business Information Systems, Computer Science, Information Technology, or a related field. Experience with Google Workspace administration and Chromebook management. Experience with desktop imaging or endpoint management tools. Experience configuring or administering web-based scheduling, survey, or workforce management platforms, such as Acuity, Qualtrics, When to Work, or comparable tools. Experience with Microsoft 365 administration, including group membership and shared mailbox access. Demonstrated knowledge of Windows 11 and familiarity with networking basics, including Active Directory, DNS, and DHCP. Prior customer service or help desk experience in a professional setting. Prior experience working with OSU Information & Technology, College of Business IT, or OSU Extension IT. Language skills, including Spanish, that support service to Oregon’s diverse communities. Working Conditions/Schedule: • This is an in-person position based in Corvallis, Oregon, working primarily in Austin Hall. Technical support is provided to VITA sites across Oregon both in person and remotely. • Expect approximately 15–24 hours per week from the start of the appointment through mid-April, not to exceed 24 hours per week. The work is heaviest in configuration and setup during fall term, when hardware is prepared and tested and the appointment, telecom, and volunteer scheduling systems are built out for the season. During the primary tax season, late January through mid-April, the emphasis shifts to user support, triage, and monitoring. Hours are reduced or unavailable between mid-April and October 1. • Scheduled weekday evening and Saturday shifts are required during the tax season. Occasional travel to off-site pop-up VITA events may be required; transportation is arranged and provided by the program. The role involves moving and setting up IT equipment, including lifting items weighing up to 40 pounds. Salary Range: N/A Anticipated Start Date: 2026-10-01 Anticipated End Date:"
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/OSU_Careers_Site/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/Student-Crew-Member---AnRS-Dairy_JR0000970": {
      "title": "Student Crew Member - AnRS Dairy",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-09\nPosted: Posted 6 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nDepartment: Animal and Rangeland Sciences Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents: 1. A Resume/Vita 2. A cover letter indicating how your qualifications and experience have prepared you for this position   For additional information please contact: Larissa Lewis at larissa.mcguire@oregonstate.edu   We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status.   This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a criminal history check and be determined to be position qualified as per OSU Standard 576-055-0000 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in youth programs may have additional criminal history checks every 24 months. This position requires driving a University vehicle or a personal vehicle on behalf of the University; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a Motor Vehicle History Check, possess and maintain a current, valid driver's license in their state of residence, be determined to be position qualified and self-report convictions (as per Voluntary and Compulsory Driver Standards OSU Standard 125-155-0200) as per OSU Standard 576-056-0000 et seq.   Note: All job offers are contingent upon Human Resources final approval. Job Description: Primary duty is the care and maintenance of animals, facilities and some crop and grazing duties. Students will be responsible for providing coordinated day today care of the cows and heifers at the Dairy Center. Typical tasks include: Milking Stall cleaning Feeding Watering Calf care Other operations related to animal husbandry and facility upkeep Transferable Skill Development   OSU is committed to ensuring students are prepared for success after graduation through intentional skill development as student employees. The competencies students develop while working at OSU are defined by the National Association of Colleges and Employers (NACE) as the essential skills employers seek in their hires. In this position, students learn and build vital career-readiness skills in the primary areas below:   Teamwork, Leadership, Professionalism, Career & Self Development Qualifications: Full Employment Eligibility Requirements can be found here:   https://hr.oregonstate.edu/student-employment-manual/500-employment-eligibility-requirements   Must be academically enrolled in a high school, community college, or university and pursuing a program or course of study Must meet Academic Standing Requirements; students on academic suspension are not eligible for employment Must meet the applicable minimal enrollment standard High School student: Regularly enrolled in a high school or participating in a home-schooling program Undergraduate and post-baccalaureate student: 6 credit hours per term Undergraduate international student: 12 credit hours per term* Graduate student officially admitted to Graduate School: 5 credit hours per term Graduate international student officially admitted to Graduate School: 9 credit hours per term* *International students may be allowed to carry fewer hours than specified above and still be considered “full-time” by the United States Citizenship and Immigration Services ( USCIS ). A reduced course load is approved by the   Office of International Services   ( OIS ), and must be provided to the Student Employment Center. Willingness to learn new skills and techniques Ability to adapt to changed environments No previous dairy experience is required, but is beneficial This is an off campus facility, employee must have a reliable form of transportation Preferred Qualifications: No previous dairy experience is required, but is beneficial Required Qualifications: Willingness to learn new skills and techniques Ability to adapt to changed environments This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a criminal history check and be determined to be position qualified as per OSU Standard 576-055-0000 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in youth programs may have additional criminal history checks every 24 months. This position requires driving a University vehicle or a personal vehicle on behalf of the University; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a Motor Vehicle History Check, possess and maintain a current, valid driver's license in their state of residence, be determined to be position qualified and self-report convictions (as per Voluntary and Compulsory Driver Standards OSU Standard 125-155-0200) as per OSU Standard 576-056-0000 et seq. Working Conditions/Schedule: • This is an off campus facility, employee must have a reliable form of transportation • Applicants must be available to work early morning shifts: 4:00 to 8:00 a.m. and 4:00 to 8:00 p.m. (will work around student schedules and clubs) • Applicants must be flexible with availability in order to split shifts between employees fairly, ensuring animals will be cared for during holidays and breaks • This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a Criminal History Check and be determined to be position qualified as per OSU Standard 576-055-0000 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in Youth Programs may have additional Criminal History Checks every 2 years • Employee may be exposed to noxious odors, infectious diseases, chemicals, loud noises, slippery floors and machinery • Employee will be exposed to inclement weather conditions • Employee may need to maneuver items that weigh up to 50pounds • Employee may be required to stoop, bend and kneel to provide care of the animals • Milking shifts begin at 04:00 a.m. and 04:00 p.m. and can last from 3.5 - 5.5 hours depending on season and number of newborn calves. (If shifts go longer than expected, employees are allowed to leave early to go to class.) • Employees are expected to work at least two weekends per month • Employee work schedules will be set according to class schedules Salary Range: 15.55 Anticipated Start Date: 2026-09-10 Anticipated End Date:"
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/OSU_Careers_Site/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/Work-Study-Student---AnRS-Main-Office_JR0001057": {
      "title": "Work Study Student - AnRS Main Office",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-09\nPosted: Posted 6 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nDepartment: Animal and Rangeland Sciences Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents: 1. A Resume/Vita 2. A cover letter indicating how your qualifications and experience have prepared you for this position   For additional information please contact: Tedd Huffman at tedd.huffman@oregonstate.edu   We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status.   This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a criminal history check and be determined to be position qualified as per OSU Standard 576-055-0000 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in youth programs may have additional criminal history checks every 24 months.   Note: All job offers are contingent upon Human Resources final approval.   OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire. Job Description: This recruitment will be used to create a pool of part-time (a maximum of 24hours per week) Student Technical Assistant positions for the Department of Animal and Rangeland Sciences at Oregon State University (OSU). This position will be filled to assist AnRS with general office work including answering phones, filing, scanning, copy and manuscript assistance. Assist undergraduate advisor and program specialist with daily duties including but not limited to; updating website content, photograph and videotape AnRS animals, farming units, students and faculty for multimedia use as requested Employee will work in Withycombe Hall providing general clerical assistance as needed. Assist advisor with website updating, digital signboard upkeep, paperwork, photograph and videotape AnRS animals, farming units, students and faculty for multimedia use as requested. General office work including answering phones, greet visitors, filing, scanning, copy and manuscript assistance. Transferable Skill Development   OSU is committed to ensuring students are prepared for success after graduation through intentional skill development as student employees. The competencies students develop while working at OSU are defined by the National Association of Colleges and Employers (NACE) as the essential skills employers seek in their hires. In this position, students learn and build vital career-readiness skills in the primary areas below:   Communication, Teamwork, Professionalism, Technology Qualifications: Full Employment Eligibility Requirements can be found here:   https://hr.oregonstate.edu/student-employment-manual/500-employment-eligibility-requirements   Must be academically enrolled in a high school, community college, or university and pursuing a program or course of study Must meet Academic Standing Requirements; students on academic suspension are not eligible for employment Must meet the applicable minimal enrollment standard High School student: Regularly enrolled in a high school or participating in a home-schooling program Undergraduate and post-baccalaureate student: 6 credit hours per term Undergraduate international student: 12 credit hours per term* Graduate student officially admitted to Graduate School: 5 credit hours per term Graduate international student officially admitted to Graduate School: 9 credit hours per term* *International students may be allowed to carry fewer hours than specified above and still be considered “full-time” by the United States Citizenship and Immigration Services ( USCIS ). A reduced course load is approved by the   Office of International Services   ( OIS ), and must be provided to the Student Employment Center. Required Willingness to learn new skills and techniques Ability to adapt to changed environments This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a criminal history check and be determined to be position qualified as per OSU Standard 576-055-0000 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in youth programs may have additional criminal history checks every 24 months. Preferred: Past experience that demonstrates the ability to work as a team Familiar with computer application such as Microsoft Suite (Word, Excel, etc.); Adobe Acrobat, Drupal (website editing), Workday – not all applications are required but note your level of competence in your cover letter Working Conditions/Schedule: Office Work in Withycombe Hall on Corvallis Main campus Schedule will be arranged around classes each term Computer use, copying, and general office duties Salary Range: 15.55 Anticipated Start Date: 2026-09-18 Anticipated End Date:"
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/OSU_Careers_Site/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/Social-Media-Producer_JR0001032": {
      "title": "Social Media Producer",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-09\nPosted: Posted 6 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nDepartment: Liberal Arts Admin (CLA) Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents: 1. A Resume/Vita 2. A cover letter indicating how your qualifications and experience have prepared you for this position   For additional information please contact: Keith Van Norman at Keith.vannorman@oregonstate.edu   We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status. Note: All job offers are contingent upon Human Resources final approval.   OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire. Job Description: This recruitment will be used to ﬁll two part-time (a maximum of 24 hours per week) Liberal Arts Social Media Producer position for the College of Liberal Arts at Oregon State University (OSU). The College of Liberal Arts is seeking a creative and engaging student who is highly motivated, enthusiastic and gifted at visual storytelling. This position will work closely with members of the CLA Marketing and Communications team to produce content for college social media accounts. Key areas of focus will generate social media content to engage prospective and current students on TikTok, Instagram and YouTube.  * Evaluate opportunities for connecting with prospective students on CLA's social media platforms and develop original content based on those opportunities. * Collaborate with team members to strategize and schedule social media posts * Produce social media content for live events, effectively representing the CLA brand to accomplish organizational goals. Transferable Skill Development OSU is committed to ensuring students are prepared for success after graduation through intentional skill development as student employees. The competencies students develop while working at OSU are defined by the National Association of Colleges and Employers (NACE) as the essential skills employers seek in their hires. In this position, students learn and build vital career-readiness skills in the primary areas below:   Communication, Critical Thinking, Teamwork Qualifications: Full Employment Eligibility Requirements can be found here:   https://hr.oregonstate.edu/student-employment-manual/500-employment-eligibility-requirements   Must be academically enrolled in a high school, community college, or university and pursuing a program or course of study Must meet Academic Standing Requirements; students on academic suspension are not eligible for employment Must meet the applicable minimal enrollment standard High School student: Regularly enrolled in a high school or participating in a home-schooling program Undergraduate and post-baccalaureate student: 6 credit hours per term Undergraduate international student: 12 credit hours per term* Graduate student officially admitted to Graduate School: 5 credit hours per term Graduate international student officially admitted to Graduate School: 9 credit hours per term* *International students may be allowed to carry fewer hours than specified above and still be considered “full-time” by the United States Citizenship and Immigration Services ( USCIS ). A reduced course load is approved by the   Office of International Services   ( OIS ), and must be provided to the Student Employment Center. Required: * Excellent written and verbal communication skills * Experience using TikTok and Instagram * Strong smartphone photography/videography skills * Conﬁdence to reach out directly to students/faculty and arrange in- person videos/photos * Ability to take direction and complete assignments on deadline Preferred: * Communications or Journalism major * Interest in public relations, communications or related ﬁelds Working Conditions/Schedule: part time, 24 hours/ week Salary Range: 15.55 Anticipated Start Date: 2026-10-30 Anticipated End Date:"
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/OSU_Careers_Site/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/Team-Oregon-Director--Internal-Employment-Opportunity-_JR0001062": {
      "title": "Team Oregon Director (Internal Employment Opportunity)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-09\nPosted: Posted 6 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nDepartment: Division of Extension and Engagement - Family and Community Health Special Instructions to Applicants: This is an Internal Employment Opportunity. When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents:   1) A resume/CV: Finalists will be required to give an open presentation which may include the distribution of their redacted curriculum vitae/resume and a short biography   2) A cover letter that addresses each required qualification 3) Licenses/Certifications: Proof of motorcycle endorsement and Team Oregon Instructor certification - please attach as separate documents The salary range listed reflects the full-time salary range and will be commensurate with the candidate’s skills, education, and experience. Due to funding, OSU will not sponsor immigration work authorization for this position (e.g., H-1B, etc.). For additional information please contact: Jeff Sherman-Duncan jeff.sherman@oregonstate.edu Job Summary: This is an Internal Employment Opportunity. The Division of Extension and Engagement's Family and Community Health program is seeking a TEAM Oregon Director. This is a full-time (1.00 FTE), 12-month, professional faculty position. The mission of the OSU Division of Extension and Engagement (division) is to work with communities throughout Oregon to support lifelong learning and co-create innovative solutions to address local challenges.  Together, we envision a future where Oregon’s people, communities and ecosystems prosper. The Team Oregon director is responsible for all aspects of the design, implementation, and management of the motorcycle rider education program in Oregon. This includes oversight of statewide training sites and rider-training courses; procurement and distribution of materials and equipment; student enrollment and scheduling; customer service; instructor recruitment, on-boarding, training, supervision, compliance, scheduling, and payroll; program budgeting, evaluation, and forecasting; outreach activities; and adherence to all Oregon State University and related state or federal policy and practice. Key Responsibilities: The Director is responsible for the leadership and supervision of all Team Oregon operational functions. The Director is expected to lead by always fostering a culture of professionalism, accountability, adherence to applicable policy, and continuous improvement. Maintains a productive working relationship with key partners at OSU and ODOT. Establishes and maintains strong collaborative relationships with stakeholders and partners in the motorcycle safety community. Additionally, the position serves as liaison with other state and federal agencies, the motorcycle industry, law enforcement, the research community, the motorcycle community, and motorcyclist rights organizations. Provides strategic leadership to and supervision of Team Oregon managers, working closely on all aspects of organizational operations, improvement, and efficiency. The Director provides a clear vision of the program mission to all partners, employees, and stakeholders. Directs fiscal planning, organizational stewardship and management of a $3 million annual operating budget. Develops and establishes an annual business plan and budget; monitors and approves expenditures; and evaluates programs for efficient and effective operation. The Director is tasked with seeking highway safety grant funding and submitting timely proposals that will improve, enhance, and sustain Team Oregon. Provides direct supervision of four professional faculty: the business manager, communications manager, operations manager, and training manager. The Director is ultimately responsible for the success of every employee within the Team Oregon unit.  The Director evaluates employee performance and supports professional development opportunities. Responds to legislative proposals and testifies before legislative committees as needed in alignment with OSU expertise and policy. Furthermore, the Director analyzes program impact of proposed changes in laws, regulations, and procedures. Qualifications: REQUIRED QUALIFICATIONS: Bachelor’s degree and eight years of relevant professional experience or twelve years of relevant professional experience demonstrating progressively responsible responsibility in program operations, management, leadership, training, or a related area; or an equivalent combination of education and relevant professional experience. Current motorcycle endorsement and a minimum of 5 years and 15,000 miles of current, on-street riding experience. Qualified Team Oregon instructor. Experience teaching the endorsement-level trainings (Team Oregon Basic or Intermediate Rider Training). Demonstrated ability to proactively manage perceived or actual conflicts of interest or conflicts of commitment in consistent alignment with guiding policies and processes.  Demonstrated ability to successfully and positively interact with and gain support of partners, funders, administrative officials, the general public, and program personnel to meet objectives while maintaining strong working relationships. Experience as a supervisor, including recruiting, hiring, mentoring and coaching, promotion and discipline. Experience as a project director, with demonstrated ability to lead simultaneous business efforts according to expected scope, budget, and timeline. Demonstrated exceptional customer service orientation in balancing compliance and customer needs. Proven ability to establish and maintain successful working relationships and positive work culture among groups of people with varying backgrounds, skillsets and expectations. Ability to work autonomously, setting own goals, and objectives to fulfill program priorities. Demonstrated ability as a leader to analyze and manage complex and sensitive issues and situations, identify challenges and solutions, forecast outcomes of proposed actions, and implement effective courses of action. A commitment to collaborative and respectful learning and working environments that are safe, inclusive, and welcoming. This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a criminal history check and be determined to be position qualified as per University Standard: 05-010 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in youth programs may have additional criminal history checks every 24 months. This position requires driving a university vehicle or a personal vehicle on behalf of the university; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a motor vehicle history check, possess and maintain a current, valid driver's license in their state of residence, be determined to be position qualified and self-report convictions as per University Policy 05-030. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS: Ten years and 50,000 miles of current, on-street riding experience.  Experience as a Team Oregon mentor instructor or guardian advisor, or offering quality assurance to other instructors.   Dedication to Land Grant mission Five years’ experience as a motorcycle safety instructor or equivalent experience as a coach or trainer in another relevant discipline.  Experience as manager of 50+ personnel, including subordinates or volunteers. Demonstrated commitment to integrity, accountability, and continuous improvement, including the ability to build trust, take responsibility for decisions and outcomes, seek and respond constructively to feedback, and consistently demonstrate sound judgment and professionalism. Working Conditions/Schedule: •The incumbent will be required to use their personal wireless devices (cellular phones or internet connections when away from OSU premises) for conducting OSU business. •TEAM OREGON courses are taught during evening hours and on weekends, so occasional weekend and evening work activity will be required. •Advisory committee meetings, instructor training, and community events are held in evenings and weekends, so some weekend and evening work activity will be required. •State, regional, and national meetings and conferences require some overnight travel, including weekends. Salary Range: $144,100- $159,300 Anticipated Start Date: 2026-10-15 Anticipated End Date: We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status. OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire. OSU is a fair chance employer committed to inclusive hiring. We encourage applications from candidates who bring a wide range of lived experience including involvement with the justice system. If this position has “critical or security-sensitive” responsibilities and/or driving duties and you are selected as a finalist, your initial job offer will be contingent upon the results of a job-related pre-employment check (such as a background check, motor vehicle history check, sexual misconduct reference check, etc.). Background check results do not automatically disqualify a candidate. Take a look at our Background Checks website including the for candidates section for more details. If you have questions or concerns about the pre-employment check, please contact OSU’s Employee and Labor Relations team at employee.relations@oregonstate.edu ."
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/OSU_Careers_Site/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/Student-Care-Triage-Coordinator--Internal-Employment-Opportunity-_JR0000616-1": {
      "title": "Student Care Triage Coordinator",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-09\nPosted: Posted 6 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nDepartment: Dean of Students Special Instructions to Applicants: This posting has been re-opened in order to expand the original applicant pool. When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents:  1) A resume 2) A cover letter indicating how your qualifications and experience have prepared you for this position. Starting salary will be commensurate with skills, education, and experience.  For additional information please contact:  Lauren Skousen Lauren.Skousen@oregonstate.edu 541-737-9035 Job Summary: Student Care in department of the Dean of Students is seeking a Student Care Triage Coordinator. This is a full-time (1.00   FTE), 12-month, professional faculty position.  The Student Care Triage Coordinator serves as a primary daytime access point for non-emergency student concerns received by Student Care. The Student Care Triage Coordinator reviews and prioritizes incoming reports and consultations; conducts timely needs, risk, and resource screening within the scope of the position; provides brief, non-clinical stabilization and case coordination; and connects students with appropriate university and community resources through warm handoffs. When established criteria are met, the Student Care Triage Coordinator may provide limited in-person response on the Corvallis campus Monday through Friday during established university business hours to support immediate stabilization, consultation, and connection to care. The Student Care Triage Coordinator collaborates closely with Student Care case managers, Counseling and Psychological Services, Public Safety, emergency responders, academic and student support units, and community partners. The Student Care Triage Coordinator also advances proactive support by providing consultation, education, and practical skill-building that help campus partners identify distress, respond effectively, and connect students to support earlier. This position does not provide psychotherapy, clinical diagnosis, emergency medical care, law-enforcement services, transportation, after-hours coverage, or an alternative to 911 or other emergency response. Ongoing and complex case management is transferred to the appropriate case manager or specialized service after initial stabilization and documented handoff. Key Responsibilities: Triage, Rapid Assessment, and Consultation Brief Case Coordination, Student Support, and Care Transitions Training, Education, and Capacity Building Limited Daytime In-Person Response Partnership, Collaboration, and Program Assessment Qualifications: Required Qualifications: Bachelor’s degree in related field. Three or more years of professional experience working with or supporting people in high need, emotional, or crisis situations.   Demonstrated skills in any number of crisis support areas, such as: suicide prevention and intervention work, sexual assault survivor support work, or crime victim advocacy work.  Demonstrable experience successfully using problem-solving skills.   Excellent written and oral communication skills and customer service skills. Demonstrated experience successfully managing confidential information.   Excellent time management skills.   Demonstrated ability and commitment to working as part of a team.   Commitment to promoting an inclusive work environment and knowledge of effective strategies for working with diverse students, faculty, and staff.   This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a criminal history check and be determined to be position qualified as per University Standard: 05-010 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in youth programs may have additional criminal history checks every 24 months. Preferred Qualifications: Master’s Degree in higher education, counseling, social work, or related field. Experience working with college students or individuals in a higher education setting (traditional, transfer, non-traditional, commuter, residential, etc.). Demonstrated knowledge of crisis de-escalation techniques. Demonstrated experience successfully building networks with colleagues. Evidence of successfully working with multiple partners to collectively solve a problem. Experience in student data or case management systems (Maxient, Banner, etc.). Working Conditions/Schedule: Mon – Fri, 8am – 5pm, office environment. Salary Range: $65,000 - $75,000 Anticipated Start Date: 2026-09-14 Anticipated End Date: We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status. OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire. OSU is a fair chance employer committed to inclusive hiring. We encourage applications from candidates who bring a wide range of lived experience including involvement with the justice system. If this position has “critical or security-sensitive” responsibilities and/or driving duties and you are selected as a finalist, your initial job offer will be contingent upon the results of a job-related pre-employment check (such as a background check, motor vehicle history check, sexual misconduct reference check, etc.). Background check results do not automatically disqualify a candidate. Take a look at our Background Checks website including the for candidates section for more details. If you have questions or concerns about the pre-employment check, please contact OSU’s Employee and Labor Relations team at employee.relations@oregonstate.edu ."
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/OSU_Careers_Site/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/Digital-Communications-Student-Assistant_JR0001066": {
      "title": "SEE Digital Communications Specialist",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-09\nPosted: Posted 6 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nDepartment: SEE Communications & Outreach Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents: A Resume A Cover Letter A Portfolio For additional information please contact: Rebekah Wilson at Rebekah.wilson@oregonstate.edu We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status.   OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire.   Note: All job offers are contingent upon Human Resources final approval Job Description: This recruitment will be used to fill one part-time (a maximum of 24 hours per week) Digital Communications Student Assistant for the SEE Communications & Outreach at Oregon State University (OSU). The Student Experiences & Engagement Outreach and Communications team promotes SEE programs, services and opportunities to help students discover ways to connect, participate and build community at Oregon State University. The SEE Digital Communications Specialist supports the work of the SEE Marketing and Communications team by capturing and editing photos/videos, maintaining website content, supporting social media and creating engaging digital content that highlights SEE programs, services, events and student experiences.  This includes, but is not limited to, digital content creation for SEE websites, social media, digital signage, newsletters and outreach communications. This student position also retains outreach responsibilities by representing SEE at campus events, supporting tabling and helping current and prospective students understand SEE programs and involvement opportunities. Position Duties: Photo and video projects, as assigned. Review the digital content calendar to coordinate multiple photo, video and content projects in advance to ensure adherence to deadlines. Capture photos and videos that showcase SEE programs, services, facilities, events and student experiences. Organize, catalog and maintain a searchable library of photos and videos for future marketing and communications use. Edit photos and videos for use across SEE digital platforms, including websites, social media, digital signage and newsletters. Adhere to Oregon State University and SEE brand standards and policies, including the administration of model release waivers Collaborate with the SEE Outreach and Communications team and, when appropriate, campus partners to develop, produce and implement engaging and educational content for SEE digital channels. Update and maintain SEE website content utilizing Drupal 10, including webpages, event information, links, images and other digital content. Promote relevant SEE programs, services and involvement opportunities at Launch, Beavers. Open House, Orange and Black Days and other outreach events. Conduct tours and tour trainings of the Student Experience Center while providing a welcoming, engaging and informative experience for visitors. Answer questions and help students identify programs, services and involvement opportunities that align with their interests and goals. Use project management software to track assignments, communicate with team members and meet project deadlines. Assist the SEE Outreach and Communications Coordinator with other outreach, marketing and communications projects as assigned. Transferable Skill Development OSU is committed to ensuring students are prepared for success after graduation through intentional skill development as student employees. The competencies students develop while working at OSU are defined by the National Association of Colleges and Employers (NACE) as the essential skills employers seek in their hires. In this position, students learn and build vital career-readiness skills in the primary areas below:   Communication, Technology, Professionalism Qualifications: Full Employment Eligibility Requirements can be found here:   https://hr.oregonstate.edu/student-employment-manual/500-employment-eligibility-requirements Must be academically enrolled in a high school, community college, or university and pursuing a program or course of study Must meet Academic Standing Requirements; students on academic suspension are not eligible for employment Must meet the applicable minimal enrollment standard High School student: Regularly enrolled in a high school or participating in a home-schooling program Undergraduate and post-baccalaureate student: 6 credit hours per term Undergraduate international student: 12 credit hours per term* Graduate student officially admitted to Graduate School: 5 credit hours per term Graduate international student officially admitted to Graduate School: 9 credit hours per term* *International students may be allowed to carry fewer hours than specified above and still be considered “full-time” by the United States Citizenship and Immigration Services ( USCIS ). A reduced course load is approved by the   Office of International Services   ( OIS ), and must be provided to the Student Employment Center. Preferred Qualifications: Competency in photography and videography, and related equipment to produce quality digital content. Experience editing video content using applications such as Adobe Premiere Pro, Final Cut Pro, DaVinci Resolve or CapCut. Experience editing, organizing photos using applications such as Adobe Lightroom, Photoshop or Flickr. Proficiency with social media platforms such as Instagram and TikTok and a commitment to staying current with emerging platforms, trends and industry best practices. Ability to work effectively and respectfully with individuals from a variety of backgrounds, experiences and perspectives. Working Conditions/Schedule: • One academic year (continuing employment is contingent upon satisfactory completion of a 30-day probationary period and satisfactory job performance as evaluated by immediate supervisor). • 10 hours per week. Early morning, late night and weekend hours may be required. Salary Range: Anticipated Start Date: 2026-10-05 Anticipated End Date:"
    },
