    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/OSU_Careers_Site/job/Physics-Research-Assistant_JR0000959": {
      "title": "Physics Research Assistant",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-08\nPosted: Posted 7 Days Ago\n\nDepartment: Science Special Instructions to Applicants: Job Description: This position performs scientific research in physics under the supervision of a faculty advisor. **Interested applicants must first contact their preferred faculty advisor to request availability and acceptance into a lab.** A list of the faculty with their research areas and contact info can be found here:  https://physics.oregonstate.edu/research . Qualifications: Full Employment Eligibility Requirements can be found here:   https://hr.oregonstate.edu/student-employment-manual/500-employment-eligibility-requirements   Must be academically enrolled in a high school, community college, or university and pursuing a program or course of study Must meet Academic Standing Requirements; students on academic suspension are not eligible for employment Must meet the applicable minimal enrollment standard High School student: Regularly enrolled in a high school or participating in a home-schooling program Undergraduate and post-baccalaureate student: 6 credit hours per term Undergraduate international student: 12 credit hours per term* Graduate student officially admitted to Graduate School: 5 credit hours per term Graduate international student officially admitted to Graduate School: 9 credit hours per term* *International students may be allowed to carry fewer hours than specified above and still be considered “full-time” by the United States Citizenship and Immigration Services ( USCIS ). A reduced course load is approved by the   Office of International Services   ( OIS ), and must be provided to the Student Employment Center. Working Conditions/Schedule: Salary Range: 15.55 - 21.50 Anticipated Start Date: 2026-08-28 Anticipated End Date:"
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/OSU_Careers_Site/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/Physics---Assistant-Equipment-Manager_JR0000921-1": {
      "title": "Physics - Assistant Equipment Manager",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-08\nPosted: Posted 7 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nDepartment: College of Science Special Instructions to Applicants: Job Description: Lab equipment Prepare and stage equipment for upcoming lab sections Set up and tear down lab equipment Maintain and organize the lab storage areas Cleaning and organization Reset lab rooms each week Clean and care for equipment, including but not limited to glassware, optics, and instrument surfaces, using the appropriate method for each Keep storage rooms, shelving, and cabinets orderly and correctly labeled Demonstrations Become familiar with the demonstration collection Set up demonstrations for classes as needed Maintain the demonstration equipment and keep the demo room organized Repair demonstration equipment where practical Prepare documentation for demonstrations Maintenance and department support Light repairs and general upkeep around the department, including tasks such as hanging posters Monitor supply levels and assist with equipment and supply purchases Help faculty locate equipment for outreach events, demonstrations, and other courses This is not the entire list of duties and special projects.  Duties will also include prep work prior to the beginning of the academic year. Qualifications: Required: Physics major at OSU. Preferred: Completion of the Introductory Physics sequence, or completion by the end of the term; a bility to work independently; r eliability Full Employment Eligibility Requirements can be found here:   https://hr.oregonstate.edu/student-employment-manual/500-employment-eligibility-requirements   Must be academically enrolled in a high school, community college, or university and pursuing a program or course of study Must meet Academic Standing Requirements; students on academic suspension are not eligible for employment Must meet the applicable minimal enrollment standard High School student: Regularly enrolled in a high school or participating in a home-schooling program Undergraduate and post-baccalaureate student: 6 credit hours per term Undergraduate international student: 12 credit hours per term* Graduate student officially admitted to Graduate School: 5 credit hours per term Graduate international student officially admitted to Graduate School: 9 credit hours per term* *International students may be allowed to carry fewer hours than specified above and still be considered “full-time” by the United States Citizenship and Immigration Services ( USCIS ). A reduced course load is approved by the   Office of International Services   ( OIS ), and must be provided to the Student Employment Center. Working Conditions/Schedule: Assistance is required by the dept. to help prepare, maintain, and organize the teaching equipment that every introductory physics student uses. Salary Range: 15.55 - 21.50 Anticipated Start Date: 2026-08-26 Anticipated End Date:"
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/OSU_Careers_Site/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/CAMP-Student-Mentor_JR0000981": {
      "title": "CAMP Student Mentor",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-08\nPosted: Posted 7 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nDepartment: College Assistance Migrant Program Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents: A Resume A Cover Letter A supplemental document answering the following questions: Explain your interest in this position, and how you feel qualified to serve as a CAMP Mentor for new CAMP students. (200 word max) An added component of this mentor role includes helping students academically. In what ways can you inspire CAMP students for academic success based on your academic growth at OSU? (200 word max) What are some of the challenges migrant students face in transitioning to college? How do you see yourself assisting students through those specific challenges? (200 word max) Only if you are a returning Mentor: What are some ways you have seen yourself grow through this position and how do you hope to grow if given another opportunity to be a Mentor? (200 word max) For additional information please contact: Maria Andrade at Maria.andrade@oregonstate.edu We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status.   OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire.   Note: All job offers are contingent upon Human Resources final approval Job Description: This recruitment will be used to fill 6 part-time (a maximum of 24 hours per week) Mentor positions for the CAMP Community College Alliance Program at Oregon State University (OSU) and the three partner community colleges at Blue Mountain Community College (BMCC), Columbia Gorge (CGCC), and Linn Benton (LBCC). CAMP Mentors will support CAMP's effort to facilitate a successful college transitional experience for the incoming CAMP cohort. Mentors will be working with CAMP during Orientation as well as the three terms of the academic year with the following structure: Fall - 6 hours/week, Winter & Spring - 3 hours/week. Participating in events or other activities will be time volunteered. As a CAMP Mentor you will help to create a welcoming environment and familiarize CAMP Scholars with college life. A special emphasis will be placed on academic growth. In addition, Mentors will help the new CAMP Scholars navigate college life by providing the students with information about available resources on campus, educating them about the expectations of higher learning, and encouraging participation in all CAMP activities. Position Duties Commit to fall, winter, and spring terms (Fall: 6 hrs/wk, Winter & Spring: 3 hours/wk) paid hourly. Meet with your assigned mentees weekly during fall and bi-weekly during winter and spring term Assist in coordinating and participating in all on-and-off-campus events Help students with transitional, personal, and/or academic issues Enforce all University \"Student Conduct Regulations\" Monitor the safety and welfare of students during CAMP activities Participate in all mentor staff meetings Transferable Skill Development OSU is committed to ensuring students are prepared for success after graduation through intentional skill development as student employees. The competencies students develop while working at OSU are defined by the National Association of Colleges and Employers (NACE) as the essential skills employers seek in their hires. In this position, students learn and build vital career-readiness skills in the primary areas below:   Teamwork, Leadership, Professionalism Qualifications: Full Employment Eligibility Requirements can be found here:   https://hr.oregonstate.edu/student-employment-manual/500-employment-eligibility-requirements Must be academically enrolled in a high school, community college, or university and pursuing a program or course of study Must meet Academic Standing Requirements; students on academic suspension are not eligible for employment Must meet the applicable minimal enrollment standard High School student: Regularly enrolled in a high school or participating in a home-schooling program Undergraduate and post-baccalaureate student: 6 credit hours per term Undergraduate international student: 12 credit hours per term* Graduate student officially admitted to Graduate School: 5 credit hours per term Graduate international student officially admitted to Graduate School: 9 credit hours per term* *International students may be allowed to carry fewer hours than specified above and still be considered “full-time” by the United States Citizenship and Immigration Services ( USCIS ). A reduced course load is approved by the   Office of International Services   ( OIS ), and must be provided to the Student Employment Center. Required Former CAMP Scholar at BMCC, CGCC, LBCC, or OSU Preferred A cumulative GPA of 2.75 or above is strongly preferred Driver's license Ability to drive 12 passenger van Bilingual - ability to speak Spanish and English Working Conditions/Schedule: Salary Range: Anticipated Start Date: 2026-09-15 Anticipated End Date:"
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/OSU_Careers_Site/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/CEOAS-Undergraduate-Learning-Assistant_JR0001037": {
      "title": "CEOAS Undergraduate Learning Assistant",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-08\nPosted: Posted 7 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nDepartment: College of Earth, Ocean, and Atmospheric Sciences Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents: A Resume A Cover Letter List of coursework completed or in progress in ATS, CLIM, ENSC, GEO, GEOG, and/or OC A supplemental document answering the following question: In 300 words or less, please describe your interest and/or experience in teaching and mentoring others. For additional information please contact: Kaplan Yalcin at Kaplan.yalcin@oregonstate.edu We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status.   OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire.   Note: All job offers are contingent upon Human Resources final approval Job Description: This recruitment will be used to fill 10 part-time (a maximum of 24 hours per week) Undergraduate Learning Assistants (ULAs) for the College of Earth, Ocean, and Atmospheric Sciences at Oregon State University (OSU). Undergraduate Learning Assistants work with faculty and graduate teaching assistants as undergraduate peer mentors to implement active learning environments in courses in Climate Science, Environmental Sciences, Geography and Geospatial Science, Geology, and Oceanography. Successful applicants will be contacted as positions become available. Applicants must be enrolled as degree-seeking undergraduate students at OSU for consideration with prior coursework in Climate Science, Environmental Sciences, Geography and Geospatial Science, Geology, or Oceanography. Position Duties Facilitate student engagement in active learning environments Prepare for facilitation by meeting with course faculty and reviewing learning materials Communicate with students, faculty, and graduate teaching assistants Serve as undergraduate peer learning mentors Participate in training and professional development for undergraduate learning assistants. Transferable Skill Development OSU is committed to ensuring students are prepared for success after graduation through intentional skill development as student employees. The competencies students develop while working at OSU are defined by the National Association of Colleges and Employers (NACE) as the essential skills employers seek in their hires. In this position, students learn and build vital career-readiness skills in the primary areas below:   Communication, Teamwork, Leadership, Professionalism Qualifications: Full Employment Eligibility Requirements can be found here:   https://hr.oregonstate.edu/student-employment-manual/500-employment-eligibility-requirements Must be academically enrolled in a high school, community college, or university and pursuing a program or course of study Must meet Academic Standing Requirements; students on academic suspension are not eligible for employment Must meet the applicable minimal enrollment standard High School student: Regularly enrolled in a high school or participating in a home-schooling program Undergraduate and post-baccalaureate student: 6 credit hours per term Undergraduate international student: 12 credit hours per term* Graduate student officially admitted to Graduate School: 5 credit hours per term Graduate international student officially admitted to Graduate School: 9 credit hours per term* *International students may be allowed to carry fewer hours than specified above and still be considered “full-time” by the United States Citizenship and Immigration Services ( USCIS ). A reduced course load is approved by the   Office of International Services   ( OIS ), and must be provided to the Student Employment Center. Required Prior coursework in Climate Science, Environmental Sciences, Geography and Geospatial Science, Geology, or Oceanography. Ability to communicate effectively with faculty and peers Demonstrated record of academic achievement in course subject matter Preferred A major in Climate Science, Environmental Sciences, Geography and Geospatial Science, Geology, or Oceanography. Working Conditions/Schedule: Salary Range: Anticipated Start Date: 2026-09-23 Anticipated End Date:"
