    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/OSU_Careers_Site/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/Student-Crew-Member---AnRS-Meat-Lab_JR0000954": {
      "title": "Student Crew Member - AnRS Meat Lab",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-08\nPosted: Posted 7 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nDepartment: Animal and Rangeland Sciences Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents: 1. A Resume/Vita 2. A cover letter indicating how your qualifications and experience have prepared you for this position   For additional information please contact: Chris Gallardo at Christopher.Gallardo@oregonstate.edu This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a criminal history check and be determined to be position qualified as per OSU Standard 576-055-0000 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in youth programs may have additional criminal history checks every 24 months. Job Description: Assist with pre and post-operational duties including, but not limited to: Plant opening/closing procedures Sanitation, slaughter and processing floor preparation Equipment maintenance and operation Cooler and freezer temperature monitoring Written documentation of aforementioned duties Assist with fabrication and further processing of whole carcasses, primals, and sub-primals into retail-ready and value-added meat products Retail store operations including, but not limited to: Product sale record keeping Assisting customers with product purchases Retail store opening/closing procedures Student employees gain valuable hands-on experience in meat processing, food safety, and product development while supporting teaching, research, and retail functions at the Clark Meat Science Center. This position offers practical skills for students pursuing careers in meat science, animal production, food safety, or related industries. Training will be provided for all lab processes and safety practices. Qualifications: Full Employment Eligibility Requirements can be found here:   https://hr.oregonstate.edu/student-employment-manual/500-employment-eligibility-requirements   Must be academically enrolled in a high school, community college, or university and pursuing a program or course of study Must meet Academic Standing Requirements; students on academic suspension are not eligible for employment Must meet the applicable minimal enrollment standard High School student: Regularly enrolled in a high school or participating in a home-schooling program Undergraduate and post-baccalaureate student: 6 credit hours per term Undergraduate international student: 12 credit hours per term* Graduate student officially admitted to Graduate School: 5 credit hours per term Graduate international student officially admitted to Graduate School: 9 credit hours per term* *International students may be allowed to carry fewer hours than specified above and still be considered “full-time” by the United States Citizenship and Immigration Services ( USCIS ). A reduced course load is approved by the   Office of International Services   ( OIS ), and must be provided to the Student Employment Center. Required Qualifications Strong attention to detail and ability to follow written and verbal instructions. Dependability, punctuality, and willingness to work in a team-oriented production environment. Preferred Qualifications: Prior experience with meat processing, fabrication, or food handling. Knowledge of safe handling practices, sanitation, and basic equipment. This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a criminal history check and be determined to be position qualified as per OSU Standard 576-055-0000 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in youth programs may have additional criminal history checks every 24 months. Working Conditions/Schedule: Must be able to lift 50lbs and stand for extended periods of time in cold environments. Must follow all safety, sanitation, and Hazard Analysis Critical Control Point (HACCP) procedures. Completion of required OSU Environmental Health and Safety (EHS) training. Personal protective equipment (PPE) must be always worn when in processing areas. Salary Range: 15.55 Anticipated Start Date: 2026-09-10 Anticipated End Date:"
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/OSU_Careers_Site/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/Associate-Director--University-Prestigious-Awards_JR0000885": {
      "title": "Associate Director, University Prestigious Awards",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-08\nPosted: Posted 7 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nDepartment: Division of Academic Affairs Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents:    1) A resume/CV; and     2) A cover letter indicating how your qualifications and experience have prepared you for this position.    Starting salary will be commensurate with skills, education, and experience.    For additional information please contact:   Shalece Rains Shalece.rains@oregonstate.edu Job Summary: The Office of Academic Faculty Excellence is seeking a University Prestigious Awards Associate Director. This is a full-time (1.00 FTE), 12-month, professional faculty position. The Associate Director, University Prestigious Awards (ADUPA) advances Oregon State University’s efforts to increase faculty recognition through national and international awards, honors, fellowships, academy memberships, and other prestigious distinctions. Reporting to the Associate Vice Provost for Faculty Excellence and housed within the Office of Academic Faculty Excellence (OAFE), this position serves as the university’s central resource for faculty recognition and awards support. The role provides organizational design, efficiency analysis, and process development for university wide faculty awards activities. The position develops and maintains strategic inventories of priority awards, analyzes and improves nomination workflows, recommends and implements solutions, develops communication plans, and provides training and assessment to strengthen institutional capacity for faculty recognition. The ADUPA collaborates with colleges, academic units, and university leaders to improve workflow, quality standards, and long term strategic planning related to faculty awards. This position plays an important role in advancing OSU’s strategic plan, Prosperity Widely Shared, by supporting faculty excellence and increasing the visibility and impact of OSU faculty. The role ensures long term institutional commitments to faculty recognition through sustained process design, capacity building, and strategic coordination. Key Responsibilities: Faculty Awards Coordination, Nomination Support, and Consultation Develop, maintain, and continuously evaluate a strategic, university wide inventory of high impact awards, honors, fellowships, academy memberships, and recognition pathways to ensure alignment with faculty accomplishments and institutional priorities. (Current awards can be found on the OSU website – over 1,000) Serve as a central liaison to colleges, academic units, and department leaders, providing guidance on award opportunities, nomination processes, workflow expectations, and candidate readiness.  Identify, assess, and cultivate prospective candidates across colleges and career stages through systematic review of faculty accomplishments, benchmarking data, and long term readiness indicators. ADUPA will proactively research available awards available nationally and internationally, identify faculty, and encourage and assist colleges and departments to apply.  Design, manage, and optimize nomination development and submission processes, including timelines, work plans, procedural steps, supporting materials, and internal review protocols to ensure efficiency and quality insuring consistency across colleges. Evaluate nomination materials for completeness, consistency, clarity, and alignment with award criteria, recommending improvements to strengthen competitiveness. Provide expertise for submission materials and documentation of submissions regardless of successful results. Coordinate and streamline external letter solicitation, internal review processes, and submission logistics to ensure accuracy, timeliness, and adherence to award requirements.  Advance institutional visibility and recognition strategies by aligning awards efforts with OSU’s strategic priorities and identifying opportunities to elevate faculty accomplishments. Develop and deliver training, including workshops, information sessions, and individualized consultations, to build faculty and unit capacity for awards readiness and long term recognition planning. In addition to workshops, ADUPA will be available for college faculty meetings, New Faculty Orientation meetings, and Promotion & Tenure trainings.  Partner with faculty and academic units to design long term recognition strategies by assessing award readiness, mapping award pathways, and recommending targeted activities that strengthen future candidacies and institutional competitiveness. Awards Administration, Data Management, and Assessment Develop, maintain, and analyze awards databases, nomination records, tracking systems, and faculty recognition datasets to monitor progress, identify trends, and inform strategic decision making. Leverage Academic Analytics and other benchmarking tools to identify potential candidates, assess research productivity indicators, and uncover emerging recognition opportunities.  Cultivate and maintain relationships with peer institutions, professional societies, and award organizations to support process improvement, benchmarking, and external collaboration.  Track and assess nomination activity, award outcomes, faculty recognition trends, and unit level engagement to inform continuous improvement and strategic planning.  Prepare reports, briefing materials, and annual summaries for academic leaders and administrators, synthesizing data to support decision making and institutional strategy.  Gather, analyze, and maintain information on peer institution practices, recognition programs, and national trends to support ongoing program enhancement and process refinement. Service and Other Duties as Assigned Participate in OAFE, Provost’s Office, and university committees and initiatives, contributing expertise in process design, recognition strategy, and faculty support.  Support unit planning, assessment, and continuous improvement activities, applying efficiency analysis and quality focused approaches to strengthen operations.  Perform additional duties in support of faculty excellence, recognition, and institutional priorities as assigned. Qualifications: Required Qualifications: Bachelor's degree from an accredited institution plus five years of professional experience OR Master’s degree plus three years of professional experience in higher education, academic administration, faculty affairs, faculty development, research administration, academic program management, advancement, fellowship advising, honors and awards programs, or a closely related field. Demonstrated experience managing complex projects involving multiple stakeholders, competing deadlines, and detailed processes. Experience collecting, organizing, analyzing, and reporting data through databases, spreadsheets, dashboards, or other tracking systems. Demonstrated commitment to advancing inclusive excellence and supporting individuals from diverse backgrounds, disciplines, and career stages. Demonstrated ability to communicate effectively in writing. Demonstrated ability to drive work forward to produce clear results. This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a criminal history check and be determined to be position qualified as per University Standard: 05-010 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in youth programs may have additional criminal history checks every   24 months.   Preferred Qualifications: Doctoral degree from an accredited institution. Experience supporting faculty nominations for awards, honors, fellowships, academy memberships, scholarly societies, or other forms of professional recognition, including knowledge of these opportunities. Demonstrated ability to build collaborative relationships with faculty, academic leaders, and administrative partners. Experience using Academic Analytics or similar research productivity, benchmarking, faculty activity reporting, or research intelligence tools to support strategic decision-making, assessment, and reporting. Experience designing and delivering workshops, consultations, or faculty development programming. Experience in a research-intensive university environment, particularly at a land-grant, R1, or AAU institution. Working Conditions/Schedule: Salary Range: $75,000 - $85,000 Anticipated Start Date: 2026-11-15 Anticipated End Date: We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status. OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire. OSU is a fair chance employer committed to inclusive hiring. We encourage applications from candidates who bring a wide range of lived experience including involvement with the justice system. If this position has “critical or security-sensitive” responsibilities and/or driving duties and you are selected as a finalist, your initial job offer will be contingent upon the results of a job-related pre-employment check (such as a background check, motor vehicle history check, sexual misconduct reference check, etc.). Background check results do not automatically disqualify a candidate. Take a look at our Background Checks website including the for candidates section for more details. If you have questions or concerns about the pre-employment check, please contact OSU’s Employee and Labor Relations team at employee.relations@oregonstate.edu ."
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/OSU_Careers_Site/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/Work-Study-Student---AnRS-Main-Office_JR0001059": {
      "title": "Work Study Student – AnRS Main Office",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-08\nPosted: Posted 7 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nDepartment: Animal and Rangeland Sciences Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents: A Resume A Cover Letter For additional information please contact: Tedd Huffman at tedd.huffman@oregonstate.edu We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status.   This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a Criminal History Check and be determined to be position qualified as per OSU Standard 576-055-0000 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in Youth Programs may have additional Criminal History Checks every 2 years.   OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire.   Note: All job offers are contingent upon Human Resources final approval Job Description: This recruitment will be used to fill multiple part-time (a maximum of 24 hours per week) Work Study Student – AnRS Main Office for the Animal and Rangeland Sciences department at Oregon State University (OSU). This position will be filled to assist AnRS with general office work including answering phones, filing, scanning, copy and manuscript assistance. Assist undergraduate advisor and program specialist with daily duties including but not limited to; updating website content, photograph and videotape AnRS animals, farming units, students and faculty for multimedia use as requested. Position Duties: Employee will work in Withycombe Hall providing general clerical assistance as needed. Assist advisor with website updating, digital signboard upkeep, paperwork, photograph and videotape AnRS animals, farming units, students and faculty for multimedia use as requested. General office work including answering phones, greet visitors, filing, scanning, copy and manuscript assistance Transferable Skill Development OSU is committed to ensuring students are prepared for success after graduation through intentional skill development as student employees. The competencies students develop while working at OSU are defined by the National Association of Colleges and Employers (NACE) as the essential skills employers seek in their hires. In this position, students learn and build vital career-readiness skills in the primary areas below:   Communication, Teamwork, Technology, Professionalism Qualifications: Full Employment Eligibility Requirements can be found here:   https://hr.oregonstate.edu/student-employment-manual/500-employment-eligibility-requirements Must be academically enrolled in a high school, community college, or university and pursuing a program or course of study Must meet Academic Standing Requirements; students on academic suspension are not eligible for employment Must meet the applicable minimal enrollment standard High School student: Regularly enrolled in a high school or participating in a home-schooling program Undergraduate and post-baccalaureate student: 6 credit hours per term Undergraduate international student: 12 credit hours per term* Graduate student officially admitted to Graduate School: 5 credit hours per term Graduate international student officially admitted to Graduate School: 9 credit hours per term* *International students may be allowed to carry fewer hours than specified above and still be considered “full-time” by the United States Citizenship and Immigration Services ( USCIS ). A reduced course load is approved by the   Office of International Services   ( OIS ), and must be provided to the Student Employment Center. Required Qualifications: Willingness to learn new skills and techniques Ability to adapt to changed environments Additional Required Qualifications: This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a Criminal History Check and be determined to be position qualified as per OSU Standard 576-055-0000 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in Youth Programs may have additional Criminal History Checks every 2 years. Preferred Qualifications: Experience that demonstrates the ability to work as a team Familiar with computer application such as Microsoft Suite (Word, Excel, etc.); Adobe Acrobat, Drupal (website editing), Workday, etc. – not all applications are required but note your level of competence in your cover letter Working Conditions/Schedule: Work is on-campus in an office environment using computers, copiers, filing, etc. Salary Range: Anticipated Start Date: 2026-09-24 Anticipated End Date:"
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/OSU_Careers_Site/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/Coordinator-of-Student-Engagement-and-Success--Internal-Employment-Opportunity-_JR0000920": {
      "title": "Coordinator of Student Engagement and Success (Internal Employment Opportunity)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-08\nPosted: Posted 7 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nDepartment: College of Agricultural Sciences Special Instructions to Applicants: This is an Internal Employment Opportunity. When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents:   1) A resume/CV; and   2) A cover letter indicating how your qualifications and experience have prepared you for this position.   Starting salary will be commensurate with skills, education, and experience. For additional information please contact: Jia Hu Jia.hu@oregonstate.edu Job Summary: This is an Internal Employment Opportunity. The College of Agricultural Sciences is seeking a Coordinator of Student Engagement and Success . This is a full-time (1.00 FTE), 12-month, professional faculty position. The College of Agricultural Sciences (CAS) is OSU's founding college. Since 1868, we have served as the engine of science that drives innovation, supports industry, and offers education and opportunity for anyone to grow into their fullest potential. Grow with us. Enrolling over 3,300 students, employing 280 professorial faculty, offering $900,000 in scholarships, and earning $90 million in research grants and contracts, CAS is at the epicenter of solving some of the world’s most pressing problems. With 13 academic departments and 12 academic programs, CAS offers one of the most diverse academic experiences with opportunities to pursue transdisciplinary research and education led by nationally renowned faculty. CAS offers 40 degree programs , with students enrolled from all 50 states and from more than 40 countries. Research in the College of Agricultural Sciences drives innovation that feeds communities, strengthens economies, and stewards the environment. We are cultivating prosperity by bridging science and society, and delivering solutions that help farmers adapt, improve food systems, and build resilience in the face of global challenges.   CAS is out there every day , working hand-in-hand with communities across the state to provide real-world application of our research to improve agricultural productivity, advance environmental stewardship, enhance community resilience and public health, and fuel entrepreneurial innovation. Come grow with us. Key Responsibilities: Development and Management of Student Engagement Programs Coordination of Student Activities and Event Planning Club Leadership Team Advisor CAS Academic Program Support/ University Service University and College Service There is an expectation that, as part of your role in the college, you   embody and advance the principles and practices conveyed in the   CAS CARE Commitment document. Qualifications: Required Qualifications: Bachelor’s degree in agriculture, natural resources, education, student services or a related   field. Two years professional experience working in student services. Excellent written and oral communication skills implemented in person, via telephone, email,   and in virtual spaces (Zoom, social media, Discussion Boards). Demonstrated competency with MS Office Suite, and Cloud-based applications (i.e., Google   drive, box, etc.). Must demonstrate independence and ability to take initiative in organizing programs,   preparing materials, and communicating with key contacts and stakeholders. Ability to collect, analyze, and synthesize data to identify trends, gaps and opportunities and   effectively present findings to internal and external audiences. Experience working with college students individually and in group settings. Experience delivering and/or supporting educational programs for audiences from diverse   backgrounds and various cultural identities. Comfortable learning and adapting educational   materials. This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a criminal history check and be determined to be position qualified as per University Standard: 05-010 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in youth programs may have additional criminal history checks every 24 months. This position requires driving a university vehicle or a personal vehicle on behalf of the university; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a motor vehicle history check, possess and maintain a current, valid driver's license in their state of residence, be determined to be position qualified and self-report convictions as per University Policy 05-030. ​ Preferred Qualifications: Master of Science in agriculture, natural resources, education, student services or a related field. Record of grant writing for funding student programs. Experience conducting/designing scientific research projects. Teaching experience at institutions of higher learning. Life experiences, education, or training that broadens capacity to equalize student success or   impact underserved audiences. Experience with event planning. Working Conditions/Schedule: Ability to perform some travel and evening/weekend work as required including the ability to travel to student recruiting events held locally and nationally. Salary Range: $58,000 - $76,000 Anticipated Start Date: 2026-09-14 Anticipated End Date: We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status. OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire. OSU is a fair chance employer committed to inclusive hiring. We encourage applications from candidates who bring a wide range of lived experience including involvement with the justice system. If this position has “critical or security-sensitive” responsibilities and/or driving duties and you are selected as a finalist, your initial job offer will be contingent upon the results of a job-related pre-employment check (such as a background check, motor vehicle history check, sexual misconduct reference check, etc.). Background check results do not automatically disqualify a candidate. Take a look at our Background Checks website including the for candidates section for more details. If you have questions or concerns about the pre-employment check, please contact OSU’s Employee and Labor Relations team at employee.relations@oregonstate.edu ."
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/OSU_Careers_Site/job/Bend/Front-Desk-Assistant_JR0000722": {
      "title": "Front Desk Assistant",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-07\nPosted: Posted 8 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nDepartment: Residential Education and Housing Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents: A Resume A Cover Letter For additional information please contact: Madi Mather at matherm@oregonstate.edu We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status.   This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a Criminal History Check and be determined to be position qualified as per OSU Standard 576-055-0000 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in Youth Programs may have additional Criminal History Checks every 2 years. OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire.   Note: All job offers are contingent upon Human Resources final approval Job Description: This recruitment will be used to fill 2 part-time (a maximum of 24 hours per week) positions for the Front Desk Assistant role at Oregon State University (OSU) – Cascades. Desk Assistants (DAs) are employees of the Office of Residential Education and Housing. Supervised by a professional staff member, DAs are expected to work towards Residential Education and Housing objectives, manage the front desk, and provide administrative support. Overall job effectiveness is tied to the DA’s ability to complete administrative tasks and provide overall support to residential students. OSU - Cascades   is   an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected   veteran , and other protected status.   A personal and professional commitment to providing excellent customer service and creating inclusive environments is a core value of Residential Education and Housing. Residential Education and Housing will provide a transformative on-campus experience that will ENGAGE our students in community, ENRICH their lives, and help them flourish and THRIVE.   Students receiving Work Study as part of their financial aid award are welcome to apply for this position.   Position Duties   Community Development   Facilitate and support the Community Development Plan as directed by your supervisor, including creating/ maintaining   bulletin boards, and   assisting   with programming.   Identify   and respond to   resident   needs through consultation and referral of campus resources.   Frame communication with respect to diversity of learning styles, varied individual communication abilities, and cultural differences.   Administration & University-Wide Support   Support administrative processes and communication related to Residential Education and Housing .   Manage the Residence Hall Front Desk by providing lockout   assistance , answering the phone(s), and referring students to appropriate resources.   Complete   administrative tasks by   identifying   the   appropriate technology   for completing specific tasks and asking questions for clarification from colleagues or supervisors.   Demonstrate dependability   and timeliness   by completing all Incident Reports and Duty Logs consistently and with minimal errors .      Communicate in a clear and organized manner and regularly check work communication channels to stay organized. (email, staff mailbox, team communication)   Prioritize tasks and   submit   all required materials in a consistent and   timely   manner. ( digital   or physical advertising ,   restocking/inventory, passive programming,   etc. )   Maintain positive and   equitable   customer service for all residents, guests, and other individuals served by the housing department .  Ensure hand-off of the duty phone to the scheduled RA-on-duty during evening shifts.     Student Behavior & Crisis Management   Identify   and r eport   potential policy violations , incidents, or student concerns to supervisor.   Multi-task in emergency situations or crisis response by   identifying   next steps, contacting professional staff members, delegating tasks to residents and other staff, and communicating effectively.   Understand, abide by, and role model behavior expected of students as outlined in the OSU Student Conduct & Community Standards and all Residential Education and Housing Policies. Meeting and Training Requirements   DAs must complete all required training sessions prior to and during service, including online modules.   DAs may be asked to stay as late as noon on Sunday after Spring Term finals to   assist   with closing responsibilities.    Exceptions to the above responsibilities of the position   may be approved at the discretion of the   Associate   Director of Residential Education and Housing .   Transferable Skill Development OSU is committed to ensuring students are prepared for success after graduation through intentional skill development as student employees. The competencies students develop while working at OSU are defined by the National Association of Colleges and Employers (NACE) as the essential skills employers seek in their hires. In this position, students learn and build vital career-readiness skills in the primary areas below:   Communication, Critical Thinking, Professionalism, Equity & Inclusion Qualifications: Full Employment Eligibility Requirements can be found here:   https://hr.oregonstate.edu/student-employment-manual/500-employment-eligibility-requirements Must be academically enrolled in a high school, community college, or university and pursuing a program or course of study Must meet Academic Standing Requirements; students on academic suspension are not eligible for employment Must meet the applicable minimal enrollment standard High School student: Regularly enrolled in a high school or participating in a home-schooling program Undergraduate and post-baccalaureate student: 6 credit hours per term Undergraduate international student: 12 credit hours per term* Graduate student officially admitted to Graduate School: 5 credit hours per term Graduate international student officially admitted to Graduate School: 9 credit hours per term* *International students may be allowed to carry fewer hours than specified above and still be considered “full-time” by the United States Citizenship and Immigration Services ( USCIS ). A reduced course load is approved by the   Office of International Services   ( OIS) and must be provided to the Student Employment Center. Required Qualifications: Be a current OSU-Cascades student or degree partnership student with OSU-Cascades and COCC with a cumulative Grade Point Average (GPA) of 2.50 or higher. Failure to do so may result in employment warning, probation, or removal from the DA position. Maintain good academic and conduct standing with both OSU-Cascades and Residential Education and Housing. Demonstrate the ability to cultivate relationships with people. Demonstrate the ability to respond to conflict. Demonstrate an understanding or open-mindedness to social justice. Demonstrate the ability to complete administrative tasks in a timely manner. Additional Required Qualifications: This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a Criminal History Check and be determined to be position qualified as per OSU Standard 576-055-0000 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in Youth Programs may have additional Criminal History Checks every 2 years. Preferred Qualifications: Demonstrated commitment to diversity and social justice education, initiatives, and programs. Demonstrated crisis management skills. Previous experience in leadership opportunities either on or off campus. Working Conditions/Schedule: • The general schedule for DA shifts is Monday - Friday from 1pm - 8pm and Sunday 3pm - 8pm, although hours may vary. • The DA is an “At Will” employee. As such, the employee, or Residential Education and Housing may terminate the position at any time. Salary Range: N/A Anticipated Start Date: 2026-09-25 Anticipated End Date:"
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/OSU_Careers_Site/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/DAS-Proctor_JR0000467": {
      "title": "DAS Proctor",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-07\nPosted: Posted 8 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nDepartment: Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents: A Resume A Cover Letter For additional information please contact: Tamara Sprague at Tamara.sprague@oregonstate.edu We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status.   OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire.   Note: All job offers are contingent upon Human Resources final approval Job Description: This recruitment will be used to fill four (4) part-time (a maximum of 24 hours per week) Proctor for the Disability Access Services at Oregon State University (OSU). Testing Proctors at Disability Access Services (DAS) administer exams to DAS students in a professional manner. A proctor monitors the testing center environment to ensure academic integrity and facilitate exam instructions by the course instructor. Position Duties: Demonstrate high level of compliance to DAS Testing Center policies and procedures. Maintain cheerful and welcoming environment.  Facilitate administration of exam per faculty instructions. Validate student ID prior to each exam.  Implement assistive technology as needed. Willingness to assist students as a 1:1 scribe or reader as needed.  Monitor the testing center environment, identifying potential academic integrity issues.  Perform sanitizing of testing spaces, high touch areas, locker keys and pens.  Facilitate delivery of completed exams on campus, as needed.  Maintain up to date availability and demonstrate ability to be on time and ready for each scheduled shift. Transferable Skill Development OSU is committed to ensuring students are prepared for success after graduation through intentional skill development as student employees. The competencies students develop while working at OSU are defined by the National Association of Colleges and Employers (NACE) as the essential skills employers seek in their hires. In this position, students learn and build vital career-readiness skills in the primary areas below:   Communication, Teamwork, Professionalism, Equity & Inclusion Qualifications: Full Employment Eligibility Requirements can be found here:   https://hr.oregonstate.edu/student-employment-manual/500-employment-eligibility-requirements Must be academically enrolled in a high school, community college, or university and pursuing a program or course of study Must meet Academic Standing Requirements; students on academic suspension are not eligible for employment Must meet the applicable minimal enrollment standard High School student: Regularly enrolled in a high school or participating in a home-schooling program Undergraduate and post-baccalaureate student: 6 credit hours per term Undergraduate international student: 12 credit hours per term* Graduate student officially admitted to Graduate School: 5 credit hours per term Graduate international student officially admitted to Graduate School: 9 credit hours per term* *International students may be allowed to carry fewer hours than specified above and still be considered “full-time” by the United States Citizenship and Immigration Services ( USCIS ). A reduced course load is approved by the   Office of International Services   ( OIS ), and must be provided to the Student Employment Center. Required Qualifications: Have availability to proctor during finals week.  Must maintain 2.5 GPA and have no prior academic violations. Working Conditions/Schedule: • Testing proctors are required to work an average of 3-10 hours per week and are eligible for 24 hours of work per week. (Work Study students may work a maximum of 20 hours per week.) • Work hours range Monday-Thursday 7:45am-10pm, and Friday 7:45am-5pm. • Proctors are expected to work during Finals week. Proctor shifts may be between 7:15am-11pm, Monday-Thursday, and Friday 7:15am-5pm. Proctors are generally scheduled 18-24 hrs. during finals. • Have availability to proctor during finals week. Salary Range: Anticipated Start Date: 2026-09-18 Anticipated End Date:"
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/OSU_Careers_Site/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/FIN-460-Student-Teaching-Assistant_JR0001060": {
      "title": "FIN 460 Student Teaching Assistant",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-04\nPosted: Posted 11 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nDepartment: College of Business – School of Accounting, Finance, and Information Systems Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents: A Resume A Cover Letter For additional information please contact: Leela Varadharajan at Leela.Varadharajan@oregonstate.edu We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status.   This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a Criminal History Check and be determined to be position qualified as per OSU Standard 576-055-0000 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in Youth Programs may have additional Criminal History Checks every 2 years.   OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire.   Note: All job offers are contingent upon Human Resources final approval Job Description: This recruitment will be used to fill one part-time (a maximum of 24 hours per week) FIN 460 Student Teaching Assistant for the College of Business – School of Accounting, Finance, and Information Systems at Oregon State University (OSU). The Teaching Assistant supports instructional delivery, grading, and student mentoring for FIN 460. This course serves as a foundational component of the CFP Board-registered financial planning curriculum, covering personal financial statement analysis, time value of money (TVM), cash flow and debt management, risk and investment planning, retirement, tax strategies, estate planning, and CFP Board practice standards and ethics. Position Duties: Ability to host weekly in-person and/or virtual Zoom office hours to guide students through course concepts, financial calculator mechanics, and case study financial plans. Availability to attend class meetings as needed to proctor exams, monitor Top Hat engagement, and assist with classroom administration. Detail oriented evaluation of homework assignments, quizzes, and comprehensive case studies while enforcing academic integrity policies. Transferable Skill Development OSU is committed to ensuring students are prepared for success after graduation through intentional skill development as student employees. The competencies students develop while working at OSU are defined by the National Association of Colleges and Employers (NACE) as the essential skills employers seek in their hires. In this position, students learn and build vital career-readiness skills in the primary areas below:   Communication, Professionalism, Career & Self Development Qualifications: Full Employment Eligibility Requirements can be found here:   https://hr.oregonstate.edu/student-employment-manual/500-employment-eligibility-requirements Must be academically enrolled in a high school, community college, or university and pursuing a program or course of study Must meet Academic Standing Requirements; students on academic suspension are not eligible for employment Must meet the applicable minimal enrollment standard High School student: Regularly enrolled in a high school or participating in a home-schooling program Undergraduate and post-baccalaureate student: 6 credit hours per term Undergraduate international student: 12 credit hours per term* Graduate student officially admitted to Graduate School: 5 credit hours per term Graduate international student officially admitted to Graduate School: 9 credit hours per term* *International students may be allowed to carry fewer hours than specified above and still be considered “full-time” by the United States Citizenship and Immigration Services ( USCIS ). A reduced course load is approved by the   Office of International Services   ( OIS ), and must be provided to the Student Employment Center. Required Qualifications: Successful completion of FIN 460 (Principles of Financial Planning) with an A or A- grade. Comprehensive understanding of core personal financial planning functional areas, including retirement planning, time value of money, investment planning etc. Additional Required Qualifications: This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a Criminal History Check and be determined to be position qualified as per OSU Standard 576-055-0000 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in Youth Programs may have additional Criminal History Checks every 2 years. Preferred Qualifications: Proficiency in using financial calculators (BA II Plus) to solve complex financial planning and TVM problems. Working Conditions/Schedule: • Hybrid work environment: Combines scheduled on-campus classroom sessions in Austin Hall with remote/flexible hours for grading and online course management via Canvas and Top Hat. • Workload will fluctuate during peak grading milestones, particularly following the submission of Cases and exams. Expected to maintain responsive communication with the instructor and students, answering course-related emails and inquiries within 1 to 2 business days. • Pay rate for this position is $16.55 per hour. Salary Range: Anticipated Start Date: 2026-09-21 Anticipated End Date:"
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/OSU_Careers_Site/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/Faculty-Research-Assistant--Glider---Ocean-Instrumentation-Technician_JR0001067": {
      "title": "Faculty Research Assistant: Glider & Ocean Instrumentation Technician",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-15\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nDepartment: Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents:   1) A resume/CV; and   2) A cover letter indicating how your qualifications and experience have prepared you for this position. Starting salary will be commensurate with skills, education, and experience. For additional information please contact: Kipp Shearman at Kipp.Shearman@oregonstate.edu or 541-737-1866.   Job Summary: The College of Earth, Ocean and Atmospheric Sciences invites applications for two full-time (1.00 FTE), 12-month, fixed-term Faculty Research Assistant: Glider & Ocean Instrumentation Technician positions.  Reappointment is at the discretion of the Dean. Key Responsibilities: The FRA will split time across projects that include: testing and installation of oceanographic sensors (e.g., temperature, conductivity, dissolved oxygen, broadband acoustics) on research platforms assembly and testing of oceanographic research platforms (e.g., autonomous underwater gliders, moorings, over-the-side profiling instruments) ground transportation of research equipment at-sea deployment and recovery of oceanographic research platforms refurbishment of oceanographic research components data processing, quality control, and dissemination computer system administration (e.g., software upgrades, maintenance) Oregon State University (OSU) conducts oceanographic research and monitoring off the Pacific Northwest and globally. This research involves a variety of sensors and platforms, including autonomous underwater vehicles, ship-based profiling, and moorings. The OSU Glider Research Group conducts studies funded by a variety of federal agencies, the state of Oregon, industry, and private foundations. OSU is a partner on a large-scale multi-year federally funded project called the National Science Foundation (NSF) Ocean Observatories Initiative (OOI) and operates OOI’s Coastal Endurance Array off the coast of Oregon and Washington. Ocean measurements from these programs are made available in near real time to national data centers and other ocean users. This Faculty Research Assistant will collaborate with a team of researchers, oceanographers and technicians to assemble, deploy, recover, and refurbish platforms and instruments used for oceanographic research and monitoring. Working with this same team, the FRA will be responsible for data processing, data quality assurance and quality control, computer systems administration, and delivery of documented data to national oceanographic data centers and other ocean users. Qualifications: Required Qualifications: B achelor’s degree in oceanography, marine science, natural science, engineering, computer science or related field. Experience in Windows or LINUX/UNIX environments. Experience with high-level scientific data analysis (e.g., Python, Matlab). Demonstrated ability to work effectively in a team environment with diverse groups of researchers, staff, and students. Hands-on troubleshooting and problems resolution experience. Demonstrated written, verbal, and presentation communication skills. Ability to work at sea. Ability to travel for extended periods. This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a criminal history check and be determined to be position qualified as per University Standard: 05-010 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in youth programs may have additional criminal history checks every 24 months. This position requires driving a university vehicle or a personal vehicle on behalf of the university; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a motor vehicle history check, possess and maintain a current, valid driver's license in their state of residence, be determined to be position qualified and self-report convictions as per University Policy 05-030. Preferred Qualifications: Experience and aptitude with electrical and mechanical systems. Master’s degree in science, engineering, earth system sciences, physical oceanography, or related field. Experience with, and knowledge of, the principles of and operation of oceanographic sensors. At least two years’ experience preparing, deploying, recovering, and maintaining oceanographic research instrumentation. Experience preparing, deploying, recovering, and maintaining underwater gliders. Certificate in ocean observing systems or similar field. Boat skills from experience on research, commercial, or recreational vessels. Experience with command line interfaces and Linux systems administration concepts. Working Conditions/Schedule: Most work will be done in a laboratory in Corvallis. • This person will have a supervisor, who will apportion their time. This person will work for multiple investigators on multiple projects as assigned by their supervisor. • This is a salaried position, not an hourly position. • Working conditions involve a mix of working at a desk in an office, standing in a laboratory, working outside. This person will need to be able to lift up to 50 lbs. and to learn how to use lifting equipment such as driving a forklift. • Work includes up to 50 days a year at sea plus associated travel. Sea time includes day trips up to field campaigns that last up to a month. See https://research.oregonstate.edu/osraa/rates for information about sea pay. Salary Range: Anticipated Start Date: 2026-11-04 Anticipated End Date: 2027-06-30 We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status. OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire. OSU is a fair chance employer committed to inclusive hiring. We encourage applications from candidates who bring a wide range of lived experience including involvement with the justice system. If this position has “critical or security-sensitive” responsibilities and/or driving duties and you are selected as a finalist, your initial job offer will be contingent upon the results of a job-related pre-employment check (such as a background check, motor vehicle history check, sexual misconduct reference check, etc.). Background check results do not automatically disqualify a candidate. Take a look at our Background Checks website including the for candidates section for more details. If you have questions or concerns about the pre-employment check, please contact OSU’s Employee and Labor Relations team at employee.relations@oregonstate.edu ."
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/OSU_Careers_Site/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/PRAx-Event-and-Program-Student-Facilitator_JR0001154": {
      "title": "PRAx Event and Program Student Facilitator",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-15\nPosted: Posted Today\nPart time\n\nDepartment: PRAx Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents: A Resume A Cover Letter Supplemental Questions Supplemental Questions: 1. What is your program of study? (Majors, minors, etc.) (Open Ended Question) 2. When is your anticipated graduation term? (Fall/winter/spring/summer & year) (Open Ended Question) 3. Describe a time you helped create a welcoming or memorable experience for someone else. What did you notice, and what did you do? (350-word maximum) (Open Ended Question) 4. This job involves public speaking and providing excellent customer service for a variety of visitors, artists, and clients. Please describe your comfort level and experience starting conversations with people you do not know from a variety of backgrounds. Share an example of a time you developed a positive connection with a stranger. (350-word maximum) For additional information please contact: Steven McIntyre at Steven.mcintyre@oregonstate.edu We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status.   OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire.   Note: All job offers are contingent upon Human Resources final approval Job Description: This recruitment will be used to fill two part-time (a maximum of 24 hours per week) Event and Program Student Facilitator for the Patricia Valian Reser Center for the Creative Arts (PRAx) at Oregon State University (OSU). The expansive mission of the Patricia Valian Reser Center for the Creative Arts is to activate the power of the arts and humanities across disciplines and in multiple settings—from the concert hall and theater to research labs and field sites. We are a space for transformative education for all, from OSU students and faculty to community members and youth in school, and we tap into the power of the arts to transform thinking across academic disciplines. PRAx seeks to provide unforgettable arts experiences at the intersections between art, humanities, science, and technology. PRAx employs qualified, currently enrolled undergraduate students as paid Event and Program Facilitators, who serve as the first point of contact for members of the campus and Corvallis communities, visiting artists and performers, and rental clients. They represent PRAx through a variety of events and programs, from full-scale performances and rental events to smaller drop-in workshops and K-12 field trips. Visitors represent a wide variety of interests and life experiences. PRAx Event and Program Facilitators are part of a team that creates a warm, welcoming presence that focuses on memorable experiences and creating opportunities for nuanced conversations. Facilitators spark conversations about art in the gallery, enhance visitor experiences at performing arts events and humanities presentations, host tours and facilitate activities for K-12 field trips, and provide hospitality for visiting artists. Facilitators also represent PRAx at large university events through tabling and outreach. Through all their job duties, facilitators share information about PRAx, its upcoming performances and art on display, and they also share their personal positive experiences at PRAx to encourage community and student engagement. All shifts are conducted in person. Student employees typically work 2–10+ hours per week on a flexible schedule that works with academic obligations. They receive ample and ongoing paid training to ensure success in the role. PRAx is seeking a team of individuals who enjoy working with people, bring a wide range of life experiences, and demonstrate enthusiasm for providing memorable arts encounters for students, faculty, youth, artists, and the wider community. Bilingual candidates are especially encouraged to apply. Position Duties: Visitor Experience and Hospitality: Serve as event host with minimal oversight or supervision, remaining attentive to client needs and visitor experience. This may include anything from providing an enthusiastic welcome to identifying issues/needs proactively and without guidance, responding appropriately, and communicating with on-site supervisors about any situations that may arise. Initiate conversations that engage a wide range of visitors about artwork, exhibitions, performances, and other programming at PRAx. Lead inspiring, educational K-12 youth field trips (will require youth policy training). Represent PRAx at tabling events on or off campus. Event Setup and Monitoring: Facilitate successful events and rentals. Act as greeter and guide for PRAx VIPs as assigned. Serve as backup usher or ticket scanner as needed. Run basic AV and presentation technology in Toomey Lobby, Stirek Gallery, and classrooms. Follow the daily notes and run-of-show/event schedule. Set up/strike tables, chairs, and other event materials and decor. Follow opening and closing procedures, including providing post-show summary information. Artist/Speaker Experience and Hospitality: Support successful experiences of visiting artists, speakers, and performers. Review daily notes and instructions for artist/speaker hospitality. Greet and introduce artists to stage crew and other members of the PRAx front of house or back of house teams as appropriate. Prepare dressing rooms and green rooms for artists and speakers according to instructions. Ensure artist/speaker needs are met. Drive the PRAx Van as assigned. Across all duties: Work across teams at PRAx, including engagements, events, gallery, and marketing. Bring enthusiastic, positive energy to both front-facing activities and in your work as part of a team. Participate in creating informed, compelling, and culturally-attuned materials to guide interactions with PRAx programming, including exhibition and building tours. Represent PRAx and serve as host with minimal oversight or supervision from staff members. Communicate effectively and professionally with all PRAx staff, volunteers, partners, clients and visitors. Take initiative to ensure the success of PRAx events. Anticipate and proactively address visitor, client, and event needs. Attend meetings to develop and maintain knowledge about exhibitions, PRAx initiatives, and performances. Follow all safety procedures. Maintain professional etiquette. C omplete other duties as assigned. Transferable Skill Development OSU is committed to ensuring students are prepared for success after graduation through intentional skill development as student employees. The competencies students develop while working at OSU are defined by the National Association of Colleges and Employers (NACE) as the essential skills employers seek in their hires. In this position, students learn and build vital career-readiness skills in the primary areas below:   Communication, Teamwork, Leadership, Professionalism Qualifications: Full Employment Eligibility Requirements can be found here:   https://hr.oregonstate.edu/student-employment-manual/500-employment-eligibility-requirements Must be academically enrolled in a high school, community college, or university and pursuing a program or course of study Must meet Academic Standing Requirements; students on academic suspension are not eligible for employment Must meet the applicable minimal enrollment standard High School student: Regularly enrolled in a high school or participating in a home-schooling program Undergraduate and post-baccalaureate student: 6 credit hours per term Undergraduate international student: 12 credit hours per term* Graduate student officially admitted to Graduate School: 5 credit hours per term Graduate international student officially admitted to Graduate School: 9 credit hours per term* *International students may be allowed to carry fewer hours than specified above and still be considered “full-time” by the United States Citizenship and Immigration Services ( USCIS ). A reduced course load is approved by the   Office of International Services   ( OIS ), and must be provided to the Student Employment Center. Required Qualifications: Excellent communication and interpersonal skills. Must be welcoming and personable – you enjoy working with the public, and it shows. Ability to represent PRAx effectively and positively. Possess well-rounded academic and co-curricular experiences, and exhibit leadership, commitment, initiative, dependability, time management, discipline, and self-confidence. Experience in initiating conversations with people you do not know. Demonstrated experience working with individuals from various backgrounds, lived experiences, perspectives, and identities. Comfortable with moderate physical activity, including standing, walking, lifting up to 40 lbs.   Must have a valid driver’s license. Additional Required Qualifications: This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a Criminal History Check and be determined to be position qualified as per OSU Standard 576-055-0000 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in Youth Programs may have additional Criminal History Checks every 2 years. This position requires driving a University vehicle or a personal vehicle on behalf of the University; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a Motor Vehicle History Check, possess and maintain a current, valid driver's license in their state of residence, be determined to be position qualified and self-report convictions (as per Voluntary and Compulsory Driver Standards OSU Standard 125-155-0200) as per OSU Standard 576-056-0000 et seq. Preferred Qualifications: Demonstrate enthusiasm to go above and beyond in making memorable experiences for all who come to PRAx. Professional experience providing hospitality via phone, email, and/or in person. Experience working or volunteering in performing or visual arts spaces . Demonstrated engagement with PRAx events, programming, and/or organization . Demonstrated engagement with OSU’s student resources, clubs and organizations, and/or the off-campus Corvallis community. Demonstrated experience with K-12 youth. Bilingual in a second language other than English . Working Conditions/Schedule: • Must be able to work flexible hours including evenings and weekends. • Hours are based on the needs of events and programs. The schedule and number of hours are variable each week. • May involve standing for long periods, lifting up to 40 lbs., and working in dim lighting or noisy environments. • All facilitators are required to attend annual fall training the week prior to the start of fall term. • Use of personal electronic devices during shifts is prohibited except during breaks or for work-related communications. Attentiveness and situational awareness, often without direct supervision, are essential in this role. • Appropriate Dress: Business Casual unless otherwise directed by a supervisor. Salary Range: Anticipated Start Date: 2026-10-11 Anticipated End Date:"
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/OSU_Careers_Site/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/Executive-Assistant-to-the-VP-University-Relations---Marketing_JR0001012": {
      "title": "Executive Assistant to the VP University Relations & Marketing",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-15\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nDepartment: University Relations & Marketing Special Instructions to Applicants: To ensure full consideration, applications must be received by September 30, 2026. Applications will continue to be accepted after the full consideration date, until a sufficient applicant pool has been achieved or the position is filled. The closing date is subject to change without notice to applicants. When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents:  1) A resume 2) A cover letter indicating how your qualifications and experience have prepared you for this position.   Starting salary will be commensurate with skills, education, and experience. For additional information please contact: Name: Michelle Schleh Email: URM@oregonstate.edu Phone: 503-237-4311 Job Summary: University Relations and Marketing​ is seeking an Executive Assistant to the VP University Relations & Marketing. This is a full-time (1.00 FTE), 12-month, professional faculty position.      The University Relations and Marketing Executive Assistant provides critical executive, project and operational support to the VP URM in addition to serving as a strategic thought partner and liaison to division staff, senior leadership, faculty and staff on behalf of the Vice President. Key areas of accountability include planning, coordinating and administering policy and processes related to media relations, corporate communications, brand communications and creative, website development, integrated marketing communications and digital marketing. The University Relations and Marketing Executive Assistant is expected to demonstrate leadership and to be a contributing member of the VP URM’s leadership team. Key Responsibilities: Work effectively in a high-level, high-profile, fast-paced administrative office with frequent interruptions, while maintaining professional composure. Enables marketing and communications support for teams across URM and the campus by triaging requests made to the VP URM, identifying or organizing resources, or arranging outside or internal support. Attends and participates in meetings, records minutes and expectations from meetings, and follows up on meeting action items to completion. Serves as a URM Leadership Team member and participates in developing and implementing strategic plans and tactics. Monitors and coordinates budget items for URM and the Vice President. Manages complex scheduling and supports the planning and activation of large-scale or organizationally important events. Qualifications: MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS: Bachelor's degree in any field Minimum of three years of experience in a position with comparable duties in a similarly complex and confidential environment. Demonstrated effective oral and written communication skills, and ability to engage respectfully with individuals from a variety of backgrounds and experiences. Demonstrable ability to: Take initiative, exercise sound judgment to make decisions, with the ability to anticipate the needs of the unit and proactively problem-solve. Communicate both effectively and diplomatically, prepare and edit documents and presentations. Effectively manage complex scheduling needs with attentiveness to detail and accuracy. Engage professionally with people from all levels within the University, and with individuals outside of the university, including, on occasion, individuals who have complaints or concerns Successfully plan, prioritize, and manage simultaneous projects, often within strict deadlines, and to manage multiple tasks and shifting priorities while coordinating a high volume of incoming work. Demonstrated commitment to maintaining confidentiality, discretion, and diplomacy. Demonstrated proficiency in using the MS Office Suite (e.g., Copilot, SharePoint, Word, Excel, Office 365, and PowerPoint). Ability to accommodate situations related to media or special events; ability to work quickly and under pressure, including outside of ordinary business hours.​ This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a criminal history check and be determined to be position qualified as per University Standard: 05-010 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in youth programs may have additional criminal history checks every 24 months. This position requires driving a university vehicle or a personal vehicle on behalf of the university; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a motor vehicle history check, possess and maintain a current, valid driver's license in their state of residence, be determined to be position qualified and self-report convictions as per University Policy 05-030.   PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Three years providing direct administrative support to an executive level administrator. Experience at Oregon State University or with another institution of higher education. Second language proficiency Two years of experience in sales, marketing or communications. Advanced communications and marketing skills and ability to do research in response to specific requests. Demonstrable marketing, media relations and/or social media skills Experience working directly with people from diverse racial, ethnic, and socioeconomic backgrounds Working Conditions/Schedule: Professional office work environment within 6th floor of university’s Kerr Administration Building. 8 a.m. to 5 p.m., although earlier or somewhat later office hours are sometimes requested. Salary Range: $80,300 - $89,300 Anticipated Start Date: 2026-10-01 Anticipated End Date: We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status. OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire. OSU is a fair chance employer committed to inclusive hiring. We encourage applications from candidates who bring a wide range of lived experience including involvement with the justice system. If this position has “critical or security-sensitive” responsibilities and/or driving duties and you are selected as a finalist, your initial job offer will be contingent upon the results of a job-related pre-employment check (such as a background check, motor vehicle history check, sexual misconduct reference check, etc.). Background check results do not automatically disqualify a candidate. Take a look at our Background Checks website including the for candidates section for more details. If you have questions or concerns about the pre-employment check, please contact OSU’s Employee and Labor Relations team at employee.relations@oregonstate.edu ."
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/OSU_Careers_Site/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/Analyst-Programmer-3--Senior-Technical-Engineer---Undergraduate-Enrollment-Systems---Integrations--Internal-Employment-Opportunity-_JR0001081": {
      "title": "Analyst Programmer 3: Senior Technical Engineer – Undergraduate Enrollment Systems & Integrations (Internal Employment Opportunity)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-15\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nDepartment: Enrollment Management Digital Engagement and Marketing Special Instructions to Applicants: This is an Internal Employment Opportunity. When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents:   1) A resume/CV; and   2) A cover letter indicating how your qualifications and experience have prepared you for this position. Starting salary will be commensurate with skills, education, and experience. For additional information please contact: Daniel Crouch at Daniel.Crouch@oregonstate.edu Job Summary: This is an Internal Employment Opportunity. This recruitment will be used to fill one full-time Analyst Programmer 3: Senior Technical Engineer – Undergraduate Enrollment Systems & Integrations position for the Office of Enrollment Management at Oregon State University (OSU). Join Oregon State University’s Digital Engagement, Marketing, and Information Technology (DEM&IT) team as the senior technical engineer for undergraduate enrollment systems. This role owns complex Slate architecture, integrations, custom applications, and data flows that support students from recruitment and application through admission and onboarding. Working across Enrollment Management and university technology partners, you will turn complex business needs into secure, accessible, reliable systems. The position offers substantial technical autonomy, opportunities to mentor colleagues, and direct contributions to student access, enrollment growth, and more effective university operations. Key Responsibilities: Provide senior technical ownership of undergraduate Slate and related enrollment systems. Design and develop complex applications, APIs, integrations, and data flows, including Banner-connected processes. Analyze cross-unit enrollment workflows and anticipate downstream system and operational impacts. Lead resolution of complex production issues and provide advanced technical consultation. Maintain architecture, integration, support, and continuity documentation and technical standards. Provide technical lead work, mentoring, and code/design review without direct personnel supervision. Maintain current technical expertise and support DEM&IT and Enrollment Management priorities. Qualifications: Required Qualifications: This classification requires a basic foundation of knowledge and skills in systems analysis and related programming support functions generally obtained by a bachelor’s degree in computer science, or an equivalent amount of training and applied experience. Experience in systems analysis, application development, CRM administration, database-supported systems, integrations, or related programming support functions. Demonstrated ability to work independently with minimal oversight and lead complex cross-functional technical work from requirements analysis and solution design through implementation and production support. Experience translating complex business processes and technical requirements into secure, maintainable, sustainable system solutions. Strong communication, writing, documentation, organizational, consultation, and customer service skills. Ability to manage multiple priorities and technical dependencies, monitor progress, and maintain accurate project, system, and operational information. Ability to interpret policy, procedure, business rules, data relationships, and technical requirements and explain implications clearly to technical and non-technical audiences. Ability to identify architecture, integration, data, operational, and project risks and independently resolve, mitigate, or escalate them appropriately. Demonstrated commitment to promoting and enhancing inclusive excellence and student success. This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a criminal history check and be determined to be position qualified as per University Standard: 05-010 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in youth programs may have additional criminal history checks every 24 months. Preferred Qualifications: Bachelor’s degree in computer science, business information systems, management information systems, data systems, or a related area of study. Three or more years of progressively responsible experience with Technolutions Slate CRM administration, configuration, reporting, systems development, or technical ownership. Experience supporting undergraduate admissions, recruitment, onboarding, scholarship, CRM communications, application processing, or other high-volume enrollment operations. Experience with SQL, XML, JavaScript, HTML, Groovy, Ruby on Rails, APIs/web services, data integrations, data mapping, or other technologies relevant to Slate and enrollment systems. Experience designing, testing, implementing, or troubleshooting integrations between Slate and Ellucian Banner or other enterprise student information systems. Experience applying higher education policies and business-process knowledge to technical solutions involving admissions, registrar, financial aid, scholarships, student data, FERPA, or academic procedures. Experience serving as technical lead or technical owner for complex systems work, including solution architecture, workflow automation, integration design, code/design review, mentoring, or establishing technical standards. Experience analyzing downstream impacts across interconnected systems, including data mappings, source formats, system-of-record dependencies, reporting, and operational workflows. Experience working in a customer-service-oriented technology team supporting multiple business units and improving or automating complex operational processes. Working Conditions/Schedule: Typical schedule is Monday through Friday during regular business hours, with occasional evening or weekend work required for migrations, testing, launches, critical incident response, or peak enrollment cycle needs. This position is eligible for hybrid or remote work consistent with OSU policy, division expectations, business needs, and approved flexible work arrangements. This position may be expected to support critical enrollment systems during emergency, inclement weather, university curtailment, or other continuity events as assigned. This position does not require driving as an essential function unless separately assigned and approved. Primary Workday location: Corvallis (Main Campus). Remote type: Hybrid. The position is not formally designated essential. Salary Range: $86,244 - $136,800 Anticipated Start Date: 2026-10-12 Anticipated End Date: We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status. OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire. OSU is a fair chance employer committed to inclusive hiring. We encourage applications from candidates who bring a wide range of lived experience including involvement with the justice system. If this position has “critical or security-sensitive” responsibilities and/or driving duties and you are selected as a finalist, your initial job offer will be contingent upon the results of a job-related pre-employment check (such as a background check, motor vehicle history check, sexual misconduct reference check, etc.). Background check results do not automatically disqualify a candidate. Take a look at our Background Checks website including the for candidates section for more details. If you have questions or concerns about the pre-employment check, please contact OSU’s Employee and Labor Relations team at employee.relations@oregonstate.edu ."
    },
    "https://oregonstate.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/OSU_Careers_Site/job/Corvallis-Main-Campus/Director-of-Industry-Engagement---Economic-Impact_JR0000906": {
      "title": "Director of Industry Engagement & Economic Impact",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-16\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nDepartment: Division of Research and Innovation Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying you will be required to attach the following electronic documents:   1) A resume/CV; and   2) A cover letter indicating how your qualifications and experience have prepared you for this position. Starting salary will be commensurate with skills, education, and experience. For additional information please contact: Konnie Handschuch or Sara Begley at Konnie.Handschuch@oregonstate.edu or Sara.Begley@oregonstate.edu     Job Summary: The Division of Research and Innovation is seeking a Director of Industry Engagement & Economic Impact. This is a full-time (1.00 FTE), 12-month, professional faculty position. Key Responsibilities: Support implementation and continuous improvement of an enterprise CRM system and associated data infrastructure for industry engagement. Develop institutional metrics, reporting frameworks, and dashboards that measure economic engagement and support the Prosperity Widely Shared strategic plan. Design and manage the university’s industry engagement “front door,” including partner intake, navigation, referral pathways, and service standards. Build institutional capacity by supporting faculty and staff through training, coaching, and development of best practices for external engagement. Partner with colleges, research centers, and administrative units to coordinate economic engagement activities and strengthen cross-campus collaboration. Develop business intelligence and actionable insights that inform strategic decision-making and demonstrate institutional economic impact. Steward select strategic industry relationships and support university-wide engagement initiatives as organizational systems mature. The Director of Industry Engagement & Economic Impact serves as a strategic leader within Oregon State University's Economic Development and Industry Relations (EDIR) organization, reporting directly to the Associate Vice President for Economic Development and Industry Relations. As a key contributor to the university's strategic plan, Prosperity Widely Shared (PWS) , this position advances the Economic Engagement & Impact Domain by helping design, implement, and scale the systems, processes, partnerships, and infrastructure that enable Oregon State to maximize its economic impact across Oregon and beyond. In this role, the Director leads the development of foundational capabilities that strengthen the university's approach to industry engagement and economic development. Key responsibilities include establishing and optimizing economic engagement systems such as customer relationship management (CRM) platforms, institutional metrics and reporting frameworks, business intelligence capabilities, faculty development initiatives, and partner intake and engagement processes. By building and integrating these core functions, the Director ensures that Oregon State can effectively coordinate and support industry engagement activities at an institutional scale. As these capabilities mature, the Director increasingly serves as a steward of strategic industry relationships and university-wide engagement efforts. Working collaboratively across colleges, research units, administrative divisions, and external stakeholders, the Director helps transform distributed activities into a cohesive institutional strategy. The position acts as a connector and collaborative problem-solver, aligning people, resources, and opportunities to foster meaningful partnerships that accelerate innovation, support workforce development, and drive economic growth. The Director is a builder of systems and a champion of service excellence, creating accessible pathways for startups, established industry partners, government agencies, economic development organizations, and global enterprises to engage with Oregon State's talent, expertise, and research capabilities. Through the development of actionable insights, strategic training opportunities, and scalable engagement models, the Director enhances the university's ability to operate at the speed of business and strengthens its role as a catalyst for equitable and widely shared prosperity. As Oregon State advances its ambitious vision for economic transformation, the Director plays a critical role in increasing the visibility, accessibility, and impact of the university's innovation ecosystem. This position requires a highly collaborative and strategic leader who can navigate complexity, build consensus, and create sustainable structures that enable cross-sector collaboration and expand Oregon State's influence as a premier partner for industry and economic development. Qualifications: Required Qualifications: Bachelor's degree in business administration, public administration, economic development, higher education administration, organizational leadership, communications, or a related field. Six to eight years of progressively responsible professional experience in one or more of the following areas: economic development, industry engagement, strategic partnerships, program management, business development, research administration, innovation and entrepreneurship, higher education administration, or a related field. Demonstrated experience developing, implementing, and managing cross-functional programs, systems, processes, or initiatives that involve multiple stakeholders and organizational units. Experience building and maintaining collaborative relationships with internal and external partners, including academic, industry, government, nonprofit, or community organizations. Demonstrated ability to analyze data, synthesize information from multiple sources, and develop recommendations that support strategic decision-making and organizational objectives. Experience managing complex projects with multiple priorities, competing deadlines, and diverse stakeholder interests. Demonstrated ability to communicate effectively with a wide range of audiences through written, verbal, presentation, and facilitation skills. Experience working in a collaborative, matrixed, or decentralized organizational environment requiring influence, coordination, and consensus-building across organizational boundaries. Demonstrated success working effectively with individuals and organizations from a wide variety of backgrounds, experiences, and perspectives. This position is designated as a critical or security-sensitive position; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a criminal history check and be determined to be position qualified as per University Standard: 05-010 et seq. Incumbents are required to self-report convictions and those in youth programs may have additional criminal history checks every 24 months. This position requires driving a university vehicle or a personal vehicle on behalf of the university; therefore, the incumbent must successfully complete a motor vehicle history check, possess and maintain a current, valid driver's license in their state of residence, be determined to be position qualified and self-report convictions as per University Policy 05-030. Preferred Qualifications: Master's degree or higher in business administration, public administration, economic development, higher education administration, organizational leadership, or a related field. Experience within a research-intensive university, economic development organization, government agency, industry association, or innovation ecosystem. Experience implementing or managing customer relationship management (CRM) systems, business intelligence platforms, data analytics, reporting frameworks, or enterprise-wide process improvement initiatives. Knowledge of university research, technology transfer, workforce development, industry-sponsored research, innovation, entrepreneurship, or economic engagement activities. Experience developing performance metrics, dashboards, impact measurement frameworks, or strategic reporting systems. Experience convening and facilitating cross-sector partnerships involving higher education, industry, government, and community stakeholders. Experience supporting organizational change management, strategic planning, or large-scale institutional initiatives. Working Conditions/Schedule: This position is located on the Corvallis campus. The position is open to hybrid work as approved by the supervisor. Some evenings and weekends may be required. Some in-state and out-of-state travel for meetings and events will be required. Salary Range: $110,000 - $145,000 Anticipated Start Date: 2026-11-01 Anticipated End Date: We are an Equal Opportunity Employer, including disability, protected veteran, and other protected status. OSU will conduct a review of the National Sex Offender Public website prior to hire. OSU is a fair chance employer committed to inclusive hiring. We encourage applications from candidates who bring a wide range of lived experience including involvement with the justice system. If this position has “critical or security-sensitive” responsibilities and/or driving duties and you are selected as a finalist, your initial job offer will be contingent upon the results of a job-related pre-employment check (such as a background check, motor vehicle history check, sexual misconduct reference check, etc.). Background check results do not automatically disqualify a candidate. Take a look at our Background Checks website including the for candidates section for more details. If you have questions or concerns about the pre-employment check, please contact OSU’s Employee and Labor Relations team at employee.relations@oregonstate.edu ."
    },
